WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.250
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Welcome to
the Huberman Lab podcast,

00:00:02.250 --> 00:00:04.920
where we discuss science
and science-based tools

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:06.412
for everyday life.

00:00:06.412 --> 00:00:09.070
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:09.070 --> 00:00:10.840
I'm Andrew Huberman
and I'm a professor

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:13.240
of neurobiology
and ophthalmology

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:14.920
at Stanford School of Medicine.

00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:17.080
Today, my guest
is Jocko Willink.

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:19.540
Jocko Willink is a
retired Navy SEAL

00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:22.810
and author of numerous important
books on leadership and team

00:00:22.810 --> 00:00:26.440
dynamics and the host
of the Jocko Podcast.

00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:28.840
During his 20-year
career with the US Navy,

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:31.390
Jocko served with SEAL
Team 3 as commander

00:00:31.390 --> 00:00:34.060
of Task Unit Bruiser
in Ramadi, Iraq,

00:00:34.060 --> 00:00:35.590
and elsewhere in
the Middle East,

00:00:35.590 --> 00:00:38.450
as well as deployments
in Asia and Europe.

00:00:38.450 --> 00:00:40.660
After retiring from
the Navy, Jocko

00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:42.250
used his experience
and knowledge

00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:45.070
gleaned from his time in
the SEAL teams as a way

00:00:45.070 --> 00:00:47.260
to develop tools
that anybody can

00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:49.750
use to develop their
leadership skills,

00:00:49.750 --> 00:00:52.540
both for leading themselves
and for leading others.

00:00:52.540 --> 00:00:54.640
That took the form of
several important books,

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:57.130
the first of which
was published in 2015

00:00:57.130 --> 00:00:59.200
and is entitled
Extreme Ownership:

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:01.870
How US Navy Seals Lead and Win.

00:01:01.870 --> 00:01:03.700
He has also authored
several books

00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:06.970
for kids about leadership,
personal development

00:01:06.970 --> 00:01:09.460
and how to navigate
various aspects of life.

00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:12.730
I've read both Extreme Ownership
and The Way of the Warrior Kid,

00:01:12.730 --> 00:01:15.340
and I found them to
be immensely useful

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:18.400
in terms of actionable
information and understanding

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:21.190
of oneself and different
kinds of relationships,

00:01:21.190 --> 00:01:23.050
both in and out
of the workplace.

00:01:23.050 --> 00:01:25.000
Typically, guests on
the Huberman Lab Podcast

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:28.060
are scientists
and/or clinicians.

00:01:28.060 --> 00:01:31.030
It was some time ago that I was
a guest on the Jocko Podcast.

00:01:31.030 --> 00:01:33.880
And during the course of our
conversation on his podcast,

00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:37.150
we quickly realized that many
of the science-based tools

00:01:37.150 --> 00:01:39.160
that my laboratory
has focused on

00:01:39.160 --> 00:01:40.780
and that I've used
over the years

00:01:40.780 --> 00:01:42.700
and shared on the
Huberman Lab Podcast

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:45.760
had direct overlap
and parallel with many

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:48.280
of the tools that Jocko and
other members of the SEAL teams

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:50.830
had arrived at
independently-- that is,

00:01:50.830 --> 00:01:52.870
without knowledge of
the underlying science.

00:01:52.870 --> 00:01:54.910
And in fact, he
had many more tools

00:01:54.910 --> 00:01:57.550
that he had incorporated during
his years in the SEAL teams

00:01:57.550 --> 00:01:59.290
as well as in
business leadership,

00:01:59.290 --> 00:02:02.470
in family and elsewhere
in life, that I quickly

00:02:02.470 --> 00:02:04.390
realized it would
be an enormously

00:02:04.390 --> 00:02:07.370
valuable conversation to
have him on this podcast

00:02:07.370 --> 00:02:10.060
in order to share those tools
with the general public.

00:02:10.060 --> 00:02:12.370
During today's episode,
we discuss numerous tools

00:02:12.370 --> 00:02:15.250
that Jocko has taught and used
over the years in a number

00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:17.800
of different contexts,
including tools for generating

00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:20.770
more physical energy and
for generating more focus

00:02:20.770 --> 00:02:22.330
and cognitive energy.

00:02:22.330 --> 00:02:25.240
And for navigating
sticking points,

00:02:25.240 --> 00:02:28.210
that is how to deal
with lack of motivation,

00:02:28.210 --> 00:02:30.160
how to deal with
difficult relationships

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:31.600
in the workplace and elsewhere.

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:34.030
And perhaps most
importantly, how

00:02:34.030 --> 00:02:36.118
to think about and
navigate the self.

00:02:36.118 --> 00:02:37.660
In fact, we spend
quite a bit of time

00:02:37.660 --> 00:02:41.530
talking about this notion of
the self and one's self identity

00:02:41.530 --> 00:02:44.050
and how self identity
plays into our ability

00:02:44.050 --> 00:02:47.950
to engage in actions of specific
types consistently over time,

00:02:47.950 --> 00:02:51.460
where it can hold us back,
how to gain better perspective

00:02:51.460 --> 00:02:54.670
and how to help others
gain better perspective so

00:02:54.670 --> 00:02:57.920
that we can work better
with them and them with us.

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:00.760
We also go deep into the
likely scientific mechanisms

00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:03.430
underlying why the tools
that Jocko teaches and uses

00:03:03.430 --> 00:03:04.838
are so effective.

00:03:04.838 --> 00:03:06.880
In fact, one thing that
you'll immediately notice

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:08.620
is that Jocko was
writing things down

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:11.260
and I was writing things down
throughout the conversation.

00:03:11.260 --> 00:03:13.450
And that just reflects the
fact that he's not just

00:03:13.450 --> 00:03:15.820
an immensely powerful
teacher, he's

00:03:15.820 --> 00:03:19.130
also a practitioner
and an avid learner.

00:03:19.130 --> 00:03:20.800
He's always seeking knowledge.

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:22.750
So we kick back
and forth our ideas

00:03:22.750 --> 00:03:25.990
about what likely does and does
not underlie different tools

00:03:25.990 --> 00:03:29.020
and techniques, focusing,
of course, mostly

00:03:29.020 --> 00:03:32.140
on what works in the
practical sense in the world.

00:03:32.140 --> 00:03:35.660
What I can assure you is that
by the end of today's episode,

00:03:35.660 --> 00:03:39.520
thanks to Jocko's immense
generosity and curiosity,

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:42.190
you will come away with
a large number of tools

00:03:42.190 --> 00:03:45.910
and much richer understanding
of how to navigate and enhance

00:03:45.910 --> 00:03:48.490
mental health, physical
health and performance

00:03:48.490 --> 00:03:49.990
in all aspects of life.

00:03:49.990 --> 00:03:51.880
Before we begin, I'd
like to emphasize

00:03:51.880 --> 00:03:54.400
that this podcast is separate
from my teaching and research

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:55.450
roles at Stanford.

00:03:55.450 --> 00:03:57.760
It is, however, part
of my desire and effort

00:03:57.760 --> 00:03:59.920
to bring zero cost to
consumer information

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:01.930
about science and
science-related tools

00:04:01.930 --> 00:04:03.220
to the general public.

