And she goes, nope.

00:10:45.540 --> 00:10:50.250
But if you had to draw a Navy
SEAL, that's what you'd draw.

00:10:50.250 --> 00:10:52.990
So I think for a
lot of people, you

00:10:52.990 --> 00:10:55.930
embody their notion of a
number of different things,

00:10:55.930 --> 00:10:57.190
some of which you talk about.

00:10:57.190 --> 00:10:59.550
But some of which,
when you open up a bit

00:10:59.550 --> 00:11:02.440
and really get specific
about work in the military

00:11:02.440 --> 00:11:05.580
and work in daily life and what
it is to be you, but really,

00:11:05.580 --> 00:11:09.360
what it is to be a human being,
some important contradictions

00:11:09.360 --> 00:11:11.460
also emerge, right?

00:11:11.460 --> 00:11:15.250
Obviously, discipline is a theme
that people associate with you,

00:11:15.250 --> 00:11:15.750
right?

00:11:15.750 --> 00:11:18.210
In my view and I think in
the view of a lot of people,

00:11:18.210 --> 00:11:19.230
you embody discipline.

00:11:19.230 --> 00:11:22.240
So today, I definitely want
to talk about routines,

00:11:22.240 --> 00:11:23.970
but also mindsets.

00:11:23.970 --> 00:11:26.880
But also things
that you do and ways

00:11:26.880 --> 00:11:30.270
that you approach things that
might not contradict, but not

00:11:30.270 --> 00:11:31.710
be so obvious to people.

00:11:31.710 --> 00:11:33.860
Might be a little
bit counterintuitive.

00:11:33.860 --> 00:11:36.360
And in addition to that, you
have a lot of different aspects

00:11:36.360 --> 00:11:36.780
to your life.

00:11:36.780 --> 00:11:38.197
In addition to
running businesses,

00:11:38.197 --> 00:11:40.050
you're a family man,
you have children

00:11:40.050 --> 00:11:41.363
and married a long time.

00:11:41.363 --> 00:11:42.780
And so you have a
lot of knowledge

00:11:42.780 --> 00:11:44.072
from different domains of life.

00:11:44.072 --> 00:11:46.740
So with your permission, I'd
like to dive into all of them

00:11:46.740 --> 00:11:48.360
over the next 26 hours.

00:11:48.360 --> 00:11:49.740
- Let's dive.

00:11:49.740 --> 00:11:51.390
- Great.

00:11:51.390 --> 00:11:55.020
I'm fascinated by this
idea of sense of self.

00:11:55.020 --> 00:11:57.870
I feel like all of us can look
back to a time early in life

00:11:57.870 --> 00:12:01.290
when we first had
some experience.

00:12:01.290 --> 00:12:04.800
Could be in art class,
could be fishing,

00:12:04.800 --> 00:12:07.890
could be sport,
doesn't really matter

00:12:07.890 --> 00:12:09.640
what the exact experience was.

00:12:09.640 --> 00:12:12.960
But where we first realized
that there are really

00:12:12.960 --> 00:12:14.160
cool things in the world.

00:12:14.160 --> 00:12:17.790
Like something that turned us
on at the level of excitement.

00:12:17.790 --> 00:12:20.110
Or maybe scared us, or
something like that.

00:12:20.110 --> 00:12:22.248
Do you have any recollection
of such an event?

00:12:22.248 --> 00:12:23.790
Maybe not the first
one, but you ever

00:12:23.790 --> 00:12:26.520
remember hearing or seeing
something as a young kid,

00:12:26.520 --> 00:12:29.400
and maybe you could tell us
how young, and just thinking,

00:12:29.400 --> 00:12:31.230
yeah, more of that please.

00:12:31.230 --> 00:12:33.630
[JOCKO CHUCKLES]

00:12:33.630 --> 00:12:35.670
- A lot of times when
people ask questions

00:12:35.670 --> 00:12:38.903
along this line of when
was there a moment--

00:12:38.903 --> 00:12:41.070
when was there a moment
that you realized discipline

00:12:41.070 --> 00:12:43.153
or when was there a moment
you realized leadership

00:12:43.153 --> 00:12:46.160
or when was there a moment
you realized detachment,

00:12:46.160 --> 00:12:47.910
kind of like your
question, when was there

00:12:47.910 --> 00:12:51.570
a moment you realized, for lack
of a better way of saying it,

00:12:51.570 --> 00:12:53.640
I'm a person.

00:12:53.640 --> 00:12:55.770
I'm a person with
my own thoughts

00:12:55.770 --> 00:12:57.760
and I can make things happen.

00:12:57.760 --> 00:13:02.190
And for me, all those answers
are usually fairly gradual.

00:13:02.190 --> 00:13:04.830
There's a little thing that
indicates, you get a clue

00:13:04.830 --> 00:13:06.630
and then you move a little
bit further down that road.

00:13:06.630 --> 00:13:07.740
Then you get another
clue and then

00:13:07.740 --> 00:13:09.130
you move a little bit
further down that road

00:13:09.130 --> 00:13:10.172
and you get another clue.

00:13:12.570 --> 00:13:15.780
So that's what I
would say, for me,

00:13:15.780 --> 00:13:20.040
life was like when
I was a little kid.

00:13:20.040 --> 00:13:26.650
I was kind of slowly
discovering that I was a person,

00:13:26.650 --> 00:13:27.325
I was a human.

00:13:30.130 --> 00:13:34.090
I remember my mom
took me shopping.

00:13:34.090 --> 00:13:36.130
I was probably
about 10 years old

00:13:36.130 --> 00:13:38.590
and I needed to get
pants for school.

00:13:38.590 --> 00:13:42.160
And my mom took me shopping.

00:13:42.160 --> 00:13:47.500
And when I went into the store,
there was a girl that was--

00:13:47.500 --> 00:13:49.780
I don't know what they're
called in a store.

00:13:49.780 --> 00:13:52.240
A retail sales girl?

00:13:52.240 --> 00:13:54.880
She was probably about 16.

00:13:54.880 --> 00:13:57.730
And I started chatting her up.

00:13:57.730 --> 00:14:00.640
And I kind of recognize
it a little bit

00:14:00.640 --> 00:14:03.460
but I sort of didn't too and I
just was chatting to this girl

00:14:03.460 --> 00:14:04.760
and I was making her laugh.

00:14:04.760 --> 00:14:06.802
And I was having a good
time with the whole thing

00:14:06.802 --> 00:14:09.850
and putting on the pants
and spinning around,

00:14:09.850 --> 00:14:11.980
and she was laughing.

00:14:11.980 --> 00:14:16.000
And I remember when we left
the store with the pants

00:14:16.000 --> 00:14:19.180
and my mom was sort
of talking to me

00:14:19.180 --> 00:14:22.120
about the fact that, "What were
you trying to do to that girl?"

00:14:22.120 --> 00:14:25.810
And I was thinking to
myself, well, I kind of

00:14:25.810 --> 00:14:27.413
liked that girl, she was pretty.

00:14:27.413 --> 00:14:29.330
And I don't know why
that popped into my head.

00:14:29.330 --> 00:14:31.247
But I just remember
thinking, hey man, there's

00:14:31.247 --> 00:14:34.735
a whole world out there and
let's go make it happen.

00:14:34.735 --> 00:14:35.860
- Yeah, it's a great story.

00:14:35.860 --> 00:14:38.230
Because I think it really
speaks to this thing

00:14:38.230 --> 00:14:40.782
that you mentioned, which
is that when we first

00:14:40.782 --> 00:14:42.490
start to realize we
have a sense of self,

00:14:42.490 --> 00:14:44.907
it has something to do with
cause and effect on the world,

00:14:44.907 --> 00:14:48.850
like we can have an impact
in some way on things outside

00:14:48.850 --> 00:14:50.740
of us, outside of our home.

00:14:50.740 --> 00:14:52.870
Lately I've been reading
a lot of psychology

00:14:52.870 --> 00:14:54.828
and I've been listening
to some of your content

00:14:54.828 --> 00:14:57.040
and I definitely want to
talk to you about a study

00:14:57.040 --> 00:14:59.380
that you covered
related to these--

00:14:59.380 --> 00:15:02.470
it's a brutal experiment with
these kids that either had

00:15:02.470 --> 00:15:04.048
stutter or didn't have stutter.

00:15:04.048 --> 00:15:05.840
I want to get into that
a little bit later.

00:15:05.840 --> 00:15:08.470
But what we do and
how we treat people

00:15:08.470 --> 00:15:12.560
and how we receive feedback and
give feedback has a big impact.

00:15:12.560 --> 00:15:15.940
But I think some of that happens
just in our own relationship

00:15:15.940 --> 00:15:16.990
to things in the world.

00:15:16.990 --> 00:15:19.330
The older Hungarian
psychologist, I'm learning,

00:15:19.330 --> 00:15:21.370
had this idea of
two kinds of people.

00:15:21.370 --> 00:15:23.620
They literally thought there
were two kinds of people.

00:15:23.620 --> 00:15:26.860
There are generators
and projectors.

00:15:26.860 --> 00:15:29.410
And generators are
people that are just--

00:15:29.410 --> 00:15:32.080
from a very young
age, they realize

00:15:32.080 --> 00:15:35.320
they can impact other people,
positively, negatively.

00:15:35.320 --> 00:15:38.950
And they want to create
things in the world,

00:15:38.950 --> 00:15:40.420
they want to generate stuff.

00:15:40.420 --> 00:15:43.270
And they go wow, I can actually
build stuff and break stuff.

00:15:43.270 --> 00:15:46.492
Blow stuff up, maybe,
but also help things.

00:15:46.492 --> 00:15:47.950
And then there are
these projectors

00:15:47.950 --> 00:15:52.080
that like to just
reflect on what they see.

00:15:52.080 --> 00:15:55.170
And they made the really
important point, I think,

00:15:55.170 --> 00:15:58.200
that it's not the generators
are good and projectors are bad.

00:15:58.200 --> 00:16:00.480
The world needs both,
that they really work

00:16:00.480 --> 00:16:02.100
in a kind of symbiotic way.

00:16:02.100 --> 00:16:03.937
But your story
captures the essence

00:16:03.937 --> 00:16:05.520
of what it is to be
a generator, which

00:16:05.520 --> 00:16:07.990
is that by doing certain
things, you can have an impact.

00:16:07.990 --> 00:16:09.657
And it feeds back to
you and it's likely

00:16:09.657 --> 00:16:11.740
that they receive
something from it as well.

00:16:11.740 --> 00:16:12.240
- Yeah.

00:16:12.240 --> 00:16:15.622
And where this all came to
fruition, as I now piece

00:16:15.622 --> 00:16:17.580
together as you're talking
through this stuff--

00:16:17.580 --> 00:16:22.050
Look, when I joined the
military, you join the military

00:16:22.050 --> 00:16:24.000
and you get a blank slate.

00:16:24.000 --> 00:16:26.340
So no one cares
where you came from,

00:16:26.340 --> 00:16:27.720
no one cares what you did.

00:16:27.720 --> 00:16:29.432
You were the captain
of the football

00:16:29.432 --> 00:16:31.390
team, captain of the
soccer team, no one cares.

00:16:31.390 --> 00:16:32.848
No one cares what
your grades were,

00:16:32.848 --> 00:16:34.773
no one cares what
you got on the SATs.

00:16:34.773 --> 00:16:35.940
No one cares about anything.

00:16:35.940 --> 00:16:37.350
You're a blank slate.

00:16:37.350 --> 00:16:41.140
And then with that
blank slate, it is, hey,

00:16:41.140 --> 00:16:44.730
if you do this thing,
if you perform this task

00:16:44.730 --> 00:16:50.100
and you perform it well,
you will get recognition.

00:16:50.100 --> 00:16:52.890
You will hopefully
get more control

00:16:52.890 --> 00:16:57.720
over your own destiny, which
is the ultimate in compensation

00:16:57.720 --> 00:16:59.040
for human beings.

00:16:59.040 --> 00:17:01.702
To have more control
over your own destiny

00:17:01.702 --> 00:17:02.910
is the ultimate compensation.

00:17:02.910 --> 00:17:05.310
You and I were talking
before we hit record,

00:17:05.310 --> 00:17:07.060
you can have all the
money in the world,

00:17:07.060 --> 00:17:09.180
but if you don't
control what you're

00:17:09.180 --> 00:17:11.369
doing every day or
at least you don't

00:17:11.369 --> 00:17:13.930
control most of what you're
doing, then it's not worth it.

00:17:13.930 --> 00:17:16.140
The reason people
try and make money

00:17:16.140 --> 00:17:19.390
is so they can have more
autonomy in their life.

00:17:19.390 --> 00:17:22.148
And so in the military,
it becomes very clear--

00:17:22.148 --> 00:17:23.940
and it became very
clear to me very quickly

00:17:23.940 --> 00:17:28.860
that if I performed well, I
actually got a lot more freedom

00:17:28.860 --> 00:17:29.850
with what I did.

00:17:29.850 --> 00:17:31.230
Even in boot camp.

00:17:31.230 --> 00:17:33.150
If you pass an
inspection in boot camp,

00:17:33.150 --> 00:17:35.102
you don't have to
redo your locker

00:17:35.102 --> 00:17:37.560
or you don't have to make your
bed again because you did it

00:17:37.560 --> 00:17:38.830
right the first time.

00:17:38.830 --> 00:17:40.870
And so you have an
extra 15 minutes.

00:17:40.870 --> 00:17:43.230
And so for me,
really, that's when

00:17:43.230 --> 00:17:47.898
I started to realize, oh,
what I'm doing right now is it

00:17:47.898 --> 00:17:49.440
going to impact not
only what's going

00:17:49.440 --> 00:17:50.815
to happen to me
in the next hour,

00:17:50.815 --> 00:17:53.010
but in the next two years,
three years, five years.

00:17:53.010 --> 00:17:55.530
And I think that's the
biggest miss that we have

00:17:55.530 --> 00:17:56.640
when we're growing up.

00:17:56.640 --> 00:17:59.220
And I know you had your
challenges and tribulations

00:17:59.220 --> 00:18:01.470
as you were growing up because
you didn't realize, oh,

00:18:01.470 --> 00:18:03.630
what I'm doing right now
is going to affect where

00:18:03.630 --> 00:18:05.548
I'm going to be in the future.

00:18:05.548 --> 00:18:07.840
And it didn't happen until
you were out of high school,

00:18:07.840 --> 00:18:09.240
when you went to junior
college and you're like,

00:18:09.240 --> 00:18:11.670
oh wait a second, I can
actually put my life together

00:18:11.670 --> 00:18:12.780
in a positive way.

00:18:12.780 --> 00:18:16.088
When you're 14,
you're thinking, hey

00:18:16.088 --> 00:18:17.380
what am I going to do tomorrow.

00:18:17.380 --> 00:18:19.118
That's basically
future operations or--

00:18:19.118 --> 00:18:20.410
what am I going to do tomorrow.

00:18:20.410 --> 00:18:21.580
- Would've been far
ahead for me at 14.

00:18:21.580 --> 00:18:24.400
I was like, where am I going to
get the Slurpee, which curves

00:18:24.400 --> 00:18:26.410
am I going to hit
skateboarding and where are we

00:18:26.410 --> 00:18:27.610
going to play
video games tonight

00:18:27.610 --> 00:18:28.930
or what girls are we
going to hang out with.

00:18:28.930 --> 00:18:30.388
That was kind of
the mindset at 14.

00:18:30.388 --> 00:18:30.888
- Yeah.

00:18:30.888 --> 00:18:33.010
And then at some point,
you learned, and so did I,

00:18:33.010 --> 00:18:37.150
oh, the actions that
I take now are either

00:18:37.150 --> 00:18:38.660
going to positively
impact my future

00:18:38.660 --> 00:18:40.410
or they're to negatively
impact my future.

00:18:40.410 --> 00:18:44.203
And the more I focused
on doing things

00:18:44.203 --> 00:18:46.120
that are going to
positively impact my future,

00:18:46.120 --> 00:18:48.250
the better my life became.

00:18:48.250 --> 00:18:51.280
And I think that's
a very huge lesson

00:18:51.280 --> 00:18:55.330
to learn that I know I didn't
figure out for quite some time.

00:18:55.330 --> 00:18:59.530
- Yeah the idea of
investments and withdrawals,

00:18:59.530 --> 00:19:01.450
or understanding
that early in life

00:19:01.450 --> 00:19:04.060
in terms of health behaviors
and intellectual behaviors.

00:19:04.060 --> 00:19:05.590
And your point
about the military

00:19:05.590 --> 00:19:07.090
is a really interesting
one, I never

00:19:07.090 --> 00:19:08.590
thought about the
military that way,

00:19:08.590 --> 00:19:11.080
that there's this blank
slate when you get in there.

00:19:11.080 --> 00:19:13.720
And before we started, we
were talking a little bit

00:19:13.720 --> 00:19:16.000
about the kinds of
mindsets and people

00:19:16.000 --> 00:19:17.770
that the military attracts.

00:19:17.770 --> 00:19:20.020
And I'd love for you to
elaborate on that again.

00:19:20.020 --> 00:19:21.520
You mentioned
something interesting,

00:19:21.520 --> 00:19:23.800
this notion of garrison.

00:19:23.800 --> 00:19:26.788
- Interesting word
in its own right.

00:19:26.788 --> 00:19:29.080
What kind of people do you
think the military attracts?

00:19:29.080 --> 00:19:30.880
And then within the
military, do you

00:19:30.880 --> 00:19:33.820
start to see some kind of
predictable bifurcations, where

00:19:33.820 --> 00:19:36.310
certain people go down
one track and other people

00:19:36.310 --> 00:19:37.120
go down another?

00:19:37.120 --> 00:19:39.700
I have a few friends from the
SEAL teams, as we both know.

00:19:39.700 --> 00:19:41.770
And I've heard sometimes
about the distinction

00:19:41.770 --> 00:19:45.100
between officers and enlisted
guys, this kind of thing.

00:19:45.100 --> 00:19:48.190
But maybe this
question I'm asking

00:19:48.190 --> 00:19:50.800
is more across the board
for all of military.

00:19:50.800 --> 00:19:54.370
And for people
listening, whether or not

00:19:54.370 --> 00:19:57.920
they are interested in military
or not for their own life,

00:19:57.920 --> 00:20:00.490
I think there's an interesting
lesson, this idea of who

00:20:00.490 --> 00:20:01.850
is attracted to the military.

00:20:01.850 --> 00:20:04.630
Is it like people who want to
instill order on themselves

00:20:04.630 --> 00:20:07.280
or is it people who want to
instill order on other people,

00:20:07.280 --> 00:20:07.990
or both?

00:20:07.990 --> 00:20:09.220
- Yeah.

00:20:09.220 --> 00:20:11.950
There's a really good
book and I ended up

00:20:11.950 --> 00:20:14.650
doing about four podcasts
on this book, which

00:20:14.650 --> 00:20:17.785
is called The Psychology
of Military Incompetence.

00:20:17.785 --> 00:20:18.910
And when I first saw that--

00:20:18.910 --> 00:20:19.930
- What an amazing title.

00:20:19.930 --> 00:20:20.430
- I know.

00:20:20.430 --> 00:20:22.900
And when I first saw that
title, I thought to myself,

00:20:22.900 --> 00:20:27.460
oh, this is some academic that's
going to look at the military

00:20:27.460 --> 00:20:29.590
and bash it.

00:20:29.590 --> 00:20:31.630
But I did a little
research and it turned out

00:20:31.630 --> 00:20:32.800
that the guy that had
written the book--

00:20:32.800 --> 00:20:34.342
I can't think of
his name right now--

00:20:34.342 --> 00:20:36.940
he was a guy that had served
in World War II, was wounded.

00:20:36.940 --> 00:20:40.250
I mean, this guy understood
what he was talking about.

00:20:40.250 --> 00:20:42.140
And it's really
an obvious concept

00:20:42.140 --> 00:20:43.600
once you think about it.

00:20:43.600 --> 00:20:47.240
The basic premise is
this, the military,

00:20:47.240 --> 00:20:49.550
when you look at it
from the outside,

00:20:49.550 --> 00:20:52.050
it's this orderly place.

00:20:52.050 --> 00:20:55.860
It's a place where
everything has a place.

00:20:55.860 --> 00:20:58.970
It's a place where if you have
a certain rank on your shoulder,

00:20:58.970 --> 00:21:03.110
you will command respect and
people have to listen to you.

00:21:03.110 --> 00:21:05.610
That's what it looks like.

00:21:05.610 --> 00:21:08.180
So it's an attractive
place for people

00:21:08.180 --> 00:21:11.600
that have an authoritarian
mindset, for people that

00:21:11.600 --> 00:21:14.590
want to just, hey, don't
question what I'm saying,

00:21:14.590 --> 00:21:16.340
just shut up and do
what I tell you to do.

00:21:16.340 --> 00:21:17.510
There's people that love that.

00:21:17.510 --> 00:21:18.950
There's people that
want to live like that.

00:21:18.950 --> 00:21:20.840
You've worked for them,
I've worked for them.

00:21:20.840 --> 00:21:24.620
We've experienced those type
of people throughout our lives.

00:21:24.620 --> 00:21:26.900
That authoritarian
mindset that just

00:21:26.900 --> 00:21:29.880
want to bark orders and
have people listen to them.

00:21:29.880 --> 00:21:33.950
And so when those people are
14 years old or 16 years old

00:21:33.950 --> 00:21:35.720
or 18 years old, they
look at the military

00:21:35.720 --> 00:21:37.220
and they see a uniform.

00:21:37.220 --> 00:21:39.290
And they see people
saluting and they

00:21:39.290 --> 00:21:40.910
see orders being carried out.

00:21:40.910 --> 00:21:42.830
And they think, that's
where I'm going to go.

00:21:42.830 --> 00:21:46.250
And I can get the
respect that I deserve.

00:21:46.250 --> 00:21:49.440
And the military certainly
attracts people like that.

00:21:49.440 --> 00:21:56.700
And those people that have that
highly-disciplined and orderly

00:21:56.700 --> 00:22:00.460
mindset can do well
inside the military,

00:22:00.460 --> 00:22:02.885
especially in garrison.

00:22:02.885 --> 00:22:05.010
Again, we were talking
about this earlier, the word

00:22:05.010 --> 00:22:05.520
garrison.

00:22:05.520 --> 00:22:08.340
I don't think there's a civilian
equivalent to this word,

00:22:08.340 --> 00:22:11.350
but it basically means
the noncombat situation.

00:22:11.350 --> 00:22:13.830
So when you're out
on the parade field,

00:22:13.830 --> 00:22:18.490
when you're going
through schooling where

00:22:18.490 --> 00:22:21.340
there's no combat involved,
when you're marching.

00:22:21.340 --> 00:22:24.310
Those kind of things,
we call that garrison.

00:22:24.310 --> 00:22:25.030
It's in the rear.

00:22:25.030 --> 00:22:26.437
It's not in combat.

00:22:26.437 --> 00:22:28.270
And the people with an
authoritarian mindset

00:22:28.270 --> 00:22:31.220
actually do pretty well
in garrison situations.

00:22:31.220 --> 00:22:31.720
Why?

00:22:31.720 --> 00:22:34.563
Because things are orderly
and you can predict what's

00:22:34.563 --> 00:22:35.230
going to happen.

00:22:35.230 --> 00:22:37.360
And you do get a
certain issue of gear

00:22:37.360 --> 00:22:39.962
and that gear is going
to be delivered on time

00:22:39.962 --> 00:22:41.920
and you're going to shoot
this number of rounds

00:22:41.920 --> 00:22:42.700
down at the range.

00:22:42.700 --> 00:22:45.160
And everything is going
to go according to plan,

00:22:45.160 --> 00:22:48.230
that's what garrison is.

00:22:48.230 --> 00:22:49.962
And so those people
join the military,

00:22:49.962 --> 00:22:51.670
they're attracted to
that and they end up

00:22:51.670 --> 00:22:54.960
doing well in peacetime.

00:22:54.960 --> 00:22:59.550
Now unfortunately for them,
combat is a lot different.

00:22:59.550 --> 00:23:01.890
Nothing goes the way
it's supposed to go.

00:23:01.890 --> 00:23:04.080
The bullets don't get
delivered on time,

00:23:04.080 --> 00:23:06.990
the enemy has a vote on the
way things are going to unfold.

00:23:06.990 --> 00:23:10.480
And you end up in combat being
in very chaotic situations.

00:23:10.480 --> 00:23:15.850
So the type of person
that thrives in combat

00:23:15.850 --> 00:23:18.800
has a more open mind,
has a more flexible mind,

00:23:18.800 --> 00:23:22.310
is paying more
attention to the input

00:23:22.310 --> 00:23:24.180
that they're receiving
as opposed to someone

00:23:24.180 --> 00:23:25.430
with an authoritarian mindset.

00:23:25.430 --> 00:23:26.847
They don't listen
to anybody else.

00:23:26.847 --> 00:23:29.120
They make up their own
mind, they bark orders.

00:23:29.120 --> 00:23:31.400
With someone that has
a more open mindset,

00:23:31.400 --> 00:23:33.920
they're listening,
they're taking input,

00:23:33.920 --> 00:23:36.620
they're evolving their plan.

00:23:36.620 --> 00:23:40.130
And those type of people
excel in a combat situation.

00:23:40.130 --> 00:23:43.220
Now unfortunately, and this is
sort of the stereotype too, you

00:23:43.220 --> 00:23:48.170
take that dog of war
and you put him back

00:23:48.170 --> 00:23:50.420
into a garrison environment,
he doesn't do well.

00:23:50.420 --> 00:23:54.470
He's not showing up on
time for the inspection,

00:23:54.470 --> 00:23:56.887
didn't get his haircut, he
doesn't have his weapon cleaned

00:23:56.887 --> 00:23:58.887
the way it's supposed to
be cleaned because he's

00:23:58.887 --> 00:24:01.580
got his weapon ready for combat,
not ready for inspection.

00:24:01.580 --> 00:24:04.920
And so you get this-- there
are these two different types

00:24:04.920 --> 00:24:05.420
of people.

00:24:05.420 --> 00:24:07.753
And of course, with those two
different types of people,

00:24:07.753 --> 00:24:09.980
there's degrees going
one direction or other.

00:24:09.980 --> 00:24:13.790
But what you hope
for is someone that

00:24:13.790 --> 00:24:16.520
can play the game
on the garrison side

00:24:16.520 --> 00:24:19.280
and yet when it comes
time to go into combat,

00:24:19.280 --> 00:24:23.870
they can also open their mind,
be flexible, be creative.

00:24:23.870 --> 00:24:26.450
That's what you really want
is you want someone that is

00:24:26.450 --> 00:24:28.613
very good at solving problems.

00:24:28.613 --> 00:24:31.280
And to do that, you need to have
a creative, open mind to figure

00:24:31.280 --> 00:24:33.072
out how we're going to
deal with something.

00:24:33.072 --> 00:24:34.722
So I think that's
the stereotype.

00:24:34.722 --> 00:24:36.680
The stereotype is that
everyone in the military

00:24:36.680 --> 00:24:41.000
is sort of robotic and
falling into the hierarchy,

00:24:41.000 --> 00:24:43.370
and we bark orders and
people follow orders.

00:24:43.370 --> 00:24:45.963
And that's just not true.

00:24:45.963 --> 00:24:47.380
There is an element
of truth to it

00:24:47.380 --> 00:24:48.800
but it's not the whole truth.

00:24:48.800 --> 00:24:53.440
And certainly, if you look at
it history, the people that

00:24:53.440 --> 00:24:56.200
excel in combat are the
people that maybe have

00:24:56.200 --> 00:24:57.700
a little bit of a
rebellious streak,

00:24:57.700 --> 00:25:01.540
people that are just more
creative and more open minded.

00:25:01.540 --> 00:25:04.060
- Some of my friends
from the SEAL teams

00:25:04.060 --> 00:25:06.370
will sometimes throw
out stereotypes

00:25:06.370 --> 00:25:08.560
about the different
divisions in the military.

00:25:08.560 --> 00:25:12.370
Is there any truth to this
idea that Air Force types are

00:25:12.370 --> 00:25:16.420
one way and Marines are one
way and Navy is one way,

00:25:16.420 --> 00:25:17.590
army is a certain way?

00:25:17.590 --> 00:25:20.080
Sort of a general
contour of personality

00:25:20.080 --> 00:25:22.480
or is that just kind of
inside ball, joking around?

00:25:22.480 --> 00:25:23.780
- It's a little bit of both.

00:25:23.780 --> 00:25:28.330
I mean certainly, the Marine
Corps is steeped in tradition.

00:25:28.330 --> 00:25:33.670
And if you make a guess
at what a marine--

00:25:33.670 --> 00:25:36.520
if you had to guess what a
marine is going to be like,

00:25:36.520 --> 00:25:39.100
you're probably going
to be pretty close.

00:25:39.100 --> 00:25:41.890
Marines have an
incredible program

00:25:41.890 --> 00:25:45.650
to indoctrinate their people
into the culture of the Marine

00:25:45.650 --> 00:25:46.150
Corps.

00:25:46.150 --> 00:25:47.770
And the Marine Corps has an
incredibly strong culture.

00:25:47.770 --> 00:25:49.040
It's a powerful culture.

00:25:49.040 --> 00:25:51.457
I love the Marine Corps, I've
worked with the Marine Corps

00:25:51.457 --> 00:25:52.750
a ton and they're outstanding.

00:25:52.750 --> 00:25:55.690
As a generality, certainly you
could make those assumptions

00:25:55.690 --> 00:25:56.898
about the Marines in general.

00:25:56.898 --> 00:25:58.982
Now, does that mean that
every Marine is the same?

00:25:58.982 --> 00:25:59.867
No, absolutely not.

00:25:59.867 --> 00:26:02.200
Same thing with the army,
same thing with the Air Force,

00:26:02.200 --> 00:26:03.250
same thing with the Navy.

00:26:03.250 --> 00:26:05.080
You've got these
kind of stereotypes

00:26:05.080 --> 00:26:07.120
that exist for a reason.

00:26:07.120 --> 00:26:08.020
It's interesting too.

00:26:08.020 --> 00:26:09.940
One of my friends
named Ben Milligan

00:26:09.940 --> 00:26:12.970
wrote an incredible
book called By Water

00:26:12.970 --> 00:26:14.920
Beneath the Walls,
which I've given him

00:26:14.920 --> 00:26:17.620
a huge hassle about because it's
the worst title of all time.

00:26:17.620 --> 00:26:20.350
But it's certainly
the best book written

00:26:20.350 --> 00:26:23.860
about the SEAL teams'
history and where

00:26:23.860 --> 00:26:25.610
the SEAL teams came from.

00:26:25.610 --> 00:26:27.130
And it's interesting,
it's something

00:26:27.130 --> 00:26:30.820
that I had heard from
a SEAL officer that

00:26:30.820 --> 00:26:33.550
had given a speech years ago
at his change of command.

00:26:33.550 --> 00:26:36.340
And what he said
was he was trying

00:26:36.340 --> 00:26:40.160
to emphasize why the
SEAL teams were good.

00:26:40.160 --> 00:26:43.440
And one of the things he
said was in the army--

00:26:43.440 --> 00:26:45.190
and he was talking
historically-- he goes,

00:26:45.190 --> 00:26:47.920
hey, in the army, if you
start to lose a battle,

00:26:47.920 --> 00:26:50.410
you can just retreat
and run away.

00:26:50.410 --> 00:26:54.220
In the Navy, traditionally,
we're fighting on board a ship.

00:26:54.220 --> 00:26:55.780
And if that ship--

00:26:55.780 --> 00:26:56.680
we can't run away.

00:26:56.680 --> 00:26:58.900
We're fighting and
if we lose, we die.

00:26:58.900 --> 00:27:00.610
So SEALS can't quit.

00:27:00.610 --> 00:27:05.080
It was this, a little
over the top, expression.

00:27:05.080 --> 00:27:07.480
But when you take that
a little bit further,

00:27:07.480 --> 00:27:10.120
when you look at the
history of the Navy.

00:27:10.120 --> 00:27:13.730
If we were in the
Navy 150 years ago,

00:27:13.730 --> 00:27:16.270
you would have to
go on deployment.

00:27:16.270 --> 00:27:18.572
You would take your ship
and you would sail somewhere

00:27:18.572 --> 00:27:20.530
and you wouldn't be able
to talk to me anymore.

00:27:20.530 --> 00:27:22.312
So you would have
to understand what

00:27:22.312 --> 00:27:23.770
it is you were
trying to accomplish

00:27:23.770 --> 00:27:25.687
and then just go out
there and make it happen.

00:27:25.687 --> 00:27:27.760
That's decentralized command.

00:27:27.760 --> 00:27:30.310
And that's something that
exists in the SEAL teams

00:27:30.310 --> 00:27:33.490
without question, very
decentralized command.

00:27:33.490 --> 00:27:36.940
And that's one of the absolute
strengths of the SEAL teams

00:27:36.940 --> 00:27:40.540
is you've got leaders at every
level inside the organization.

00:27:40.540 --> 00:27:42.213
That if they don't
know exactly--

00:27:42.213 --> 00:27:44.380
if they're not told what
to do, they're going to go,

00:27:44.380 --> 00:27:45.970
OK, I haven't been
told what to do

00:27:45.970 --> 00:27:47.387
but I'm going to
go figure it out.

00:27:47.387 --> 00:27:51.010
And that's one of the
strengths of the SEAL teams.

00:27:51.010 --> 00:27:54.070
We have more doctrine now, but
when I came in the SEAL teams,

00:27:54.070 --> 00:27:55.870
there was no doctrine.

00:27:55.870 --> 00:27:57.490
It was all word of mouth.

00:27:57.490 --> 00:27:59.830
And so the army and
the Marine Corps,

00:27:59.830 --> 00:28:03.160
if you have to
conduct an ambush,

00:28:03.160 --> 00:28:05.230
you can pull out
a manual and you

00:28:05.230 --> 00:28:07.480
can look up how to conduct
an ambush-- platoon ambush,

00:28:07.480 --> 00:28:09.520
how to conduct it-- and
it's all written very clear.

00:28:09.520 --> 00:28:10.790
And they're great documents.

00:28:10.790 --> 00:28:14.260
That's the FM 7-8 infantry
platoon and squad,

00:28:14.260 --> 00:28:15.845
I think is the army doctrine.

00:28:15.845 --> 00:28:18.220
- I can see the little neurons
in your hippocampus firing

00:28:18.220 --> 00:28:18.500
in sequence.

00:28:18.500 --> 00:28:18.720
- Yeah.

00:28:18.720 --> 00:28:20.140
- It's embedded
in there forever.

00:28:20.140 --> 00:28:22.360
- And it's a great manual.

00:28:22.360 --> 00:28:24.130
And you can pull that
thing out and you

00:28:24.130 --> 00:28:25.330
have a place to start from.

00:28:25.330 --> 00:28:27.590
In the SEAL teams, we
didn't have that at all.

00:28:27.590 --> 00:28:30.460
So you would hear from
your platoon chief,

00:28:30.460 --> 00:28:31.990
this is how you
conduct an ambush.

00:28:31.990 --> 00:28:33.940
And he had heard it from his
platoon chief, who heard it

00:28:33.940 --> 00:28:35.380
from his platoon
chief, who heard it

00:28:35.380 --> 00:28:37.963
from his platoon chief, and that
platoon chief was in Vietnam.

00:28:37.963 --> 00:28:41.260
So it's getting passed down but
you can make adjustments to it.

00:28:41.260 --> 00:28:45.790
And you can alter the plan
a little bit because hey,

00:28:45.790 --> 00:28:47.320
the terrain is
different, or hey,

00:28:47.320 --> 00:28:49.870
the night vision we now have.

00:28:49.870 --> 00:28:51.460
So there's changes
that we can make

00:28:51.460 --> 00:28:53.880
because there's no doctrine.

00:28:53.880 --> 00:28:57.390
So not having any doctrine,
in many ways, is a strength.

00:28:57.390 --> 00:28:59.400
Also it can be a weakness.

00:28:59.400 --> 00:29:02.430
Because if you've got a new
platoon commander that's never

00:29:02.430 --> 00:29:05.860
done an ambush before and he
has no idea what he's doing,

00:29:05.860 --> 00:29:08.400
this platoon chief has been
out of the loop for a long time

00:29:08.400 --> 00:29:11.250
and he doesn't know what he's
doing, there's no reference.

00:29:11.250 --> 00:29:12.930
So there's strengths
and weaknesses,

00:29:12.930 --> 00:29:14.160
just like any characteristic.

00:29:14.160 --> 00:29:15.720
Everybody's characteristics,
you've got strength

00:29:15.720 --> 00:29:16.770
and you've got weaknesses.

00:29:16.770 --> 00:29:18.300
And your weaknesses
can be strengths

00:29:18.300 --> 00:29:20.400
and your strength
can be weaknesses.

00:29:20.400 --> 00:29:23.250
To get back to your
original question,

00:29:23.250 --> 00:29:26.400
are there stereotypes inside of
each of the military branches?

00:29:26.400 --> 00:29:27.210
Sure.

00:29:27.210 --> 00:29:29.635
But are there outliers in
each of the military branches?

00:29:29.635 --> 00:29:30.510
There are absolutely.

00:29:30.510 --> 00:29:34.290
And that's why you can't
judge a book by its cover.

00:29:34.290 --> 00:29:38.130
- For people listening to this
who are not in the military,

00:29:38.130 --> 00:29:40.830
maybe have some military
lineage in their family

00:29:40.830 --> 00:29:43.800
or not, but who want to
understand a little bit better

00:29:43.800 --> 00:29:46.800
about how structure
and lack of structure

00:29:46.800 --> 00:29:50.860
can both support being
effective in life,

00:29:50.860 --> 00:29:56.580
in relationships, in daily
life, in fitness, in business,

00:29:56.580 --> 00:29:57.192
in school--

00:29:57.192 --> 00:29:58.650
I think those are
the big domains--

00:29:58.650 --> 00:30:00.117
in creative endeavors.

00:30:00.117 --> 00:30:02.700
I think it would be useful for
them to understand a little bit

00:30:02.700 --> 00:30:06.990
about how you in particular
balance discipline

00:30:06.990 --> 00:30:09.270
and structure with,
dare I say, lack

00:30:09.270 --> 00:30:10.890
of discipline and structure.

00:30:10.890 --> 00:30:12.348
- Well, you could
actually just say

00:30:12.348 --> 00:30:14.700
the word freedom, because
that's what it turns into.

00:30:14.700 --> 00:30:16.230
- Or maybe even play.

00:30:16.230 --> 00:30:18.420
I bring this up in
part because I've

00:30:18.420 --> 00:30:20.910
seen some posts that you put
up of you playing the guitar

00:30:20.910 --> 00:30:22.170
with friends or music.

00:30:22.170 --> 00:30:23.880
One of them was a
tribute to someone

00:30:23.880 --> 00:30:27.030
who either was killed in
combat or had passed away.

00:30:27.030 --> 00:30:29.010
So these moments of
connection between people

00:30:29.010 --> 00:30:31.680
sometimes are working
together, but sometimes

00:30:31.680 --> 00:30:34.040
are in relaxation and play,
in these kinds of things.

00:30:34.040 --> 00:30:36.082
I think it was a really
important post for people

00:30:36.082 --> 00:30:38.910
to see that while Jocko Willink
kicks back with a guitar,

00:30:38.910 --> 00:30:40.560
not trying to take over stages--

00:30:40.560 --> 00:30:41.430
maybe you are.

00:30:41.430 --> 00:30:42.360
Maybe you have a plan.

00:30:42.360 --> 00:30:44.730
If anyone could do it,
you'd probably be the one.

00:30:44.730 --> 00:30:49.530
But what is the balance for
you in terms of structure

00:30:49.530 --> 00:30:50.570
and lack of structure?

00:30:50.570 --> 00:30:52.570
And I'm not going to ask
for your daily routine.

00:30:52.570 --> 00:30:54.060
We know that you get
up early, you train.

00:30:54.060 --> 00:30:55.602
But I do have some
specific questions

00:30:55.602 --> 00:30:57.780
that I think would be
helpful in putting some meat

00:30:57.780 --> 00:31:00.528
on the notions about you.

00:31:00.528 --> 00:31:02.320
And again, this isn't
a pick into your life

00:31:02.320 --> 00:31:04.710
but more to grab-- well,
it's to pick into your life.

00:31:04.710 --> 00:31:07.560
The-- so a question I
asked you in the lobby

00:31:07.560 --> 00:31:09.403
because it's one
that having seen

00:31:09.403 --> 00:31:11.820
your content for a long time
and really benefited from it,

00:31:11.820 --> 00:31:12.840
I was curious.

00:31:12.840 --> 00:31:15.480
You get up early
at about 4:30, you

00:31:15.480 --> 00:31:19.560
train every morning, how
long do you train for?

00:31:19.560 --> 00:31:21.317
And is there any global
structure to that?

00:31:21.317 --> 00:31:23.400
And, of course, everyone
needs different programs,

00:31:23.400 --> 00:31:25.398
but is it like weight
training one day,

00:31:25.398 --> 00:31:27.690
cardio training the next day,
or you're combining them?

00:31:27.690 --> 00:31:29.640
Is it always an hour or
is it always half an hour?

00:31:29.640 --> 00:31:31.770
I think people would benefit
from getting a little bit

00:31:31.770 --> 00:31:33.900
more understanding of what
that looks like for you,

00:31:33.900 --> 00:31:36.120
with the caveat that
everyone has different needs

00:31:36.120 --> 00:31:39.090
levels of background,
et cetera, but I'm

00:31:39.090 --> 00:31:40.680
intensely curious
about this and I'm

00:31:40.680 --> 00:31:42.010
certain I'm not the only one.

00:31:42.010 --> 00:31:43.350
JOCKO WILLINK: So do you want
to talk about weightlifting

00:31:43.350 --> 00:31:45.370
or rock and roll on the guitar?

00:31:45.370 --> 00:31:47.160
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I
want to talk about--

00:31:47.160 --> 00:31:50.130
let's your let's talk about
the most structured first part

00:31:50.130 --> 00:31:52.140
of your day, and then let's talk
about the least structured part

00:31:52.140 --> 00:31:53.765
of your day, at least
the part that you

00:31:53.765 --> 00:31:55.350
can share with the world.

00:31:55.350 --> 00:31:57.660
JOCKO WILLINK: Yeah,
waking up early

00:31:57.660 --> 00:31:59.380
and I'm going to work out.

00:31:59.380 --> 00:32:02.430
And depending on what's
going on that day,

00:32:02.430 --> 00:32:05.040
if I have an early
flight, I might work out

00:32:05.040 --> 00:32:07.710
for eight minutes.

00:32:07.710 --> 00:32:10.620
I might go in and do
2,000 meters on the rower,

00:32:10.620 --> 00:32:13.077
get a sweat going as hard
as I can, and then I'm done,

00:32:13.077 --> 00:32:14.910
and-- because I've got
to go catch a flight.

00:32:14.910 --> 00:32:17.010
So that could be happening.

00:32:17.010 --> 00:32:20.250
Maybe I'm supposed to go surfing
in the morning, I wake up,

00:32:20.250 --> 00:32:23.100
the waves are terrible, and
so now I've got nothing to do.

00:32:23.100 --> 00:32:26.400
I planned out to be surfing
for two or three hours,

00:32:26.400 --> 00:32:27.930
and now I'm not
going to go surfing,

00:32:27.930 --> 00:32:30.390
so I'm going to go lift and
I'm going to go play in the gym

00:32:30.390 --> 00:32:31.200
and do a bunch of stuff.

00:32:31.200 --> 00:32:33.158
I'm going to spend two
or three hours in there.

00:32:33.158 --> 00:32:34.900
I love doing that.

00:32:34.900 --> 00:32:37.980
So the workout could be
anywhere between what

00:32:37.980 --> 00:32:40.050
I just say 8
minutes and 3 hours,

00:32:40.050 --> 00:32:42.100
and it could be
anything in between.

00:32:42.100 --> 00:32:47.320
I fully enjoy the physical
aspect of working out.

00:32:47.320 --> 00:32:52.000
So if I have more time to spend
in the gym, I'll spend it.

00:32:52.000 --> 00:32:54.100
I remember my dad
saying at one point,

00:32:54.100 --> 00:32:57.280
if I retired I wouldn't
know what to do.

00:32:57.280 --> 00:33:00.160
And I was thinking to myself,
are you serious right now?

00:33:00.160 --> 00:33:02.770
If I didn't have anything to
do, I'd spend six hours a day

00:33:02.770 --> 00:33:06.130
in the gym, I'd spend four hours
doing jiu-jitsu I could fill

00:33:06.130 --> 00:33:06.700
my day--

00:33:06.700 --> 00:33:10.390
I could fill every day with
just physical activity, things

00:33:10.390 --> 00:33:12.670
that I just like doing.

00:33:12.670 --> 00:33:15.520
So wake up early,
get a sweat going.

00:33:15.520 --> 00:33:17.230
And do I lift?

00:33:17.230 --> 00:33:17.860
Yes.

00:33:17.860 --> 00:33:19.460
Do I do cardio?

00:33:19.460 --> 00:33:19.960
Yes.

00:33:19.960 --> 00:33:20.620
Do I run?

00:33:20.620 --> 00:33:20.890
Yes.

00:33:20.890 --> 00:33:21.460
Do I sprint?

00:33:21.460 --> 00:33:21.960
Yes.

00:33:21.960 --> 00:33:23.180
Do I lift heavy weights?

00:33:23.180 --> 00:33:23.680
Yes.

00:33:23.680 --> 00:33:24.610
Do I swing kettlebells?

00:33:24.610 --> 00:33:25.110
Yes.

00:33:25.110 --> 00:33:29.170
I do everything and anything,
and I enjoy all of it,

00:33:29.170 --> 00:33:30.970
and I'm not really
good at any of it.

00:33:30.970 --> 00:33:38.080
I'm not really good at any one
aspect of physical activity.

00:33:38.080 --> 00:33:40.480
There's people that are
infinitely better at me

00:33:40.480 --> 00:33:42.670
in every aspect,
and I'm not just

00:33:42.670 --> 00:33:44.680
talking about, Oh, this
guy's a world-class, no.

00:33:44.680 --> 00:33:47.380
There's like a guy named
Fred down at the gym that can

00:33:47.380 --> 00:33:49.060
deadlift more than
me, there was a guy--

00:33:49.060 --> 00:33:52.460
when I was at SEAL Team 2,
there was a guy who was,

00:33:52.460 --> 00:33:56.810
it's probably 5'7", and
he looked kind of chubby,

00:33:56.810 --> 00:34:00.697
and he was older than me, and
he could run faster than me,

00:34:00.697 --> 00:34:02.030
and he could bench more than me.

00:34:02.030 --> 00:34:03.260
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Those
guys are out there.

00:34:03.260 --> 00:34:05.062
JOCKO WILLINK: It was
just so bothersome.

00:34:05.062 --> 00:34:06.770
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah,
they're out there.

00:34:06.770 --> 00:34:09.255
They've got some engine in
there related to something.

00:34:09.255 --> 00:34:11.630
I mean, I do think there are
genetic differences in terms

00:34:11.630 --> 00:34:13.639
of people's resilience
and workout,

00:34:13.639 --> 00:34:16.820
but even just grip
strength is highly, highly

00:34:16.820 --> 00:34:18.440
subject to like
genetic influences,

00:34:18.440 --> 00:34:19.531
maximum grip strength.

00:34:19.531 --> 00:34:20.989
But of course,
there's a huge range

00:34:20.989 --> 00:34:22.730
in what people can develop.

00:34:22.730 --> 00:34:26.330
But I guarantee your grip
strength is greater than mine.

00:34:26.330 --> 00:34:27.830
People ask me this
all the time, who

00:34:27.830 --> 00:34:31.386
would win in arm wrestling
between you and Jocko.

00:34:31.386 --> 00:34:33.469
JOCKO WILLINK: You know
there's a lot of technique

00:34:33.469 --> 00:34:34.302
in arm wrestling.

00:34:34.302 --> 00:34:35.719
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
I have to imagine

00:34:35.719 --> 00:34:36.800
they're putting
their body behind it,

00:34:36.800 --> 00:34:38.179
they're putting
their back in that.

00:34:38.179 --> 00:34:40.130
JOCKO WILLINK: There's
a legitimate technique

00:34:40.130 --> 00:34:41.330
in arm wrestling--

00:34:41.330 --> 00:34:43.040
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Which
worked for me yet.

00:34:43.040 --> 00:34:45.770
JOCKO WILLINK: No, if we could
bring a female arm wrestler

00:34:45.770 --> 00:34:48.228
in here that knows how to arm
wrestle, because I don't know

00:34:48.228 --> 00:34:50.929
how to arm wrestle either,
and she would beat both of us

00:34:50.929 --> 00:34:53.870
because there's a lot more
technique in arm wrestling

00:34:53.870 --> 00:34:56.788
than most people recognize.

00:34:56.788 --> 00:34:58.830
There's all these little
games that are going on.

00:34:58.830 --> 00:35:00.990
There's all this little
arm position that you get.

00:35:00.990 --> 00:35:03.527
So just like everything
else, it's technique.

00:35:03.527 --> 00:35:05.360
There's a lot of technique
in arm wrestling.

00:35:05.360 --> 00:35:05.930
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
That's good to know.

00:35:05.930 --> 00:35:07.555
I didn't know that
about arm wrestling.

00:35:07.555 --> 00:35:10.760
I think we all start off with
some genetic predispositions,

00:35:10.760 --> 00:35:13.790
both good and bad, for different
things, and then there's--

00:35:13.790 --> 00:35:15.680
as far as we know,
there's a huge range

00:35:15.680 --> 00:35:18.140
based through neuroplasticity
and muscle adaptation,

00:35:18.140 --> 00:35:19.860
et cetera, in what
we can obtain.

00:35:19.860 --> 00:35:21.770
So I never want
genetic predisposition

00:35:21.770 --> 00:35:23.030
to serve as a barrier.

00:35:23.030 --> 00:35:25.670
No one knows also what the upper
limits of any of these things

00:35:25.670 --> 00:35:26.210
are.

00:35:26.210 --> 00:35:28.793
And some of the best examples
we know from sport and certainly

00:35:28.793 --> 00:35:30.800
from academia are
people who knew

00:35:30.800 --> 00:35:32.420
they were at a
disadvantage and just

00:35:32.420 --> 00:35:34.280
worked 10 times harder
than everybody else

00:35:34.280 --> 00:35:35.780
