00:37:18.290 --> 00:37:21.170
And let's face it, if you just
want to be a good deadlifter,

00:37:21.170 --> 00:37:23.090
you're not going
to be that fast.

00:37:23.090 --> 00:37:26.437
You're going to be
slow on long runs,

00:37:26.437 --> 00:37:28.520
so you don't want to go
too deep into deadlifting.

00:37:28.520 --> 00:37:30.710
And you also don't want
to be so good at long runs

00:37:30.710 --> 00:37:34.020
that you can't deadlift
a good amount of weight.

00:37:34.020 --> 00:37:36.200
So I kind of go through
phases, and I'll

00:37:36.200 --> 00:37:37.280
get into something
for a while, and then

00:37:37.280 --> 00:37:38.250
I'll get into something else.

00:37:38.250 --> 00:37:40.708
So I do log down what I'm doing
so that way I can look back

00:37:40.708 --> 00:37:43.940
and say, Oh, dang, I'm not
even close to as strong

00:37:43.940 --> 00:37:46.585
as I used to be, need
to get back to that.

00:37:46.585 --> 00:37:48.960
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I'm fascinated
by the concept of energy.

00:37:48.960 --> 00:37:50.880
I think it's one of the most
interesting aspects in all

00:37:50.880 --> 00:37:52.960
of biology, all of
psychology, and all of life.

00:37:52.960 --> 00:37:56.370
And when I say energy,
I mean the distinction

00:37:56.370 --> 00:37:58.200
between being back
on your heels,

00:37:58.200 --> 00:38:00.810
flat footed, or
forward center of mass.

00:38:00.810 --> 00:38:03.193
And I get the
impression, and I think

00:38:03.193 --> 00:38:04.860
everyone gets the
impression that you're

00:38:04.860 --> 00:38:08.640
somebody with a lot of energy,
and I wonder whether or not

00:38:08.640 --> 00:38:11.250
you wake up with a
lot of energy and you

00:38:11.250 --> 00:38:14.130
feel like you have to burn it
off with this physical activity

00:38:14.130 --> 00:38:17.020
and work and other
demands in your life,

00:38:17.020 --> 00:38:19.830
or do you find that you
wake up and your energy

00:38:19.830 --> 00:38:23.070
is kind of neutral, and
exercise and physical activity

00:38:23.070 --> 00:38:24.480
gives you energy?

00:38:24.480 --> 00:38:27.920
Because I think this is one
of the key things out there,

00:38:27.920 --> 00:38:29.670
I think, that acts as
a barrier for people

00:38:29.670 --> 00:38:32.435
doing more with their
body because maybe they

00:38:32.435 --> 00:38:33.810
don't want to tire
themselves out

00:38:33.810 --> 00:38:36.018
or maybe they don't feel
like they have enough energy

00:38:36.018 --> 00:38:37.290
to begin with.

00:38:37.290 --> 00:38:40.530
It's also feeds into this
idea that some people just

00:38:40.530 --> 00:38:41.590
have a lot of energy.

00:38:41.590 --> 00:38:43.632
They're really physical,
and other people aren't.

00:38:43.632 --> 00:38:46.710
So let's just say
on most days do

00:38:46.710 --> 00:38:48.900
you wake up feeling
like you want

00:38:48.900 --> 00:38:51.480
to burn off energy,
build energy, what

00:38:51.480 --> 00:38:52.560
is exercise mean to you?

00:38:52.560 --> 00:38:53.580
And then maybe we
can talk about some

00:38:53.580 --> 00:38:55.955
of the underlying stuff going
on there because I think we

00:38:55.955 --> 00:38:57.390
both might find it interesting.

00:38:57.390 --> 00:38:59.170
JOCKO WILLINK: I
would say it's both.

00:38:59.170 --> 00:39:01.253
There's no way I could sit
here and say, oh, yeah,

00:39:01.253 --> 00:39:03.280
every day that alarm
clock goes off,

00:39:03.280 --> 00:39:05.340
and I'm like, oh, yeah,
let's rock and roll.

00:39:05.340 --> 00:39:06.905
No, certainly
that's not the case.

00:39:06.905 --> 00:39:09.030
It's also certainly not
the case that every day I'm

00:39:09.030 --> 00:39:10.410
like, oh, god, not again.

00:39:10.410 --> 00:39:13.750
No, I'd say most of the time
the alarm clock goes off,

00:39:13.750 --> 00:39:16.140
and I don't think a bunch.

00:39:16.140 --> 00:39:18.990
When my alarm clock goes
off, I don't think a bunch.

00:39:18.990 --> 00:39:20.490
I don't debate with myself.

00:39:20.490 --> 00:39:22.470
I'm not negotiating.

00:39:22.470 --> 00:39:25.440
The thing goes off, and I'm
doing what I'm supposed to do.

00:39:25.440 --> 00:39:26.880
Sort of robotic.

00:39:26.880 --> 00:39:30.003
Now this much I can say,
when you go and work out,

00:39:30.003 --> 00:39:31.170
you're going to feel better.

00:39:31.170 --> 00:39:33.510
You will get energy
from working out.

00:39:33.510 --> 00:39:35.590
That is a guarantee.

00:39:35.590 --> 00:39:37.590
If you go work out, you're
going to feel better.

00:39:37.590 --> 00:39:39.640
If you go break a sweat,
you're going to feel better.

00:39:39.640 --> 00:39:41.307
You're going to get
more energy from it.

00:39:41.307 --> 00:39:43.830
And look, you got
to go really, really

00:39:43.830 --> 00:39:49.380
hard to where now you feel
more tired when you're done.

00:39:49.380 --> 00:39:51.930
And even that, I mean,
you've got to go psycho.

00:39:51.930 --> 00:39:55.110
I'll do that occasionally, but I
don't do that on a daily basis.

00:39:55.110 --> 00:40:01.470
At the end of the day, if
I wake up, lift, run, surf,

00:40:01.470 --> 00:40:03.630
and then I do jiu-jitsu
in the afternoon,

00:40:03.630 --> 00:40:09.040
at the end of that day,
I'm tired and I feel tired.

00:40:09.040 --> 00:40:12.857
But normal day working out
just makes you feel better.

00:40:12.857 --> 00:40:14.940
Definitely gives you--
definitely gives me energy,

00:40:14.940 --> 00:40:16.940
I should say, because I
guess I'm not everybody.

00:40:16.940 --> 00:40:19.620
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah, I think
it's a very important point

00:40:19.620 --> 00:40:22.590
because one of the things that
we are learning from circadian

00:40:22.590 --> 00:40:25.680
biology, time of day effects
and sunlight and all that stuff

00:40:25.680 --> 00:40:27.090
that we talk about
in our podcast

00:40:27.090 --> 00:40:28.522
that you've done
intuitively, this

00:40:28.522 --> 00:40:29.730
is what we kind of arrive to.

00:40:29.730 --> 00:40:30.430
JOCKO WILLINK:
It's kind of crazy.

00:40:30.430 --> 00:40:31.800
ANDREW HUBERMAN: As last
time we had a conversation

00:40:31.800 --> 00:40:33.550
is that so many of the
things that science

00:40:33.550 --> 00:40:35.760
is telling us to do
and that we emphasize

00:40:35.760 --> 00:40:38.292
on this podcast
you've been doing

00:40:38.292 --> 00:40:40.500
or are built into military
schedules, and one of them

00:40:40.500 --> 00:40:42.120
is this notion of
waking up early

00:40:42.120 --> 00:40:44.430
and getting physical
early in the day.

00:40:44.430 --> 00:40:47.070
And I suppose if we were
to just throw one blanket

00:40:47.070 --> 00:40:51.390
rule on the table to encompass
the broadest number of themes

00:40:51.390 --> 00:40:56.220
it's that once every 24
hours, we each and all

00:40:56.220 --> 00:40:58.650
get a big increase in this
release of the hormone

00:40:58.650 --> 00:41:01.480
cortisol, which everyone says,
oh, cortisol it's terrible,

00:41:01.480 --> 00:41:03.480
he's going to burn you
out, adrenal burnout, all

00:41:03.480 --> 00:41:06.930
that stuff, but it's
a non-negotiable peak.

00:41:06.930 --> 00:41:10.830
And you want it to arrive
early in the day and viewing

00:41:10.830 --> 00:41:13.830
sunlight, physical
activity, caffeine,

00:41:13.830 --> 00:41:16.590
and in particular
intense exercise,

00:41:16.590 --> 00:41:19.350
all amplify that cortisol peak.

00:41:19.350 --> 00:41:21.090
In fact, I think the
numbers I'm seeing

00:41:21.090 --> 00:41:23.280
is just sunlight
viewing gives you

00:41:23.280 --> 00:41:25.560
a 50% increase in that cortisol.

00:41:25.560 --> 00:41:28.560
Exercise on top of that,
another 50% to 75% increase.

00:41:28.560 --> 00:41:30.990
So this huge release in this
hormone that everyone thinks

00:41:30.990 --> 00:41:33.360
is terrible but actually
sets this huge wave

00:41:33.360 --> 00:41:36.400
in motion for the rest of
the day, which gives you

00:41:36.400 --> 00:41:40.210
more energy, higher levels of
immune function, more focus,

00:41:40.210 --> 00:41:42.040
et cetera, and does
indeed as you mentioned

00:41:42.040 --> 00:41:43.870
in your example of
your daily life sets

00:41:43.870 --> 00:41:47.380
a timer so that about 14 to
16 hours later, you're sleepy,

00:41:47.380 --> 00:41:50.230
which is what you want 14 to 68
hours later, unless, of course,

00:41:50.230 --> 00:41:52.192
you're running vampire
shifts in the military

00:41:52.192 --> 00:41:54.650
or you're on shift work, but
most people aren't, of course.

00:41:54.650 --> 00:41:58.240
So I think the idea that
movement and exercise gives us

00:41:58.240 --> 00:42:00.850
energy I think is an important
idea and it's something

00:42:00.850 --> 00:42:04.180
that frankly I was hoping
your answer would be that, as

00:42:04.180 --> 00:42:07.030
opposed to that you
wake up every day

00:42:07.030 --> 00:42:09.963
and you just want to just attack
the world because you have

00:42:09.963 --> 00:42:12.130
so much energy getting out
of bed because frankly, I

00:42:12.130 --> 00:42:13.240
never feel that way.

00:42:13.240 --> 00:42:15.460
But I always feel
better after I train.

00:42:15.460 --> 00:42:16.270
Always.

00:42:16.270 --> 00:42:17.920
And of course,
there are times when

00:42:17.920 --> 00:42:21.610
I crash in the early afternoon
if I train really, really hard,

00:42:21.610 --> 00:42:25.000
but usually that's
when I over-caffeinated

00:42:25.000 --> 00:42:28.750
to an outrageous degree, and
then I don't nourish after,

00:42:28.750 --> 00:42:30.160
or I over nourish.

00:42:30.160 --> 00:42:33.040
So this is the other thing
that eating, the whole rest

00:42:33.040 --> 00:42:36.250
and digesting, the digest
word in there is meant to--

00:42:36.250 --> 00:42:37.660
it's there for a
reason, which is

00:42:37.660 --> 00:42:39.220
that when we eat
a really big meal,

00:42:39.220 --> 00:42:41.050
we actually need to slow down.

00:42:41.050 --> 00:42:43.242
So I hate to get
into daily schedules

00:42:43.242 --> 00:42:44.950
at the level of
nitpicking, and nutrition

00:42:44.950 --> 00:42:49.000
is about the most controversial
topic on the internet,

00:42:49.000 --> 00:42:52.460
but do you nourish
after you train?

00:42:52.460 --> 00:42:55.060
And if you do, do you
do it to the point

00:42:55.060 --> 00:42:58.220
where you're kind of like, OK,
I'm mostly full or I'm full?

00:42:58.220 --> 00:43:00.190
Are you trying to
really nourish yourself,

00:43:00.190 --> 00:43:02.570
or do you find that
eating slows you down?

00:43:02.570 --> 00:43:04.570
JOCKO WILLINK: I find
that eating slows me down.

00:43:04.570 --> 00:43:08.680
And I would say, again, it's
weird how some of this stuff

00:43:08.680 --> 00:43:09.340
is.

00:43:09.340 --> 00:43:11.548
The main reason I got in
the habit of waking up early

00:43:11.548 --> 00:43:13.228
and working out is
because if you do it

00:43:13.228 --> 00:43:15.520
before anyone else is awake,
then they can't bother you

00:43:15.520 --> 00:43:16.630
and you can get stuff done.

00:43:16.630 --> 00:43:18.100
You go to the SEAL
Team and you get

00:43:18.100 --> 00:43:20.450
there before anyone else is
there, no one can say, hey,

00:43:20.450 --> 00:43:21.100
can you help us with this?

00:43:21.100 --> 00:43:22.350
Hey, no one sent you an email.

00:43:22.350 --> 00:43:26.050
So you get that time, you
get it done, and it's yours.

00:43:26.050 --> 00:43:28.070
I remember when you
were on my podcast,

00:43:28.070 --> 00:43:31.750
and I don't wear sunglasses
when I run in the morning

00:43:31.750 --> 00:43:33.970
because I sweat and it
fills my sunglasses.

00:43:33.970 --> 00:43:37.690
It's not because I want to
let the UV light into my eyes,

00:43:37.690 --> 00:43:38.767
that's not cortisol.

00:43:38.767 --> 00:43:39.850
It's not for the cortisol.

00:43:39.850 --> 00:43:40.642
I didn't know that.

00:43:40.642 --> 00:43:43.840
It's cool that I know it
now, but I just did it

00:43:43.840 --> 00:43:46.870
because I don't like to
sweat in my sunglasses,

00:43:46.870 --> 00:43:49.780
can't see, so I just run
without, I don't put a hat on.

00:43:49.780 --> 00:43:54.040
As far as eating, I don't like
to do physically active things

00:43:54.040 --> 00:43:56.590
with food in my stomach.

00:43:56.590 --> 00:43:57.820
That's just the way it is.

00:43:57.820 --> 00:43:58.270
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Yeah, me either.

00:43:58.270 --> 00:43:59.980
JOCKO WILLINK: And
so I don't want it.

00:43:59.980 --> 00:44:01.690
And what really
keeps that in line

00:44:01.690 --> 00:44:03.940
for me is I'm doing
jiu-jitsu in the afternoon,

00:44:03.940 --> 00:44:06.160
and so if I'm
eating a big lunch,

00:44:06.160 --> 00:44:09.358
by the time the afternoon
rolls around, I'm kind of I

00:44:09.358 --> 00:44:11.650
got food in my gut, and I
just don't like that feeling.

00:44:11.650 --> 00:44:14.698
So no, I don't eat
a big meal until I'm

00:44:14.698 --> 00:44:16.990
kind of done with the physical
stuff for the day, which

00:44:16.990 --> 00:44:19.107
is usually at night,
6, 7 o'clock at night,

00:44:19.107 --> 00:44:21.190
which I guess there's some
bad things about that I

00:44:21.190 --> 00:44:22.030
eat too late.

00:44:22.030 --> 00:44:24.880
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Well,
the data say if you're--

00:44:24.880 --> 00:44:27.040
yeah, we could go down
a rabbit hole with this

00:44:27.040 --> 00:44:28.120
and then someone's
going to pull up

00:44:28.120 --> 00:44:29.860
some little clinical
study and then another one

00:44:29.860 --> 00:44:30.760
that counters that.

00:44:30.760 --> 00:44:32.260
I mean I think the
data essentially

00:44:32.260 --> 00:44:35.590
say that having a regular
meal schedule that

00:44:35.590 --> 00:44:37.900
allows you to sleep well at
night, whatever that means

00:44:37.900 --> 00:44:40.840
for you, and that allows you
to be active and focused when

00:44:40.840 --> 00:44:42.340
you need to be
active and focused,

00:44:42.340 --> 00:44:44.080
that's the ideal schedule.

00:44:44.080 --> 00:44:46.448
JOCKO WILLINK: When I'm
working with clients,

00:44:46.448 --> 00:44:48.490
so I have a leadership
consulting company Echelon

00:44:48.490 --> 00:44:48.730
Front.

00:44:48.730 --> 00:44:49.850
When I'm going to
work with a client,

00:44:49.850 --> 00:44:52.120
I'm not eating because
they're going to be asking me

00:44:52.120 --> 00:44:53.770
questions, we're going
to be diving into what's

00:44:53.770 --> 00:44:55.120
happening inside their
business, there's

00:44:55.120 --> 00:44:56.120
a lot of stuff going on.

00:44:56.120 --> 00:44:57.520
It's a lot of cognitive work.

00:44:57.520 --> 00:44:59.620
So I'm not eating
before a podcast.

00:44:59.620 --> 00:45:01.210
I'm not eating before a podcast.

00:45:01.210 --> 00:45:02.230
Before I'm recording
a podcast, I'm

00:45:02.230 --> 00:45:03.670
not eating because
I don't want to have

00:45:03.670 --> 00:45:04.960
a bunch of food in my stomach.

00:45:04.960 --> 00:45:07.360
You get a certain
level of mental clarity

00:45:07.360 --> 00:45:09.250
when you haven't
eaten a bunch of food.

00:45:09.250 --> 00:45:11.435
So going out on
missions, I never

00:45:11.435 --> 00:45:13.060
would eat before I
go out on a mission.

00:45:13.060 --> 00:45:14.712
I would eat when I come home.

00:45:14.712 --> 00:45:16.420
You get home at 4
o'clock in the morning,

00:45:16.420 --> 00:45:18.040
3 o'clock in the morning
from doing operation, cool,

00:45:18.040 --> 00:45:19.960
then I'll eat because
then I'm going

00:45:19.960 --> 00:45:22.870
to do a debrief for 15
minutes, clean weapons,

00:45:22.870 --> 00:45:25.600
and then eat a big
meal, go to sleep.

00:45:25.600 --> 00:45:26.485
Cool.

00:45:26.485 --> 00:45:28.360
Yeah, I don't want to
have food in my stomach

00:45:28.360 --> 00:45:31.580
when I've got to perform
or execute anything.

00:45:31.580 --> 00:45:34.840
So again, I think that's
just kind of a fluke

00:45:34.840 --> 00:45:36.490
that I ended up
living like that,

00:45:36.490 --> 00:45:38.200
but that's kind of how I live.

00:45:38.200 --> 00:45:39.825
ANDREW HUBERMAN: It's
a fortunate fluke

00:45:39.825 --> 00:45:43.330
for all of us because so
much of what you embody

00:45:43.330 --> 00:45:46.240
and what you do I think centers
around this idea of discipline,

00:45:46.240 --> 00:45:47.950
of course, but also energy.

00:45:47.950 --> 00:45:52.060
It's the intuitive sense I
get about why people are so

00:45:52.060 --> 00:45:57.520
drawn to your messaging and
what you do and how you do it.

00:45:57.520 --> 00:45:59.912
Energy we know, of
course, is caloric energy.

00:45:59.912 --> 00:46:01.870
And I think that's what
most people default to.

00:46:01.870 --> 00:46:04.425
They go, oh, how many
calories are energy

00:46:04.425 --> 00:46:06.550
and how many calories in
that and you need calories

00:46:06.550 --> 00:46:07.390
to fuel things.

00:46:07.390 --> 00:46:10.090
But the energy that
you're describing I think

00:46:10.090 --> 00:46:11.140
is the one that--

00:46:11.140 --> 00:46:13.940
well, it really maps the
Eastern traditions more directly

00:46:13.940 --> 00:46:15.370
to how yin and yang thing--

00:46:15.370 --> 00:46:17.830
yin and yang, I always
get that wrong-- so which

00:46:17.830 --> 00:46:19.990
is the notion of neural energy.

00:46:19.990 --> 00:46:23.920
And so there's a particular
cluster of chemicals in us

00:46:23.920 --> 00:46:26.000
as a fancy name called
the catecholamines.

00:46:26.000 --> 00:46:27.980
But that's dopamine,
epinephrine,

00:46:27.980 --> 00:46:29.740
which is adrenaline
and norepinephrine,

00:46:29.740 --> 00:46:31.840
and then you've got cortisol.

00:46:31.840 --> 00:46:35.110
And those four hang out
together and basically give us

00:46:35.110 --> 00:46:39.730
enough energy to run our
brain and body for 50 days.

00:46:39.730 --> 00:46:40.517
50 days.

00:46:40.517 --> 00:46:42.850
So the idea that you have to
eat before you train, sure,

00:46:42.850 --> 00:46:44.930
for some people that might
work better than others,

00:46:44.930 --> 00:46:46.472
but I think what
people don't realize

00:46:46.472 --> 00:46:49.540
is that anytime we're
taking in caloric energy,

00:46:49.540 --> 00:46:53.170
it takes neural energy in
order to digest that and put it

00:46:53.170 --> 00:46:54.010
into storage.

00:46:54.010 --> 00:46:55.960
And so the way you
describe your day of, yeah,

00:46:55.960 --> 00:46:57.293
I also don't eat before I train.

00:46:57.293 --> 00:46:59.050
I like to hydrate
and caffeinate.

00:46:59.050 --> 00:47:01.090
I have been drinking
these before I train.

00:47:01.090 --> 00:47:03.730
I have to limit myself to two
before because otherwise, I'm

00:47:03.730 --> 00:47:04.438
like picking up--

00:47:04.438 --> 00:47:07.360
I'm already quaking a little
bit at the second one.

00:47:07.360 --> 00:47:09.620
But I have a pretty
high caffeine tolerance.

00:47:09.620 --> 00:47:11.650
So I like to train first also.

00:47:11.650 --> 00:47:13.310
And then I find it
gives me energy.

00:47:13.310 --> 00:47:16.570
But then the moment that I eat a
meal that's a little too large,

00:47:16.570 --> 00:47:18.197
all of a sudden
I'm out of energy.

00:47:18.197 --> 00:47:19.030
And what's the deal?

00:47:19.030 --> 00:47:20.290
These calories
are energy, right?

00:47:20.290 --> 00:47:22.040
You're supposed to have energy
in order to think and move.

00:47:22.040 --> 00:47:24.500
And I think I think a lot of
the world has this backwards.

00:47:24.500 --> 00:47:26.680
And this isn't a push
for intermittent fasting

00:47:26.680 --> 00:47:29.095
or any particular
style of eating really,

00:47:29.095 --> 00:47:30.970
I don't care if people
are carnivores, vegan,

00:47:30.970 --> 00:47:32.450
doesn't matter to
me whatever works.

00:47:32.450 --> 00:47:33.575
I happen to be an omnivore.

00:47:33.575 --> 00:47:35.800
But I think once
people understand

00:47:35.800 --> 00:47:39.690
the energy to do
things is neural,

00:47:39.690 --> 00:47:42.330
and yet, of course, it relies
on having glycogen and all

00:47:42.330 --> 00:47:45.660
this stuff around, but neural
energy is what's really about,

00:47:45.660 --> 00:47:47.887
then your schedule and
the way you function

00:47:47.887 --> 00:47:49.470
and the way you
describe your schedule

00:47:49.470 --> 00:47:51.460
really makes a ton of sense.

00:47:51.460 --> 00:47:55.440
So you described getting up
and lifting, running, surfing,

00:47:55.440 --> 00:47:57.220
and jiu-jitsu in the same day.

00:47:57.220 --> 00:47:59.310
So on a day like that,
you're hydrating, correct?

00:47:59.310 --> 00:48:00.810
JOCKO WILLINK: Oh,
yeah, definitely.

00:48:00.810 --> 00:48:01.710
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Because that's vital.

00:48:01.710 --> 00:48:03.640
And I know in the Teams,
in the SEAL Teams,

00:48:03.640 --> 00:48:05.580
there's a lot of discussion
about hydration is important,

00:48:05.580 --> 00:48:06.660
even though you guys
I know are supposed

00:48:06.660 --> 00:48:09.240
to be able to eat sand and
survive on sunlight and dirt

00:48:09.240 --> 00:48:10.363
and drink your own blood.

00:48:10.363 --> 00:48:11.280
JOCKO WILLINK: Not me.

00:48:11.280 --> 00:48:13.200
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Hydration
is taken seriously, right?

00:48:13.200 --> 00:48:14.908
JOCKO WILLINK: Yeah,
and different people

00:48:14.908 --> 00:48:16.710
need different
amount of hydration.

00:48:16.710 --> 00:48:17.995
And I, unfortunately, was one.

00:48:17.995 --> 00:48:19.620
I always needed to
bring a lot of water

00:48:19.620 --> 00:48:22.050
in the field which sucked
because water is heavy.

00:48:22.050 --> 00:48:23.460
And I have friends.

00:48:23.460 --> 00:48:26.880
One of my friends, Tony, BTF
Tony, he'd go in the field

00:48:26.880 --> 00:48:30.170
with like a can of Copenhagen
and coffee in his canteen

00:48:30.170 --> 00:48:31.740
and go like three
straight days--

00:48:31.740 --> 00:48:32.460
ANDREW HUBERMAN: He's
like a desert turtle.

00:48:32.460 --> 00:48:34.050
JOCKO WILLINK: He's
a desert rat, man.

00:48:34.050 --> 00:48:35.770
He could just survive.

00:48:35.770 --> 00:48:38.260
And I would always have
to bring this water.

00:48:38.260 --> 00:48:40.860
I sweat a ton when
I work out or when

00:48:40.860 --> 00:48:45.690
I'm doing anything that
requires physical output.

00:48:45.690 --> 00:48:48.990
I sweat a ton, so I have to
drink a lot of water for sure.

00:48:48.990 --> 00:48:51.210
But not everyone's the same.

00:48:51.210 --> 00:48:53.700
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah, I think
if most people focus more

00:48:53.700 --> 00:48:56.730
on hydration and movement.

00:48:56.730 --> 00:48:58.793
They would find they have two--

00:48:58.793 --> 00:49:00.210
I'm going to venture
a guess here,

00:49:00.210 --> 00:49:03.300
this is not a scientific study,
but two to four times more

00:49:03.300 --> 00:49:06.242
energy than if they focused on
caloric energy and what to eat.

00:49:06.242 --> 00:49:08.700
JOCKO WILLINK: Yeah, and I
think the cool thing about this,

00:49:08.700 --> 00:49:11.040
you're using the term energy.

00:49:11.040 --> 00:49:17.450
And what's cool about this
is you or I create energy.

00:49:17.450 --> 00:49:21.290
I create energy by, like I
said, by going and lifting

00:49:21.290 --> 00:49:23.510
in the morning, by
going and doing--

00:49:23.510 --> 00:49:24.560
you go do burpees?

00:49:24.560 --> 00:49:26.660
You go do 100 burpees like
you're creating energy.

00:49:26.660 --> 00:49:28.910
You're going to be tired,
you're going to be sweating,

00:49:28.910 --> 00:49:30.350
but you just created energy.

00:49:30.350 --> 00:49:32.570
So that stuff is
totally true I'm

00:49:32.570 --> 00:49:34.460
glad there's neuroscience
to back it up.

00:49:34.460 --> 00:49:35.300
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Yeah, I'm actually

00:49:35.300 --> 00:49:37.258
thinking about devoting
some of my lab to this.

00:49:37.258 --> 00:49:40.040
One of the best examples,
another familiar territory

00:49:40.040 --> 00:49:41.330
for you is cold water.

00:49:41.330 --> 00:49:43.790
Nowadays there's a lot about
ice baths and cold baths

00:49:43.790 --> 00:49:46.980
and showers and all that, and
I always like to just say,

00:49:46.980 --> 00:49:50.660
listen it's all just a
reliable source of inducing

00:49:50.660 --> 00:49:51.680
adrenaline release.

00:49:51.680 --> 00:49:54.770
And you get out of a cold
shower, you have more energy,

00:49:54.770 --> 00:49:57.200
and that energy couldn't
have been caloric energy,

00:49:57.200 --> 00:49:58.430
it's adrenaline.

00:49:58.430 --> 00:50:02.120
And again, it's fair
to say I'm obsessed

00:50:02.120 --> 00:50:05.607
by the ideas of identity,
which is a little bit how

00:50:05.607 --> 00:50:07.940
we started off and I want to
get back to it, and energy.

00:50:07.940 --> 00:50:09.440
I feel like identity
and energy can

00:50:09.440 --> 00:50:14.010
account for 75% of what
it is to live a good life

00:50:14.010 --> 00:50:16.380
if you can master
those, because then it

00:50:16.380 --> 00:50:17.967
all seems to fall into bins.

00:50:17.967 --> 00:50:19.050
Of course, you need sleep.

00:50:19.050 --> 00:50:19.780
Why?

00:50:19.780 --> 00:50:22.500
Well, to restore
your neural energy.

00:50:22.500 --> 00:50:25.378
At some point, you just
fade out of neural energy

00:50:25.378 --> 00:50:26.170
if you don't sleep.

00:50:26.170 --> 00:50:28.620
So sleep then falls
into a particular bin

00:50:28.620 --> 00:50:30.720
with a particular
purpose, and then exercise

00:50:30.720 --> 00:50:32.470
becomes not a way
to burn energy but,

00:50:32.470 --> 00:50:33.720
as you said, to create energy.

00:50:33.720 --> 00:50:35.470
And we actually are
starting to understand

00:50:35.470 --> 00:50:37.620
why this is if you'll
indulge me for a second

00:50:37.620 --> 00:50:38.520
on some neuroscience.

00:50:38.520 --> 00:50:39.930
We didn't talk about
this last time.

00:50:39.930 --> 00:50:42.240
We have neural circuits that
control deliberate action.

00:50:42.240 --> 00:50:44.220
We have neural circuits that
control deliberate actions

00:50:44.220 --> 00:50:46.303
that when we forget that
we're doing like walking.

00:50:46.303 --> 00:50:47.887
And then we have
neural circuits which

00:50:47.887 --> 00:50:49.530
are called central
pattern generators.

00:50:49.530 --> 00:50:51.060
And these are the
neural circuits

00:50:51.060 --> 00:50:54.113
that love to just work on their
own, and in the background

00:50:54.113 --> 00:50:55.530
just kind of hum
in the background

00:50:55.530 --> 00:50:57.863
and take care of all the stuff
like heartbeat, breathing

00:50:57.863 --> 00:50:59.640
and movement that is repetitive.

00:50:59.640 --> 00:51:01.200
If you're just
marching and you don't

00:51:01.200 --> 00:51:02.580
have to adjust
your cadence much,

00:51:02.580 --> 00:51:04.705
or maybe you're hiking even
and stepping this rock,

00:51:04.705 --> 00:51:07.740
that rock, once those central
pattern generators get going,

00:51:07.740 --> 00:51:09.360
it's very automatic.

00:51:09.360 --> 00:51:12.570
And we know that once your
central pattern generators get

00:51:12.570 --> 00:51:16.320
going, there's the release
of those catecholamines,

00:51:16.320 --> 00:51:19.470
those three or four
molecules that then feed

00:51:19.470 --> 00:51:20.678
all the other neural systems.

00:51:20.678 --> 00:51:22.512
They're called neuro
modulators for a reason

00:51:22.512 --> 00:51:24.060
because they set
the gain higher.

00:51:24.060 --> 00:51:27.120
So when you go out for a run
or a jog or a hike or something

00:51:27.120 --> 00:51:29.070
or you pedal or
you row, and then

00:51:29.070 --> 00:51:31.560
your whole system
is at a higher RPM

00:51:31.560 --> 00:51:34.770
so when you say create
energy, neuroscientists

00:51:34.770 --> 00:51:37.770
are starting to understand what
that is, repetitive movement

00:51:37.770 --> 00:51:40.405
that allows you to forget
the motor commands that

00:51:40.405 --> 00:51:42.030
are required to
generate that movement.

00:51:42.030 --> 00:51:44.572
You might think about your row
stroke or something like that,

00:51:44.572 --> 00:51:47.100
but you can do it
without thinking much.

00:51:47.100 --> 00:51:51.270
You come off of that and you
now are set at a higher RPM

00:51:51.270 --> 00:51:52.840
to do more deliberate stuff.

00:51:52.840 --> 00:51:54.973
And none of this, again,
involve like eating

00:51:54.973 --> 00:51:57.390
enough carbohydrates or making
sure you had enough ketones

00:51:57.390 --> 00:51:58.500
or enough protein.

00:51:58.500 --> 00:52:00.300
It's like you got
plenty of that stuff

00:52:00.300 --> 00:52:03.720
provided you nourish at some
point every 24 hours or so.

00:52:03.720 --> 00:52:07.380
I think we know a little bit
about the science behind Jocko

00:52:07.380 --> 00:52:11.880
Willink's schedule now,
but I will ask this,

00:52:11.880 --> 00:52:14.250
are there certain
forms of exercise

00:52:14.250 --> 00:52:16.170
like weight lifting
versus cardio

00:52:16.170 --> 00:52:20.032
that you find give you
an especially big boost

00:52:20.032 --> 00:52:21.240
in what we're calling energy?

00:52:21.240 --> 00:52:22.907
And here this could
be cognitive energy,

00:52:22.907 --> 00:52:25.747
it could be physical energy, a
readiness for the next thing?

00:52:25.747 --> 00:52:28.080
JOCKO WILLINK: Yeah, first I
got to back you up on this.

00:52:28.080 --> 00:52:31.410
I love backing up your science.

00:52:31.410 --> 00:52:34.700
So do you ever ruck march like
put on a heavyweight and ruck?

00:52:34.700 --> 00:52:36.450
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah,
sorry to interrupt.

00:52:36.450 --> 00:52:38.250
Yeah, Peter Attia
got me into this.

00:52:38.250 --> 00:52:40.140
He got me into doing
a long Sunday--

00:52:40.140 --> 00:52:41.790
instead of a long
Sunday, run throwing

00:52:41.790 --> 00:52:43.800
on a lightweight vest
or a ruck, and going out

00:52:43.800 --> 00:52:45.000
for like three hours.

00:52:45.000 --> 00:52:47.490
And the first 20
minutes, I find I always

00:52:47.490 --> 00:52:49.360
want to go faster
and get it over with.

00:52:49.360 --> 00:52:51.840
But then I've learned
that the real pain in it

00:52:51.840 --> 00:52:53.905
sets in around an hour,
and then the beauty

00:52:53.905 --> 00:52:55.530
sets in around 90
minutes, where you're

00:52:55.530 --> 00:52:58.570
like, I could do this
all day, all night,

00:52:58.570 --> 00:52:59.670
and I never want to stop.

00:52:59.670 --> 00:53:01.753
JOCKO WILLINK: See that's
when you were describing

00:53:01.753 --> 00:53:03.870
how these chemicals
get released,

00:53:03.870 --> 00:53:06.120
and once you're in
that automatic mode--

00:53:06.120 --> 00:53:09.480
because in the
SEAL Teams, you're

00:53:09.480 --> 00:53:11.910
doing Maritime
operations for a month,

00:53:11.910 --> 00:53:13.868
and then you're going to
do some kill shooting,

00:53:13.868 --> 00:53:15.743
and so you're not carrying
a bunch of weight.

00:53:15.743 --> 00:53:17.310
And then you go
out to the desert

00:53:17.310 --> 00:53:20.100
and now you're
putting on 80 pounds

00:53:20.100 --> 00:53:22.660
and you're going on day
one, and you get out there,

00:53:22.660 --> 00:53:24.570
you're going on an
80 pounds ruck march.

00:53:24.570 --> 00:53:29.130
And the first freaking 17
minutes, the first 23 minutes

00:53:29.130 --> 00:53:30.190
just suck.

00:53:30.190 --> 00:53:31.080
They just suck.

00:53:31.080 --> 00:53:33.480
And what was beautiful
was by the time I

00:53:33.480 --> 00:53:35.718
was 23, 24 years
old, like, oh, yeah,

00:53:35.718 --> 00:53:37.260
this is going to
suck for 17 minutes,

00:53:37.260 --> 00:53:38.190
and then it's going to be--

00:53:38.190 --> 00:53:40.060
I'm going to be a robot and
it doesn't matter anymore.

00:53:40.060 --> 00:53:41.380
I can just keep going forever.

00:53:41.380 --> 00:53:43.140
So now it sounds
like what you're

00:53:43.140 --> 00:53:46.992
saying is what I experienced
basically my whole adult life.

00:53:46.992 --> 00:53:49.200
There's going to be a little
break in period mentally

00:53:49.200 --> 00:53:51.660
where you think
this totally sucks,

00:53:51.660 --> 00:53:55.330
and then you just can keep going
for a really, really long time,

00:53:55.330 --> 00:53:57.120
and it's not that big of a deal.

00:53:57.120 --> 00:54:01.050
To your question of is there any
form of exercise that gives me

00:54:01.050 --> 00:54:04.710
that energy, I would have to
say like the high intensity

00:54:04.710 --> 00:54:08.340
sort of anaerobic blast,
whether it's on the bike

00:54:08.340 --> 00:54:12.210
or on the rower or swing and
a kettlebell hard, something

00:54:12.210 --> 00:54:14.430
like that that lasts
10, 15 minutes, that's

00:54:14.430 --> 00:54:19.090
a really good way to peak
my mentality for the day.

00:54:19.090 --> 00:54:21.090
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Do you
do the cold water thing?

00:54:21.090 --> 00:54:22.950
I mean, you certainly did a
lot of it in BUD/S. I mean,

00:54:22.950 --> 00:54:24.910
do you force yourself into cold
water and the you release--

00:54:24.910 --> 00:54:27.180
JOCKO WILLINK: I have a
cold bath in my house,

00:54:27.180 --> 00:54:28.350
and I get in every day.

00:54:28.350 --> 00:54:29.670
ANDREW HUBERMAN: How long
are you spending in there?

00:54:29.670 --> 00:54:31.980
JOCKO WILLINK: Usually
around five minutes.

00:54:31.980 --> 00:54:32.610
Five minutes.

00:54:32.610 --> 00:54:34.620
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Before you
train or after you train?

00:54:34.620 --> 00:54:35.170
JOCKO WILLINK: After.

00:54:35.170 --> 00:54:37.128
So this is something I
haven't played with yet,

00:54:37.128 --> 00:54:40.538
and for me, I'm like such a--

00:54:40.538 --> 00:54:43.520
I don't like to make a bunch
of effort for something.

00:54:43.520 --> 00:54:46.700
So for me going downstairs
getting in the ice tub--

00:54:46.700 --> 00:54:48.280
and I guess you
only need to do it--

00:54:48.280 --> 00:54:50.050
before you work out, you
only go a minute, right?

00:54:50.050 --> 00:54:51.280
ANDREW HUBERMAN: You
do a minute to three.

00:54:51.280 --> 00:54:53.260
Joe and I have been texting
back and forth about this.

00:54:53.260 --> 00:54:55.180
There's a lab at Stanford,
Craig Heller's lab that

00:54:55.180 --> 00:54:57.138
works on cold and
performance, and the athletes

00:54:57.138 --> 00:54:59.260
at Stanford, mainly
the cross-country team

00:54:59.260 --> 00:55:00.700
and the football
players are doing

00:55:00.700 --> 00:55:04.300
cold before their training
because of the huge increase--

00:55:04.300 --> 00:55:07.450
huge long lasting increase in
dopamine and adrenaline that's

00:55:07.450 --> 00:55:08.410
caused by that.

00:55:08.410 --> 00:55:10.090
They're finding it
increases performance

00:55:10.090 --> 00:55:11.965
mainly by waking people
up and getting them--

00:55:11.965 --> 00:55:13.180
it creates energy, basically.

00:55:13.180 --> 00:55:15.520
And students, everyone
thinks of like, oh, athletes

00:55:15.520 --> 00:55:17.030
are all super motivated.

00:55:17.030 --> 00:55:19.900
This is no pick against
Stanford athletes in particular.

00:55:19.900 --> 00:55:22.120
A lot of athletes
are excellent at what

00:55:22.120 --> 00:55:25.360
they do because they're very
lazy when they're not training.

00:55:25.360 --> 00:55:26.090
This is true.

00:55:26.090 --> 00:55:28.010
Not all athletes, but
a lot of athletes are.

00:55:28.010 --> 00:55:29.650
And so they're really good
at resting and recovering

00:55:29.650 --> 00:55:30.670
so they can train more.

00:55:30.670 --> 00:55:34.180
But a lot of athletes have a
hard time getting into gear

00:55:34.180 --> 00:55:37.720
to train every day, and the
cold is a great stimulus.

00:55:37.720 --> 00:55:40.780
It's like a four shot of
espresso kind of stimulus

00:55:40.780 --> 00:55:42.460
without all the jitters.

00:55:42.460 --> 00:55:44.890
JOCKO WILLINK: I think maybe
going in there for a minute

00:55:44.890 --> 00:55:46.330
would be cool before a workout.

00:55:46.330 --> 00:55:47.560
I will say this.

00:55:47.560 --> 00:55:52.510
So I had a long workout and
it was a Saturday, which means

00:55:52.510 --> 00:55:55.390
on Saturday I do jiu-jitsu in
the morning around 10 o'clock,

00:55:55.390 --> 00:56:00.370
and I had like a long
workout, went for a long run,

00:56:00.370 --> 00:56:02.530
it was hot, and I just
got in the ice bath

00:56:02.530 --> 00:56:06.340
and I sat in there for like 7
minutes like the deep chill.

00:56:06.340 --> 00:56:09.290
I got out and then I went right
to jiu-jitsu, and I felt awful.

00:56:09.290 --> 00:56:11.980
I felt absolutely
awful like tight cold,

00:56:11.980 --> 00:56:15.490
and it took me an extra three
rounds to get warmed up again.

00:56:15.490 --> 00:56:20.542
So that kind of left a bad taste
in my mouth for pre-work icing.

00:56:20.542 --> 00:56:22.750
But I'm going to try this
short because I was talking

00:56:22.750 --> 00:56:24.750
to another friend of mine,
they're like, oh, no,

00:56:24.750 --> 00:56:25.765
only go a minute before.

00:56:25.765 --> 00:56:26.890
Maybe I'll give that a try.

00:56:26.890 --> 00:56:29.590
ANDREW HUBERMAN: If it's really
cold, 30 seconds to a minute

00:56:29.590 --> 00:56:31.660
is going to get you
this big release

00:56:31.660 --> 00:56:33.003
and adrenaline and dopamine.

00:56:33.003 --> 00:56:34.420
JOCKO WILLINK:
Actually one time I

00:56:34.420 --> 00:56:38.947
did try the chamber that
blasts cold air on you.

00:56:38.947 --> 00:56:40.030
ANDREW HUBERMAN: The cryo.

00:56:40.030 --> 00:56:40.810
JOCKO WILLINK: Yeah,
the cryo, and I

00:56:40.810 --> 00:56:42.760
did that for like a
minute or whatever,

00:56:42.760 --> 00:56:46.390
and that did make me feel
pre-workout pretty good.

00:56:46.390 --> 00:56:49.120
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah, I think
that the whole notion of cold

00:56:49.120 --> 00:56:50.890
for metabolism,
people say, well, it's

00:56:50.890 --> 00:56:53.020
not that big of an increase
in metabolism, look,

00:56:53.020 --> 00:56:55.360
as far as I'm concerned,
the main function

00:56:55.360 --> 00:56:56.860
of the cold for
most people is going

00:56:56.860 --> 00:57:01.510
to be the discipline of doing
it, the sense of resilience

00:57:01.510 --> 00:57:04.450
that you can build up over time
just being familiar with having

00:57:04.450 --> 00:57:05.710
adrenaline in your system.

00:57:05.710 --> 00:57:08.500
And then the fact that the
dopamine increases are huge

00:57:08.500 --> 00:57:11.483
and long lasting, I mean,
they're like 2.5x increases.

00:57:11.483 --> 00:57:13.150
There's a colleague
of mine at Stanford,

00:57:13.150 --> 00:57:15.730
an alumni who runs our dual
diagnosis addiction clinic.

00:57:15.730 --> 00:57:18.490
She had a patient getting
off cocaine addiction who

00:57:18.490 --> 00:57:22.317
decided to use cold ice baths as
a way to kind of assist himself

00:57:22.317 --> 00:57:22.900
along the way.

00:57:22.900 --> 00:57:24.730
He wasn't getting dopamine
from cocaine anymore,

00:57:24.730 --> 00:57:26.495
so he decided to get
it from the ice bath.

00:57:26.495 --> 00:57:28.870
The difference is cocaine
gives you these sharp increases

00:57:28.870 --> 00:57:31.150
and then decreases that
drop you way below baseline,

00:57:31.150 --> 00:57:33.233
so what do people do if
they go seek more cocaine,

00:57:33.233 --> 00:57:35.890
it's really pernicious that
way, whereas the ice bath

00:57:35.890 --> 00:57:38.730
and cold showers will give
this long arc lasting two

00:57:38.730 --> 00:57:40.670
to three hours or more.

00:57:40.670 --> 00:57:43.270
And that's really
something to treasure,

00:57:43.270 --> 00:57:46.570
the idea that you can basically
save on your heating bill

00:57:46.570 --> 00:57:48.642
give yourself this
huge dopamine increase.

00:57:48.642 --> 00:57:50.350
And I think everything
points to the fact

00:57:50.350 --> 00:57:51.892
that it's healthy
and good but I mean

00:57:51.892 --> 00:57:54.392
obviously it's working for you
to do it after your training.

00:57:54.392 --> 00:57:56.890
I think all the gym rats who
want more hypertrophy you're

00:57:56.890 --> 00:57:59.240
trying to get an extra 1/8
of an inch on their tricep

00:57:59.240 --> 00:58:01.448
or whatever, they freak out
because they hear that it

00:58:01.448 --> 00:58:03.820
can inhibit hypertrophy.

00:58:03.820 --> 00:58:05.780
And then for whatever
reason, there's this--

00:58:05.780 --> 00:58:06.700
JOCKO WILLINK: So
am I doing it wrong?

00:58:06.700 --> 00:58:09.310
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Well, I
mean, yeah, clearly you're

00:58:09.310 --> 00:58:10.300
doing it right now.

00:58:10.300 --> 00:58:13.270
No, you're not-- I don't think
your hypertrophy is suffering.

00:58:13.270 --> 00:58:15.550
I actually am of the
mind that if you're

00:58:15.550 --> 00:58:18.190
training really hard, sure,
getting in the cold afterwards

00:58:18.190 --> 00:58:22.180
might blunt some hypertrophy
that is what the data tell us.

00:58:22.180 --> 00:58:25.000
Andy Galpin is kind of the
expert on that literature.

00:58:25.000 --> 00:58:28.240
But frankly, I don't
know anyone that

00:58:28.240 --> 00:58:29.698
trains really hard
with the weights

00:58:29.698 --> 00:58:31.823
and then gets into the cold
that looks like they're

00:58:31.823 --> 00:58:32.980
suffering from hypertrophy.

00:58:32.980 --> 00:58:34.690
I know a lot of
people, however, who

00:58:34.690 --> 00:58:37.855
love to point fingers at
and poke at cold exposure.

00:58:37.855 --> 00:58:39.730
This seems to be a big
thing on social media.

00:58:39.730 --> 00:58:42.430
People who don't like the
cold love to point out

00:58:42.430 --> 00:58:45.460
the studies showing that the
cold screws up everything,

00:58:45.460 --> 00:58:48.370
and most of them look like they
can eat a few sets in the gym

00:58:48.370 --> 00:58:48.970
to me.

00:58:48.970 --> 00:58:50.830
And I feel comfortable
with poking at them

00:58:50.830 --> 00:58:53.740
because I feel like all
of these are just tools.

00:58:53.740 --> 00:58:56.890
And in any case, I'm a big fan
of deliberate cold exposure,

00:58:56.890 --> 00:58:58.420
mostly for the neural effects.

00:58:58.420 --> 00:59:01.060
Again I'm obsessing over
this concept of energy,

00:59:01.060 --> 00:59:03.370
and it's something that
I can't help but ask

00:59:03.370 --> 00:59:08.140
that is the cognitive side of
this and the effects of winning

00:59:08.140 --> 00:59:08.860
and losing.

00:59:08.860 --> 00:59:12.250
So you obviously have
a lot of deployments

00:59:12.250 --> 00:59:16.210
and a lot of wins, whatever
in the context that meant.

00:59:16.210 --> 00:59:19.840
Kill the target, capture
the hostage, et cetera.

00:59:19.840 --> 00:59:24.110
And then as is
the case with war,

00:59:24.110 --> 00:59:25.930
there have been some
cases of losses.

00:59:25.930 --> 00:59:27.370
You've lost people.

00:59:27.370 --> 00:59:29.968
Maybe there were targets
that weren't accomplished,

00:59:29.968 --> 00:59:30.760
this kind of thing.

00:59:30.760 --> 00:59:33.940
And you've posted about
these, and these are always

00:59:33.940 --> 00:59:35.980
things that are
hard to see, but I

00:59:35.980 --> 00:59:38.800
think it's really it's
important that you

00:59:38.800 --> 00:59:41.950
post about people that you've
lost because, first of all,

00:59:41.950 --> 00:59:43.888
these people served, but
second of all, things

00:59:43.888 --> 00:59:45.680
don't always work out
the way that we want,

00:59:45.680 --> 00:59:50.650
and sometimes to really
catastrophic consequences.

00:59:50.650 --> 00:59:53.520
There's a theory in
biology that when we win,

00:59:53.520 --> 00:59:55.950
we somehow get more
energy to win more

00:59:55.950 --> 00:59:58.680
through the release of,
no surprise, dopamine

00:59:58.680 --> 00:59:59.880
and some related molecules.

00:59:59.880 --> 01:00:02.040
And in fact, testosterone
in both men and women

01:00:02.040 --> 01:00:04.500
is another close cousin
of the dopamine system.

01:00:04.500 --> 01:00:07.740
They're actually released
from the same general--

01:00:07.740 --> 01:00:10.080
patterns of release are
from the same general areas

01:00:10.080 --> 01:00:12.160
in the brain, believe
it or not, and body.

01:00:12.160 --> 01:00:15.415
But when we win, we feel
like we can keep going.

01:00:15.415 --> 01:00:17.040
You look at the team
that wins and it's

01:00:17.040 --> 01:00:18.840
like they'll play another game.

01:00:18.840 --> 01:00:20.400
The Super Bowl
winners you imagine

01:00:20.400 --> 01:00:22.567
they're jumping up and down,
and they could probably

01:00:22.567 --> 01:00:23.940
play another Super Bowl.

01:00:23.940 --> 01:00:26.670
Losing we know can
sure can drop things

01:00:26.670 --> 01:00:29.160
like testosterone and dopamine
for some period of time.

01:00:29.160 --> 01:00:32.890
But when you were
in the Teams what

01:00:32.890 --> 01:00:35.350
was your observation about
how winning and losing

01:00:35.350 --> 01:00:37.485
would impact people in
the short and long term?

01:00:37.485 --> 01:00:38.860
In other words,
would you observe

01:00:38.860 --> 01:00:40.840
people that had a
quick reset button

01:00:40.840 --> 01:00:46.220
and could just say that was
terrible, and then transmute, I

01:00:46.220 --> 01:00:49.010
guess I'm getting into
the Eastern language

01:00:49.010 --> 01:00:54.930
now, to convert that into energy
to go do better the next time?

01:00:54.930 --> 01:00:56.810
Whereas we also see
people, military

01:00:56.810 --> 01:01:00.140
and in the civilian
world that a loss,

01:01:00.140 --> 01:01:02.720
in particular severe
losses, basically set them

01:01:02.720 --> 01:01:05.690
down the path of less
energy, and certainly

01:01:05.690 --> 01:01:06.650
is in less calories.

01:01:06.650 --> 01:01:07.850
In fact, most of the
time it's the other way.

01:01:07.850 --> 01:01:09.080
They start consuming
more calories

01:01:09.080 --> 01:01:10.070
and that doesn't get them going.

01:01:10.070 --> 01:01:12.440
So again, this notion of
energy, and now I'm asking,

01:01:12.440 --> 01:01:15.560
wins versus losses
what did you observe

01:01:15.560 --> 01:01:17.840
and from the perspective
of leadership,

01:01:17.840 --> 01:01:21.050
and maybe more importantly from
the perspective of yourself,

01:01:21.050 --> 01:01:23.010
how do you work with that?

01:01:23.010 --> 01:01:24.830
How do you calibrate
wins and losses?

01:01:24.830 --> 01:01:27.590
How do you transmute
losses into energy?

01:01:27.590 --> 01:01:29.720
Because winds we know
we convert to energy,

01:01:29.720 --> 01:01:32.990
but losses oftentimes can
sup our energy way, way down.

01:01:37.440 --> 01:01:39.270
JOCKO WILLINK: To
start with, I think

01:01:39.270 --> 01:01:42.900
that the selection process
to get into the SEAL Teams

01:01:42.900 --> 01:01:47.220
is going to weed out a bunch of
people that can't recover very

01:01:47.220 --> 01:01:49.440
quickly from something bad.

01:01:49.440 --> 01:01:53.100
So you probably heard these
type of stories before.

01:01:53.100 --> 01:01:55.840
The kid that was the star
of the football team,

01:01:55.840 --> 01:01:58.230
the star of the basketball
team, the captain of this,

01:01:58.230 --> 01:02:00.480
the captain of that, he's
been winning his whole life.

01:02:00.480 --> 01:02:04.500
He goes to BUD/S and he quits,
because in BUD/S, you're

01:02:04.500 --> 01:02:05.650
not going to win.

01:02:05.650 --> 01:02:07.530
You're certainly not
going to win everything.

01:02:07.530 --> 01:02:10.140
They're going to find
what you're not good at,

01:02:10.140 --> 01:02:14.190
and they're going to exploit
that, and you're going to lose.

01:02:14.190 --> 01:02:15.700
This is what happens.

01:02:15.700 --> 01:02:20.280
So a lot of guys
that may lose and it

01:02:20.280 --> 01:02:23.670
disrupts their motivation,
they're probably

01:02:23.670 --> 01:02:25.330
just going to quit.

01:02:25.330 --> 01:02:28.140
And so that's why you get
this massive attrition

01:02:28.140 --> 01:02:30.780
rate with guys that are studs.

01:02:30.780 --> 01:02:34.640
I mean, we're talking
Division 1 athletes.

01:02:34.640 --> 01:02:36.390
Division 1 athletes,
Division 1 wrestlers,

01:02:36.390 --> 01:02:38.340
Division 1 football
players, Division 1 runners

01:02:38.340 --> 01:02:38.882
and swimmers.

01:02:38.882 --> 01:02:40.680
They all come to
BUD/S. They all quit.

01:02:40.680 --> 01:02:42.480
all of them quit
but there's plenty

01:02:42.480 --> 01:02:47.370
of examples of the highest level
of collegiate athlete in sports

01:02:47.370 --> 01:02:50.850
that translate very
well to what you're

01:02:50.850 --> 01:02:54.150
doing in basic SEAL
training, and they quit.

01:02:54.150 --> 01:02:56.860
And sometimes it's because
they don't know how to lose,

01:02:56.860 --> 01:03:00.960
they don't know how to recover
from a loss, and they're just--

01:03:00.960 --> 01:03:04.140
so I think already once
you get to a SEAL Team,

01:03:04.140 --> 01:03:08.320
you've got people that
are generally speaking,

01:03:08.320 --> 01:03:09.870
going to be pretty
resilient when it

01:03:09.870 --> 01:03:12.642
comes to dealing with a loss.

01:03:12.642 --> 01:03:14.100
Not only that, I
mean, you just get

01:03:14.100 --> 01:03:16.350
used to talk about
losing people,

01:03:16.350 --> 01:03:17.925
you're friends
with this guy, you

01:03:17.925 --> 01:03:19.800
meet this guy in SEAL
training, hey, this guy

01:03:19.800 --> 01:03:22.290
seems like a stud, oh,
he's just going to quit.

01:03:22.290 --> 01:03:24.400
And you're going
to lose five, six,

01:03:24.400 --> 01:03:26.070
seven people, eight people.

01:03:26.070 --> 01:03:28.612
People quit so fast you cant'
even keep track of them.

01:03:28.612 --> 01:03:29.820
So you're just going to lose.

01:03:29.820 --> 01:03:32.210
You're just going
to get used to it.

01:03:32.210 --> 01:03:33.230
So there's that.

01:03:33.230 --> 01:03:36.370
Now once you're in
the Teams, what you're

01:03:36.370 --> 01:03:38.598
talking about is now you
start taking much more

01:03:38.598 --> 01:03:40.390
significant loss, you're
not losing a race,

01:03:40.390 --> 01:03:41.660
you're losing of
one of your friends.

01:03:41.660 --> 01:03:43.810
And this is what from a
leadership perspective

01:03:43.810 --> 01:03:45.650
you have to pay attention to.

01:03:45.650 --> 01:03:50.310
So when you're a leader
in any organization,

01:03:50.310 --> 01:03:52.830
you're basically
in charge of a mob.

01:03:52.830 --> 01:03:56.238
When it comes to what their
morale is, they're a mob,

01:03:56.238 --> 01:03:57.780
and they feed off
of each other, just

01:03:57.780 --> 01:03:59.520
like a mob riding in
the streets going,

01:03:59.520 --> 01:04:00.728
oh, we can break this window.

01:04:00.728 --> 01:04:03.570
Let's break all the windows, and
they move this mob mentality.

01:04:03.570 --> 01:04:07.020
And that happens with
morale inside of a team.

01:04:07.020 --> 01:04:11.190
And you as a leader can't
get caught up with the mob.

01:04:11.190 --> 01:04:13.320
You can't let that happen.

01:04:13.320 --> 01:04:16.620
You have to detach yourself
from the mob mentally so

01:04:16.620 --> 01:04:18.750
that you don't get caught
up in their emotions

01:04:18.750 --> 01:04:22.110
and their morale because if you
get caught up in their emotions

01:04:22.110 --> 01:04:25.860
and you get caught up in their
morale, you can't correct it.

01:04:25.860 --> 01:04:29.940
So we go out on a mission,
the mission goes great,

01:04:29.940 --> 01:04:33.300
we get into a gunfight,
kill a couple of bad guys,

01:04:33.300 --> 01:04:36.075
everyone's OK, high fives,
everyone's feeling great.

01:04:36.075 --> 01:04:39.120
You come back to base, hey,
we don't need a debrief.

01:04:39.120 --> 01:04:39.870
That was perfect.

01:04:39.870 --> 01:04:43.200
Hey, we don't need to
get our gear maintained,

01:04:43.200 --> 01:04:45.580
we can just go to
bed, we're awesome.

01:04:45.580 --> 01:04:48.450
That's when the leader has
to say, oh, we've got the mob

01:04:48.450 --> 01:04:51.450
and the mob is becoming
slightly arrogant.

01:04:51.450 --> 01:04:53.798
Hey, guys, real quick,
that was a good op,

01:04:53.798 --> 01:04:55.590
but there are some
things we could improve.

01:04:55.590 --> 01:04:58.800
You got to bring that mob
back and bring them back

01:04:58.800 --> 01:04:59.670
to center line.

01:04:59.670 --> 01:05:01.230
Same thing in the
other direction.

01:05:01.230 --> 01:05:03.180
You go out an operation,
it doesn't go well.

01:05:03.180 --> 01:05:05.160
You go out an operation,
you take casualties.

01:05:05.160 --> 01:05:08.460
Now you come back to base,
you see guys moping around,

01:05:08.460 --> 01:05:12.090
you see the spirit starting
to break, and same thing.

01:05:12.090 --> 01:05:16.052
If you're part of that
mob, you'll be with them,

01:05:16.052 --> 01:05:18.510
your morale will be breaking,
your spirit will be breaking.

01:05:18.510 --> 01:05:20.927
You've got to look up and say,
oh, I see what's happening.

01:05:20.927 --> 01:05:22.830
Hey, guys, listen up.

01:05:22.830 --> 01:05:23.940
That was tough.

01:05:23.940 --> 01:05:26.040
Didn't go the way
we wanted it to go.

01:05:26.040 --> 01:05:27.720
We need to learn some lessons.

01:05:27.720 --> 01:05:29.350
Here are some things
I can do better.

01:05:29.350 --> 01:05:33.150
What can we do better to make
sure that never happens again?

01:05:33.150 --> 01:05:35.730
What can we do to make sure
we have the opportunity

01:05:35.730 --> 01:05:39.930
to go out and avenge our
brother on the battlefield?

01:05:39.930 --> 01:05:41.740
What can we do to move
this thing forward?

01:05:41.740 --> 01:05:44.490
So as a leader when it
comes to winning and losing,

01:05:44.490 --> 01:05:47.910
you're generally going to be the
person countering what the mob

01:05:47.910 --> 01:05:50.820
mentality is because when
the mob starts winning,

01:05:50.820 --> 01:05:53.730
they want to keep winning
and they might get arrogant.

01:05:53.730 --> 01:05:56.700
When the mob is
losing, they might

01:05:56.700 --> 01:06:00.357
start to lose more because their
attitude goes down the drain.

01:06:00.357 --> 01:06:02.190
So that's where you
have to pay attention to

01:06:02.190 --> 01:06:03.440
from a leadership perspective.

01:06:03.440 --> 01:06:11.260
For me personally,
I know what I did

01:06:11.260 --> 01:06:17.920
when I lost guys was
focused on, all right,

01:06:17.920 --> 01:06:25.540
we need to celebrate the life,
we need to mourn the loss,

01:06:25.540 --> 01:06:27.010
and then we need to go to work.

01:06:27.010 --> 01:06:28.480
We need to get our gear back on.

01:06:28.480 --> 01:06:30.290
We need to lock and
load our weapons.

01:06:30.290 --> 01:06:32.210
We need to get back out there.

01:06:32.210 --> 01:06:34.570
I know that that's
what we needed to do.

01:06:34.570 --> 01:06:42.170
So often, the best way
to contend with problems,

01:06:42.170 --> 01:06:49.380
with issues, with adversity is
action, is by taking action.

01:06:49.380 --> 01:06:51.680
The more you sit
and the more you

01:06:51.680 --> 01:06:55.430
wait and the more time you
spend with that adversity

01:06:55.430 --> 01:06:57.920
with the upper hand
inside your head,

01:06:57.920 --> 01:07:00.260
the worse it's going to get.

01:07:00.260 --> 01:07:04.340
So for me always taking action,
making something happen,

01:07:04.340 --> 01:07:06.160
it doesn't have to
be huge, it doesn't

01:07:06.160 --> 01:07:10.840
have to be some mammoth
triumph that you're

01:07:10.840 --> 01:07:13.640
going to go and
pursue, but if you say,

01:07:13.640 --> 01:07:16.713
hey, listen, this what happened.

01:07:16.713 --> 01:07:18.130
Didn't go the way
we wanted it to.

01:07:18.130 --> 01:07:19.900
We're going to get
our gear back on.

01:07:19.900 --> 01:07:21.130
We're going to go back out.

01:07:21.130 --> 01:07:23.530
We're going to do
this other mission.

01:07:23.530 --> 01:07:25.630
And that's what I think.

01:07:25.630 --> 01:07:28.508
Taking action, and it's
in your personal life too,

01:07:28.508 --> 01:07:30.550
something doesn't go the
way you wanted it to go,

01:07:30.550 --> 01:07:31.810
you didn't get the
job you wanted,

01:07:31.810 --> 01:07:34.060
you didn't get the hire, you
didn't get the promotion,

01:07:34.060 --> 01:07:36.910
you can go home and sit
there and dwell on it.

01:07:36.910 --> 01:07:38.410
That's not getting
you any progress.

01:07:38.410 --> 01:07:41.083
Or you say, OK, you know what,
let me do a quick analysis why

01:07:41.083 --> 01:07:42.250
didn't I get that promotion.

01:07:42.250 --> 01:07:44.710
Oh, it's because I didn't
get this qualification

01:07:44.710 --> 01:07:47.960
or I hadn't jumped
through this gate.

01:07:47.960 --> 01:07:48.460
OK.

01:07:48.460 --> 01:07:48.960
Cool.

01:07:48.960 --> 01:07:49.710
What do I need?

01:07:49.710 --> 01:07:51.640
Let me look into how do I
get to jump through that gate

01:07:51.640 --> 01:07:52.830
so next time I will
get the promotion?

01:07:52.830 --> 01:07:54.230
And you start taking action.

01:07:54.230 --> 01:07:57.460
So action for me is a
cure for a lot of problems

01:07:57.460 --> 01:07:59.952
that we have in life.

01:07:59.952 --> 01:08:02.160
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I love this
because the image that's

01:08:02.160 --> 01:08:04.500
arising my mind, I'll
share it with you

01:08:04.500 --> 01:08:07.860
and tell me where it agrees with
and maybe violates what you're

01:08:07.860 --> 01:08:10.380
telling me, but what I was
imagining it when you were

01:08:10.380 --> 01:08:14.100
talking about leadership in
the mob is a bunch of candles,

01:08:14.100 --> 01:08:16.788
but not wax candles,
these are oil candles.

01:08:16.788 --> 01:08:18.330
And you're talking
about a win, so it

01:08:18.330 --> 01:08:20.040
could be an op to go out
and win, it could be a team,

01:08:20.040 --> 01:08:22.859
it could be an individual taking
an exam and they get an A-plus,

01:08:22.859 --> 01:08:25.830
it doesn't matter, but
they're riding high.

01:08:25.830 --> 01:08:28.410
I mean, those winds we know
crank up those catecholamines,

01:08:28.410 --> 01:08:30.810
and it's as if the
intensity of that flame

01:08:30.810 --> 01:08:32.910
starts going up
as a consequence.

01:08:32.910 --> 01:08:33.750
That's natural.

01:08:33.750 --> 01:08:36.149
But the oil and the candle
is continuing to burn down.

01:08:36.149 --> 01:08:38.888
Like you need to celebrate wins,
but you're burning that oil.

01:08:38.888 --> 01:08:40.680
And so what we're really
talking about here

01:08:40.680 --> 01:08:44.010
is how to moderate and
then reclaim energy.

01:08:44.010 --> 01:08:45.840
And I was imagining
as the leader who

01:08:45.840 --> 01:08:47.850
like OK, guys,
great, but listen,

01:08:47.850 --> 01:08:49.920
you're burning that oil.

01:08:49.920 --> 01:08:51.990
That oil is what
got you the win.

01:08:51.990 --> 01:08:55.609
Let's not just clamp it like
we can do a few fist bumps

01:08:55.609 --> 01:08:57.359
and maybe celebrate
it in some other ways,

01:08:57.359 --> 01:08:59.819
but then let's take
the energy we got

01:08:59.819 --> 01:09:02.880
and put it to the next thing
rather than just go crazy.

01:09:02.880 --> 01:09:04.979
Drugs of abuse, in
particular drugs

01:09:04.979 --> 01:09:07.740
that tap into the dopamine
system, namely cocaine

01:09:07.740 --> 01:09:10.890
and amphetamine, and just
because there's no way

01:09:10.890 --> 01:09:13.410
to avoid this if we're
being true to the biology,

01:09:13.410 --> 01:09:15.633
the energy the
dopamine system was

01:09:15.633 --> 01:09:17.550
designed for foraging
for all sorts of things,

01:09:17.550 --> 01:09:20.189
food, so people that
overindulge food or seek out

01:09:20.189 --> 01:09:24.210
food, sex, people that
overindulge in these things.

01:09:24.210 --> 01:09:25.800
Those things, and
again this sort

01:09:25.800 --> 01:09:27.899
of leans to Eastern
philosophy a bit but,

01:09:27.899 --> 01:09:30.180
there's Western
neuroscience or neuroscience

01:09:30.180 --> 01:09:31.710
we should just
say to support it,

01:09:31.710 --> 01:09:34.319
you start to deplete
these dopamine systems.

01:09:34.319 --> 01:09:36.149
The baseline starts to drop.

01:09:36.149 --> 01:09:38.670
And so I'm imagining that
the leader, you in this case,

01:09:38.670 --> 01:09:40.859
is saying like, listen,
let's tone it down,

01:09:40.859 --> 01:09:43.290
use the energy that we've
gained and put it to good use,

01:09:43.290 --> 01:09:45.420
rather than just burn
it up enjoying it.

01:09:45.420 --> 01:09:47.939
And then, of course,
after a loss when those--

01:09:47.939 --> 01:09:51.149
I sort of think of the
candles going dimmer

01:09:51.149 --> 01:09:52.670
but the oil reserve
is still there,

01:09:52.670 --> 01:09:54.420
and it's like how do
you start to tap back

01:09:54.420 --> 01:09:55.320
into the oil reserve?

01:09:55.320 --> 01:09:57.960
Well, you have to actually
ramp the candle up again.

01:09:57.960 --> 01:10:02.070
You can't just sit there waiting
for the intensity of the flame

01:10:02.070 --> 01:10:02.850
to come back.

01:10:02.850 --> 01:10:04.350
You actually have to
do something in order

01:10:04.350 --> 01:10:05.142
for that to happen.

01:10:05.142 --> 01:10:06.660
So maybe this isn't
the best analogy

01:10:06.660 --> 01:10:10.028
because it lacks exactly
what is the person turning

01:10:10.028 --> 01:10:11.820
the intensity up and
down on these candles,

01:10:11.820 --> 01:10:13.110
but that's what comes to mind.

01:10:13.110 --> 01:10:14.527
And in Eastern
traditions, there's

01:10:14.527 --> 01:10:17.850
this idea of Qi of
energy, energy to fight,

01:10:17.850 --> 01:10:20.970
energy to seek mates, energy
to seek food, energy for sex,

01:10:20.970 --> 01:10:22.958
energy for all of it
is the same energy.

01:10:22.958 --> 01:10:25.500
And I actually believe that the
energy that they're referring

01:10:25.500 --> 01:10:27.060
to are these catecholamines.

01:10:27.060 --> 01:10:27.787
I really do.

01:10:27.787 --> 01:10:29.370
Now there are other
energy systems too

01:10:29.370 --> 01:10:33.120
relating to child rearing,
pair bonding, oxytocin,

01:10:33.120 --> 01:10:36.247
all the kind of fuzzy warm
stuff, that's super important.

01:10:36.247 --> 01:10:37.830
I mean, we wouldn't
exist as a species

01:10:37.830 --> 01:10:41.160
the way we do we didn't honor
that energy system also.

01:10:41.160 --> 01:10:44.100
And that energy system that
we normally think of as love

01:10:44.100 --> 01:10:47.100
as opposed to forward
center of mass synergizes

01:10:47.100 --> 01:10:48.120
with this system.

01:10:48.120 --> 01:10:51.360
When you're working with
