about when we're
forward center of mass

01:24:23.030 --> 01:24:26.253
and we have to control the
intensity of the candle

01:24:26.253 --> 01:24:28.670
and the level of the oil, I
don't know what those actually

01:24:28.670 --> 01:24:29.720
mapped to exactly.

01:24:29.720 --> 01:24:32.330
We could probably figure it
out if we really parsed it,

01:24:32.330 --> 01:24:37.640
but that's the idea
here, the analogy.

01:24:37.640 --> 01:24:41.900
In a kind of
surprising way, we know

01:24:41.900 --> 01:24:46.950
that for sure one way to restore
levels of motivation drive

01:24:46.950 --> 01:24:50.100
enthusiasm, and to some
degree confidence that things

01:24:50.100 --> 01:24:54.970
could be different is through
deep rest, things like sleep.

01:24:54.970 --> 01:24:56.470
When things are
really, really hard,

01:24:56.470 --> 01:24:59.260
when kids are just like they
were like falling apart,

01:24:59.260 --> 01:25:01.180
it's like you put them
to sleep, they wake up

01:25:01.180 --> 01:25:02.320
and they're like
delightful, they're

01:25:02.320 --> 01:25:03.850
running around their
pajamas when they're little,

01:25:03.850 --> 01:25:05.990
and a teenager wakes up
after a good night's sleep,

01:25:05.990 --> 01:25:08.650
they might be a little
like surly, but we're back.

01:25:08.650 --> 01:25:09.790
Adults are like this.

01:25:09.790 --> 01:25:11.710
The world is falling apart.

01:25:11.710 --> 01:25:15.640
You go to sleep, wake up,
OK, I might be able to manage

01:25:15.640 --> 01:25:17.960
this kind of mindset.

01:25:17.960 --> 01:25:19.040
So sleep.

01:25:19.040 --> 01:25:20.720
And then the other
one is we know

01:25:20.720 --> 01:25:26.570
that play, the kind of physical
activity or mental activity

01:25:26.570 --> 01:25:28.610
where it might be a
little competitive,

01:25:28.610 --> 01:25:31.705
but the stakes are low, and it's
really more about connection

01:25:31.705 --> 01:25:33.830
with the activity or
connection with somebody else.

01:25:33.830 --> 01:25:35.930
Like we're going to
play a game of whatever,

01:25:35.930 --> 01:25:38.180
I won't play chess with Lex
because he'll kick my ass,

01:25:38.180 --> 01:25:39.805
I won't do jiu-jitsu
with either of you

01:25:39.805 --> 01:25:42.110
because you'll beat me
up both of you, kindly,

01:25:42.110 --> 01:25:43.490
but you'll do it.

01:25:43.490 --> 01:25:45.710
But if we were to play a
game, it's just us yeah,

01:25:45.710 --> 01:25:47.085
we might be a
little competitive,

01:25:47.085 --> 01:25:49.810
but the stakes are low.

01:25:49.810 --> 01:25:55.380
We know that play and
social connection and sleep

01:25:55.380 --> 01:25:58.800
are basically the
reservoir or the location

01:25:58.800 --> 01:26:02.770
that you go to refill the oil
in the candle every single time.

01:26:02.770 --> 01:26:06.190
And so for you, where do those
things play into your routine?

01:26:06.190 --> 01:26:09.360
You mention you can go hard all
day and then in the evening,

01:26:09.360 --> 01:26:12.092
is it dinner with family,
typically if you're at home,

01:26:12.092 --> 01:26:13.300
and what does that look like?

01:26:13.300 --> 01:26:14.280
I know we're kind
of parsing in--

01:26:14.280 --> 01:26:17.010
I don't want to carve into
your personal life too deeply,

01:26:17.010 --> 01:26:18.630
obviously, there are
boundaries there,

01:26:18.630 --> 01:26:19.990
but what does that look like?

01:26:19.990 --> 01:26:22.290
Is it everyone at the
table, phones away,

01:26:22.290 --> 01:26:24.330
and you're talking
about the day?

01:26:24.330 --> 01:26:26.785
Or is it-- yeah, share
with us a little bit

01:26:26.785 --> 01:26:28.410
of what that looks
like because I think

01:26:28.410 --> 01:26:32.027
it is an important contour
to what you're about

01:26:32.027 --> 01:26:34.110
and what we're talking
about that most people just

01:26:34.110 --> 01:26:35.167
don't have a window into.

01:26:35.167 --> 01:26:37.000
JOCKO WILLINK: Yeah,
so, well, first of all,

01:26:37.000 --> 01:26:40.170
I mean, just the refilling
the tank with games, I mean,

01:26:40.170 --> 01:26:41.212
that's what jiu-jitsu is.

01:26:41.212 --> 01:26:42.670
You're going to go
and you're going

01:26:42.670 --> 01:26:44.310
to have social
connection with people,

01:26:44.310 --> 01:26:46.185
you're going to talk to
people that you know,

01:26:46.185 --> 01:26:48.277
you're going to
joke about whatever,

01:26:48.277 --> 01:26:49.360
then you're going to roll.

01:26:49.360 --> 01:26:50.850
You're going to have
a good time rolling,

01:26:50.850 --> 01:26:52.110
you're going to get
a little sweat on,

01:26:52.110 --> 01:26:54.485
you're going to feel good,
then you're going to high five

01:26:54.485 --> 01:26:55.770
like there's huge--

01:26:55.770 --> 01:26:59.640
that's there, and your
brain is kind of off.

01:26:59.640 --> 01:27:03.630
When you're training
jiu-jitsu at a certain level,

01:27:03.630 --> 01:27:05.380
you're not going to
be thinking anymore.

01:27:05.380 --> 01:27:06.380
Same thing with surfing.

01:27:06.380 --> 01:27:08.220
You go surfing and
you catch a wave.

01:27:08.220 --> 01:27:10.175
I mean, you're not
thinking about, oh, I

01:27:10.175 --> 01:27:11.550
need to put my
balance over here.

01:27:11.550 --> 01:27:13.592
No, it's happening and
you're having a good time.

01:27:13.592 --> 01:27:18.370
So I would say that restoration
for me comes from those two

01:27:18.370 --> 01:27:19.630
things for sure.

01:27:19.630 --> 01:27:23.170
And then yeah, I mean, my wife
and my kids when I get home--

01:27:23.170 --> 01:27:27.820
and my kids are older now, and
they're out of the house except

01:27:27.820 --> 01:27:30.800
for one.

01:27:30.800 --> 01:27:33.910
And when I was in the
Navy when they were young,

01:27:33.910 --> 01:27:37.120
I wasn't around at all.

01:27:37.120 --> 01:27:39.550
We would rarely have dinner
together because I was gone,

01:27:39.550 --> 01:27:41.680
coming home late, you're
working all the time,

01:27:41.680 --> 01:27:46.030
you can never get all your work
done, I'm training jiu-jitsu,

01:27:46.030 --> 01:27:51.670
we rarely ate dinner as a
family when I was in the Teams.

01:27:51.670 --> 01:27:55.420
And now when I'm home, we
can eat together for sure.

01:27:55.420 --> 01:27:57.880
My daughter that's
still at home,

01:27:57.880 --> 01:28:01.180
she's going to-- we sit out
there and eat dinner and talk

01:28:01.180 --> 01:28:04.570
about just normal things
that people talk about,

01:28:04.570 --> 01:28:08.920
like how to conduct
a night ambush or--

01:28:08.920 --> 01:28:15.040
let's talk about normal daily
things and what's going on.

01:28:15.040 --> 01:28:18.310
And my daughter,
she's 13 right now

01:28:18.310 --> 01:28:21.160
and she talks to me
about all kinds of stuff,

01:28:21.160 --> 01:28:23.290
and it's awesome.

01:28:23.290 --> 01:28:27.580
Yeah, I'm definitely
enjoying that aspect

01:28:27.580 --> 01:28:31.720
of being around more than I
was when I was in the Teams

01:28:31.720 --> 01:28:34.420
and we didn't have dinner.

01:28:34.420 --> 01:28:35.170
Didn't happen.

01:28:35.170 --> 01:28:39.430
So I would take my
kids to jiu-jitsu,

01:28:39.430 --> 01:28:41.260
I taught jiu-jitsu
classes when I could

01:28:41.260 --> 01:28:45.430
when I was in the Teams,
would do workouts with them

01:28:45.430 --> 01:28:48.190
in the morning if I had time
on the weekends for sure,

01:28:48.190 --> 01:28:52.810
stuff like that with my kids,
that's kind of what I did.

01:28:52.810 --> 01:28:55.030
But now yeah, we--

01:28:55.030 --> 01:29:01.360
my wife is awesome, and
she is a great cook now

01:29:01.360 --> 01:29:05.410
because when we first got
married, it was questionable.

01:29:05.410 --> 01:29:07.975
I just was harassing her
about this the other day.

01:29:10.750 --> 01:29:13.360
She's an unbelievable cook
now, and it's awesome.

01:29:13.360 --> 01:29:16.730
And when we first met,
by her own admission,

01:29:16.730 --> 01:29:18.095
she will tell you she was not.

01:29:18.095 --> 01:29:19.720
She's from England,
and so they're just

01:29:19.720 --> 01:29:21.642
not cooking what we're liking.

01:29:21.642 --> 01:29:23.350
ANDREW HUBERMAN: No,
the food over there,

01:29:23.350 --> 01:29:25.300
at least when I was growing
up in the few times I made it

01:29:25.300 --> 01:29:27.320
over there, the food
was pretty dreadful.

01:29:27.320 --> 01:29:30.640
I mean, there were some
exceptions to that,

01:29:30.640 --> 01:29:32.480
and they drank a lot over there.

01:29:32.480 --> 01:29:34.660
So I've been to some
scientific meetings over there

01:29:34.660 --> 01:29:37.330
where they would start with
like Sherri in the afternoon,

01:29:37.330 --> 01:29:38.390
and then beer after work.

01:29:38.390 --> 01:29:39.460
It was outrageous.

01:29:39.460 --> 01:29:42.467
I mean, the amount of alcohol
intake was just absurd.

01:29:42.467 --> 01:29:43.300
JOCKO WILLINK: Yeah.

01:29:43.300 --> 01:29:44.552
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Sorry, Brits.

01:29:44.552 --> 01:29:45.760
And again I'm not trying to--

01:29:45.760 --> 01:29:47.170
I did an episode on
alcohol, a lot of people

01:29:47.170 --> 01:29:49.160
were angry about that episode
because it's basically said,

01:29:49.160 --> 01:29:50.590
once you get past
two drinks a week,

01:29:50.590 --> 01:29:52.173
you're starting to
head into territory

01:29:52.173 --> 01:29:54.245
that can deplete
your health, so you

01:29:54.245 --> 01:29:56.120
got to do a lot of other
things to offset it.

01:29:56.120 --> 01:29:58.218
But they drink a lot.

01:29:58.218 --> 01:30:00.010
JOCKO WILLINK: Yeah,
the Brits drink a lot.

01:30:00.010 --> 01:30:03.850
I have spent time over
there with my father-in-law,

01:30:03.850 --> 01:30:08.080
my brother-in-law, and we
definitely drank a lot.

01:30:08.080 --> 01:30:10.780
So I'm glad I don't live there
and had to drink with them.

01:30:10.780 --> 01:30:13.120
I'm glad I don't have to
drink with them all the time.

01:30:13.120 --> 01:30:14.710
I'd probably be dead.

01:30:14.710 --> 01:30:15.910
ANDREW HUBERMAN: You were
straight edge growing up,

01:30:15.910 --> 01:30:16.510
right?

01:30:16.510 --> 01:30:18.470
JOCKO WILLINK: Yeah, I
mean, when I was a kid,

01:30:18.470 --> 01:30:21.970
and then when I joined the
Navy, I started drinking.

01:30:21.970 --> 01:30:24.430
That's like part of the
culture that I bought into.

01:30:24.430 --> 01:30:29.980
And I wanted to be
a good SEAL, and I'm

01:30:29.980 --> 01:30:33.250
looking around at the guys that
were considered good SEALs,

01:30:33.250 --> 01:30:34.510
oh, so we're drinking?

01:30:34.510 --> 01:30:36.740
OK, that's what we're doing.

01:30:36.740 --> 01:30:38.275
And that's what I did.

01:30:43.590 --> 01:30:46.642
Looking back now,
I didn't really--

01:30:46.642 --> 01:30:48.600
I didn't think of it as
a big deal at the time.

01:30:48.600 --> 01:30:51.183
I wasn't like-- well, first of
all, even when I was growing up

01:30:51.183 --> 01:30:55.980
and I didn't drink and didn't
smoke, didn't do drugs,

01:30:55.980 --> 01:31:00.630
I wasn't like a guy putting an X
on my hand, although my friends

01:31:00.630 --> 01:31:02.520
and I, we all didn't
drink, didn't smoke.

01:31:02.520 --> 01:31:04.630
So we definitely-- look, I
was listening to Minor Threat

01:31:04.630 --> 01:31:05.338
when I was a kid.

01:31:05.338 --> 01:31:08.070
I mean, I get it, but
I wasn't I running

01:31:08.070 --> 01:31:11.430
around telling everyone
I was straight edge,

01:31:11.430 --> 01:31:13.770
but I was on that path for sure.

01:31:13.770 --> 01:31:16.260
And then when I got in the
SEAL Teams, it's like, OK,

01:31:16.260 --> 01:31:17.928
this is a different culture.

01:31:17.928 --> 01:31:18.720
I'm not used to it.

01:31:18.720 --> 01:31:22.620
And I didn't really even
understand what drinking was.

01:31:22.620 --> 01:31:25.680
I mean, I never had been drunk.

01:31:25.680 --> 01:31:27.990
So got on the SEAL Team
so I was like, OK, well,

01:31:27.990 --> 01:31:33.060
once I turned 21, hey, these
guys were going to have fun,

01:31:33.060 --> 01:31:35.850
and I kind of just, OK,
that's what we're doing,

01:31:35.850 --> 01:31:38.350
and then I drank a ton while
I was in the SEAL Teams.

01:31:38.350 --> 01:31:44.870
And then as I retired from
the SEAL Teams and went out,

01:31:44.870 --> 01:31:48.480
we basically went to every
bar that we would normally

01:31:48.480 --> 01:31:51.630
go to like as SEALs.

01:31:51.630 --> 01:31:55.230
I think we closed out the
night at the Park Shores in OB.

01:31:55.230 --> 01:31:57.835
And when I went
home that morning,

01:31:57.835 --> 01:31:59.460
I woke up the next
morning, worked out,

01:31:59.460 --> 01:32:03.450
and then I just kind of
stopped drinking because--

01:32:03.450 --> 01:32:05.760
and now I just
definitely, I mean,

01:32:05.760 --> 01:32:07.440
now I just don't
really drink anymore.

01:32:07.440 --> 01:32:11.015
So that's that.

01:32:11.015 --> 01:32:12.390
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Yeah, when I had

01:32:12.390 --> 01:32:13.950
my lab in San Diego
for five years,

01:32:13.950 --> 01:32:16.348
that's where my lab
started, I definitely

01:32:16.348 --> 01:32:17.640
saw a lot of Team guys in bars.

01:32:17.640 --> 01:32:20.160
You guys would come
in and take over bars.

01:32:20.160 --> 01:32:21.900
These were little takeovers.

01:32:21.900 --> 01:32:22.745
JOCKO WILLINK: Yeah.

01:32:22.745 --> 01:32:24.120
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
I wouldn't leave

01:32:24.120 --> 01:32:26.340
because I wouldn't like
give them the satisfaction

01:32:26.340 --> 01:32:29.817
or me the dissatisfaction, but
it was a little frustrating.

01:32:29.817 --> 01:32:31.650
You're in there, you're
having a decent time

01:32:31.650 --> 01:32:33.600
and then just this
enormous pack of Team guys

01:32:33.600 --> 01:32:35.220
comes in and sort
of like, all right,

01:32:35.220 --> 01:32:38.010
maybe it's time to
close out the night.

01:32:38.010 --> 01:32:39.577
I was friends with
enough of them,

01:32:39.577 --> 01:32:41.160
but if you're not
really a part of it,

01:32:41.160 --> 01:32:42.993
you're not really a
part of it, and I always

01:32:42.993 --> 01:32:44.310
knew that and understood that.

01:32:44.310 --> 01:32:49.110
One thing that I think really
comes through now especially

01:32:49.110 --> 01:32:51.450
but throughout our
discussion and all the things

01:32:51.450 --> 01:32:53.337
I've seen of you,
and it gets me back

01:32:53.337 --> 01:32:55.170
to this thing that came
up at the beginning,

01:32:55.170 --> 01:32:58.460
I'd really like to
return to is that you

01:32:58.460 --> 01:33:03.562
seem to have a very strong
sense of context and self.

01:33:03.562 --> 01:33:05.270
I'll just say it, I'm
not a psychologist,

01:33:05.270 --> 01:33:07.940
I'm not here to play
one, but what you just

01:33:07.940 --> 01:33:09.928
described was that
in one context,

01:33:09.928 --> 01:33:11.720
you know it made sense
to be straight edge,

01:33:11.720 --> 01:33:14.345
and you mentioned Minor Threat,
great band, straight edge band,

01:33:14.345 --> 01:33:17.990
but when it came time to run
around with your friends then

01:33:17.990 --> 01:33:21.763
and it made sense to be without
alcohol or drugs or anything

01:33:21.763 --> 01:33:23.930
at that stage and then you
get into another context,

01:33:23.930 --> 01:33:25.440
it's like OK, I can do this.

01:33:25.440 --> 01:33:27.783
I can drink and still
perform well and do

01:33:27.783 --> 01:33:28.950
all the things I need to do.

01:33:28.950 --> 01:33:32.165
And then when that closes out,
I'm going to do something else.

01:33:32.165 --> 01:33:34.040
And some people are like
that because they're

01:33:34.040 --> 01:33:36.170
kind of a chameleon.

01:33:36.170 --> 01:33:38.480
They're switching themselves
depending on the context,

01:33:38.480 --> 01:33:40.413
and they're kind
of getting accents

01:33:40.413 --> 01:33:42.080
when they're in one
location or another,

01:33:42.080 --> 01:33:43.490
but that's not you at all.

01:33:43.490 --> 01:33:46.250
I can tell with certainty
that's not you at all.

01:33:46.250 --> 01:33:50.100
There seems to be a very
strong sense of self

01:33:50.100 --> 01:33:53.690
so that when we have a
sense of self that's firm,

01:33:53.690 --> 01:33:55.290
we can go into
different contexts.

01:33:55.290 --> 01:33:56.915
We can even change
our behavior, but we

01:33:56.915 --> 01:33:58.625
don't lose who we
are, which means

01:33:58.625 --> 01:33:59.750
we can always return to it.

01:33:59.750 --> 01:34:02.255
The image in my
mind is like I've

01:34:02.255 --> 01:34:04.130
done a little bit of
scuba diving, not a lot,

01:34:04.130 --> 01:34:07.010
but during some of the more
advanced training for us,

01:34:07.010 --> 01:34:09.060
this wasn't SEAL
training, of course,

01:34:09.060 --> 01:34:11.628
was following a line in the
dark, like a night dive.

01:34:11.628 --> 01:34:13.670
And you're following a
line, and sometimes you're

01:34:13.670 --> 01:34:15.420
navigating with tools,
but suddenly you're

01:34:15.420 --> 01:34:16.610
following a line underwater.

01:34:16.610 --> 01:34:18.993
And this idea that you
can let go of that line,

01:34:18.993 --> 01:34:21.410
but you have to remember where
it is you can return to it.

01:34:21.410 --> 01:34:23.493
And that's kind of how I
imagine the sense of self

01:34:23.493 --> 01:34:25.580
because in different
contexts, most healthy people

01:34:25.580 --> 01:34:26.690
modify themselves a little bit.

01:34:26.690 --> 01:34:28.640
We act differently at
a wedding or a funeral

01:34:28.640 --> 01:34:32.005
than we do in class or
out with our friends.

01:34:32.005 --> 01:34:33.380
Of course, it's
an important part

01:34:33.380 --> 01:34:35.150
of being a functional human.

01:34:35.150 --> 01:34:37.880
So it seems to me that
from a pretty early stage,

01:34:37.880 --> 01:34:41.283
you had a pretty
good sense of self.

01:34:41.283 --> 01:34:42.950
Now I don't know if
you sit in your room

01:34:42.950 --> 01:34:45.530
and meditate on who is
Jocko Willink and touch

01:34:45.530 --> 01:34:47.300
that central chord of self.

01:34:47.300 --> 01:34:48.320
I'm guessing you don't.

01:34:48.320 --> 01:34:50.790
But as I say this,
I have to ask,

01:34:50.790 --> 01:34:54.770
was there something in your
upbringing, your parents

01:34:54.770 --> 01:34:59.390
or was it just kind of how
you always imagine yourself is

01:34:59.390 --> 01:35:00.770
like, yeah, this is who I am.

01:35:00.770 --> 01:35:04.385
No matter what happens around
me, I kind of know who I am.

01:35:04.385 --> 01:35:06.260
Even if I'm engaging in
some of the behaviors

01:35:06.260 --> 01:35:07.885
that I might not do
in another context,

01:35:07.885 --> 01:35:09.530
I know who I am
because I actually

01:35:09.530 --> 01:35:14.030
think that many people do not
have a very firm sense of self,

01:35:14.030 --> 01:35:17.960
or their sense of self is
so rigid that they can only

01:35:17.960 --> 01:35:20.780
operate in this narrow
trench of one domain of life

01:35:20.780 --> 01:35:23.180
and they end up
very, very isolated.

01:35:23.180 --> 01:35:25.790
So I'd love for you to share
with us what your recollections

01:35:25.790 --> 01:35:29.420
are like the first time you
realized like, yeah, I'm

01:35:29.420 --> 01:35:31.740
good in a bunch of
different places.

01:35:31.740 --> 01:35:34.670
I'm safe or I'm whatever,
because I think this is also

01:35:34.670 --> 01:35:35.960
relates to confidence.

01:35:39.190 --> 01:35:41.440
JOCKO WILLINK: I'm
glad you are giving me

01:35:41.440 --> 01:35:43.780
the benefit of the doubt
on going in the Teams

01:35:43.780 --> 01:35:47.193
and being like, oh, hey, man,
looks like guys are drinking

01:35:47.193 --> 01:35:48.610
and I haven't
really drank before,

01:35:48.610 --> 01:35:51.350
and it seems like these
guys are having a good time.

01:35:51.350 --> 01:35:52.450
Let's go have a good
time with these guys,

01:35:52.450 --> 01:35:53.950
and that's kind of
what I did right.

01:35:56.550 --> 01:36:03.010
As far as, and I look at it
now and unfortunately for me,

01:36:03.010 --> 01:36:05.620
I look at alcohol now as just--

01:36:05.620 --> 01:36:07.960
I've seen it destroy
so many people

01:36:07.960 --> 01:36:13.110
that I've now kind of look
at it and go, man, I don't

01:36:13.110 --> 01:36:14.550
think people should drink.

01:36:14.550 --> 01:36:15.690
And look, I get it.

01:36:15.690 --> 01:36:17.100
I'm kind of an example.

01:36:17.100 --> 01:36:19.480
I used to drink and go
out and have a good time,

01:36:19.480 --> 01:36:20.855
and it wasn't that
big of a deal.

01:36:20.855 --> 01:36:23.040
It didn't negatively impact
me in some dramatic way,

01:36:23.040 --> 01:36:28.680
but I have so many friends
that it is horrible for.

01:36:28.680 --> 01:36:31.740
It is all but
ruined their lives.

01:36:31.740 --> 01:36:36.720
And it's totally legal,
which is kind of bizarre.

01:36:36.720 --> 01:36:40.830
So I think that figuring
that out when I look back,

01:36:40.830 --> 01:36:45.130
and the culture in the
SEAL Teams was very--

01:36:45.130 --> 01:36:47.620
there was very alcohol centric.

01:36:47.620 --> 01:36:52.120
And part of that is because
it's just like a fraternity is

01:36:52.120 --> 01:36:55.152
alcohol centric,
or a football team

01:36:55.152 --> 01:36:56.860
is because you got a
bunch of young dudes

01:36:56.860 --> 01:36:58.735
that are going to drink
and have a good time.

01:36:58.735 --> 01:37:02.440
So it's just sort of a
young dude kind of thing,

01:37:02.440 --> 01:37:07.480
and unfortunately, it ends up
ruining a lot of people's lives

01:37:07.480 --> 01:37:10.000
and they make bad decisions
and they do stupid things

01:37:10.000 --> 01:37:11.680
and it's just not good.

01:37:11.680 --> 01:37:14.680
And I think the culture
is moving away from that

01:37:14.680 --> 01:37:17.230
a little bit in the SEAL Teams.

01:37:17.230 --> 01:37:22.235
My alcohol brief
used to say, I used

01:37:22.235 --> 01:37:26.450
to tell my guys if you
go out, you get a DUI,

01:37:26.450 --> 01:37:29.400
you get put in jail
for a fight, you

01:37:29.400 --> 01:37:32.460
get hurt because you're
doing something drunk,

01:37:32.460 --> 01:37:34.890
you just did Al
Qaeda's job for them.

01:37:34.890 --> 01:37:36.360
You just did al-Qaeda's job.

01:37:36.360 --> 01:37:37.740
They want to take you
off the battlefield,

01:37:37.740 --> 01:37:39.150
and you just took yourself
off the battlefield.

01:37:39.150 --> 01:37:40.560
You can't go on deployment now.

01:37:40.560 --> 01:37:43.080
And that would always hit guys.

01:37:43.080 --> 01:37:46.140
And I think that
that's the SEAL Teams

01:37:46.140 --> 01:37:47.880
kind of leaning in
that direction more,

01:37:47.880 --> 01:37:51.390
realizing the
negativity of alcohol.

01:37:51.390 --> 01:37:54.343
I wish I would have
realized that earlier.

01:37:54.343 --> 01:37:56.010
I wish I would have
been a better leader

01:37:56.010 --> 01:37:57.930
and recognize that
in an earlier stage,

01:37:57.930 --> 01:38:00.360
and recognize that just because
I was kind of getting away

01:38:00.360 --> 01:38:05.110
with it, meaning I didn't
wake up in the morning,

01:38:05.110 --> 01:38:06.360
oh, man, I can't wait to quit.

01:38:06.360 --> 01:38:09.245
I never really had that feeling.

01:38:09.245 --> 01:38:11.620
I wish I would have realized
that there's other guys that

01:38:11.620 --> 01:38:17.072
do, and there's people that
can operate and be functional

01:38:17.072 --> 01:38:18.780
and it doesn't really
impact their lives,

01:38:18.780 --> 01:38:20.405
but there's a lot of
people that don't.

01:38:20.405 --> 01:38:22.962
And I don't think it's worth
the dice roll to start drinking.

01:38:22.962 --> 01:38:24.920
I just don't think it's
worth the dice for what

01:38:24.920 --> 01:38:26.280
do you get out of it?

01:38:26.280 --> 01:38:31.190
So I think that
overall that's why if--

01:38:31.190 --> 01:38:34.310
when I think about alcohol, I
just think about all the lives

01:38:34.310 --> 01:38:36.500
that it's ruined,
and I don't like it.

01:38:36.500 --> 01:38:38.750
And I wish I would have
done a better job of saying,

01:38:38.750 --> 01:38:40.970
you know what, this
is probably not good,

01:38:40.970 --> 01:38:42.290
and we shouldn't do this.

01:38:42.290 --> 01:38:45.320
And unfortunately I didn't, and
I try and convey that message

01:38:45.320 --> 01:38:46.550
as much as I can now.

01:38:46.550 --> 01:38:50.210
And it did bring me back to my
roots because when I was a kid,

01:38:50.210 --> 01:38:54.482
it was like alcohol is weak,
and you look at these guys,

01:38:54.482 --> 01:38:55.940
they don't know
what they're doing,

01:38:55.940 --> 01:38:59.340
they're acting like idiots,
I'm not going to be like that.

01:38:59.340 --> 01:39:02.150
And so as I got older, once
I got on the SEAL Teams,

01:39:02.150 --> 01:39:04.640
I went back to that.

01:39:04.640 --> 01:39:10.610
As far as where I became
me, I just actually

01:39:10.610 --> 01:39:14.060
have to give a lot of credit
to the music that I grew up

01:39:14.060 --> 01:39:18.740
listening to, and the attitude
that we had back then listening

01:39:18.740 --> 01:39:23.090
to hardcore music,
being able to stand up

01:39:23.090 --> 01:39:26.090
against what other people were
saying which is what you're

01:39:26.090 --> 01:39:31.310
doing when you're in that scene,
and the whole DIY nature of it.

01:39:31.310 --> 01:39:33.560
Hey, we can just
do this ourselves.

01:39:33.560 --> 01:39:36.350
We can just make this
happen for ourselves.

01:39:36.350 --> 01:39:37.490
We don't need anybody else.

01:39:37.490 --> 01:39:38.840
We can do this.

01:39:38.840 --> 01:39:41.750
And that hardcore attitude
and sticking by your friends

01:39:41.750 --> 01:39:44.000
and standing up and
getting in fights

01:39:44.000 --> 01:39:47.570
and that's what you're going to
do, that's kind of my attitude.

01:39:47.570 --> 01:39:52.580
And I got interviewed
for a documentary

01:39:52.580 --> 01:39:56.000
that they're making about Harley
Flanagan and the Cro-mags,

01:39:56.000 --> 01:40:03.030
and when I was a kid and
actually through my whole life,

01:40:03.030 --> 01:40:05.440
that music is the
soundtrack to my life.

01:40:05.440 --> 01:40:08.400
And so I always would have
that music running in my head.

01:40:08.400 --> 01:40:12.390
But to your question,
I had something in me

01:40:12.390 --> 01:40:17.587
that when I heard that music for
the first time, I was like, OK,

01:40:17.587 --> 01:40:18.870
here it is.

01:40:18.870 --> 01:40:19.980
Here it is.

01:40:19.980 --> 01:40:23.130
I hear the Beatles, I
hear the Grateful Dead,

01:40:23.130 --> 01:40:25.740
I hear the Rolling
Stones, I hear whoever,

01:40:25.740 --> 01:40:27.690
and you go, OK, that's fine.

01:40:27.690 --> 01:40:30.740
But when I heard hardcore
music for the first time,

01:40:30.740 --> 01:40:33.240
when I heard the Cro-Mags, when
I heard Agnostic Front, when

01:40:33.240 --> 01:40:35.460
I heard the Bad
Brains, I thought

01:40:35.460 --> 01:40:39.630
that was just like it
was part of me already.

01:40:39.630 --> 01:40:42.540
And then it was the
attitude, and again you

01:40:42.540 --> 01:40:44.880
can listen to my podcast
with Harley Flanagan,

01:40:44.880 --> 01:40:47.160
the way I viewed Harley
Flanagan, the way I

01:40:47.160 --> 01:40:50.770
viewed the Cro-Mags was not
the way they actually were.

01:40:50.770 --> 01:40:55.863
I mean, Harley was doing
drugs, I mean, horrible drugs.

01:40:55.863 --> 01:40:57.280
He was on heroin,
everything else,

01:40:57.280 --> 01:41:03.165
but his image was like straight
edge, kind of spiritual,

01:41:03.165 --> 01:41:05.540
they had all that stuff going
on and I thought, OK, well,

01:41:05.540 --> 01:41:05.800
that's who they are.

01:41:05.800 --> 01:41:07.780
But I didn't know,
I'm like a kid living

01:41:07.780 --> 01:41:10.090
in the woods in
Connecticut, I'm just

01:41:10.090 --> 01:41:11.950
glad to be hearing
what I'm hearing.

01:41:11.950 --> 01:41:13.660
He listened to
the lyrics, listen

01:41:13.660 --> 01:41:16.000
to the lyrics of Minor
Threat, listen to the lyrics

01:41:16.000 --> 01:41:19.220
and you go, OK, this stuff,
I agree with this stuff.

01:41:19.220 --> 01:41:25.730
And I just think
that that kind of

01:41:25.730 --> 01:41:30.300
set a datum in my head of
being OK with being outside,

01:41:30.300 --> 01:41:33.800
being OK with saying no,
being OK with being a rebel,

01:41:33.800 --> 01:41:37.372
being OK with not
going along with what

01:41:37.372 --> 01:41:38.330
everyone else is doing.

01:41:38.330 --> 01:41:41.780
And that came that became
very important when

01:41:41.780 --> 01:41:44.600
I was in the
military, and I looked

01:41:44.600 --> 01:41:47.180
at what leadership might
be telling me to do

01:41:47.180 --> 01:41:48.680
and might think to
myself, hey, that

01:41:48.680 --> 01:41:50.420
doesn't seem like a
good idea, and having

01:41:50.420 --> 01:41:51.895
the wherewithal
to say, hey, boss,

01:41:51.895 --> 01:41:53.270
I'm not sure this
is a good idea.

01:41:53.270 --> 01:41:55.010
Not to be a jerk
about it, but just

01:41:55.010 --> 01:41:57.180
to say there might be a
better way to get this done.

01:41:57.180 --> 01:41:57.710
What do you think of this?

01:41:57.710 --> 01:41:59.752
Or, hey boss can I ask
you a question about that?

01:41:59.752 --> 01:42:04.640
So I think if I had to trace it
back having Black Flag My War

01:42:04.640 --> 01:42:08.300
side too on my record player
for like a year and a half

01:42:08.300 --> 01:42:11.090
straight, that's going
to leave a mark, man.

01:42:11.090 --> 01:42:13.950
And I think it
left a mark on me.

01:42:13.950 --> 01:42:17.370
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I love what you
just said, and when I say love,

01:42:17.370 --> 01:42:19.980
I really mean it
because as we both know

01:42:19.980 --> 01:42:22.490
we share a common love
for certain music,

01:42:22.490 --> 01:42:24.990
in particular Minor Threat and
some stuff from the punk rock

01:42:24.990 --> 01:42:25.200
scene.

01:42:25.200 --> 01:42:27.300
In particular for me, the
Northeast punk rock scene,

01:42:27.300 --> 01:42:29.580
the Bruisers, all borrowed from
the Bruisers, and now people

01:42:29.580 --> 01:42:31.290
know him as the lead singer
for the Dropkick Murphys.

01:42:31.290 --> 01:42:32.498
Before that was the Bruisers.

01:42:32.498 --> 01:42:34.230
And we run the
risk of going deep

01:42:34.230 --> 01:42:36.818
down a rabbit hole of music
that most people may or may not

01:42:36.818 --> 01:42:38.610
be familiar with,
although most people have

01:42:38.610 --> 01:42:41.260
heard the dropkicks, but yeah,
I'm right there with you.

01:42:41.260 --> 01:42:42.635
I mean, I remember
the first time

01:42:42.635 --> 01:42:44.430
hearing Stiff Little
Fingers or Rancid

01:42:44.430 --> 01:42:47.240
and even Bouncing
Souls for New Jersey--

01:42:47.240 --> 01:42:48.240
not even Bouncing Souls.

01:42:48.240 --> 01:42:48.740
Sorry.

01:42:48.740 --> 01:42:49.320
Sorry.

01:42:49.320 --> 01:42:50.430
Sorry, Brian and Greg.

01:42:50.430 --> 01:42:54.390
No, the Bouncing Souls
and going, yes, that's me.

01:42:54.390 --> 01:42:54.990
That's it.

01:42:54.990 --> 01:42:56.970
But as you said, it
was already in you.

01:42:56.970 --> 01:43:00.090
It's like a recognition,
and I bring this up,

01:43:00.090 --> 01:43:02.490
and I want to highlight
it, not because we

01:43:02.490 --> 01:43:05.402
share this, although I do find
that to be an amazing kinship

01:43:05.402 --> 01:43:07.860
that we felt right away, and
it was like we probably riffed

01:43:07.860 --> 01:43:10.230
for 25 hours just on that.

01:43:10.230 --> 01:43:12.660
But it brings me back
to this idea that--

01:43:12.660 --> 01:43:13.920
certainly is not my idea.

01:43:13.920 --> 01:43:15.600
Actually the first
time I heard about it

01:43:15.600 --> 01:43:18.120
was from Robert Greene
who wrote the book

01:43:18.120 --> 01:43:23.107
Mastery, and some important
writings, what was it?

01:43:23.107 --> 01:43:23.940
I think it was the--

01:43:23.940 --> 01:43:26.760
I forget how many laws of power,
but those books, Robert Green

01:43:26.760 --> 01:43:29.100
Mastery is actually a book
that I highly recommend.

01:43:29.100 --> 01:43:31.920
People read because it talks
about mentorship and finding

01:43:31.920 --> 01:43:33.930
mentors and the fact that we're
supposed to break up and move

01:43:33.930 --> 01:43:35.970
on from mentors, and that
mentors aren't always people

01:43:35.970 --> 01:43:37.637
that we know or that
know us, et cetera.

01:43:37.637 --> 01:43:39.360
Amazing book, really.

01:43:39.360 --> 01:43:41.070
But he was the
first person I ever

01:43:41.070 --> 01:43:44.012
heard describe this idea that
if we think back long enough,

01:43:44.012 --> 01:43:46.470
there's some seed moment, they
were shopping for the pants,

01:43:46.470 --> 01:43:49.530
it sounds like it was, but also
music where you see something

01:43:49.530 --> 01:43:53.280
and it's like, yes,
that's me and I'm that.

01:43:53.280 --> 01:43:55.290
And then that becomes
a sort of soundtrack

01:43:55.290 --> 01:43:58.200
or visual image or
something for your life

01:43:58.200 --> 01:44:00.760
that you carry forward with you.

01:44:00.760 --> 01:44:02.350
The neuroscientist
in me wants to say

01:44:02.350 --> 01:44:04.600
that that is the first
time that we really

01:44:04.600 --> 01:44:11.080
tap into this dopamine system
in a way that is unique to us,

01:44:11.080 --> 01:44:13.660
because every child
responds to food

01:44:13.660 --> 01:44:16.840
with a little bit of dopamine
when we're hungry, responds

01:44:16.840 --> 01:44:20.620
to warmth when we're cold,
responds to a warm dry diaper

01:44:20.620 --> 01:44:23.098
after we just wet
ourselves, which we all did.

01:44:23.098 --> 01:44:25.390
I don't know maybe, Jocko,
you change your own diapers.

01:44:25.390 --> 01:44:27.158
[LAUGHTER]

01:44:27.158 --> 01:44:29.200
But I'm guessing that
someone changed your diaper

01:44:29.200 --> 01:44:31.910
at one point, not an image
path we need to go down.

01:44:31.910 --> 01:44:34.630
But the idea is that we all
have these universal sources

01:44:34.630 --> 01:44:37.420
of having our needs met,
going from discomfort

01:44:37.420 --> 01:44:41.240
to comfort and back again,
which is basically childhood.

01:44:41.240 --> 01:44:43.370
But at some point,
something comes along

01:44:43.370 --> 01:44:45.200
that we really feel
is unique to us,

01:44:45.200 --> 01:44:47.242
and it may be the thing
that everyone else likes,

01:44:47.242 --> 01:44:48.842
maybe it's top 40
or whatever it is.

01:44:48.842 --> 01:44:50.300
Maybe it's the shoe
that everyone's

01:44:50.300 --> 01:44:52.020
wearing that seems good.

01:44:52.020 --> 01:44:54.740
But I do think that there
are certain people who

01:44:54.740 --> 01:44:58.670
are kind of 10 or 20 or maybe
even 180 degrees off from what

01:44:58.670 --> 01:45:01.340
everyone else likes and
they're like, that thing

01:45:01.340 --> 01:45:04.200
is what's really cool,
and it's a felt thing.

01:45:04.200 --> 01:45:09.160
And so along the lines of felt
things as opposed to things

01:45:09.160 --> 01:45:13.850
that everyone values, what are
your sources of motivation.

01:45:13.850 --> 01:45:18.880
And I'm going to guess that
some of them are internal.

01:45:18.880 --> 01:45:20.880
We could point ahead or
we could point to heart.

01:45:20.880 --> 01:45:23.430
Doesn't really
matter, but like when

01:45:23.430 --> 01:45:26.170
you think of sources
of motivation,

01:45:26.170 --> 01:45:29.230
do you have a palette of
them that you can dip into?

01:45:29.230 --> 01:45:31.510
Do you even feel the
need to dip into them,

01:45:31.510 --> 01:45:33.550
or is it really just
all about action steps

01:45:33.550 --> 01:45:34.600
throughout your day?

01:45:34.600 --> 01:45:39.813
Or if I can even venture
into somewhat harder stories

01:45:39.813 --> 01:45:41.230
that I've heard
you talk about, do

01:45:41.230 --> 01:45:42.732
you sometimes
think back, listen,

01:45:42.732 --> 01:45:45.190
I'm going to do this because
there are a bunch of guys that

01:45:45.190 --> 01:45:46.810
are dead now that
can't, and so I'm

01:45:46.810 --> 01:45:49.900
going to do it because I can.

01:45:49.900 --> 01:45:53.590
What are the paints on your
motivation pallet, if you will?

01:45:59.545 --> 01:46:00.920
JOCKO WILLINK:
Well, you probably

01:46:00.920 --> 01:46:03.950
heard me say that
motivation isn't something

01:46:03.950 --> 01:46:08.355
that I am going to count
on because it's just

01:46:08.355 --> 01:46:09.980
an emotion that's
going to come and go,

01:46:09.980 --> 01:46:14.852
and it's just like
feeling happy.

01:46:14.852 --> 01:46:16.310
You feel happy
right now, maybe you

01:46:16.310 --> 01:46:17.630
won't feel happy in 15 minutes.

01:46:17.630 --> 01:46:19.850
You feel sad now, maybe you
won't feel sad in 15 minutes.

01:46:19.850 --> 01:46:21.642
You feel motivated
right now, you might not

01:46:21.642 --> 01:46:22.890
feel motivated in 15 minutes.

01:46:22.890 --> 01:46:24.750
Therefore I can't rely on it.

01:46:24.750 --> 01:46:28.070
So I'm not going to put any--

01:46:28.070 --> 01:46:30.740
I'm not going to put
any money on just

01:46:30.740 --> 01:46:34.340
being motivated because it
doesn't really matter to me.

01:46:34.340 --> 01:46:40.040
So the daily actions that I
take aren't from motivation,

01:46:40.040 --> 01:46:41.570
they're just from discipline.

01:46:41.570 --> 01:46:43.170
Like I said earlier,
I'm not going

01:46:43.170 --> 01:46:46.410
to get up and go through
some big debate about,

01:46:46.410 --> 01:46:48.325
well, do I really
feel like doing this?

01:46:48.325 --> 01:46:50.450
No, I don't feel like doing
it, but doesn't matter,

01:46:50.450 --> 01:46:51.700
so I'm just going to go do it.

01:46:55.290 --> 01:47:03.760
Now if we start to look at a
broader movement through life

01:47:03.760 --> 01:47:11.200
and continuing to try and
move forward and move on,

01:47:11.200 --> 01:47:18.260
my buddy Seth died, and he was
the Delta Platoon commander

01:47:18.260 --> 01:47:20.330
in tasking a bruiser.

01:47:20.330 --> 01:47:26.620
And he died in 2017, and it
was in a parachute accident.

01:47:31.740 --> 01:47:34.500
I mean, it's
definitely unexpected,

01:47:34.500 --> 01:47:39.090
and also he'd already been
through multiple deployments,

01:47:39.090 --> 01:47:42.810
was with me in the
Battle of Ramadi,

01:47:42.810 --> 01:47:46.770
he then went back into
Sadr City and led a ton

01:47:46.770 --> 01:47:49.830
of very dangerous operations.

01:47:49.830 --> 01:47:53.880
And then he did
other deployments,

01:47:53.880 --> 01:47:57.660
and was kind of done with his
deployments, kind of done.

01:47:57.660 --> 01:48:01.590
And now he's just talking about
when he's going to retire,

01:48:01.590 --> 01:48:03.900
and he's a couple of years
away from retirement,

01:48:03.900 --> 01:48:05.730
and I'm talking
about, hey, we're

01:48:05.730 --> 01:48:07.720
going to work together again.

01:48:07.720 --> 01:48:11.610
And it all seems like
we're on a pretty good path

01:48:11.610 --> 01:48:13.530
to just move forward.

01:48:13.530 --> 01:48:17.040
And then he ends up dying
in a parachute accident.

01:48:17.040 --> 01:48:20.970
And he's a guy that was
really just kind of you're

01:48:20.970 --> 01:48:22.810
not going to be able to replace.

01:48:27.780 --> 01:48:31.410
There's a uniqueness to him that
is you're not going to find,

01:48:31.410 --> 01:48:34.470
and I've got some
stuff that he wrote.

01:48:34.470 --> 01:48:39.985
He was an incredible writer,
and I try and write something

01:48:39.985 --> 01:48:41.610
like him, and we
can't do it because he

01:48:41.610 --> 01:48:46.302
had a bigger vocabulary and a
more articulate way of writing.

01:48:46.302 --> 01:48:48.510
And so I can't write anything
as well as he wrote it.

01:48:48.510 --> 01:48:53.220
He was incredible at guitar, he
played guitar, played ukulele

01:48:53.220 --> 01:48:57.120
like sick, like an
incredible at playing guitar,

01:48:57.120 --> 01:48:58.920
and he's a total
knuckle dragger.

01:48:58.920 --> 01:49:02.640
Like a total meat
head knuckle dragger.

01:49:02.640 --> 01:49:05.310
His nickname was
Unfrozen Caveman

01:49:05.310 --> 01:49:10.200
because he just look
like a big caveman

01:49:10.200 --> 01:49:16.260
and yet he spoke French, and
could recite French poetry.

01:49:16.260 --> 01:49:18.780
And was really good
at learning languages,

01:49:18.780 --> 01:49:21.790
and he was an artist,
and he had-- do

01:49:21.790 --> 01:49:23.362
you know what synesthesia is?

01:49:23.362 --> 01:49:24.673
Do you know that is?

01:49:24.673 --> 01:49:26.340
ANDREW HUBERMAN: A
merging of the senses

01:49:26.340 --> 01:49:28.203
so people that can
see colors and--

01:49:28.203 --> 01:49:30.120
JOCKO WILLINK: So he
didn't know what it was--

01:49:30.120 --> 01:49:31.050
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I'm
sorry, see colors.

01:49:31.050 --> 01:49:32.800
I hope most people can
see colors-- sorry,

01:49:32.800 --> 01:49:37.170
that can hear colors and can
associate particular colors

01:49:37.170 --> 01:49:39.570
with sound like particular
keys on the piano.

01:49:39.570 --> 01:49:41.340
It's pretty rare.

01:49:41.340 --> 01:49:43.620
Some people think they have
it, but true synesthesia

01:49:43.620 --> 01:49:48.800
are pretty rare, but they don't
have to fight for this trait.

01:49:48.800 --> 01:49:50.390
It just kind of
emerges for them.

01:49:50.390 --> 01:49:51.450
JOCKO WILLINK: He
didn't know it.

01:49:51.450 --> 01:49:52.890
But one day he
was talking to me,

01:49:52.890 --> 01:49:55.190
and he was embarrassed
to tell me.

01:49:55.190 --> 01:50:00.320
He's like, what's weird is
when I think of numbers,

01:50:00.320 --> 01:50:03.740
I have colors in my head.

01:50:03.740 --> 01:50:05.340
And I go, what do you mean?

01:50:05.340 --> 01:50:08.542
And he says, hollow was zero,
and I remember seven is yellow.

01:50:08.542 --> 01:50:10.250
I don't remember any
of them, but he just

01:50:10.250 --> 01:50:13.220
rattled off like 123456789.

01:50:13.220 --> 01:50:19.143
He says, hollow, white, clear,
just rattled off these colors.

01:50:19.143 --> 01:50:20.810
He had that synesthesia,
and it gave him

01:50:20.810 --> 01:50:23.090
some kind of weird ability
to memorize numbers.

01:50:23.090 --> 01:50:26.648
So he'd be in a bar and talk
to some girl and he'd say,

01:50:26.648 --> 01:50:27.440
what's your number?

01:50:27.440 --> 01:50:29.565
And he would just-- he
would know it for two years.

01:50:29.565 --> 01:50:30.870
He would just know it.

01:50:30.870 --> 01:50:33.050
And that also made
him incredibly

01:50:33.050 --> 01:50:36.110
good at playing guitar
because now like the scale

01:50:36.110 --> 01:50:39.560
and the fretboard of a guitar,
it's a mathematical thing

01:50:39.560 --> 01:50:47.060
that he has all in this weird
coloration scenario going on.

01:50:47.060 --> 01:50:53.240
So he's this guy and
a very emotional guy.

01:50:53.240 --> 01:50:55.610
A very emotional guy who would--

01:50:55.610 --> 01:50:59.300
I was talking earlier about
being a balance for someone.

01:50:59.300 --> 01:51:02.810
I had to balance this dude out
on a daily basis sometimes.

01:51:02.810 --> 01:51:05.270
He'd be so mad about something.

01:51:05.270 --> 01:51:07.460
One day he'd be,
I hate the Teams.

01:51:07.460 --> 01:51:08.780
I hate all these guys.

01:51:08.780 --> 01:51:10.382
And I'd say, yeah,
I get it, man.

01:51:10.382 --> 01:51:12.590
And the next day, I'm never
getting out of the Teams.

01:51:12.590 --> 01:51:16.400
He would oscillate that
bad, and I would tell him,

01:51:16.400 --> 01:51:18.410
hey, bro, you're
oscillating again,

01:51:18.410 --> 01:51:20.360
and just would do anything.

01:51:20.360 --> 01:51:27.390
And he loved these guys and
would do anything for his guys.

01:51:27.390 --> 01:51:37.810
And so when he died, we're at
his-- it's not his funeral,

01:51:37.810 --> 01:51:38.860
it's before the funeral.

01:51:38.860 --> 01:51:42.370
It's like the open casket
thing, the wake thing,

01:51:42.370 --> 01:51:51.580
and myself, his brother,
Alex, Leif Babin who

01:51:51.580 --> 01:51:53.830
I wrote Extreme Ownership
with and JP O'Donnell, who's

01:51:53.830 --> 01:51:57.400
one of my brothers who works
with us at Arsenal on Front

01:51:57.400 --> 01:52:00.100
now is with us in Ramadi
and was very close to Seth,

01:52:00.100 --> 01:52:04.750
and everyone kind of
cleared out for us.

01:52:04.750 --> 01:52:13.040
And we go in there, and
I think JP gave him--

01:52:13.040 --> 01:52:17.570
JP had one of those Memorial
bracelets with guys' names

01:52:17.570 --> 01:52:20.700
on it that had died and
JP gave that to him.

01:52:20.700 --> 01:52:24.170
And I think Leif gave him some
surf wax because also Seth

01:52:24.170 --> 01:52:29.570
was a surfer, and I gave Seth
his black belt because he

01:52:29.570 --> 01:52:33.020
started training jiu-jitsu with
me and he had his purple belt.

01:52:33.020 --> 01:52:36.980
He had gotten his purple belt,
I gave him his black belt

01:52:36.980 --> 01:52:39.400
and everyone was just quiet.

01:52:39.400 --> 01:52:46.570
And JP was telling the
story the other day

01:52:46.570 --> 01:52:57.110
and I just said, we
will not fail him,

01:52:57.110 --> 01:53:07.400
meaning that him Mark,
Mikey, Chris, Seth,

01:53:07.400 --> 01:53:14.080
and countless other
guys they're not here.

01:53:14.080 --> 01:53:16.950
They don't have the opportunity
to do the things we do.

01:53:16.950 --> 01:53:20.260
They don't have the opportunity
to get up in the morning.

01:53:20.260 --> 01:53:28.720
So that's what it is, man.

01:53:28.720 --> 01:53:31.042
I won't fail those guys.

01:53:31.042 --> 01:53:32.750
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Thanks
for sharing that.

01:53:35.890 --> 01:53:38.680
Yeah, thank God.

01:53:38.680 --> 01:53:41.350
Anyone listening
to this feels what

01:53:41.350 --> 01:53:48.880
I feel right now, which is very
clear that the depth of emotion

01:53:48.880 --> 01:53:50.860
for people that we
care about and lose

01:53:50.860 --> 01:53:54.290
has everything to do
with our love for them.

01:53:54.290 --> 01:53:55.870
There's just no
question about it.

01:53:55.870 --> 01:53:59.950
The grief and love are
so intimately tied.

01:53:59.950 --> 01:54:04.690
They are a direct
reflection of one another.

01:54:04.690 --> 01:54:09.540
And I hesitate to kind
of stay on hard themes,

01:54:09.540 --> 01:54:13.170
but I think it's really
important for a couple

01:54:13.170 --> 01:54:14.850
of reasons.

01:54:14.850 --> 01:54:19.920
Last time we spoke, it was in
your office and your podcast,

01:54:19.920 --> 01:54:21.905
and after we wrapped
up, we started

01:54:21.905 --> 01:54:23.280
talking about some
people we know

01:54:23.280 --> 01:54:25.447
and some things that had
happened in your community,

01:54:25.447 --> 01:54:27.810
and kind of spooled
into some discussions

01:54:27.810 --> 01:54:30.000
about things that happened
in nearby communities

01:54:30.000 --> 01:54:33.110
and civilian culture.

01:54:33.110 --> 01:54:36.780
And one of the things
that's so perplexing

01:54:36.780 --> 01:54:44.040
I think to people, including
me but maybe with time less so,

01:54:44.040 --> 01:54:48.980
and this actually came up last
night in a discussion at home

01:54:48.980 --> 01:54:52.610
because of some recent events,
not close to me, but is

01:54:52.610 --> 01:54:57.380
that some people go through
things where there's loss,

01:54:57.380 --> 01:54:59.190
they go through life
and there's hardship.

01:54:59.190 --> 01:55:01.460
I think most people experience
some hardship certainly

01:55:01.460 --> 01:55:03.270
some more than others.

01:55:03.270 --> 01:55:09.410
And every once in a while
and far too often, and even

01:55:09.410 --> 01:55:13.850
in the SEAL Team community, and
even in the various communities

01:55:13.850 --> 01:55:16.340
within the SEAL Team community
where one would never

01:55:16.340 --> 01:55:23.220
expect it, these are highly
trained tight community,

01:55:23.220 --> 01:55:27.600
hard guys, that's the
language we sometimes hear,

01:55:27.600 --> 01:55:30.060
but as you point out,
these are people often

01:55:30.060 --> 01:55:32.080
that have tremendous
emotional depth.

01:55:32.080 --> 01:55:34.650
And I'm so glad that you
brought that up because I think

01:55:34.650 --> 01:55:42.320
we sometimes think of emotion as
weakness, but there was a time,

01:55:42.320 --> 01:55:47.543
not long ago, 40, 50 years ago,
where emotions like jealousy

01:55:47.543 --> 01:55:48.960
and intensity,
think the character

01:55:48.960 --> 01:55:52.230
Sonny and the Godfather, he
would get so pissed, he would

01:55:52.230 --> 01:55:57.610
bite his own fist, that's
a pretty emotional response

01:55:57.610 --> 01:56:03.430
where intensity and emotion were
kind of interchangeable words

01:56:03.430 --> 01:56:06.170
at some level.

01:56:06.170 --> 01:56:07.282
I'm going to be direct.

01:56:07.282 --> 01:56:09.740
There have been a lot of guys
coming out of the SEAL Teams.

01:56:09.740 --> 01:56:12.860
I've been surprised to
hear they kill themselves,

01:56:12.860 --> 01:56:15.230
and yesterday there
was a major suicide.

01:56:15.230 --> 01:56:16.610
I didn't know this
guy in person,

01:56:16.610 --> 01:56:18.610
we actually followed each
other on social media,

01:56:18.610 --> 01:56:21.780
but I admittedly was not close
to him, didn't know him at all.

01:56:21.780 --> 01:56:24.980
In fact, his name was Twitch or
something, and he was this guy,

01:56:24.980 --> 01:56:26.750
he was a public facing figure.

01:56:26.750 --> 01:56:30.380
But listen, happens all
too often, and even happens

01:56:30.380 --> 01:56:32.470
in former operators.

01:56:32.470 --> 01:56:36.370
And suicide is something that
fascinates me and intrigues me

01:56:36.370 --> 01:56:39.830
and scares the hell out of me
because for the life of me,

01:56:39.830 --> 01:56:44.470
I cannot map it to any specific
thing in the brain or body that

01:56:44.470 --> 01:56:50.070
we're aware of, and yet I've had
several friends commit suicide,

01:56:50.070 --> 01:56:52.680
had my undergrad advisor
committed suicide.

01:56:52.680 --> 01:56:54.970
The point is not
them or their story.

01:56:54.970 --> 01:57:00.350
The point is what in the
world dictates whether or not

01:57:00.350 --> 01:57:07.330
somebody who has a community,
who's doing well and then less

01:57:07.330 --> 01:57:12.720
well decides to offer themselves
to end their own life,

01:57:12.720 --> 01:57:15.872
versus decide I'm
going to keep going?

01:57:15.872 --> 01:57:17.830
I mean, this is I'm
raising this as a question.

01:57:17.830 --> 01:57:20.220
I wish I had an answer I used to
have ideas like, Oh, it's time

01:57:20.220 --> 01:57:20.730
perception.

01:57:20.730 --> 01:57:22.920
These people are so miserable,
feeling so miserable

01:57:22.920 --> 01:57:24.390
that they feel it's
going to go on forever.

01:57:24.390 --> 01:57:26.598
But then you start reading
the literature on suicide,

01:57:26.598 --> 01:57:28.080
and I've started
to go into this.

01:57:28.080 --> 01:57:29.250
For those of you
that can stomach it,

01:57:29.250 --> 01:57:30.930
and I don't know that I
want to recommend this movie

01:57:30.930 --> 01:57:33.330
but I'm just going to say it
exists, the movie The Bridge

01:57:33.330 --> 01:57:35.955
where they fix the camera on the
Golden Gate Bridge for a year,

01:57:35.955 --> 01:57:39.420
it turns out one person a day
on average tries to jump off.

01:57:39.420 --> 01:57:41.130
They talk to a guy
who survived it.

01:57:41.130 --> 01:57:43.560
By the way he jumped off the
moment he went off there.

01:57:43.560 --> 01:57:45.810
He thought I wish
I hadn't done that.

01:57:45.810 --> 01:57:46.680
He survived.

01:57:46.680 --> 01:57:47.730
This kid with bipolar.

01:57:47.730 --> 01:57:50.430
Bipolar disorder, especially
males, 20 to 30 times

01:57:50.430 --> 01:57:53.040
higher incidents of suicide.

01:57:53.040 --> 01:57:56.790
Massive increase.

01:57:56.790 --> 01:57:59.820
The point is that
there's something

01:57:59.820 --> 01:58:03.540
that happens in the brain
where somehow people also

01:58:03.540 --> 01:58:06.840
will get the idea, and you hear
this, that this is something

01:58:06.840 --> 01:58:09.210
they're excited to
do, or that they're

01:58:09.210 --> 01:58:11.850
going to write the world
somehow by doing this,

01:58:11.850 --> 01:58:14.823
or that somehow it's like
a gift to themselves.

01:58:14.823 --> 01:58:16.740
Again, I'm not encouraging
anybody to do this.

01:58:16.740 --> 01:58:18.060
I want to be very clear,
but these are the things

01:58:18.060 --> 01:58:18.960
that you hear.

01:58:18.960 --> 01:58:22.620
And sadly, your community
has lost a number of people

01:58:22.620 --> 01:58:28.970
through suicide, and yet there
are a lot of guys that thrive.

01:58:28.970 --> 01:58:30.580
And so more as a
template for trying

01:58:30.580 --> 01:58:34.810
to understand mental health and
depression and suicide, what

01:58:34.810 --> 01:58:40.680
are your thoughts on why
some people seem to thrive,

01:58:40.680 --> 01:58:43.950
and some people just
go all the way down?

01:58:43.950 --> 01:58:47.280
JOCKO WILLINK: Clearly, that's
a very complex question,

01:58:47.280 --> 01:58:50.620
and there's probably a
lot of different answers.

01:58:50.620 --> 01:58:56.120
And I certainly am not one to
be able to answer that question.

01:58:56.120 --> 01:59:03.030
However, probably
where my thought

01:59:03.030 --> 01:59:05.723
has gone on this
lately because I've

01:59:05.723 --> 01:59:07.890
known some guys that have
killed themselves and I've

01:59:07.890 --> 01:59:14.020
been totally shocked, and just
been completely and totally

01:59:14.020 --> 01:59:17.800
shocked that guys that I
knew killed themselves.

01:59:17.800 --> 01:59:20.170
Guys that you would
think, Oh, this guy

01:59:20.170 --> 01:59:22.940
would never do this
in a million years.

01:59:22.940 --> 01:59:25.600
And that's the feeling I've
had about a lot of the guys

01:59:25.600 --> 01:59:29.510
that I know that have
killed themselves.

01:59:29.510 --> 01:59:32.960
I had a woman on my podcast
named Sarah Wilkinson,

01:59:32.960 --> 01:59:37.610
and she's an awesome woman,
and she was married to a SEAL.

01:59:37.610 --> 01:59:38.690
His name was Chad.

01:59:38.690 --> 01:59:39.620
I didn't know him.

01:59:42.465 --> 01:59:43.215
He killed himself.

01:59:46.290 --> 01:59:51.440
And to hear her
describe the story,

01:59:51.440 --> 01:59:53.690
the shocking thing
about the story

01:59:53.690 --> 02:00:01.400
is that the guy that she
knew, the guy that she married

02:00:01.400 --> 02:00:07.110
was not the same guy
that killed himself.

02:00:07.110 --> 02:00:15.840
Something happened to him that
made him a different person.

02:00:15.840 --> 02:00:20.400
And look, they're getting
all this information

02:00:20.400 --> 02:00:26.910
now about CTE and the brain
trauma that you go through,

02:00:26.910 --> 02:00:30.410
and people are exposed to that.

02:00:30.410 --> 02:00:34.730
And I think if you've ever
seen George Foreman, he

02:00:34.730 --> 02:00:37.670
seems totally normal and
good to go, and Muhammad Ali,

02:00:37.670 --> 02:00:38.450
not so much.

02:00:38.450 --> 02:00:41.150
And I've known
fighters, and you can

02:00:41.150 --> 02:00:44.180
look at any number of boxers
that have had a career,

02:00:44.180 --> 02:00:47.000
and some of them are
fine, and some of them

02:00:47.000 --> 02:00:49.370
have some real significant--

02:00:49.370 --> 02:00:49.870
what is it?

02:00:49.870 --> 02:00:52.060
Pugilistic.

02:00:52.060 --> 02:00:54.010
There's a pugilistic syndrome.

02:00:54.010 --> 02:00:57.610
They've been punched too
much and they have problems.

02:00:57.610 --> 02:01:02.740
You can expose different
people to blast impact,

02:01:02.740 --> 02:01:05.000
and it's going to have a
different impact over time.

02:01:05.000 --> 02:01:11.400
And I think that, again, to
hear Sarah explain that story

02:01:11.400 --> 02:01:14.100
and what she saw
from her husband

02:01:14.100 --> 02:01:16.350
and how different
he was when they

02:01:16.350 --> 02:01:19.950
got married compared
to where they ended up,

02:01:19.950 --> 02:01:21.150
it's totally different.

02:01:21.150 --> 02:01:23.750
A totally different person.

02:01:23.750 --> 02:01:28.190
I had another friend of mine
on who named Marcus Capone,

02:01:28.190 --> 02:01:32.720
and his wife came on with him,
and she said the same thing.

02:01:32.720 --> 02:01:37.390
The guy that she married
was not the same guy

02:01:37.390 --> 02:01:41.830
that was ready to kill himself,
and he didn't, thankfully.

02:01:41.830 --> 02:01:44.080
It was a different person.

02:01:44.080 --> 02:01:49.240
Look, if Fred is
married to some woman,

02:01:49.240 --> 02:01:52.990
and Fred and Jessica
grow apart over time

02:01:52.990 --> 02:01:56.620
and he ends up with some other
girl, it's still the same guy.

02:01:56.620 --> 02:01:59.950
it's still the same guy that
is now, look, they grew apart,

02:01:59.950 --> 02:02:01.760
they're getting
divorced, we get it.

02:02:01.760 --> 02:02:06.190
But the way that both of
them described their husbands

02:02:06.190 --> 02:02:09.840
as being different people.

02:02:09.840 --> 02:02:13.501
That's what stuck with me
more than anything else.

02:02:13.501 --> 02:02:19.300
So I-- again, I'm
throwing this out there

02:02:19.300 --> 02:02:21.940
only because it's what I've
observed through the people

02:02:21.940 --> 02:02:25.970
that I know, and
seeing and hearing

02:02:25.970 --> 02:02:31.220
those stories of people
being totally different.

02:02:31.220 --> 02:02:33.380
And I've known a few people--

02:02:33.380 --> 02:02:35.360
one of my friends,
Dave, killed himself.

02:02:35.360 --> 02:02:37.235
I never would have
guessed in a million years

02:02:37.235 --> 02:02:38.940
that Dave would kill himself.

02:02:38.940 --> 02:02:40.970
It just it doesn't compute.

02:02:40.970 --> 02:02:42.650
It doesn't compute.

02:02:42.650 --> 02:02:47.060
And so my suspicion is there
has to be something going

02:02:47.060 --> 02:02:53.450
on mechanically or chemically
with the brain that causes them

02:02:53.450 --> 02:02:57.920
to get into a mode
where they're depressed

02:02:57.920 --> 02:03:00.550
and they don't see a
way out, and that's

02:03:00.550 --> 02:03:01.540
the way they feel now.

02:03:01.540 --> 02:03:03.730
And again, what's
interesting about this is

02:03:03.730 --> 02:03:07.930
we already talked about the
fact that the selection process

02:03:07.930 --> 02:03:10.600
weeds out guys that are
going to take a loss

02:03:10.600 --> 02:03:12.220
and not be able to get up again.

02:03:12.220 --> 02:03:13.330
No, you've got--

02:03:13.330 --> 02:03:15.305
SEALS can take a loss
and get back up again.

02:03:15.305 --> 02:03:16.930
That's what you learn
how to do in bed.

02:03:16.930 --> 02:03:19.097
Well, that's what you-- you
don't lean how to do it,

02:03:19.097 --> 02:03:19.850
you have it.

02:03:19.850 --> 02:03:21.558
And if you make it
through that training,

02:03:21.558 --> 02:03:24.070
you have that ability to
take a loss, all right, cool.

02:03:24.070 --> 02:03:25.780
Shake it off, get
back up, keep going.

02:03:25.780 --> 02:03:27.900
They're going to do that to you.

02:03:27.900 --> 02:03:30.360
So now you've got guys
that are taking a loss

02:03:30.360 --> 02:03:31.960
and they don't see
a way out anymore,

02:03:31.960 --> 02:03:33.502
in fact, to such a
point that they're

02:03:33.502 --> 02:03:38.235
going to take their
own lives, it's

02:03:38.235 --> 02:03:40.610
my suspicion is at some point
they're going to figure out

02:03:40.610 --> 02:03:47.767
that this exposure to the
adrenaline, the explosions

02:03:47.767 --> 02:03:48.350
all the time--

02:03:48.350 --> 02:03:50.340
I mean, you go out
to a range-- just

02:03:50.340 --> 02:03:52.250
in peacetime-- you
go out to a range,

02:03:52.250 --> 02:03:54.873
and you shoot a Carl Gustav.

02:03:54.873 --> 02:03:56.290
And you're a range
safety officer,

02:03:56.290 --> 02:03:57.998
so you go out there
with every guy that's

02:03:57.998 --> 02:04:00.370
shooting a Carl Gustav, it's
a big, giant, like bazooka

02:04:00.370 --> 02:04:01.300
looking weapon.

02:04:01.300 --> 02:04:04.120
And every time you shoot it, it
rattles your cage a little bit.

02:04:04.120 --> 02:04:07.030
Well, if you're a range safety
officer, and you're out there,

02:04:07.030 --> 02:04:09.130
and you're going to watch
everyone in the platoon

02:04:09.130 --> 02:04:12.850
shoot three of those, it's
going to have an impact.

02:04:12.850 --> 02:04:15.730
Then you go overseas,
and you're a breacher,

02:04:15.730 --> 02:04:17.290
or you're part of
a breach team--

02:04:17.290 --> 02:04:21.320
I mean, I had a time
where one of my guys

02:04:21.320 --> 02:04:23.500
were doing an assault
on a compound.

02:04:23.500 --> 02:04:24.940
I'm on the ladder--

02:04:24.940 --> 02:04:27.050
most buildings in Iraq
have a wall around them.

02:04:27.050 --> 02:04:29.830
So the way we would conduct
these raids, put the ladder up,

02:04:29.830 --> 02:04:30.730
the assault--

02:04:30.730 --> 02:04:32.650
breach team would
climb over the ladder,

02:04:32.650 --> 02:04:34.597
and I would go right
behind the breach team,

02:04:34.597 --> 02:04:35.930
and I would stand on the ladder.

02:04:35.930 --> 02:04:38.445
So I'm looking at the
building, observing,

02:04:38.445 --> 02:04:39.820
making sure there's
no waking up,

02:04:39.820 --> 02:04:41.570
make sure there's no threats.

02:04:41.570 --> 02:04:44.140
And then as I'm watching,
this guy in particular,

02:04:44.140 --> 02:04:46.293
he puts the breach on the
door, explosive breach,

02:04:46.293 --> 02:04:47.710
so he's going to
blow the door up.

02:04:47.710 --> 02:04:49.360
He's got his little
team with him.

02:04:49.360 --> 02:04:51.202
He puts the breach on the door.

02:04:51.202 --> 02:04:53.660
He starts to back away, and
there's like an obstacle there.

02:04:53.660 --> 02:04:56.200
It was like a freaking table,
or a lawn chair, or something.

02:04:56.200 --> 02:04:57.763
And he couldn't get around it.

02:04:57.763 --> 02:04:58.680
And I'm sitting there.

02:04:58.680 --> 02:04:59.160
It's dark.

02:04:59.160 --> 02:05:00.090
I mean, it's the
middle of the night.

02:05:00.090 --> 02:05:01.020
It's 2:00 o'clock
in the morning.

02:05:01.020 --> 02:05:02.895
And I'm like, I wonder
what he's going to do.

02:05:02.895 --> 02:05:04.865
And he seem stuck for a second.

02:05:04.865 --> 02:05:06.240
And then I just
see him lay down.

02:05:06.240 --> 02:05:08.407
And I'm like oh, he's just
going to take this thing.

02:05:08.407 --> 02:05:11.070
And sure enough, three
or four feet away

02:05:11.070 --> 02:05:14.880
from this breech point, he
lays down and just clacks off

02:05:14.880 --> 02:05:16.920
this explosive charge.

02:05:16.920 --> 02:05:18.460
And I jump over the wall.

02:05:18.460 --> 02:05:21.420
And as I'm going in, I'm trying
to get the rest of the platoon

02:05:21.420 --> 02:05:24.790
to go and commence this
assault. I look at him,

02:05:24.790 --> 02:05:26.730
he looked like he just
got hit in the head

02:05:26.730 --> 02:05:28.700
with a baseball bat.

02:05:28.700 --> 02:05:30.290
And guess what he
did the next night?

02:05:30.290 --> 02:05:31.850
Another breach on
another target.

02:05:31.850 --> 02:05:33.225
Guess what he did
the next night?

02:05:33.225 --> 02:05:34.760
Another breach on
another target.

02:05:34.760 --> 02:05:39.800
So you get guys that have
that exposure, which is--

02:05:39.800 --> 02:05:41.600
I mean, every seal
is eating a breach.

02:05:41.600 --> 02:05:43.850
I mean, you're
eating flashbangs,

02:05:43.850 --> 02:05:46.260
you're eating breaches,
you're shooting 50 cals.

02:05:46.260 --> 02:05:50.100
You're eating some
traumatic brain injury.

02:05:50.100 --> 02:05:52.580
But then you must
have some people

02:05:52.580 --> 02:05:56.480
that have some
genetic propensity

02:05:56.480 --> 02:05:58.820
to have this negative
thing happen.

02:05:58.820 --> 02:06:01.850
And I can only
guess, man, that this

02:06:01.850 --> 02:06:06.620
has something to do with
it because otherwise, we

02:06:06.620 --> 02:06:08.570
wouldn't be hearing so
many of these stories.

02:06:08.570 --> 02:06:10.862
And of course, we're just
talking about the SEAL Teams.

02:06:10.862 --> 02:06:13.640
We're not talking about the
military writ large, which

02:06:13.640 --> 02:06:16.520
is in the same exact boat.

02:06:16.520 --> 02:06:22.420
I'm also nervous about the
social contagion of suicide

02:06:22.420 --> 02:06:24.430
within the veteran community.

02:06:24.430 --> 02:06:26.505
I'm nervous about that.

02:06:26.505 --> 02:06:27.880
It's one of the
things that makes

02:06:27.880 --> 02:06:29.380
me apprehensive about
talking about it,

02:06:29.380 --> 02:06:31.010
but I obviously I've
talked about it.

02:06:31.010 --> 02:06:34.450
I've had people on my podcast
to discuss these things,

02:06:34.450 --> 02:06:40.420
but I am worried about that
social contagion of man,

02:06:40.420 --> 02:06:41.560
Fred did it.

02:06:41.560 --> 02:06:43.570
He got all this attention,
and he doesn't have

02:06:43.570 --> 02:06:45.640
to deal with anything anymore.

02:06:45.640 --> 02:06:46.900
I'm going to do it too.

02:06:46.900 --> 02:06:51.640
There's got to be some level
of that going on as well.

02:06:51.640 --> 02:06:53.970
So it's a horrible situation.

02:06:53.970 --> 02:06:55.970
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah,
the social contagion part

02:06:55.970 --> 02:06:58.012
is one that hits home from
a different dimension.

02:06:58.012 --> 02:06:59.720
The high school I
went to which is--

02:06:59.720 --> 02:07:00.680
name isn't important.

02:07:00.680 --> 02:07:04.910
But Gunn High School, G-U-N-N.
Gunn High School is famous

02:07:04.910 --> 02:07:06.910
for being one of the
better public high schools.

02:07:06.910 --> 02:07:08.890
Not that I attended as
much as I should have.

02:07:08.890 --> 02:07:10.630
I can tell you more about
the curbs in the parking lot

02:07:10.630 --> 02:07:12.588
than I could tell you
about the classes I took.

02:07:12.588 --> 02:07:14.171
And that's just not
encouraging people

02:07:14.171 --> 02:07:15.255
to not attend high school.

02:07:15.255 --> 02:07:15.850
Please do it.

02:07:15.850 --> 02:07:18.295
Took a lot of really hard
work to climb back up

02:07:18.295 --> 02:07:20.170
to where I should have
been when I graduated.

02:07:20.170 --> 02:07:23.950
But it has an
infamous reputation,

02:07:23.950 --> 02:07:25.840
or I'll say reputation
as an infamous school

02:07:25.840 --> 02:07:28.000
because it's one of the
highest suicide rates than any

02:07:28.000 --> 02:07:29.380
of the schools in the
country because there's

02:07:29.380 --> 02:07:30.838
train tracks that
run through town,

02:07:30.838 --> 02:07:33.190
and there was a contagion
of kids throwing themselves

02:07:33.190 --> 02:07:34.090
on the train tracks.

02:07:34.090 --> 02:07:36.280
This was happening a lot.

02:07:36.280 --> 02:07:38.560
This was written up in
various newspapers, et cetera.

02:07:38.560 --> 02:07:40.240
Fortunately, it seems
to have died down.

02:07:40.240 --> 02:07:42.862
Again, I'm also hesitant
to talk about this

02:07:42.862 --> 02:07:44.320
because no one
wants to spark this.

02:07:44.320 --> 02:07:48.160
But there does seem to be
something about lack of ability

02:07:48.160 --> 02:07:50.170
to see into a future obviously.

02:07:50.170 --> 02:07:51.970
Or the future that
people are seeing into

02:07:51.970 --> 02:07:54.910
is so dark that somehow they
lose touch with the idea

02:07:54.910 --> 02:07:56.200
that emotions come and go.

02:07:56.200 --> 02:07:59.560
You said it about motivation,
emotions come and go.

02:07:59.560 --> 02:08:01.540
But somehow people
lose touch with that.

02:08:01.540 --> 02:08:03.910
And then I will venture a guess.

02:08:03.910 --> 02:08:06.160
And here I'm hoping someone's
going to figure this out

02:08:06.160 --> 02:08:08.070
at some point so
we can have a more

02:08:08.070 --> 02:08:09.820
concrete conversation
about the mechanisms

02:08:09.820 --> 02:08:10.820
and what to do about it.

02:08:10.820 --> 02:08:13.640
But I think there's also
something about identity,

02:08:13.640 --> 02:08:18.460
about loss of a place
to put one's energy.

02:08:18.460 --> 02:08:19.780
Something useful in the world.

02:08:19.780 --> 02:08:21.880
If you're agenda-- what
you're talking about here

02:08:21.880 --> 02:08:24.310
are guys that are generators.

02:08:24.310 --> 02:08:26.590
They are not projectors,
they're generators.

02:08:26.590 --> 02:08:31.420
They live to have effective
action on the world for good

02:08:31.420 --> 02:08:33.760
and then end up
killing themselves.

02:08:33.760 --> 02:08:37.150
In youth, it's a little more
complicated to put a finger

02:08:37.150 --> 02:08:39.610
on because what's
going on there.

02:08:39.610 --> 02:08:41.020
We assume depression.

02:08:41.020 --> 02:08:43.390
But then in learning
more about suicide,

02:08:43.390 --> 02:08:46.360
there is also this
excitement for certain people

02:08:46.360 --> 02:08:47.890
about solving
something that seemed

02:08:47.890 --> 02:08:49.370
unsolvable any other way.

02:08:49.370 --> 02:08:51.370
And again, I'm certainly
not encouraging this.

02:08:51.370 --> 02:08:54.220
I-- strongly discouraging people
from taking their own life,

02:08:54.220 --> 02:08:55.480
obviously.

02:08:55.480 --> 02:08:59.873
But something about time and the
loss of perception about time.

02:08:59.873 --> 02:09:01.540
And one thing that
we know for sure here

02:09:01.540 --> 02:09:03.280
we can really hang
our hat on something

02:09:03.280 --> 02:09:09.340
is that if you do the forensics
on somebody that was suicidal,

02:09:09.340 --> 02:09:12.860
attempted, or took their
life, in the preceding

02:09:12.860 --> 02:09:17.930
days and weeks, their sleep-wake
schedule was completely wacked.

02:09:17.930 --> 02:09:21.620
They exit the normal
routine of most people.

02:09:21.620 --> 02:09:25.420
They isolate through
inversions of time.

02:09:25.420 --> 02:09:27.155
And I do wonder
sometimes whether or not

02:09:27.155 --> 02:09:28.780
the vampire ships,
as they're sometimes

02:09:28.780 --> 02:09:30.100
called, the
nighttime deployment,

02:09:30.100 --> 02:09:31.683
and the back and
forth, I mean, that's

02:09:31.683 --> 02:09:33.520
a lot for a system to take.

02:09:33.520 --> 02:09:37.090
Shift workers kill themselves
far more than nonshift workers.

02:09:37.090 --> 02:09:39.110
So I do think there's
something there.

02:09:39.110 --> 02:09:40.777
And again, I'm not
saying everyone needs

02:09:40.777 --> 02:09:42.897
to be in bed by
9:00 and up by 4:00.

02:09:42.897 --> 02:09:45.230
Although that would be a great
schedule for most people.

02:09:45.230 --> 02:09:48.370
But I do think that there are
some universal laws of what

02:09:48.370 --> 02:09:51.580
makes the human body
and brain healthy.

02:09:51.580 --> 02:09:54.520
And if you violate
those laws long enough

02:09:54.520 --> 02:09:57.550
with CTE, or with disruption
in your schedules,

02:09:57.550 --> 02:10:00.490
you run the risk, especially if
there's a predisposition there.

02:10:00.490 --> 02:10:02.710
And then other factors
start to layer in.

02:10:02.710 --> 02:10:04.900
Again-- and I have to
apologize because I

02:10:04.900 --> 02:10:08.380
don't have any real answers,
or more biology, or psychology

02:10:08.380 --> 02:10:10.655
to firmly throw out
this except the warning

02:10:10.655 --> 02:10:13.030
for people who are bipolar or
know somebody with bipolar.

02:10:13.030 --> 02:10:15.820
They are 20 to 30 times more
likely to kill themselves.

02:10:15.820 --> 02:10:17.770
And males in particular
are more likely to use

02:10:17.770 --> 02:10:20.103
methods that will kill them
in the first time as opposed

02:10:20.103 --> 02:10:20.870
to survive.

02:10:20.870 --> 02:10:23.050
There's a big sex
difference there.

02:10:23.050 --> 02:10:24.280
JOCKO WILLINK: Yeah.

02:10:24.280 --> 02:10:28.320
You throw alcohol in there too.

02:10:28.320 --> 02:10:30.520
And Marcus is running
an organization now

02:10:30.520 --> 02:10:35.405
where they're taking vets
down to do the psychedelic--

02:10:35.405 --> 02:10:36.280
what do they call it?

02:10:36.280 --> 02:10:36.880
A journey.

02:10:36.880 --> 02:10:38.440
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah,
this is Veterans Solutions?

02:10:38.440 --> 02:10:39.280
JOCKO WILLINK: Yeah.

02:10:39.280 --> 02:10:40.840
ANDREW HUBERMAN: They
are a great organization.

02:10:40.840 --> 02:10:41.140
JOCKO WILLINK: Yeah.

02:10:41.140 --> 02:10:42.010
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And I
should mention a very

02:10:42.010 --> 02:10:43.450
bipartisan organization.

02:10:43.450 --> 02:10:45.520
I attended one
event on Coronado.

02:10:45.520 --> 02:10:47.187
And there were people
from the far-left,

02:10:47.187 --> 02:10:48.895
the far-right politicians,
and everything

02:10:48.895 --> 02:10:50.830
in between talking about
how critical this is.

02:10:50.830 --> 02:10:52.330
So this is not a
political issue.

02:10:52.330 --> 02:10:53.990
This is a mental health issue.

02:10:53.990 --> 02:10:56.680
JOCKO WILLINK: Yeah, and even
when I reflected on Marcus

02:10:56.680 --> 02:10:59.440
talking to me, he
was talking about how

02:10:59.440 --> 02:11:01.258
he's in this downward spiral.

02:11:01.258 --> 02:11:03.550
Part of the downward spiral,
he was drinking every day.

02:11:03.550 --> 02:11:07.580
And I'm thinking to
myself, man, like--

02:11:07.580 --> 02:11:09.170
I didn't think of
it during the show.

02:11:09.170 --> 02:11:11.330
I was thinking
about it afterwards.

02:11:11.330 --> 02:11:14.880
I was thinking, man, if
you're drinking all the time,

02:11:14.880 --> 02:11:17.660
you're on a downward spiral.

02:11:17.660 --> 02:11:21.590
That's one thing you
should just stop.

02:11:21.590 --> 02:11:23.780
Let's stop that immediately.

02:11:23.780 --> 02:11:26.020
So I just wanted to
throw that out there.

02:11:26.020 --> 02:11:30.440
I think that's another sign
from the outside looking in.

02:11:30.440 --> 02:11:33.230
If someone's self-medicating
with alcohol,

02:11:33.230 --> 02:11:35.473
it's not a good place,
not a good place.

02:11:35.473 --> 02:11:37.640
They're not in a good place,
and they could probably

02:11:37.640 --> 02:11:38.820
use some help.

02:11:38.820 --> 02:11:40.010
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I appreciate
you saying it again.

02:11:40.010 --> 02:11:41.302
We did this episode on alcohol.

02:11:41.302 --> 02:11:42.810
I went into it
totally open minded.

02:11:42.810 --> 02:11:43.700
I've never been a big drinker.

02:11:43.700 --> 02:11:44.200
I've had it.

02:11:44.200 --> 02:11:45.470
I can drink or not drink.

02:11:45.470 --> 02:11:49.083
I was never really
into drugs at all.

02:11:49.083 --> 02:11:50.750
Dabbled a little bit
when I was younger.

02:11:50.750 --> 02:11:52.010
I regret it frankly.

02:11:52.010 --> 02:11:54.020
Brain is plastic early
on, I didn't need that.

02:11:54.020 --> 02:11:55.100
Never did hard drugs.

02:11:55.100 --> 02:11:57.058
Never touched cocaine,
amphetamine, or anything

02:11:57.058 --> 02:11:58.400
like that.

02:11:58.400 --> 02:11:59.920
And if I had, I would say.

02:11:59.920 --> 02:12:02.420
I'm comfortable enough in my
position in life that if I had,

02:12:02.420 --> 02:12:03.350
I'd certainly say.

02:12:03.350 --> 02:12:08.300
But I think that it's pretty
clear that alcohol is bad

02:12:08.300 --> 02:12:11.630
for us certainly past a
certain two drink a week limit.

02:12:11.630 --> 02:12:14.480
People especially with a
propensity for alcoholism

02:12:14.480 --> 02:12:17.390
or who are dealing with other
issues, that's especially

02:12:17.390 --> 02:12:19.730
the case for not drinking.

02:12:19.730 --> 02:12:22.460
And I guess we've gotten into
some hard territory here.

02:12:22.460 --> 02:12:27.060
But I think if this
conversation cues up

02:12:27.060 --> 02:12:30.030
an awareness to anybody,
which I hope it would,

02:12:30.030 --> 02:12:32.325
either people that are in
that space of wondering

02:12:32.325 --> 02:12:34.950
if they should continue or not,
or that know somebody who might

02:12:34.950 --> 02:12:39.470
be, I do think that the
takeaway is very clear,

02:12:39.470 --> 02:12:44.490
which is that there are
ways to avoid these traps

02:12:44.490 --> 02:12:46.590
and to avoid making
these traps worse.

02:12:46.590 --> 02:12:49.050
And I think with regular
sleep-wake schedules,

02:12:49.050 --> 02:12:51.240
understanding that--
and I wrote this down

02:12:51.240 --> 02:12:55.560
that you've particular have
said several times that

02:12:55.560 --> 02:12:58.590
rather than looking for sources
of things outside like a job,

02:12:58.590 --> 02:13:00.600
or a relationship,
which are all great.

02:13:00.600 --> 02:13:03.240
But as sources of energy, or
motivation, or inspiration,

02:13:03.240 --> 02:13:06.540
to use positive action
as a source of energy.

02:13:06.540 --> 02:13:07.680
I think is just--

02:13:07.680 --> 02:13:11.340
if I could put that up on a
billboard in Times Square,

02:13:11.340 --> 02:13:12.330
I would.

02:13:12.330 --> 02:13:14.200
And I'd put your
name next to it,

02:13:14.200 --> 02:13:17.580
which is that positive
action is a source of energy

02:13:17.580 --> 02:13:19.840
that then you can
recycle into more things.

02:13:19.840 --> 02:13:20.340
I think--

02:13:20.340 --> 02:13:20.790
JOCKO WILLINK: Yeah.

02:13:20.790 --> 02:13:22.590
ANDREW HUBERMAN: --going back
to the fundamentals, right?

02:13:22.590 --> 02:13:23.423
JOCKO WILLINK: Yeah.

02:13:23.423 --> 02:13:26.700
And positive action is when you
have to contend with something.

02:13:26.700 --> 02:13:29.490
When there's something that
you're afraid of, step into it,

02:13:29.490 --> 02:13:30.543
move towards it.

02:13:30.543 --> 02:13:32.460
That's how you're going
to solve that problem.

02:13:32.460 --> 02:13:33.877
You don't you don't
solve problems

02:13:33.877 --> 02:13:35.310
by running away from them.

02:13:35.310 --> 02:13:38.070
You solve them by
moving towards them

02:13:38.070 --> 02:13:40.290
and figuring out
what's going on.

02:13:40.290 --> 02:13:42.180
And I mean, alcohol is
obviously a clear way

02:13:42.180 --> 02:13:45.688
to avoid the problem at least
for the next four hours.

02:13:45.688 --> 02:13:47.730
And you wake up, and that
problem is still there.

02:13:47.730 --> 02:13:49.440
And that's not good.

02:13:49.440 --> 02:13:52.260
But when you've got some
problem-- and listen,

02:13:52.260 --> 02:13:54.180
then we could go
down the whole path

02:13:54.180 --> 02:13:56.790
of talking about the
indirect approach, which

02:13:56.790 --> 02:14:01.660
is a theory of combat which
I completely believe in.

02:14:01.660 --> 02:14:05.130
But it also applies to
interacting with other people.

02:14:05.130 --> 02:14:07.937
If you have a problem with
some other person, to think,

02:14:07.937 --> 02:14:09.270
oh, I got a problem with Andrew.

02:14:09.270 --> 02:14:11.130
I'm going to go
confront him on it.

02:14:11.130 --> 02:14:13.107
And that might not
be the best solution.

02:14:13.107 --> 02:14:14.940
In fact, it's probably
not the best solution

02:14:14.940 --> 02:14:16.680
for me to go confront
you with the problem

02:14:16.680 --> 02:14:18.680
because now we're going
to have a confrontation.

02:14:18.680 --> 02:14:21.240
It might be better for me
to take an indirect approach

02:14:21.240 --> 02:14:23.105
and not confront
you but instead,

02:14:23.105 --> 02:14:24.480
engage in a
conversation with you

02:14:24.480 --> 02:14:27.450
about something that's maybe
adjacent to the problem

02:14:27.450 --> 02:14:28.080
that I have.

02:14:28.080 --> 02:14:30.300
And then eventually that
builds our relationship

02:14:30.300 --> 02:14:32.440
to a point where you
start to recognize,

02:14:32.440 --> 02:14:35.280
oh, I bet Jocko feels
something about this thing.

02:14:35.280 --> 02:14:38.490
And we can move towards a
solution instead of me trying

02:14:38.490 --> 02:14:41.310
to punch you in the
face with some truth

02:14:41.310 --> 02:14:43.590
that I believe versus the
truth that you believe.

02:14:43.590 --> 02:14:46.288
And now we're engaged in
combat, and that's bad.

02:14:46.288 --> 02:14:47.580
There's going to be casualties.

02:14:47.580 --> 02:14:49.560
I'd rather take an
indirect approach

02:14:49.560 --> 02:14:51.780
and build a relationship
where we can solve problems

02:14:51.780 --> 02:14:53.682
in a more positive way.

02:14:53.682 --> 02:14:54.890
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I love that.

02:14:54.890 --> 02:14:57.080
And I love it because
it really speaks

02:14:57.080 --> 02:15:00.170
to the power of thinking
carefully and being

02:15:00.170 --> 02:15:03.095
patient and a little
bit slower at times.

02:15:03.095 --> 02:15:04.970
I also heard you say
something earlier that's

02:15:04.970 --> 02:15:10.470
still flagging in my mind,
which is not thinking too much.

02:15:10.470 --> 02:15:13.860
I think seems to be a time
where thinking too much

02:15:13.860 --> 02:15:15.240
is very dangerous.

02:15:15.240 --> 02:15:17.520
And again, we can
keep the discussion

02:15:17.520 --> 02:15:19.415
that we were having
about mental health,

02:15:19.415 --> 02:15:21.790
or we could even just call it
what it is, mental illness.

02:15:21.790 --> 02:15:24.540
I think everyone would agree
suicide is some reflection

02:15:24.540 --> 02:15:27.088
of an unhealthy state.

02:15:27.088 --> 02:15:29.380
We can keep that in the
backdrop as we talk about this,

02:15:29.380 --> 02:15:33.730
but this isn't necessarily
only about that.

02:15:33.730 --> 02:15:36.697
You seem to have an
ability to engage in things

02:15:36.697 --> 02:15:37.780
without thinking too much.

02:15:37.780 --> 02:15:41.950
And yet also to sit back
and be pretty observant.

02:15:41.950 --> 02:15:44.815
And you've talked about
third personing of the self.

02:15:44.815 --> 02:15:46.690
I'd love to talk a little
bit more about this

02:15:46.690 --> 02:15:49.040
even though it's a topic
you've delved into before.

02:15:49.040 --> 02:15:51.220
And in particular the
topic of meditation.

02:15:51.220 --> 02:15:53.930
I've been reading
more about meditation.

02:15:53.930 --> 02:15:57.440
I've meditated for different
stretches in my life,

02:15:57.440 --> 02:15:59.130
different amounts of time.

02:15:59.130 --> 02:16:01.550
And there are two basic
forms of meditation

02:16:01.550 --> 02:16:03.770
that I only recently
learned about.

02:16:03.770 --> 02:16:06.335
One is a focused
attention meditation.

02:16:06.335 --> 02:16:09.470
Your sitting, closing your eyes,
focusing on your breath, body,

02:16:09.470 --> 02:16:11.630
body surface, or
even a visual target

02:16:11.630 --> 02:16:13.430
in your environment
and just focus.

02:16:13.430 --> 02:16:16.867
And we know that enhances
one's ability to focus.

02:16:16.867 --> 02:16:18.450
If you do too late
in the day, it also

02:16:18.450 --> 02:16:21.060
enhance your ability
to not fall asleep.

02:16:21.060 --> 02:16:23.310
A lot of people don't realize
that meditation too late

02:16:23.310 --> 02:16:24.870
in the day, if it's
a focused meditation,

02:16:24.870 --> 02:16:26.340
you're just ramping
up the activity

02:16:26.340 --> 02:16:27.580
in the prefrontal cortex.

02:16:27.580 --> 02:16:31.440
It's a great tool for
getting better at focusing.

02:16:31.440 --> 02:16:34.110
But then there's also
this type of meditation

02:16:34.110 --> 02:16:37.290
called open observer meditation
where you purposefully

02:16:37.290 --> 02:16:40.709
don't include a target in
your mind, or in your vision,

02:16:40.709 --> 02:16:41.760
or in your hearing.

02:16:41.760 --> 02:16:45.030
And you just sit there, eyes
closed, your eyes open, and you

02:16:45.030 --> 02:16:49.440
go into a place of
whatever comes up,

02:16:49.440 --> 02:16:51.780
but you don't hover
there too long.

02:16:51.780 --> 02:16:53.969
The goal is to not
hover on any one thing,

02:16:53.969 --> 02:16:57.280
which sounds like deliberate
attention deficit disorder.

02:16:57.280 --> 02:16:59.340
But it's actually a
pretty cool method

02:16:59.340 --> 02:17:02.670
it turns out for
restoring our ability

02:17:02.670 --> 02:17:06.240
to engage in focus but also
for one particular thing

02:17:06.240 --> 02:17:08.879
that you mentioned earlier,
which is creativity

02:17:08.879 --> 02:17:11.490
and creative problem solving,
which of course, requires

02:17:11.490 --> 02:17:13.770
accessing, let's
just say, more colors

02:17:13.770 --> 02:17:15.809
on the palette than
your vision might be on.

02:17:15.809 --> 02:17:18.090
Realizing, oh, there's
also all these other colors

02:17:18.090 --> 02:17:19.882
over here in the
periphery that I'm missing

02:17:19.882 --> 02:17:21.990
because I'm hyperfocused.

02:17:21.990 --> 02:17:22.910
Do you meditate?

02:17:22.910 --> 02:17:26.299
If you do meditate, is it
more of an open monitoring

02:17:26.299 --> 02:17:27.750
or a focused meditation?

02:17:27.750 --> 02:17:29.959
And if you don't do a
standard meditation,

02:17:29.959 --> 02:17:32.209
are there times throughout
your day, and your routine,

02:17:32.209 --> 02:17:34.879
and your week where as
I'm describing this,

02:17:34.879 --> 02:17:38.629
it maps to something that
feels like open monitoring

02:17:38.629 --> 02:17:40.400
or focused meditation?

02:17:40.400 --> 02:17:42.530
JOCKO WILLINK: Yeah, so
no, I don't meditate.

02:17:42.530 --> 02:17:43.850
I haven't ever.

02:17:43.850 --> 02:17:45.920
I don't think I've
ever actually meditated

02:17:45.920 --> 02:17:47.299
for one second in my life.

02:17:47.299 --> 02:17:48.334
And I--

02:17:48.334 --> 02:17:49.709
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
And I refuse to.

02:17:49.709 --> 02:17:51.626
JOCKO WILLINK: --no, no,
it's not that at all.

02:17:51.626 --> 02:17:53.052
I know Sam Harris
sent me his app,

02:17:53.052 --> 02:17:54.469
and we were going
to do a podcast.

02:17:54.469 --> 02:17:56.261
And I said, dude, I'll
do it for two weeks,

02:17:56.261 --> 02:17:58.615
and we'll do a podcast, and
I'll be more enlightened

02:17:58.615 --> 02:17:59.240
and everything.

02:17:59.240 --> 02:18:01.100
And I didn't even give
Sam Harris two weeks.

02:18:01.100 --> 02:18:05.270
So I still owe Sam Harris two
weeks on his app of meditation

02:18:05.270 --> 02:18:07.010
so we can see how it impacts me.

02:18:07.010 --> 02:18:08.870
But no I've never
done it before.

02:18:08.870 --> 02:18:10.520
I've never tried to do it.

02:18:10.520 --> 02:18:14.540
And that being
said, if the goal is

02:18:14.540 --> 02:18:17.900
to take a step back and
detach from what's going on,

02:18:17.900 --> 02:18:20.100
I do that all day, every day.

02:18:20.100 --> 02:18:23.690
And so that is something
that I've talked about,

02:18:23.690 --> 02:18:25.700
and it's something
I tried to teach.

02:18:25.700 --> 02:18:28.959
I tried to teach the
young SEAL leaders not

02:18:28.959 --> 02:18:30.709
to get caught up in
what's happening right

02:18:30.709 --> 02:18:32.167
in front of them
but to take a step

02:18:32.167 --> 02:18:35.760
back, detach from the situation,
detach from their emotions.

02:18:35.760 --> 02:18:38.600
See more of what's happening.

02:18:38.600 --> 02:18:41.090
You're talking about seeing
more colors of the palette,

02:18:41.090 --> 02:18:42.980
well, on the battlefield,
I want people

02:18:42.980 --> 02:18:46.910
to be able to see more
angles, more maneuvers, more

02:18:46.910 --> 02:18:49.549
opportunities, more of
what the enemy might do,

02:18:49.549 --> 02:18:50.389
more perspective.

02:18:50.389 --> 02:18:53.610
That's what I always
try to achieve.

02:18:53.610 --> 02:18:57.170
And so I'm sorry, I
apologize, I can't give you

02:18:57.170 --> 02:18:58.969
any good discussion
on meditation

02:18:58.969 --> 02:19:00.860
because I haven't tried it.

02:19:00.860 --> 02:19:02.990
ANDREW HUBERMAN: No
apology necessary.

02:19:02.990 --> 02:19:05.180
But I will ask,
when you go surfing,

02:19:05.180 --> 02:19:07.549
and you're sitting in the
water waiting for a wave,

02:19:07.549 --> 02:19:09.830
are you focused on one
particular location

02:19:09.830 --> 02:19:13.250
on the horizon, or are
you in open monitoring,

02:19:13.250 --> 02:19:15.680
just enjoying just bouncing
up and down in the water.

02:19:15.680 --> 02:19:18.657
Like where is your attention
during activities like that?

02:19:18.657 --> 02:19:19.490
JOCKO WILLINK: Yeah.

02:19:19.490 --> 02:19:23.296
So surfing-- oftentimes
surfing, you're a monkey mind,

02:19:23.296 --> 02:19:25.129
and you're just not
thinking about anything.

02:19:25.129 --> 02:19:26.129
Same thing with jujitsu.

02:19:26.129 --> 02:19:29.020
Also with surfing, if
you're waiting for a wave,

02:19:29.020 --> 02:19:31.010
your mind is just going.

02:19:31.010 --> 02:19:33.482
I mean, it's in another
universe sometimes as you're

02:19:33.482 --> 02:19:35.690
sitting there waiting because
you're just looking out

02:19:35.690 --> 02:19:36.590
at the horizon.

02:19:36.590 --> 02:19:41.389
And your mind-- I mean, my mind,
I'm thinking about all kinds--

02:19:41.389 --> 02:19:43.830
I have to come home
sometimes and write notes

02:19:43.830 --> 02:19:45.830
because I thought of this,
I thought of an idea,

02:19:45.830 --> 02:19:46.850
I thought of a perspective.

02:19:46.850 --> 02:19:48.100
That happens to me at jujitsu.

02:19:48.100 --> 02:19:50.455
That happens to me talking
to people where someone's

02:19:50.455 --> 02:19:52.080
talking about something,
I'm like oh, I

02:19:52.080 --> 02:19:52.850
got an idea right now.

02:19:52.850 --> 02:19:53.975
I would go write that down.

02:19:53.975 --> 02:19:55.500
I have to go and write it down.

02:19:55.500 --> 02:19:59.150
I have notes in my phone
like pages and pages

02:19:59.150 --> 02:20:02.060
and pages of notes in my
phone of ideas that I have,

02:20:02.060 --> 02:20:03.258
and I write them down.

02:20:03.258 --> 02:20:05.300
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Do you
voice memo things or do--

02:20:05.300 --> 02:20:06.040
JOCKO WILLINK: No, I type them.

02:20:06.040 --> 02:20:07.040
ANDREW HUBERMAN: --yeah.

02:20:07.040 --> 02:20:09.620
JOCKO WILLINK: I only need
to type like seven words on

02:20:09.620 --> 02:20:12.248
and then I have the
whole ideas in my head.

02:20:12.248 --> 02:20:14.540
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I put a lot
into the notes in my phone

02:20:14.540 --> 02:20:15.040
as well.

02:20:15.040 --> 02:20:17.750
And it probably looks like
gibberish to a lot of people.

02:20:17.750 --> 02:20:20.660
I go back through them when
I'm on the plane, especially

02:20:20.660 --> 02:20:23.253
problems I was challenged with
10 years ago or something.

02:20:23.253 --> 02:20:24.920
And I look like, oh,
my God, I'm dealing

02:20:24.920 --> 02:20:29.990
with the same thing, different
situation, same me, right?

02:20:29.990 --> 02:20:30.950
JOCKO WILLINK: Yeah.

02:20:30.950 --> 02:20:33.380
ANDREW HUBERMAN: We had
an amazing psychiatrist

02:20:33.380 --> 02:20:36.620
on the show, but I'd love
to hear a conversation

02:20:36.620 --> 02:20:38.420
between the two of you.

02:20:38.420 --> 02:20:39.705
His name is Paul Conti.

02:20:39.705 --> 02:20:41.330
He was trained at
Stanford and Harvard.

02:20:41.330 --> 02:20:43.790
He's an amazing guy.

02:20:43.790 --> 02:20:46.390
Actually, talks openly
about the tragedy

02:20:46.390 --> 02:20:48.140
that his brother killed
himself, which was

02:20:48.140 --> 02:20:49.850
what drove him into psychiatry.

02:20:49.850 --> 02:20:52.670
And he's an interesting guy
because he's obviously highly

02:20:52.670 --> 02:20:54.320
educated, incredibly smart.

02:20:54.320 --> 02:20:57.050
He wrote a book on trauma, but
he has incredible knowledge

02:20:57.050 --> 02:20:59.070
about a number of other
areas of psychology,

02:20:59.070 --> 02:21:01.220
including narcissism,
sociopathy.

02:21:01.220 --> 02:21:03.560
He's worked in a lot of
really interesting domains

02:21:03.560 --> 02:21:05.690
with interesting people
that everyone listening

02:21:05.690 --> 02:21:07.860
on his podcast would recognize.

02:21:07.860 --> 02:21:11.180
Of course, he's not going to
reveal who those people are.

02:21:11.180 --> 02:21:17.220
And he talks about
the fact that you

02:21:17.220 --> 02:21:18.978
can look at different people.

02:21:18.978 --> 02:21:21.270
He was actually the one that
shared with me this notion

02:21:21.270 --> 02:21:24.650
of generators and
projectors, and directed me

02:21:24.650 --> 02:21:25.650
towards that literature.

02:21:25.650 --> 02:21:28.650
But when he came
on the podcast, he

02:21:28.650 --> 02:21:31.110
talked a little bit
about that stuff,

02:21:31.110 --> 02:21:33.290
but he talked
mostly about trauma.

02:21:33.290 --> 02:21:36.470
But then we were
talking about ways

02:21:36.470 --> 02:21:39.590
in which people engage in the
world and different archetypes.

02:21:39.590 --> 02:21:42.980
And how you start looking at
stories throughout history,

02:21:42.980 --> 02:21:45.710
and you start seeing the
same themes over and over.

02:21:45.710 --> 02:21:48.110
Westerns, this
idea of a guy rides

02:21:48.110 --> 02:21:50.840
into town and does
some repair work like

02:21:50.840 --> 02:21:54.022
defeats the sinister
person or things

02:21:54.022 --> 02:21:56.480
that are imposing all the time
then rides to the next town.

02:21:56.480 --> 02:21:58.940
It's always like it ends with--
it's going to keep continuing.

02:21:58.940 --> 02:22:01.357
But then when he got into this
discussion of relationship,

02:22:01.357 --> 02:22:04.220
he talks about-- he said this
on the podcast of a patient who

02:22:04.220 --> 02:22:08.720
said, I've been in 10
abusive relationships.

02:22:08.720 --> 02:22:10.790
And he'd say to that
patient, no, you've

02:22:10.790 --> 02:22:14.300
been in one relationship 10
times, which is essentially

02:22:14.300 --> 02:22:17.140
it's all about your issue.

02:22:17.140 --> 02:22:18.220
That's not you.

02:22:18.220 --> 02:22:21.520
I'm not-- purposely didn't point
at you for those listening.

02:22:21.520 --> 02:22:24.310
And I think that those
features of ourselves

02:22:24.310 --> 02:22:27.430
that we bring from condition
to condition can be negative

02:22:27.430 --> 02:22:28.510
or they can be positive.

02:22:28.510 --> 02:22:30.820
One thing that's
interesting-- and here I'm

02:22:30.820 --> 02:22:33.367
not trying to solve or
understand the SEAL team

02:22:33.367 --> 02:22:35.200
community per se, but
I think they represent

02:22:35.200 --> 02:22:37.510
an important archetype
because they are selected

02:22:37.510 --> 02:22:42.520
for this ability to take
hard conditions and failures

02:22:42.520 --> 02:22:43.600
and turn them into wins.

02:22:43.600 --> 02:22:47.020
That's one of the selection
criteria, seems to be.

02:22:47.020 --> 02:22:50.490
They're finding who
has that capability.

02:22:50.490 --> 02:22:53.520
I see a lot of-- and I happen to
know a few people from the SEAL

02:22:53.520 --> 02:22:57.638
Teams who get out
and do really well.

02:22:57.638 --> 02:22:58.680
They have great business.

02:22:58.680 --> 02:23:00.180
You're a shining
example of this.

02:23:00.180 --> 02:23:01.780
And you have a
family, and you know

02:23:01.780 --> 02:23:03.270
you've got German shepherd too.

02:23:03.270 --> 02:23:04.350
We'll talk about
animals in a little bit.

02:23:04.350 --> 02:23:04.980
You have a dog.

02:23:04.980 --> 02:23:06.563
And it's like you
surf, and you train.

02:23:06.563 --> 02:23:09.660
And I'm sure you have your
dark places, dark moments

02:23:09.660 --> 02:23:11.160
and challenges
like anybody else,

02:23:11.160 --> 02:23:13.690
but things look to me like
they're going pretty well.

02:23:13.690 --> 02:23:16.050
And then I also know
people from the SEAL Teams.

02:23:16.050 --> 02:23:18.900
OK, they don't go down the
path of suicide fortunately.

02:23:18.900 --> 02:23:23.620
But it's they don't do as
well as I would have thought.

02:23:23.620 --> 02:23:25.660
And I'm certainly not
picking on this community.

02:23:25.660 --> 02:23:28.077
I also see this from people
who are professional athletes.

02:23:28.077 --> 02:23:30.767
I know kids that were
phenomenal in high school.

02:23:30.767 --> 02:23:32.350
I mean, these are
like early admission

02:23:32.350 --> 02:23:34.100
to all the Ivy League
schools because that

02:23:34.100 --> 02:23:36.280
was what happened in the
town that I grew up in.

02:23:36.280 --> 02:23:40.190
And I look at them now, and
I'm like, wow, That-- gosh,

02:23:40.190 --> 02:23:42.398
it didn't-- somehow
it's not working out.

02:23:42.398 --> 02:23:43.940
And I think it's
important for people

02:23:43.940 --> 02:23:47.150
to hear that yes, winning
creates the propensity for more

02:23:47.150 --> 02:23:47.990
winning.

02:23:47.990 --> 02:23:53.020
But then why do you think
it is that in a community

02:23:53.020 --> 02:23:56.140
where people are trained to
solve problems, adapt, and make

02:23:56.140 --> 02:24:00.100
things work, some people
flourish outside the military,

02:24:00.100 --> 02:24:02.860
and some flourish
less, and some--

02:24:02.860 --> 02:24:06.460
we already talked about--
really go down the dark traps.

02:24:06.460 --> 02:24:08.980
What do you think is
the quality that allows

02:24:08.980 --> 02:24:10.990
people to be really adaptable?

02:24:10.990 --> 02:24:14.140
In particular, because most
of us live in a landscape

02:24:14.140 --> 02:24:17.260
where we have to deal with
people who are not us.

02:24:17.260 --> 02:24:20.487
Like people that are not
good at what we're good at.

02:24:20.487 --> 02:24:22.570
And sometimes that's an
asset, sometimes it's not.

02:24:22.570 --> 02:24:25.390
You seem to be particularly
good at understanding

02:24:25.390 --> 02:24:28.340
the human animal and
working with that.

02:24:28.340 --> 02:24:30.378
So again, this is
a broad question.

02:24:30.378 --> 02:24:32.920
We're going very broadband here,
and we'll get narrower again

02:24:32.920 --> 02:24:33.587
in a little bit.

02:24:33.587 --> 02:24:35.770
But I'd love your
thoughts on why is it?

02:24:35.770 --> 02:24:37.360
How is it?

02:24:37.360 --> 02:24:39.250
What determines
whether or not somebody

02:24:39.250 --> 02:24:41.605
thrives in novel environments?

02:24:41.605 --> 02:24:43.230
JOCKO WILLINK: I have
to start off just

02:24:43.230 --> 02:24:45.690
by saying I wrote a note
as you were talking.

02:24:45.690 --> 02:24:49.320
I just put SEAL and then
I put the not equals sign.

02:24:49.320 --> 02:24:52.950
Because you can't say that
a SEAL equals anything.

02:24:52.950 --> 02:24:54.600
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Excellent point.

02:24:54.600 --> 02:24:57.000
JOCKO WILLINK: I
mean, there's guys

02:24:57.000 --> 02:25:00.830
that have been in the SEAL
Teams that are murderers.

02:25:00.830 --> 02:25:02.580
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Like
real life murderers.

02:25:02.580 --> 02:25:03.830
JOCKO WILLINK: Like murderers.

02:25:03.830 --> 02:25:06.480
Like rapists and murderers that
went through SEAL training.

02:25:06.480 --> 02:25:07.950
Rapists, murderers,
and they're in prison

02:25:07.950 --> 02:25:09.117
for the rest of their lives.

02:25:09.117 --> 02:25:10.140
Like that's a thing.

02:25:10.140 --> 02:25:13.860
There's people that have been in
the SEAL Teams that got kicked

02:25:13.860 --> 02:25:16.350
out of SEAL Teams for drugs.

02:25:16.350 --> 02:25:18.930
I mean, you name it,
and we've got that.

02:25:18.930 --> 02:25:22.098
And we've got guys that are just
lazy, and we've got guys that--

02:25:22.098 --> 02:25:23.640
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
And physicians-- you

02:25:23.640 --> 02:25:24.973
could say this about physicians.

02:25:24.973 --> 02:25:29.785
There have been sociopathic
serial killer physicians

02:25:29.785 --> 02:25:32.160
and then there are ones that
are in third world countries

02:25:32.160 --> 02:25:36.780
right now that would not
accept $1,000,000,000 to stop

02:25:36.780 --> 02:25:39.780
serving people at the
level of the basic medicine

02:25:39.780 --> 02:25:40.800
that they deserve.

02:25:40.800 --> 02:25:42.600
JOCKO WILLINK: So
there's the seals

02:25:42.600 --> 02:25:45.917
that get out and just volunteer
to go help in the worst

02:25:45.917 --> 02:25:46.750
places in the world.

02:25:46.750 --> 02:25:49.440
So you've got a full
spectrum of people.

02:25:49.440 --> 02:25:53.820
So to say a SEAL equals
success in any domain,

02:25:53.820 --> 02:25:56.408
the only domain you can
say that they're successful

02:25:56.408 --> 02:25:58.200
is they made it through
basic SEAL training

02:25:58.200 --> 02:25:59.610
because guys make it
through basic SEAL training,

02:25:59.610 --> 02:26:01.200
and they're not good SEALs.

02:26:01.200 --> 02:26:02.550
That happens.

02:26:02.550 --> 02:26:05.772
So you get guys that make it
through basic SEAL training,

02:26:05.772 --> 02:26:07.980
and they make it through
SEAL qualification training,

02:26:07.980 --> 02:26:09.723
and they make it to
a SEAL team, and they

02:26:09.723 --> 02:26:12.390
get kicked out of the SEAL Teams
because they're not good SEALs.

02:26:12.390 --> 02:26:14.940
They weren't meant
to do that job.

02:26:14.940 --> 02:26:16.000
That happens.

02:26:16.000 --> 02:26:19.050
So all they've proven by making
it through basic SEAL training

02:26:19.050 --> 02:26:20.820
is they can suck
it up for a while.

02:26:20.820 --> 02:26:23.850
And there's also guys that make
it through basic SEAL training

02:26:23.850 --> 02:26:26.220
because they learned how to
maneuver through the system.

02:26:26.220 --> 02:26:27.607
They learned what
to do, and what

02:26:27.607 --> 02:26:29.190
the minimum requirements
were, and how

02:26:29.190 --> 02:26:29.910
they could skate through this.

02:26:29.910 --> 02:26:31.350
There's guys like that.

02:26:31.350 --> 02:26:34.093
It's not a huge number, but
they're absolutely there.

02:26:34.093 --> 02:26:35.760
ANDREW HUBERMAN: We
see them in science.

02:26:35.760 --> 02:26:39.660
People that go to a lab, figure
out who the director of the lab

02:26:39.660 --> 02:26:42.225
is at the level of psychology--
and this is actually

02:26:42.225 --> 02:26:44.100
one of the more dangerous
aspects of science.

02:26:44.100 --> 02:26:46.470
It actually negatively
impacts all of society.

02:26:46.470 --> 02:26:47.465
I'll just say it.

02:26:47.465 --> 02:26:49.590
And any scientist will know
what I'm talking about.

02:26:49.590 --> 02:26:51.960
They find the big famous
labs, they figure out

02:26:51.960 --> 02:26:54.210
who that leader of the
lab is, and they get

02:26:54.210 --> 02:26:55.930
that person the data they want.

02:26:55.930 --> 02:26:59.790
They might not make the data
up, but they will certainly

02:26:59.790 --> 02:27:02.700
discard the data
that don't fit, which

02:27:02.700 --> 02:27:05.670
is one way of making
data up by exclusion.

02:27:05.670 --> 02:27:08.130
It's not literally like
painting pictures of cells

02:27:08.130 --> 02:27:09.422
that aren't there or something.

02:27:09.422 --> 02:27:12.270
But that happens a lot, and
those people often go far.

02:27:12.270 --> 02:27:15.390
They rarely go all the
way because pretty soon

02:27:15.390 --> 02:27:18.030
their reputation
expands to the point

02:27:18.030 --> 02:27:20.610
where people go like, yeah,
no one can repeat that result.

02:27:20.610 --> 02:27:22.920
But these people sit
in very high positions.

02:27:22.920 --> 02:27:24.135
Not at Stanford.

02:27:24.135 --> 02:27:26.010
I will say, I don't know
any of my colleagues

02:27:26.010 --> 02:27:29.460
at Stanford that
meet those criteria.

02:27:29.460 --> 02:27:33.480
But you see them, and you
see what they're doing,

02:27:33.480 --> 02:27:36.450
and they're basically solving
a social engineering thing.

02:27:36.450 --> 02:27:38.342
They just happen to be
doing it in science.

02:27:38.342 --> 02:27:40.050
Now, why anyone would
do that in science?

02:27:40.050 --> 02:27:41.790
I don't know because you
don't get rich in science.

02:27:41.790 --> 02:27:42.870
You certainly don't get famous.

02:27:42.870 --> 02:27:44.580
But for whatever
reason, they figured it

02:27:44.580 --> 02:27:45.900
out that that's where
they're going to do it.

02:27:45.900 --> 02:27:47.100
And I'm sure you
see it in law firms.

02:27:47.100 --> 02:27:48.990
I'm sure you see it in
every single domain--

02:27:48.990 --> 02:27:49.540
JOCKO WILLINK: Everything

02:27:49.540 --> 02:27:50.310
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

02:27:50.310 --> 02:27:51.768
JOCKO WILLINK: Now,
you pointed out

02:27:51.768 --> 02:27:54.390
that there's some people that
make it to the Ivy League

02:27:54.390 --> 02:27:56.598
schools, and they graduate
from an Ivy League school,

02:27:56.598 --> 02:27:57.590
and they don't do well.

02:27:57.590 --> 02:28:00.090
And to me, that's very similar
to someone that might make it

02:28:00.090 --> 02:28:02.110
to the SEAL Teams, do
well in the SEAL Teams,

02:28:02.110 --> 02:28:04.380
and then they get out,
and they don't do well.

02:28:04.380 --> 02:28:07.020
It's probably because of what
I talked about earlier when

02:28:07.020 --> 02:28:08.637
I went to Navy boot camp.

02:28:08.637 --> 02:28:09.720
Here's what you got to do.

02:28:09.720 --> 02:28:11.800
If you do it well,
you'll be rewarded.

02:28:11.800 --> 02:28:15.250
Well, if you're in high school,
and your mom and dad say, hey,

02:28:15.250 --> 02:28:16.952
if you do good in
high school, you're

02:28:16.952 --> 02:28:18.660
going to get into this
Ivy League school,

02:28:18.660 --> 02:28:19.980
and you get into that
Ivy League school.

02:28:19.980 --> 02:28:21.690
And here's what you need
to do in high school,

02:28:21.690 --> 02:28:22.620
you need to get good grades.

02:28:22.620 --> 02:28:24.213
You need to be part
of the Glee club.

02:28:24.213 --> 02:28:26.380
You need whatever the things
are that you got to do.

02:28:26.380 --> 02:28:28.088
You've got to speak
a different language.

02:28:28.088 --> 02:28:30.603
You've got to go volunteer in
Guatemala in the summertime.

02:28:30.603 --> 02:28:32.520
You've got to do these
things, and then you'll

02:28:32.520 --> 02:28:33.600
get into the good college.

02:28:33.600 --> 02:28:34.590
Once you get into
the good college,

02:28:34.590 --> 02:28:35.640
you've got to get this degree.

02:28:35.640 --> 02:28:36.723
Once you get that degree--

02:28:36.723 --> 02:28:40.227
So they've had a path laid out
for them of boxes to check,

02:28:40.227 --> 02:28:41.560
and they go and check the boxes.

02:28:41.560 --> 02:28:43.527
And then when they
get done, no one

02:28:43.527 --> 02:28:45.360
has put any more boxes
out in front of them,

02:28:45.360 --> 02:28:46.680
so they don't know what to do.

02:28:46.680 --> 02:28:51.390
And that can certainly happen
from someone in the SEAL Teams,

02:28:51.390 --> 02:28:53.935
or someone in the military
that what they've been-- hey,

02:28:53.935 --> 02:28:54.810
this is your mission.

02:28:54.810 --> 02:28:55.740
This is what you've got to do.

02:28:55.740 --> 02:28:57.550
Here's what you need
to do, do it well.

02:28:57.550 --> 02:28:58.187
Check the box.

02:28:58.187 --> 02:28:58.770
Check the box.

02:28:58.770 --> 02:28:59.250
Check the box.

02:28:59.250 --> 02:28:59.670
Check the box.

02:28:59.670 --> 02:29:02.003
And then it's time to retire,
and there's no one putting

02:29:02.003 --> 02:29:03.420
a box in front of him to check.

02:29:03.420 --> 02:29:05.670
And so unless someone--

02:29:05.670 --> 02:29:07.170
some guys get out
of the SEAL Teams,

02:29:07.170 --> 02:29:09.760
and they go into a big corporate
structure, and they kick ass.

02:29:09.760 --> 02:29:12.010
Because there's someone in
the corporate world saying,

02:29:12.010 --> 02:29:13.150
hey, here's what
you got to do next.

02:29:13.150 --> 02:29:14.010
And they do great.

02:29:14.010 --> 02:29:16.110
And that's super good for
them, and they actually

02:29:16.110 --> 02:29:16.920
really like it.

02:29:16.920 --> 02:29:18.060
I was talking to a
guy the other day.

02:29:18.060 --> 02:29:19.518
He's like full on
in a corporation.

02:29:19.518 --> 02:29:20.960
He's doing a great job.

02:29:20.960 --> 02:29:22.080
He likes what he's doing.

02:29:22.080 --> 02:29:24.120
It's awesome.

02:29:24.120 --> 02:29:28.350
But I think you get some
guys that they don't really

02:29:28.350 --> 02:29:32.883
have the open mind to see
where opportunities are.

02:29:32.883 --> 02:29:34.800
And one thing that's
nice about the SEAL Teams

02:29:34.800 --> 02:29:38.580
is there's a lot of--

02:29:38.580 --> 02:29:40.260
you get a lot of
freedom of maneuver.

02:29:40.260 --> 02:29:43.485
You can really do a lot of
stuff that you want to do.

02:29:43.485 --> 02:29:45.360
And so when they look
at the corporate world,

02:29:45.360 --> 02:29:46.380
they don't see that,
and they think,

02:29:46.380 --> 02:29:48.010
oh, I'm not going to do that.

02:29:48.010 --> 02:29:52.157
But then they're not quite
sure how to take the next step.

02:29:52.157 --> 02:29:54.240
So I think that's why you
might see some guys that

02:29:54.240 --> 02:29:59.430
aren't super successful
because they don't really

02:29:59.430 --> 02:30:00.720
know what to do.

02:30:00.720 --> 02:30:03.570
And they don't really
have a mind that's

02:30:03.570 --> 02:30:06.850
open to look for opportunities.

02:30:06.850 --> 02:30:09.092
And also you got some
guys that success for them

02:30:09.092 --> 02:30:11.050
is they're going to hang
out with their family,

02:30:11.050 --> 02:30:13.390
and they're going to
get in good shape,

02:30:13.390 --> 02:30:15.970
and they're going to run some
triathlons or compete in--

02:30:15.970 --> 02:30:18.303
whatever they're going to do,
they're going to go do it,

02:30:18.303 --> 02:30:21.370
and that's what they're looking
to do, which is also awesome.

02:30:21.370 --> 02:30:23.650
Go take time, go enjoy
your family, man.

02:30:23.650 --> 02:30:25.240
You gave enough to your country.

02:30:25.240 --> 02:30:26.930
Go hang out with your kids.

02:30:26.930 --> 02:30:29.228
That's success as far
as I'm concerned too.

02:30:29.228 --> 02:30:30.520
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I agree there.

02:30:30.520 --> 02:30:32.140
I know far too
many people who are

02:30:32.140 --> 02:30:33.730
successful in their
professional lives

02:30:33.730 --> 02:30:35.770
but who have very
diminished personal lives,

02:30:35.770 --> 02:30:37.780
and it is not a pretty picture.

02:30:37.780 --> 02:30:43.390
You mentioned the parent driving
the kid, do this, do that.

02:30:43.390 --> 02:30:45.940
In that scenario, I
sense tons of fear.

02:30:45.940 --> 02:30:47.740
It's all about not
being a failure.

02:30:47.740 --> 02:30:52.023
It's not actually about love of
your craft or what you enjoy.

02:30:52.023 --> 02:30:53.440
Pretty early in
my science career,

02:30:53.440 --> 02:30:56.140
I learned that there are certain
people are just ambitious.

02:30:56.140 --> 02:30:57.230
They just like to win.

02:30:57.230 --> 02:30:58.743
And I used to joke around.

02:30:58.743 --> 02:31:00.160
It's not polite,
but I used to go,

02:31:00.160 --> 02:31:02.890
we should all just
tell that person

02:31:02.890 --> 02:31:05.470
that the new cool thing
is trying to understand

02:31:05.470 --> 02:31:06.970
the biology of like--

02:31:06.970 --> 02:31:08.708
and I would say
feces or something.

02:31:08.708 --> 02:31:10.000
And they'd probably work on it.

02:31:10.000 --> 02:31:11.470
They'd be like, yeah, it's
like the greatest thing

02:31:11.470 --> 02:31:13.060
because they actually don't
care what they're working on.

02:31:13.060 --> 02:31:14.560
For them it's just the hunt.

02:31:14.560 --> 02:31:16.690
And I learned that
actually people like that

02:31:16.690 --> 02:31:20.293
can serve an important role
because like well, there's

02:31:20.293 --> 02:31:21.460
actually a whole microbiome.

02:31:21.460 --> 02:31:23.377
There are actually labs
that do work on feces,

02:31:23.377 --> 02:31:25.690
so forgive me my colleagues
that work on microbiomes.

02:31:25.690 --> 02:31:30.495
But in all seriousness,
people who are just ambitious

02:31:30.495 --> 02:31:31.870
can be very
effective because you

02:31:31.870 --> 02:31:33.580
put a problem in front of
them, and it's like a dog,

02:31:33.580 --> 02:31:34.600
they'll just retrieve.

02:31:34.600 --> 02:31:36.040
It's they're just go.

02:31:36.040 --> 02:31:38.770
As opposed to love
of retrieving--

02:31:38.770 --> 02:31:40.090
for retrieving sake.

02:31:40.090 --> 02:31:42.670
It's like you give that same
dog-- the analogy here would

02:31:42.670 --> 02:31:44.087
be give that same
dog a high jump,

02:31:44.087 --> 02:31:45.850
and they're into high
jumping, or whatever

02:31:45.850 --> 02:31:47.530
it is, or diving underwater.

02:31:47.530 --> 02:31:51.580
But I think of people
more like animals and more

02:31:51.580 --> 02:31:53.140
like different dog species.

02:31:53.140 --> 02:31:57.940
Like we-- as your case was
with music, particular music,

02:31:57.940 --> 02:32:00.940
and communities, or the example
of shopping for the pants

02:32:00.940 --> 02:32:02.710
and that experience
of the first time

02:32:02.710 --> 02:32:05.620
you tap into something that
really feels unique to you.

02:32:05.620 --> 02:32:08.200
You're like there's
something here.

02:32:08.200 --> 02:32:11.710
To be able to find work that
includes that but also is

02:32:11.710 --> 02:32:14.240
hard and also allows
you to evolve over time,

02:32:14.240 --> 02:32:17.980
I mean, I think that's
the real gift that I

02:32:17.980 --> 02:32:19.630
think most people are seeking.

02:32:19.630 --> 02:32:21.790
And of course,
there's no shortcut

02:32:21.790 --> 02:32:23.620
to that except
perhaps one, which

02:32:23.620 --> 02:32:26.830
is to be able to sense the
difference between ambition.

02:32:26.830 --> 02:32:28.615
And there's no
better word for it,

02:32:28.615 --> 02:32:30.490
let's just call it what
it is, which is love.

02:32:30.490 --> 02:32:31.990
Like I love this.

02:32:31.990 --> 02:32:34.133
And the reason I think
that love is so powerful--

02:32:34.133 --> 02:32:35.800
and here I'm sounding
like Lex Fridman--

02:32:35.800 --> 02:32:38.350
but I don't mean
interelational love.

02:32:38.350 --> 02:32:44.630
I mean, being able to sense what
that feels like is that I do

02:32:44.630 --> 02:32:48.830
believe that it allows us to
tap into an enormous number

02:32:48.830 --> 02:32:53.690
of things that fear alone and
ambition alone and just being

02:32:53.690 --> 02:32:57.350
a hard driving person alone
will not allow us to tap into.

02:32:57.350 --> 02:33:00.915
Things like
adaptiveness, creativity.

02:33:00.915 --> 02:33:03.290
And I think there's a really
obvious reason for it, which

02:33:03.290 --> 02:33:05.810
is that the one thing we
know about our species

02:33:05.810 --> 02:33:07.760
is that we want to
make more of ourselves

02:33:07.760 --> 02:33:09.260
and to take good
care of our young.

02:33:09.260 --> 02:33:11.510
Whether or not everyone has
kids or not is irrelevant.

02:33:11.510 --> 02:33:14.060
The point is that every species
not only wants to do that

02:33:14.060 --> 02:33:15.230
but need to do that.

02:33:15.230 --> 02:33:17.480
And the feeling
of love is really

02:33:17.480 --> 02:33:19.080
what allows us to be adaptable.

02:33:19.080 --> 02:33:22.220
I don't think there's anything
that trains up adaptability as

02:33:22.220 --> 02:33:24.570
much as being around kids.

02:33:24.570 --> 02:33:26.663
You just have to be
adaptable because they're

02:33:26.663 --> 02:33:28.080
one moment they're
up then they're

02:33:28.080 --> 02:33:31.380
down then they're disappointed.

02:33:31.380 --> 02:33:33.810
And you shared a
really important story

02:33:33.810 --> 02:33:36.570
about loss of somebody
that clearly you loved

02:33:36.570 --> 02:33:41.725
and that clearly loved the
community he worked in.

02:33:41.725 --> 02:33:43.350
It wasn't just that
you guys loved him,

02:33:43.350 --> 02:33:45.450
It's that he loved you guys.

02:33:45.450 --> 02:33:49.260
And I think that being able to
tap into these feelings of love

02:33:49.260 --> 02:33:52.290
for things, for people,
and for experiences,

02:33:52.290 --> 02:33:53.830
I think is so critical.

02:33:53.830 --> 02:33:57.030
And I don't meditate
much these days,

02:33:57.030 --> 02:33:59.820
but I have heard of this
love and kindness meditation.

02:33:59.820 --> 02:34:01.043
And it sounds so soft to me.

02:34:01.043 --> 02:34:03.210
I was thinking like, gosh,
what am I supposed to do?

02:34:03.210 --> 02:34:04.890
Then float like
levitate at the end?

02:34:04.890 --> 02:34:08.050
And wear a mumu or
whatever it's called.

02:34:08.050 --> 02:34:09.300
I don't know what's the thing.

02:34:09.300 --> 02:34:11.928
But my friend who I'm
fortunate to call a friend.

02:34:11.928 --> 02:34:13.470
Not trying to name
drop here, but I'm

02:34:13.470 --> 02:34:14.970
very fortunate to call
Rick Rubin, the music

02:34:14.970 --> 02:34:16.050
producer, a friend.

02:34:16.050 --> 02:34:18.630
And he was the one
who started turning me

02:34:18.630 --> 02:34:20.130
on to different
forms of meditation,

02:34:20.130 --> 02:34:21.297
the ones I mentioned before.

02:34:21.297 --> 02:34:23.380
And this idea that there
are forms of meditation

02:34:23.380 --> 02:34:27.513
which put us in touch with
what he calls the source.

02:34:27.513 --> 02:34:29.430
Now, this is really
getting a little mystical,

02:34:29.430 --> 02:34:32.040
but I think this all maps
back to the same thing, which

02:34:32.040 --> 02:34:34.570
is that there are
sources of dopamine

02:34:34.570 --> 02:34:37.140
and the other
neurotransmitters in us that

02:34:37.140 --> 02:34:41.670
give us a super power
to adapt to anything.

02:34:41.670 --> 02:34:47.130
And I think it's at
least includes love

02:34:47.130 --> 02:34:49.740
because that's the most
adaptable emotion by definition

02:34:49.740 --> 02:34:51.640
because of what's
required for evolution.

02:34:51.640 --> 02:34:55.410
So the question
therefore is, in yourself

02:34:55.410 --> 02:34:58.490
and in your observation of
people that you've worked with,

02:34:58.490 --> 02:35:04.440
did you ever sense that just
being hard driving was great

02:35:04.440 --> 02:35:05.990
but it was limiting?

02:35:05.990 --> 02:35:08.180
Did you ever sense that
by liking the people

02:35:08.180 --> 02:35:12.020
you work with you could perform
much better even if they

02:35:12.020 --> 02:35:14.570
perhaps were not as hard--

02:35:14.570 --> 02:35:17.870
to kind of borrow the common
parlance around this--

02:35:17.870 --> 02:35:19.760
they weren't as hard
as everybody else?

02:35:19.760 --> 02:35:21.920
That because you
like each other so,

02:35:21.920 --> 02:35:24.800
so much that you
can do anything?

02:35:24.800 --> 02:35:28.890
JOCKO WILLINK: Well, if you
have a team of 10 people,

02:35:28.890 --> 02:35:30.510
and you all have a
great relationship

02:35:30.510 --> 02:35:32.843
and you get along well, and
you're going against my team

02:35:32.843 --> 02:35:35.510
and we all hate each
other, who's going to win?

02:35:35.510 --> 02:35:36.470
ANDREW HUBERMAN: The team
that loves each other

02:35:36.470 --> 02:35:37.730
is going to win, I would hope.

02:35:37.730 --> 02:35:38.880
JOCKO WILLINK: It's
not even close.

02:35:38.880 --> 02:35:40.255
It's not even--
a matter of fact,

02:35:40.255 --> 02:35:43.040
if you work for me
and you don't like me,

02:35:43.040 --> 02:35:44.900
what performance are
you going to give me?

02:35:44.900 --> 02:35:45.930
ANDREW HUBERMAN: It's
going to be tough.

02:35:45.930 --> 02:35:47.300
JOCKO WILLINK: What
if you love me,

02:35:47.300 --> 02:35:49.890
and I've looked out for you, and
I've done everything for you,

02:35:49.890 --> 02:35:51.590
and I've taken care of you,
what kind of performance

02:35:51.590 --> 02:35:52.520
are you going to give me?

02:35:52.520 --> 02:35:53.120
Everything you've got.

02:35:53.120 --> 02:35:54.495
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
I'd die for you.

02:35:54.495 --> 02:35:56.930
JOCKO WILLINK: So
yeah, earlier you

02:35:56.930 --> 02:36:00.210
asked about the human
animal and human nature.

02:36:00.210 --> 02:36:03.347
And this is part of leadership.

02:36:03.347 --> 02:36:05.180
I got asked this question
the other day by--

02:36:05.180 --> 02:36:07.100
I was working with a company.

02:36:07.100 --> 02:36:09.830
And the guy says,
how do I identify--

02:36:09.830 --> 02:36:12.530
what are the characteristics
of someone that can execute,

02:36:12.530 --> 02:36:16.580
and how do I identify those
characteristics in a person

02:36:16.580 --> 02:36:18.800
so that I can get those people?

02:36:18.800 --> 02:36:23.145
And I said, well, first of
all, the characteristics

02:36:23.145 --> 02:36:25.650
are the characteristics that
everybody is-- obviously,

02:36:25.650 --> 02:36:27.330
someone that's driven, someone
that communicates well,

02:36:27.330 --> 02:36:29.122
someone that's going
to make things happen,

02:36:29.122 --> 02:36:30.480
those are pretty simple to know.

02:36:30.480 --> 02:36:31.470
We know what they are.

02:36:31.470 --> 02:36:32.512
How do you identify them?

02:36:32.512 --> 02:36:33.930
It's pretty simple as well.

02:36:33.930 --> 02:36:35.250
I give you a task.

02:36:35.250 --> 02:36:36.510
I give Andrew a task.

02:36:36.510 --> 02:36:37.770
It's pretty simple task.

02:36:37.770 --> 02:36:39.480
If you get it done, cool.

02:36:39.480 --> 02:36:41.688
Give you a little bit
more complex task.

02:36:41.688 --> 02:36:42.480
Do you get it done?

02:36:42.480 --> 02:36:43.180
Yes.

02:36:43.180 --> 02:36:45.443
I give the same task to
Fred, he doesn't get it done.

02:36:45.443 --> 02:36:47.110
He comes back with a
bunch of questions.

02:36:47.110 --> 02:36:47.818
He slow rolls it.

02:36:47.818 --> 02:36:49.610
He's got all kinds of
excuses and problems.

02:36:49.610 --> 02:36:51.270
I give you an even
more complex task,

02:36:51.270 --> 02:36:53.130
you come back, you get it done.

02:36:53.130 --> 02:36:55.000
And then I'm going
to realize, OK,

02:36:55.000 --> 02:36:56.850
Andrew is the guy that
makes things happen.

02:36:56.850 --> 02:37:01.350
He's a guy that can
actually execute.

02:37:01.350 --> 02:37:02.560
A little bit what you said.

02:37:02.560 --> 02:37:06.480
I mean, there are certain breeds
of dogs but even that is--

02:37:06.480 --> 02:37:12.930
they're not as different
as human beings are.

02:37:12.930 --> 02:37:15.330
And there are-- so now
there are some guys--

02:37:15.330 --> 02:37:18.150
I've got Andrew who
will make things happen.

02:37:18.150 --> 02:37:19.680
Here's the problem with Andrew.

02:37:19.680 --> 02:37:25.240
When I say, hey, Andrew, here's
this nebulous idea that I have.

02:37:25.240 --> 02:37:26.830
Can you turn this
into a reality?

02:37:26.830 --> 02:37:29.440
And you're like,
where do I start?

02:37:29.440 --> 02:37:31.010
I'm not sure where
you want me to go.

02:37:31.010 --> 02:37:32.510
Meanwhile, I gave
it to the guy that

02:37:32.510 --> 02:37:35.950
didn't make anything happen with
specific tasks that I gave him.

02:37:35.950 --> 02:37:37.337
And he comes back and says--

02:37:37.337 --> 02:37:38.920
I said, hey, I got
this nebulous idea.

02:37:38.920 --> 02:37:40.510
Can you see-- he goes, oh yeah.

02:37:40.510 --> 02:37:42.010
And all of a
sudden, he takes it.

02:37:42.010 --> 02:37:44.260
He says, hey, I figured out
a way to make this happen.

02:37:44.260 --> 02:37:46.640
So you might have someone
that's very good at executing,

02:37:46.640 --> 02:37:47.830
but they're not very creative.

02:37:47.830 --> 02:37:49.390
I might have somebody
that's very creative,

02:37:49.390 --> 02:37:51.130
but they're not very
good at executing.

02:37:51.130 --> 02:37:52.510
So what do I do?

02:37:52.510 --> 02:37:54.910
I build a team where
I've got Andrew and Fred,

02:37:54.910 --> 02:37:55.960
and they work together.

02:37:55.960 --> 02:37:58.060
And Fred comes up
with good ideas,

02:37:58.060 --> 02:38:00.800
and we bring them to Andrew, and
Andrew goes and executes them.

02:38:00.800 --> 02:38:03.340
So that's what we're
doing from a leadership

02:38:03.340 --> 02:38:09.490
perspective is we're letting
people's nature execute.

02:38:09.490 --> 02:38:12.760
We're putting people
into roles where

02:38:12.760 --> 02:38:15.170
their nature is beneficial.

02:38:15.170 --> 02:38:18.437
I'm not going to take someone
that's shy and introverted

02:38:18.437 --> 02:38:20.020
and put them out in
a lead sales role.

02:38:20.020 --> 02:38:21.562
I'm not going to
take somebody that's

02:38:21.562 --> 02:38:24.345
boisterous and extroverted
and put them into a cubicle

02:38:24.345 --> 02:38:25.720
where they're
going to be looking

02:38:25.720 --> 02:38:27.010
at spreadsheets all day.

02:38:27.010 --> 02:38:28.580
Clearly, I'm not
going to do that.

02:38:28.580 --> 02:38:30.400
So what we have to
do as leaders is

02:38:30.400 --> 02:38:32.710
we have to find the right
people for the right role,

02:38:32.710 --> 02:38:34.460
and we place them
into those roles.

02:38:34.460 --> 02:38:36.670
Now, does it mean
that I abandon all

02:38:36.670 --> 02:38:38.290
hope that the guy
that's an introvert

02:38:38.290 --> 02:38:41.068
will ever develop more
communication skills?

02:38:41.068 --> 02:38:42.610
No, I'm still going
to work with him.

02:38:42.610 --> 02:38:44.710
And over time, we'll
get him a little bit

02:38:44.710 --> 02:38:46.353
moving in the right direction.

02:38:46.353 --> 02:38:47.770
But I'm not going
to take somebody

02:38:47.770 --> 02:38:52.120
that's a total
introvert and turn them

02:38:52.120 --> 02:38:53.110
into a lead sales guy.

02:38:53.110 --> 02:38:54.730
That's not going
to happen anymore

02:38:54.730 --> 02:38:56.522
than I'm going to change
a tiger's stripes.

02:38:56.522 --> 02:39:00.900
So that's what we have to do
is we have to help people--

02:39:00.900 --> 02:39:03.180
as leaders, we have to
help people find the role

02:39:03.180 --> 02:39:05.430
and find the thing
that they're good at.

02:39:05.430 --> 02:39:10.320
Now, does that mean if I have
someone that loves their job,

02:39:10.320 --> 02:39:11.850
they're going to
do better at it?

02:39:11.850 --> 02:39:13.290
Absolutely.

02:39:13.290 --> 02:39:15.330
Does it mean that
if I have somebody

02:39:15.330 --> 02:39:19.090
that's driven just
by achievement

02:39:19.090 --> 02:39:20.950
that they're going to
be good at their job?

02:39:20.950 --> 02:39:21.520
No.

02:39:21.520 --> 02:39:23.223
In fact, well, they can be.

02:39:23.223 --> 02:39:25.390
There's going to be certain
roles I can put them in.

02:39:25.390 --> 02:39:27.057
If I've got a sales
number I need to hit

02:39:27.057 --> 02:39:29.430
and Andrew is super
into achievement,

02:39:29.430 --> 02:39:31.240
he wants to be the
golden child, he

02:39:31.240 --> 02:39:34.870
wants to have his
picture on the magazine

02:39:34.870 --> 02:39:37.600
that we put out about
our industry, cool.

02:39:37.600 --> 02:39:40.360
I can throw this task
at you, and you're

02:39:40.360 --> 02:39:42.200
going to go and get it.

02:39:42.200 --> 02:39:44.800
The problem is if
there's something that's

02:39:44.800 --> 02:39:48.310
going to take more perseverance
and the reward isn't that high,

02:39:48.310 --> 02:39:50.890
or it's a long-term
goal, you're probably not

02:39:50.890 --> 02:39:52.600
the right guy for the job.

02:39:52.600 --> 02:39:57.770
So liking your job is
absolutely critical.

02:39:57.770 --> 02:40:00.100
And if you love your
job, you're going

02:40:00.100 --> 02:40:01.960
to be able to most
likely excel at it.

02:40:01.960 --> 02:40:04.750
Now, you could be an unfortunate
person that loves your job

02:40:04.750 --> 02:40:05.860
and is not good at it.

02:40:05.860 --> 02:40:07.360
That happens occasionally.

02:40:07.360 --> 02:40:07.900
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Yeah, but it seems--

02:40:07.900 --> 02:40:09.400
JOCKO WILLINK: It's like--
sure reminds me of your--

02:40:09.400 --> 02:40:10.390
ANDREW HUBERMAN: But
it seems pretty rare.

02:40:10.390 --> 02:40:12.130
JOCKO WILLINK: It reminds me
of your skateboarding career.

02:40:12.130 --> 02:40:15.050
You loved skateboarding, but you
just weren't that good at it.

02:40:15.050 --> 02:40:15.880
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I
wasn't that good at it.

02:40:15.880 --> 02:40:15.940
JOCKO WILLINK: Unfortunately.

02:40:15.940 --> 02:40:18.357
ANDREW HUBERMAN: You what I
loved more than skateboarding?

02:40:18.357 --> 02:40:19.870
I loved the community I was in.

02:40:19.870 --> 02:40:21.993
I loved the community I was in.

02:40:21.993 --> 02:40:24.160
And I probably would have
gone to the industry side,

02:40:24.160 --> 02:40:26.380
or worked on a company
side and not been

02:40:26.380 --> 02:40:28.940
on the actual skateboard side,
or just skateboard for fun.

02:40:28.940 --> 02:40:30.940
So there's a guy in the
skateboarding community.

02:40:30.940 --> 02:40:31.982
His name is Jim Thiebaud.

02:40:31.982 --> 02:40:35.327
And he's the not-so-hidden
secret in that community.

02:40:35.327 --> 02:40:36.160
He's an amazing guy.

02:40:36.160 --> 02:40:40.060
And he early on left
professional skateboarding

02:40:40.060 --> 02:40:42.707
to run a company, Real, Deluxe,
a bunch of other companies.

02:40:42.707 --> 02:40:43.540
He's an amazing guy.

02:40:43.540 --> 02:40:44.207
And he told me--

02:40:44.207 --> 02:40:45.790
we've become friends
recently-- and he

02:40:45.790 --> 02:40:48.553
said he realized he wasn't
going to be one of the big guys,

02:40:48.553 --> 02:40:50.470
but he knew he wanted
to be in this community,

02:40:50.470 --> 02:40:51.568
so he found his place.

02:40:51.568 --> 02:40:53.110
And I think everyone
in skateboarding

02:40:53.110 --> 02:40:54.595
looks to Jim as like the guy.

02:40:57.250 --> 02:41:00.410
He truly cares about the
sport and about the people.

02:41:00.410 --> 02:41:02.110
And so he learned to
just wrap his arms

02:41:02.110 --> 02:41:04.550
and his heart around the whole
thing, and it just works.

02:41:04.550 --> 02:41:09.400
And so I do think everyone has
a certain place in a community

02:41:09.400 --> 02:41:10.240
or in a team.

02:41:10.240 --> 02:41:11.980
I think that as you're
describing this,

02:41:11.980 --> 02:41:15.580
I have to imagine that people
are listening and thinking,

02:41:15.580 --> 02:41:18.850
wow, this team thing is awesome.

02:41:18.850 --> 02:41:20.060
It's just amazing.

02:41:20.060 --> 02:41:21.490
I wish I had that.

02:41:21.490 --> 02:41:23.260
I'm fortunate to have
that in my podcast.

02:41:23.260 --> 02:41:24.310
I've had that in my lab.

02:41:24.310 --> 02:41:26.830
Certainly, in my
podcast team, I always

02:41:26.830 --> 02:41:30.260
say, these guys go, I go.

02:41:30.260 --> 02:41:33.140
It's not just people that press
buttons, and run equipment,

02:41:33.140 --> 02:41:33.800
take photos.

02:41:33.800 --> 02:41:34.790
They go, I go.

02:41:34.790 --> 02:41:35.750
They go, it's over.

02:41:35.750 --> 02:41:36.960
And I'm fine with that.

02:41:36.960 --> 02:41:39.200
I actually love that
because yes, it's

02:41:39.200 --> 02:41:41.390
about the podcast and
about the information

02:41:41.390 --> 02:41:43.820
and getting it out there, but
it's as much about the team

02:41:43.820 --> 02:41:46.020
and working together just like
it was with skateboarding.

02:41:46.020 --> 02:41:47.690
So hopefully, I'm
better at podcasting

02:41:47.690 --> 02:41:48.650
than I was at skateboarding.

02:41:48.650 --> 02:41:50.450
I kept getting broke off
as the skateboarders say

02:41:50.450 --> 02:41:51.575
in skateboarding too often.

02:41:51.575 --> 02:41:54.380
But I want to ask
in your family life,

02:41:54.380 --> 02:41:55.940
do you look at that as a team?

02:41:55.940 --> 02:41:59.030
Do you think this is my
team, and they're different

02:41:59.030 --> 02:42:00.320
and how can we synergize?

02:42:00.320 --> 02:42:01.370
JOCKO WILLINK:
Yeah, and you've got

02:42:01.370 --> 02:42:02.420
to look at every team like that.

02:42:02.420 --> 02:42:04.503
What are the strengths and
weaknesses of the team?

02:42:04.503 --> 02:42:06.110
And who's going to
be good at what?

02:42:06.110 --> 02:42:08.870
And how do you put people
in positions where they're

02:42:08.870 --> 02:42:10.550
going to be able to excel?

02:42:10.550 --> 02:42:12.180
I mean, what's his
name, Thiebaud?

02:42:12.180 --> 02:42:12.680
Is that--

02:42:12.680 --> 02:42:13.370
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Jim Thiebaud.

02:42:13.370 --> 02:42:14.360
JOCKO WILLINK: Jim Thiebaud?

02:42:14.360 --> 02:42:15.277
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

02:42:15.277 --> 02:42:18.410
JOCKO WILLINK: Imagine if he
had a mentor that was saying,

02:42:18.410 --> 02:42:20.690
listen, you got to
be a pro skater.

02:42:20.690 --> 02:42:22.110
This is your only opportunity.

02:42:22.110 --> 02:42:23.030
There's nothing else.

02:42:23.030 --> 02:42:25.700
And he didn't have the ability
to take a step back and say,

02:42:25.700 --> 02:42:29.690
you know what, man, I'm not
going to be good enough,

02:42:29.690 --> 02:42:32.960
but I really love this industry.

02:42:32.960 --> 02:42:36.440
So luckily for him
he figured that out.

02:42:36.440 --> 02:42:38.480
And you talked
about the superpower

02:42:38.480 --> 02:42:40.340
of being like loving your job.

02:42:40.340 --> 02:42:43.550
The one thing I claim to be
a superpower is the ability

02:42:43.550 --> 02:42:45.560
to take a step back and
detach, which I guess

02:42:45.560 --> 02:42:47.185
is going back to your
meditation thing.

02:42:47.185 --> 02:42:51.230
But being able to take a step
back and look at your life

02:42:51.230 --> 02:42:53.423
and say, man, I've
been skateboarding

02:42:53.423 --> 02:42:55.340
longer than that guy,
and he's better than me.

02:42:55.340 --> 02:42:57.632
And I've been skateboarding
longer than that other guy,

02:42:57.632 --> 02:42:59.245
and he's better than me.

02:42:59.245 --> 02:43:01.370
I'm probably not going to
be-- this probably is not

02:43:01.370 --> 02:43:02.640
the right job for me.

02:43:02.640 --> 02:43:05.810
What could I do where I
could use my skill set?

02:43:05.810 --> 02:43:08.320
And obviously, he had some
entrepreneurial spirit

02:43:08.320 --> 02:43:09.990
and was able to figure that out.

02:43:09.990 --> 02:43:12.860
So being able to be
a part of a team.

02:43:12.860 --> 02:43:15.560
And it goes to what I was
saying earlier about the mob.

02:43:15.560 --> 02:43:17.720
Being able to be part of
a team, part of the mob,

02:43:17.720 --> 02:43:19.880
part of the gang but
still have the ability

02:43:19.880 --> 02:43:21.650
to take a step back,
detach from that,

02:43:21.650 --> 02:43:26.730
and assess what is the best way
for this team to move forward.

02:43:26.730 --> 02:43:29.300
I mean, you could have
this brilliant idea

02:43:29.300 --> 02:43:33.170
that from now on you're going
to make all of your podcasts

02:43:33.170 --> 02:43:36.935
about the molecular
structure of whatever.

02:43:39.440 --> 02:43:41.000
And the rest of
the team probably

02:43:41.000 --> 02:43:42.500
need to pull you
aside and say, hey,

02:43:42.500 --> 02:43:45.320
man, I know you really care
about that, and that's awesome,

02:43:45.320 --> 02:43:49.710
but everyone really wants to
hear about this other stuff.

02:43:49.710 --> 02:43:51.600
So let's tie it in together.

02:43:51.600 --> 02:43:54.570
Let's expand what the specific
thing you want to talk about.

02:43:54.570 --> 02:43:56.930
So being able to take
a step back, detach,

02:43:56.930 --> 02:44:03.860
and see the bigger picture to me
is the true superpower of life.

02:44:03.860 --> 02:44:06.650
And it's a lot harder
than it sounds.

02:44:06.650 --> 02:44:09.770
And this goes back
to when you start

02:44:09.770 --> 02:44:13.850
talking about people that
are going through struggles

02:44:13.850 --> 02:44:14.990
in life.

02:44:14.990 --> 02:44:19.370
And I've described this before
as if I'm looking at you,

02:44:19.370 --> 02:44:22.040
and you're in a bad state.

02:44:22.040 --> 02:44:23.180
You're depressed.

02:44:23.180 --> 02:44:24.020
You're sad.

02:44:24.020 --> 02:44:25.100
You're moping around.

02:44:25.100 --> 02:44:27.820
You're not getting
anything done.

02:44:27.820 --> 02:44:29.770
And I'm looking at
you from the outside,

02:44:29.770 --> 02:44:33.100
and I'm thinking for me,
I see this storm cloud

02:44:33.100 --> 02:44:34.090
around your head.

02:44:34.090 --> 02:44:36.100
I see the storm cloud
around your head.

02:44:36.100 --> 02:44:41.140
And you're in there, and
all you see no matter what

02:44:41.140 --> 02:44:43.060
direction you look is storm.

02:44:43.060 --> 02:44:44.662
All you see is darkness.

02:44:44.662 --> 02:44:46.870
I'm outside and I'm looking,
oh, hey, man, this guy's

02:44:46.870 --> 02:44:47.860
got a great education.

02:44:47.860 --> 02:44:48.760
He's healthy.

02:44:48.760 --> 02:44:50.243
He's got a good team around him.

02:44:50.243 --> 02:44:51.910
He's got all these
things going for him.

02:44:51.910 --> 02:44:58.240
But you in that state, you
literally cannot see anything

02:44:58.240 --> 02:45:00.290
but the darkness of the storm.

02:45:00.290 --> 02:45:04.240
And that's what's so scary
about when people enter

02:45:04.240 --> 02:45:06.073
that mode is you can look--

02:45:06.073 --> 02:45:07.990
I can look at it from
the outside and be like,

02:45:07.990 --> 02:45:11.080
Andrew, you've just got to
move like four feet forward,

02:45:11.080 --> 02:45:12.850
and you're going to
be through this thing.

02:45:12.850 --> 02:45:15.880
And yet you might hear me say
that, and you go, no, Jock.

02:45:15.880 --> 02:45:18.340
I'm looking ahead, there's
nothing but darkness.

02:45:18.340 --> 02:45:22.330
So helping people move
forward, take action, and be

02:45:22.330 --> 02:45:24.610
able to get that
perspective, detach,

02:45:24.610 --> 02:45:28.167
and get outside themselves,
get outside their own heads.

02:45:28.167 --> 02:45:29.750
Tim Ferriss said get
out of your head.

02:45:29.750 --> 02:45:30.542
Get into your body.

02:45:30.542 --> 02:45:31.820
That's one way to do it.

02:45:31.820 --> 02:45:32.470
Take action.

02:45:32.470 --> 02:45:33.370
Go do things.

02:45:33.370 --> 02:45:35.313
But it's very scary.

02:45:35.313 --> 02:45:37.480
And I'm sure you've had
this experience where you're

02:45:37.480 --> 02:45:40.990
talking to someone that
you know, and they're

02:45:40.990 --> 02:45:43.420
bogged down in
whatever problem it is,

02:45:43.420 --> 02:45:45.880
whatever stress they're under.

02:45:45.880 --> 02:45:47.710
And you're looking
at them going, hey,

02:45:47.710 --> 02:45:49.900
man, it's going to be OK.

02:45:49.900 --> 02:45:53.110
You can clearly
see that whatever

02:45:53.110 --> 02:45:56.710
is bothering them and dragging
them, now, you can clearly see.

02:45:56.710 --> 02:45:58.690
A lot of times it's
a relationship.

02:45:58.690 --> 02:46:01.450
The girl or the guy
they got dumped,

02:46:01.450 --> 02:46:03.910
and you go, hey, man, that
person was a disaster anyways.

02:46:03.910 --> 02:46:05.500
You're better off without them.

02:46:05.500 --> 02:46:07.540
And they cannot compute that.

02:46:07.540 --> 02:46:08.870
They are stuck there.

02:46:08.870 --> 02:46:11.110
Or maybe it's the school
that they didn't get into,

02:46:11.110 --> 02:46:12.460
or the job that they didn't get.

02:46:12.460 --> 02:46:14.410
And they get so
wrapped up in that.

02:46:14.410 --> 02:46:17.080
They can't get
out of that storm.

02:46:17.080 --> 02:46:23.290
And it's such a helpless feeling
to sit there and tell someone,

02:46:23.290 --> 02:46:27.850
hey you just move a
little bit towards me,

02:46:27.850 --> 02:46:29.900
and you're going to
get out of this storm.

02:46:29.900 --> 02:46:34.060
And it's so much
easier said than done.

02:46:34.060 --> 02:46:35.947
And that's why trying
to engage with people

02:46:35.947 --> 02:46:38.530
and trying to give people that
super power of detachment where

02:46:38.530 --> 02:46:39.610
they can take a
step back and say,

02:46:39.610 --> 02:46:41.200
you know what,
you're right, man.

02:46:41.200 --> 02:46:44.950
That girl she wasn't who
I really thought she was.

02:46:44.950 --> 02:46:45.820
I should move on.

02:46:45.820 --> 02:46:46.750
Yes.

02:46:46.750 --> 02:46:50.093
But easier said than done.

02:46:50.093 --> 02:46:51.760
And that's one of the
biggest challenges

02:46:51.760 --> 02:46:55.360
I think that we have
as friends and parents

02:46:55.360 --> 02:47:02.140
and teammates is helping
people learn to detach,

02:47:02.140 --> 02:47:05.770
learn to see the bigger picture,
learn to see that the problem

02:47:05.770 --> 02:47:09.580
that you have that your
whole world is actually

02:47:09.580 --> 02:47:11.150
not that big of a deal.

02:47:11.150 --> 02:47:12.650
I've written a bunch
of kids' books.

02:47:12.650 --> 02:47:15.010
And one of the things that
triggered me to write kids

02:47:15.010 --> 02:47:20.260
books is realizing that-- one
day my daughter came home--

02:47:20.260 --> 02:47:23.680
it's my oldest daughter-- and
she came home from school,

02:47:23.680 --> 02:47:26.860
and she says, I'm stupid.

02:47:26.860 --> 02:47:28.270
What do you mean you're stupid?

02:47:28.270 --> 02:47:28.840
I'm stupid.

02:47:28.840 --> 02:47:29.393
I'm dumb.

02:47:29.393 --> 02:47:30.310
Why do you think that?

02:47:30.310 --> 02:47:31.900
You know whatever grade
it is when you're supposed

02:47:31.900 --> 02:47:33.280
to know your timetables?

02:47:33.280 --> 02:47:34.870
I don't know my timetables.

02:47:34.870 --> 02:47:38.590
I said, oh, well, how
much have you studied?

02:47:38.590 --> 02:47:41.290
She gave me the confused look.

02:47:41.290 --> 02:47:43.210
What do you mean studied?

02:47:43.210 --> 02:47:46.230
I said, have you studied
yet have you made flashcards

02:47:46.230 --> 02:47:48.030
to learn them?

02:47:48.030 --> 02:47:49.230
And she didn't.

02:47:49.230 --> 02:47:49.747
She hadn't.

02:47:49.747 --> 02:47:51.330
She thought she
should just know them.

02:47:51.330 --> 02:47:52.930
From the teacher went
over what they are,

02:47:52.930 --> 02:47:55.347
now she should know them like
some other kids in the class

02:47:55.347 --> 02:47:56.692
did.

02:47:56.692 --> 02:47:58.650
And so I'm sitting there
going, oh, yeah, cool.

02:47:58.650 --> 02:48:00.070
We'll make some flashcards.

02:48:00.070 --> 02:48:01.070
And she made flashcards.

02:48:01.070 --> 02:48:03.990
She learned her timetables in
45 minutes, and we were good.

02:48:03.990 --> 02:48:08.830
But what struck me was to me,
I was like, oh, no big deal.

02:48:08.830 --> 02:48:10.913
To her, it was her whole life.

02:48:10.913 --> 02:48:12.830
And then I got to see
that with my other kids.

02:48:12.830 --> 02:48:15.230
Somebody said something to
them in the recess yard.

02:48:15.230 --> 02:48:16.850
And I'm like, oh,
screw that kid.

02:48:16.850 --> 02:48:18.080
They don't know what--

02:48:18.080 --> 02:48:19.230
don't worry about them.

02:48:19.230 --> 02:48:24.430
But when you're-- that's
their whole world and that

02:48:24.430 --> 02:48:27.430
unfortunately doesn't
only apply to kids.

02:48:27.430 --> 02:48:29.260
It applies to adults as well.

02:48:29.260 --> 02:48:33.400
And they get this
problem in their world

02:48:33.400 --> 02:48:37.300
that seems so insurmountable
and so massive

02:48:37.300 --> 02:48:40.150
because that little ecosystem
that they're stuck in

02:48:40.150 --> 02:48:41.290
is their world.

02:48:41.290 --> 02:48:43.960
And they run into
this problem, and it's

02:48:43.960 --> 02:48:46.330
disruptive in that
world, and they don't

02:48:46.330 --> 02:48:48.403
know how to get out of it.

02:48:48.403 --> 02:48:50.320
I did a podcast talking
about these ecosystems

02:48:50.320 --> 02:48:51.195
that people get into.

02:48:51.195 --> 02:48:52.570
And there's all
these ecosystems.

02:48:52.570 --> 02:48:53.770
You're in an ecosystem.

02:48:53.770 --> 02:48:56.950
We're both in a shared
ecosystem of podcasting

02:48:56.950 --> 02:49:01.035
and we could be
like, oh, my gosh,

02:49:01.035 --> 02:49:03.240
Lex just came out
with a new podcast,

02:49:03.240 --> 02:49:05.280
and it's been the
biggest success.

02:49:05.280 --> 02:49:08.248
And what can I do to
catch up with Lex?

02:49:08.248 --> 02:49:10.290
And all of a sudden, I
could get really bothered,

02:49:10.290 --> 02:49:12.790
you could get really bothered,
we could be bothered by that.

02:49:12.790 --> 02:49:14.280
And think, man, I'm a failure.

02:49:14.280 --> 02:49:17.250
Meanwhile, there's people
that don't listen to podcasts.

02:49:17.250 --> 02:49:19.620
There's people that don't
even know what a podcast is.

02:49:19.620 --> 02:49:22.590
And yet it's our whole
world if we let it be.

02:49:22.590 --> 02:49:24.030
You're in the academic world.

02:49:24.030 --> 02:49:28.320
Hey, you go-- you're a
professor at Stanford, which

02:49:28.320 --> 02:49:32.170
is a big deal in that world.

02:49:32.170 --> 02:49:35.650
I know people that
don't give a rat's ass.

02:49:35.650 --> 02:49:37.270
They don't know
where Stanford is.

02:49:37.270 --> 02:49:38.170
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I
get that all the time.

02:49:38.170 --> 02:49:39.160
JOCKO WILLINK: They don't
know where Stanford is.

02:49:39.160 --> 02:49:39.910
It's no big deal.

02:49:39.910 --> 02:49:41.260
In the SEAL Teams, same thing.

02:49:41.260 --> 02:49:43.760
Somebody has a problem
in the SEAL Teams,

02:49:43.760 --> 02:49:47.410
and they think this is the
whole world, and I blew it.

02:49:47.410 --> 02:49:49.150
And now what are
they going to do?

02:49:49.150 --> 02:49:51.340
When you're facing a
significant problem

02:49:51.340 --> 02:49:54.460
in life, a relationship,
a problem with a job,

02:49:54.460 --> 02:49:56.800
you got to remember that
you're in one ecosystem,

02:49:56.800 --> 02:50:00.490
and if you step outside of that
ecosystem, no one really cares.

02:50:00.490 --> 02:50:02.740
And you could go move into
a whole totally different

02:50:02.740 --> 02:50:05.500
ecosystem and find
happiness there.

02:50:05.500 --> 02:50:09.220
But at least utilize that
to get out of that storm

02:50:09.220 --> 02:50:10.890
cloud that you're
in, and you're going

02:50:10.890 --> 02:50:13.390
to find that there's plenty of
light out there in the world.

02:50:13.390 --> 02:50:17.750
Move towards that and it's going
to be a much better situation.

02:50:17.750 --> 02:50:19.250
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Well, in the spirit

02:50:19.250 --> 02:50:21.680
of authenticity, everything
you're saying hits

02:50:21.680 --> 02:50:24.078
directly home for me.

02:50:24.078 --> 02:50:26.120
I don't know what people's
perceptions of me are.

02:50:26.120 --> 02:50:28.662
I actually try not to spend too
much time thinking about that

02:50:28.662 --> 02:50:31.340
and just really try and stay
in touch with the source.

02:50:31.340 --> 02:50:35.360
And I really do believe in this
notion that our love of things

02:50:35.360 --> 02:50:36.590
is what can generate energy.

02:50:36.590 --> 02:50:38.690
And I try and use action
to generate energy

02:50:38.690 --> 02:50:40.562
but also I happen to
also love exercise.

02:50:40.562 --> 02:50:42.020
So that's an easy
one there for me.

02:50:42.020 --> 02:50:44.190
But I try and stay in that mode.

02:50:44.190 --> 02:50:48.440
But to be quite honest, I've
spent much of my adult life

02:50:48.440 --> 02:50:53.570
and probably too much of
my teenage life and 20s--

02:50:53.570 --> 02:50:55.370
you're not quite an
adult in your 20s,

02:50:55.370 --> 02:50:57.200
at least I certainly wasn't--

02:50:57.200 --> 02:51:03.250
in challenging relationships
that admittedly

02:51:03.250 --> 02:51:07.390
were challenging because of my
role in them also, of course.

02:51:07.390 --> 02:51:10.420
And each and every
time, I remember

02:51:10.420 --> 02:51:14.110
thinking, moving on from this is
like this insurmountable thing.

02:51:14.110 --> 02:51:17.080
In part because I am a
caretaker, and I cared.

02:51:17.080 --> 02:51:19.447
And it wasn't just about
me and a selfishness,

02:51:19.447 --> 02:51:21.280
it was about wanting
to right all the wrongs

02:51:21.280 --> 02:51:23.170
of that person's past.

02:51:23.170 --> 02:51:26.050
I found myself trying to be
a time machine for people.

02:51:26.050 --> 02:51:28.330
I found myself trying to
fix their family lives.

02:51:28.330 --> 02:51:30.730
I found myself doing
all of that and also

02:51:30.730 --> 02:51:32.650
ignoring all the things
I need to focus on

02:51:32.650 --> 02:51:35.710
in terms of bettering myself
and making sure I was showing

02:51:35.710 --> 02:51:37.930
up correct and on and on.

02:51:37.930 --> 02:51:42.010
And there are data in the world
in the form of these people

02:51:42.010 --> 02:51:44.950
that know me very, very
well that I think would

02:51:44.950 --> 02:51:47.620
say that and a whole lot more.

02:51:47.620 --> 02:51:49.630
The point isn't those
specific relationships

02:51:49.630 --> 02:51:54.970
but each and every time someone
would come along and say,

02:51:54.970 --> 02:51:57.940
listen, if this isn't good for
you, it's not good for them,

02:51:57.940 --> 02:51:59.785
or this is a bad
situation, or this isn't

02:51:59.785 --> 02:52:00.910
serving either of you well.

02:52:00.910 --> 02:52:03.700
But I was myopic, this big.

02:52:03.700 --> 02:52:05.590
And not even soda
straw view of the world

02:52:05.590 --> 02:52:08.838
like sand speck of the
world and trying to solve

02:52:08.838 --> 02:52:09.880
because that's my nature.

02:52:09.880 --> 02:52:10.610
I'm going to solve this.

02:52:10.610 --> 02:52:11.180
I'm going to solve it.

02:52:11.180 --> 02:52:12.097
I'm going to solve it.

02:52:12.097 --> 02:52:14.690
And sometimes things were
solved for some period of time

02:52:14.690 --> 02:52:15.830
and sometimes they weren't.

02:52:15.830 --> 02:52:23.020
And I think one thing that just
as a confessional, I will say,

02:52:23.020 --> 02:52:27.810
I could really learn
the art of detachment.

02:52:27.810 --> 02:52:30.798
I could really learn
to focus on that more,

02:52:30.798 --> 02:52:32.340
if that's the proper
language for it.

02:52:32.340 --> 02:52:35.250
I think I'm pretty
good at adapting.

02:52:35.250 --> 02:52:37.570
I think I'm pretty good
at finding good people.

02:52:37.570 --> 02:52:40.500
I'm certainly love
my team, and that all

02:52:40.500 --> 02:52:42.210
feels like natural
synergy although it's

02:52:42.210 --> 02:52:44.310
hard work in lab
and in the podcast.

02:52:44.310 --> 02:52:49.350
But I think the tendency that
I have as a problem solver

02:52:49.350 --> 02:52:51.690
is to assume that every
problem can be solved,

02:52:51.690 --> 02:52:54.540
and therefore, staying on this
problem until it is solved

02:52:54.540 --> 02:52:55.740
is the answer.

02:52:55.740 --> 02:53:00.030
And maybe the art of detachment
and getting some perspective

02:53:00.030 --> 02:53:01.780
would help because
if I look back,

02:53:01.780 --> 02:53:04.200
I certainly don't regret the
experiences that I've had.

02:53:04.200 --> 02:53:06.420
But I wasted far too much time.

02:53:06.420 --> 02:53:08.370
And frankly, I probably
wasted far too much

02:53:08.370 --> 02:53:11.010
of other people's time
trying to solve problems

02:53:11.010 --> 02:53:12.850
that could not be solved.

02:53:12.850 --> 02:53:17.910
And I think without going
into this in any more detail,

02:53:17.910 --> 02:53:22.270
and of course, you can send me
a bill at the end by the way.

02:53:22.270 --> 02:53:22.770
I think--

02:53:22.770 --> 02:53:24.120
JOCKO WILLINK: Confessions
sessions are free, man

02:53:24.120 --> 02:53:24.330
ANDREW HUBERMAN: --yeah.

02:53:24.330 --> 02:53:26.700
So I think that being a
problem solver is great,

02:53:26.700 --> 02:53:28.170
being forward center
mass is great.

02:53:28.170 --> 02:53:30.600
I think learning the systems
of the brain and body

02:53:30.600 --> 02:53:34.170
and understanding psychology
and learning about oneself.

02:53:34.170 --> 02:53:37.230
The Oracle had it right,
know thyself in ways

02:53:37.230 --> 02:53:39.330
that you can
maneuver functionally

02:53:39.330 --> 02:53:41.700
in your life and career and
relationship, et cetera.

02:53:41.700 --> 02:53:42.200
Great.

02:53:42.200 --> 02:53:43.920
But I think there's
also a downside

02:53:43.920 --> 02:53:46.080
to being overly fixated.

02:53:46.080 --> 02:53:47.580
It's like my bulldog,
Costello, used

02:53:47.580 --> 02:53:49.170
to be like chewing on
something, chewing on something.

02:53:49.170 --> 02:53:51.445
Next thing I know he's
chewing on his foot.

02:53:51.445 --> 02:53:52.320
And you're like, hey.

02:53:52.320 --> 02:53:54.112
And you'd have to rip
him off his own foot.

02:53:54.112 --> 02:53:56.130
He'd be like--
[PANTING] --because that

02:53:56.130 --> 02:53:58.320
chew reflex was just so
strong that sometimes it

02:53:58.320 --> 02:53:59.630
would turn on himself.

02:53:59.630 --> 02:54:01.287
That's how it feels.

02:54:01.287 --> 02:54:03.870
JOCKO WILLINK: Yeah, I wrote a
book called Leadership Strategy

02:54:03.870 --> 02:54:04.380
and Tactics.

02:54:04.380 --> 02:54:06.720
And one of the things that
I wrote about in that book

02:54:06.720 --> 02:54:09.400
is understanding what's
important and what's not.

02:54:09.400 --> 02:54:12.840
And this is very similar to
what you're talking about.

02:54:12.840 --> 02:54:16.110
Looking at a problem
and taking a step back.

02:54:16.110 --> 02:54:19.560
And going, well, A, is
this important or not?

02:54:19.560 --> 02:54:21.910
And B, is this
solvable or is it not?

02:54:21.910 --> 02:54:24.090
And C, what's the ROI
on getting it solved?

02:54:24.090 --> 02:54:25.140
And what's the effort
it's going to take

02:54:25.140 --> 02:54:26.265
to get this problem solved?

02:54:26.265 --> 02:54:29.130
And how much is it really going
to impact my world and my life

02:54:29.130 --> 02:54:31.440
if I focus on it?

02:54:31.440 --> 02:54:34.440
So knowing and
understanding when

02:54:34.440 --> 02:54:38.070
something is important or
not is a very good skill.

02:54:38.070 --> 02:54:41.370
And again, it's a skill that's
directly related to detachment.

02:54:41.370 --> 02:54:43.770
Because when you're
in that relationship--

02:54:43.770 --> 02:54:46.236
this is another thing I've
been telling people lately--

02:54:46.236 --> 02:54:48.750
the solution to
your problem is not

02:54:48.750 --> 02:54:50.280
going to be found
in the problem.

02:54:50.280 --> 02:54:51.780
It's not going to
be found in there.

02:54:51.780 --> 02:54:53.500
You have to get
out of the problem

02:54:53.500 --> 02:54:55.950
so that you can look at
it, make an assessment.

02:54:55.950 --> 02:54:57.840
And you can assess how
to solve the problem

02:54:57.840 --> 02:55:00.040
or whether you need to
solve the problem or not.

02:55:00.040 --> 02:55:01.890
I mean, there's a lot
of things in my life

02:55:01.890 --> 02:55:05.170
right now where I shrug
my shoulders and go, OK.

02:55:05.170 --> 02:55:07.240
But-- It's OK.

02:55:07.240 --> 02:55:10.100
Oh, someone's saying this, OK.

02:55:10.100 --> 02:55:12.140
Roger that.

02:55:12.140 --> 02:55:13.340
Carry on.

02:55:13.340 --> 02:55:14.540
No factor.

02:55:14.540 --> 02:55:16.370
Move on.

02:55:16.370 --> 02:55:18.140
And then occasionally,
you go, OK, this

02:55:18.140 --> 02:55:19.190
is something I need
to contend with.

02:55:19.190 --> 02:55:20.300
This is something I
need to deal with this.

02:55:20.300 --> 02:55:23.030
This is something I need
to shape or adjust or move

02:55:23.030 --> 02:55:27.200
or solve, to use your word.

02:55:27.200 --> 02:55:32.470
The reason I laugh when I
say that is because problems

02:55:32.470 --> 02:55:34.070
you have to get in there.

02:55:34.070 --> 02:55:36.190
But if you take a step
back, you can usually

02:55:36.190 --> 02:55:37.750
say, oh, a little
adjustment here,

02:55:37.750 --> 02:55:39.458
a little adjustment
there, and that thing

02:55:39.458 --> 02:55:40.660
is going to sort itself out.

02:55:40.660 --> 02:55:44.297
So detachment is a superpower.

02:55:44.297 --> 02:55:46.380
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Certainly
is and it certainly one

02:55:46.380 --> 02:55:48.420
that I need to focus on more.

02:55:48.420 --> 02:55:52.590
I'm grateful for you
bringing that up.

02:55:52.590 --> 02:55:56.580
This is the biologist
in me, but what

02:55:56.580 --> 02:56:00.360
is your process for engaging
detachment or for disengaging?

02:56:00.360 --> 02:56:02.610
Is it an active
process, where you go,

02:56:02.610 --> 02:56:04.080
I'm going to detach from this.

02:56:04.080 --> 02:56:05.970
I'm going to put myself
in a situation that

02:56:05.970 --> 02:56:07.260
is pulling on me.

02:56:07.260 --> 02:56:08.700
There's this
gravitational force,

02:56:08.700 --> 02:56:11.790
and I'm going to create some
imagery in my mind of walking

02:56:11.790 --> 02:56:12.480
away from it.

02:56:12.480 --> 02:56:14.340
Do I physically
walk away from it?

02:56:14.340 --> 02:56:16.300
Do I outsource it
to somebody else?

02:56:16.300 --> 02:56:17.923
What are some tools
for detachment?

02:56:17.923 --> 02:56:20.340
JOCKO WILLINK: Yeah, this is
one of those situations where

02:56:20.340 --> 02:56:22.800
you and I had a discussion
about the science

02:56:22.800 --> 02:56:26.070
and the practical
application aligned.

02:56:26.070 --> 02:56:30.620
So my original experience
with detachment

02:56:30.620 --> 02:56:32.870
was-- and this is one
of those moments where

02:56:32.870 --> 02:56:35.600
I said a lot of times things are
just small moments over time,

02:56:35.600 --> 02:56:36.975
and you make a
little adjustment.

02:56:36.975 --> 02:56:39.493
This is one of those
moments in my life.

02:56:39.493 --> 02:56:41.660
And I wrote about in
Leadership Strategy and Tactics

02:56:41.660 --> 02:56:47.740
where I recognized like in
a moment what detachment was

02:56:47.740 --> 02:56:48.820
and how helpful it was.

02:56:48.820 --> 02:56:53.260
I'm on an oil rig doing
a training mission.

02:56:53.260 --> 02:56:56.380
My whole platoon is
in a skirmish line

02:56:56.380 --> 02:56:59.742
looking at a large
area of the oil rig

02:56:59.742 --> 02:57:01.200
that we're supposed
to be clearing.

02:57:01.200 --> 02:57:02.290
Again, this is not combat.

02:57:02.290 --> 02:57:03.082
This is in the 90s.

02:57:03.082 --> 02:57:04.123
There's nothing going on.

02:57:04.123 --> 02:57:05.230
We're just doing training.

02:57:05.230 --> 02:57:07.563
And I'm standing in
the skirmish line.

02:57:07.563 --> 02:57:09.730
And by the way, I'm the
youngest and most junior guy

02:57:09.730 --> 02:57:10.450
in my platoon.

02:57:10.450 --> 02:57:13.840
And I'm standing there looking
down the sight of my weapon,

02:57:13.840 --> 02:57:15.670
and I'm waiting for
someone to make a call

02:57:15.670 --> 02:57:17.710
and tell us what to do.

02:57:17.710 --> 02:57:20.290
And I wait for 5 seconds,
and I wait for 10 seconds,

02:57:20.290 --> 02:57:22.750
and I wait for 20 seconds,
and no one's saying anything.

02:57:22.750 --> 02:57:25.990
And we're waiting for
a leader in my platoon

02:57:25.990 --> 02:57:30.090
to make a call to tell us what
to do, to tell me what to do.

02:57:30.090 --> 02:57:33.870
And finally after 30 seconds
which seems like an eternity,

02:57:33.870 --> 02:57:35.550
I can't take it anymore.

02:57:35.550 --> 02:57:41.060
And so I take a step like a
foot, a 1-foot step, 12 inches.

02:57:41.060 --> 02:57:43.520
I take a step off
the skirmish line.

02:57:43.520 --> 02:57:44.528
I look to my left.

02:57:44.528 --> 02:57:45.320
I look to my right.

02:57:45.320 --> 02:57:47.960
And what I see is every
other guy in my platoon

02:57:47.960 --> 02:57:50.420
is staring down their
weapon, staring down

02:57:50.420 --> 02:57:54.050
the sight of the weapon, which
means their field of view

02:57:54.050 --> 02:57:54.990
is tiny.

02:57:54.990 --> 02:57:56.690
It's like a 20-degree
field of view.

02:57:56.690 --> 02:57:58.700
You're looking down the
scope of your weapon,

02:57:58.700 --> 02:58:00.500
or the sight of your
weapon, and that's

02:58:00.500 --> 02:58:01.480
how big their field of view is.

02:58:01.480 --> 02:58:02.897
And I'm looking,
and I'm thinking,

02:58:02.897 --> 02:58:04.490
oh, there's my
platoon commander.

02:58:04.490 --> 02:58:05.800
He's looking down the scope--

02:58:05.800 --> 02:58:06.800
the sight of his weapon.

02:58:06.800 --> 02:58:08.090
There's my platoon
chief, he's looking down

02:58:08.090 --> 02:58:08.660
the sight of his weapon.

02:58:08.660 --> 02:58:09.680
There's my leading
petty officer,

02:58:09.680 --> 02:58:10.880
he's looking down the
sight of his weapon.

02:58:10.880 --> 02:58:12.020
There's my assistant
platoon commander,

02:58:12.020 --> 02:58:14.030
he's looking down-- so
everyone in the platoon

02:58:14.030 --> 02:58:16.072
is looking down sight side
of their weapon, which

02:58:16.072 --> 02:58:21.408
means they all have a very
narrow field of vision.

02:58:21.408 --> 02:58:23.450
Well, when I take a step
back and look to my left

02:58:23.450 --> 02:58:25.520
and look to my right, guess what
kind of field of vision I got?

02:58:25.520 --> 02:58:26.600
I got a massive one.

02:58:26.600 --> 02:58:29.720
I can see the whole scene,
and I can see exactly what

02:58:29.720 --> 02:58:31.720
it is we need to do.

02:58:31.720 --> 02:58:33.312
And at that moment--

02:58:33.312 --> 02:58:35.520
look, as a new guy, you need
to keep your mouth shut.

02:58:35.520 --> 02:58:37.290
You don't say anything.

02:58:37.290 --> 02:58:40.380
And I'm thinking, well, but no
one else is saying anything.

02:58:40.380 --> 02:58:45.470
So I muster up all the courage
I can, and I open my mouth

02:58:45.470 --> 02:58:48.670
and I say, hold left,
clear right, which

02:58:48.670 --> 02:58:51.010
is a basic tactical call.

02:58:51.010 --> 02:58:54.235
No, this is not a
patent-level genius maneuver.

02:58:54.235 --> 02:58:56.860
It's just a normal call to make
in a situation that we were in.

02:58:56.860 --> 02:58:59.320
I say, hold left, clear right.

02:58:59.320 --> 02:59:00.880
And I'm expecting
to get slapped,

02:59:00.880 --> 02:59:02.420
told, shut up, new guy.

02:59:02.420 --> 02:59:03.880
But instead,
everyone just repeats

02:59:03.880 --> 02:59:06.255
the call, hold left, clear
right, hold left, clear right.

02:59:06.255 --> 02:59:08.260
And we execute the
maneuver, and we finish

02:59:08.260 --> 02:59:10.390
the clearance of this oil rig.

02:59:10.390 --> 02:59:12.220
And we get done,
we get to the top

02:59:12.220 --> 02:59:14.512
of the oil rig, which means
we cleared the whole thing.

02:59:14.512 --> 02:59:17.050
We're on the helo deck at the
top, and we go into a debrief.

02:59:17.050 --> 02:59:18.610
And now I'm expecting,
OK, now, I'm

02:59:18.610 --> 02:59:20.443
going to get told, hey,
what were you doing?

02:59:20.443 --> 02:59:22.210
You need to keep
your mouth shut.

02:59:22.210 --> 02:59:23.740
And instead, the
platoon chief goes,

02:59:23.740 --> 02:59:28.730
hey, Jocko, good call on
the cellar deck down there.

02:59:28.730 --> 02:59:30.732
And I was like,
yeah, that's right.

02:59:30.732 --> 02:59:32.690
But then I thought to
myself, hold on a second.

02:59:32.690 --> 02:59:36.740
Why if I'm the youngest most
junior guy in this platoon,

02:59:36.740 --> 02:59:38.780
why was I able to
see what we needed

02:59:38.780 --> 02:59:40.430
to do and make that call?

02:59:40.430 --> 02:59:42.510
Why did that just happen?

02:59:42.510 --> 02:59:46.250
And then I realized it was
because I took a step back.

02:59:46.250 --> 02:59:48.920
To use your term, I
broadened my field

02:59:48.920 --> 02:59:51.620
of view, which
allowed me to think

02:59:51.620 --> 02:59:54.830
more clearly because instead of
being hyperfocused and narrowly

02:59:54.830 --> 02:59:57.140
focused, I broadened
my range of vision.

02:59:57.140 --> 03:00:00.050
I took a breath
before I made my call.

03:00:00.050 --> 03:00:05.820
I had take a nice
breath to speak clearly.

03:00:05.820 --> 03:00:09.870
And I realized that taking
a step back and detaching,

03:00:09.870 --> 03:00:12.420
I got to see infinitely
more than even the most

03:00:12.420 --> 03:00:14.710
experienced guys in my platoon.

03:00:14.710 --> 03:00:16.960
And I started doing
it all the time.

03:00:16.960 --> 03:00:18.940
And I started doing
it in land warfare.

03:00:18.940 --> 03:00:20.530
I started doing it
in urban combat.

03:00:20.530 --> 03:00:23.310
I started doing it in all these
tactical training scenarios.

03:00:23.310 --> 03:00:24.310
These are just training.

03:00:24.310 --> 03:00:24.940
This is the '90s.

03:00:24.940 --> 03:00:26.648
I started doing these
training scenarios,

03:00:26.648 --> 03:00:33.310
and it always allowed me to
see what we needed to do.

03:00:33.310 --> 03:00:36.500
And then I started doing it
when I was having conversations

03:00:36.500 --> 03:00:37.000
with people.

03:00:37.000 --> 03:00:38.590
And having a conversation
with my platoon chief,

03:00:38.590 --> 03:00:39.940
and I can see that
he's starting to turn

03:00:39.940 --> 03:00:40.982
a little red in the face.

03:00:40.982 --> 03:00:43.027
And we're about to
argue about something.

03:00:43.027 --> 03:00:44.110
I said, oh, wait a second.

03:00:44.110 --> 03:00:46.735
I'm taking a step back look and
go, he's getting mad right now,

03:00:46.735 --> 03:00:48.100
and he's the platoon chief.

03:00:48.100 --> 03:00:51.315
You better just de-escalate
this thing real quick.

03:00:51.315 --> 03:00:53.690
And I'd say, hey, you know
what, chief, that sounds good.

03:00:53.690 --> 03:00:56.960
Let me go relook at
the plan or whatever.

03:00:56.960 --> 03:01:01.900
And so I started to do
this with my normal life.

03:01:01.900 --> 03:01:04.480
It was to not get wrapped
up in my own emotions.

03:01:04.480 --> 03:01:07.840
Not get wrapped up in the gun
fight that was happening right

03:01:07.840 --> 03:01:08.920
in front of my face.

03:01:08.920 --> 03:01:12.400
Not to get wrapped up in the
details of what was going on,

03:01:12.400 --> 03:01:17.560
but instead, take a step
back, detach, look around

03:01:17.560 --> 03:01:20.710
and then you can make a
much, much better decision.

03:01:20.710 --> 03:01:25.550
And it's not-- it's exponential.

03:01:25.550 --> 03:01:28.140
If you're looking down
the sights of your weapon,

03:01:28.140 --> 03:01:30.960
and you take a step back,
and you look around,

03:01:30.960 --> 03:01:33.450
it's exponential how
much more you can see.

03:01:33.450 --> 03:01:36.120
Now, listen, if you are the
only person in a gunfight,

03:01:36.120 --> 03:01:37.470
it's going to be harder for
you to do that because you've

03:01:37.470 --> 03:01:39.512
got to be focused on
whatever you're shooting at.

03:01:39.512 --> 03:01:42.105
But when you have 16 guys
or 20 guys they're all

03:01:42.105 --> 03:01:43.980
looking in the same
direction, it's very easy

03:01:43.980 --> 03:01:46.240
to be the guy that goes,
I'm going take a step back,

03:01:46.240 --> 03:01:48.970
look around, make a call.

03:01:48.970 --> 03:01:51.990
So when you talk
about the mechanics--

03:01:51.990 --> 03:01:54.300
when I teach this
to people now--

03:01:54.300 --> 03:01:58.580
the mechanics of it, take
a step back literally.

03:01:58.580 --> 03:02:00.442
You and I are at
a meeting, there's

03:02:00.442 --> 03:02:02.650
a bunch of people this starts
to get heated argument,

03:02:02.650 --> 03:02:06.040
I will literally push my chair
back away from the table.

03:02:06.040 --> 03:02:09.220
Change my perspective.

03:02:09.220 --> 03:02:13.490
Widen my field of view.

03:02:13.490 --> 03:02:17.210
The other thing in the SEAL
Teams you don't want to sound

03:02:17.210 --> 03:02:20.860
panicked on the radio
for a couple of reasons.

03:02:20.860 --> 03:02:22.860
Number one, because when
you panic on the radio,

03:02:22.860 --> 03:02:23.970
it's going to cause
other people to panic.

03:02:23.970 --> 03:02:26.100
Number two, if you panic
on the radio, and you sound

03:02:26.100 --> 03:02:28.050
panicked, everyone's
going to make fun of you.

03:02:28.050 --> 03:02:29.070
So when you get back
from the mission

03:02:29.070 --> 03:02:29.520
everyone is going to go--

03:02:29.520 --> 03:02:29.820
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Double whammy.

03:02:29.820 --> 03:02:30.090
JOCKO WILLINK: --yeah.

03:02:30.090 --> 03:02:31.980
You sounded like
a baby out there.

03:02:31.980 --> 03:02:36.400
So what would I do before
I would key up my radio,

03:02:36.400 --> 03:02:38.060
take a breath.

03:02:38.060 --> 03:02:41.800
And so here I'm manually
slowing down my breath.

03:02:41.800 --> 03:02:43.490
I'm broadening my field of view.

03:02:43.490 --> 03:02:46.750
So if you're in a
meeting, or you're in a--

03:02:46.750 --> 03:02:49.270
you're at the
supermarket parking lot,

03:02:49.270 --> 03:02:52.180
and someone starts
to yell at you,

03:02:52.180 --> 03:02:56.000
take a step back, take a breath,
broaden your field of view.

03:02:56.000 --> 03:02:57.980
Detach from those
emotions that you're

03:02:57.980 --> 03:03:01.120
having and make some space.

03:03:01.120 --> 03:03:04.900
And that's how I go through
the mechanics of detachment.

03:03:04.900 --> 03:03:07.700
Now, I can tell you
right now I mean,

03:03:07.700 --> 03:03:11.990
when you do this all the time,
which I do this all the time.

03:03:11.990 --> 03:03:13.838
Yeah, I don't really
have to step back.

03:03:13.838 --> 03:03:16.130
But when you're starting to
be able to try and do this,

03:03:16.130 --> 03:03:16.910
absolutely.

03:03:16.910 --> 03:03:18.710
Make-- And I'll tell
you here's another

03:03:18.710 --> 03:03:25.300
like weird little nuance
thing, lift your chin up,

03:03:25.300 --> 03:03:26.560
and put your hands down.

03:03:26.560 --> 03:03:29.140
Now, this is not in a combat
situation, not in a fight.

03:03:29.140 --> 03:03:33.803
But here's the thing,
when I get defensive

03:03:33.803 --> 03:03:34.720
what am I going to do?

03:03:34.720 --> 03:03:36.100
I'll raise my hands up
and put my chin down.

03:03:36.100 --> 03:03:37.370
That's like a fighting mode.

03:03:37.370 --> 03:03:39.100
So if you and I are
having a discussion,

03:03:39.100 --> 03:03:41.475
and I'm starting to get heated,
and I'm starting to like,

03:03:41.475 --> 03:03:42.730
oh, he's not listening to me.

03:03:42.730 --> 03:03:47.290
Instead of me putting my chin
down and put my hands like

03:03:47.290 --> 03:03:49.540
up to where I can put them
in your face a little bit,

03:03:49.540 --> 03:03:51.290
no, I'm actually going
to take a step back

03:03:51.290 --> 03:03:53.137
and say, put my chin up.

03:03:53.137 --> 03:03:54.970
It changes my perspective
a little bit more,

03:03:54.970 --> 03:03:57.550
changes my visual
perspective just by changing

03:03:57.550 --> 03:03:58.730
the angle of my head.

03:03:58.730 --> 03:04:00.338
Take a step back,
put my hands down.

03:04:00.338 --> 03:04:01.630
I'm not being in a defensible--

03:04:01.630 --> 03:04:04.520
I actually want to hear
what you have to say.

03:04:04.520 --> 03:04:07.000
And if I start listening
to what you have to say

03:04:07.000 --> 03:04:07.960
and not talking--

03:04:07.960 --> 03:04:10.420
it's very hard to be
detached when you're talking.

03:04:10.420 --> 03:04:12.010
It's another key component.

03:04:12.010 --> 03:04:14.530
You want to detach,
shut your mouth.

03:04:14.530 --> 03:04:18.465
So I'm in meetings in a
bunch of different companies.

03:04:18.465 --> 03:04:19.840
I'm running-- I
have a bunch of--

03:04:19.840 --> 03:04:21.382
I own a bunch of
different companies.

03:04:21.382 --> 03:04:22.825
I'm in a meeting
in my companies,

03:04:22.825 --> 03:04:24.700
I'm not the one that's
doing all the talking.

03:04:24.700 --> 03:04:26.410
In fact, I'm doing
mostly listening.

03:04:26.410 --> 03:04:29.410
When I'm in Task Unit
Bruiser, my task unit,

03:04:29.410 --> 03:04:31.875
I'm not sitting there
giving the entire brief.

03:04:31.875 --> 03:04:33.250
No, I'm letting
the platoon chief

03:04:33.250 --> 03:04:36.820
and the platoon commanders
give those briefs.

03:04:36.820 --> 03:04:37.960
And that way, I'm detached.

03:04:37.960 --> 03:04:39.790
I'm listening to what
they have to say.

03:04:39.790 --> 03:04:45.280
I'm more capable of seeing what
holes there are in their plans

03:04:45.280 --> 03:04:50.080
by not moving my mouth,
not talking, I'm listening.

03:04:50.080 --> 03:04:52.840
So those are some of the
methodologies that I use

03:04:52.840 --> 03:04:57.580
and that I advise people to use
in order to effectively start

03:04:57.580 --> 03:05:03.040
down the pathway of being able
to detach in various scenarios.

03:05:03.040 --> 03:05:04.720
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I love it.

03:05:04.720 --> 03:05:06.850
Again, I'm saying I love
it because I do love it.

03:05:06.850 --> 03:05:07.570
And thank you.

03:05:07.570 --> 03:05:09.070
I think it's a
wonderful technique.

03:05:09.070 --> 03:05:10.660
We've talked before
on your podcast

03:05:10.660 --> 03:05:13.960
and some of my listeners,
maybe not all to this podcast

03:05:13.960 --> 03:05:16.330
will be familiar with the
fact that when we narrowly

03:05:16.330 --> 03:05:19.450
focus our gaze on one target,
a number of things happen.

03:05:19.450 --> 03:05:21.850
Our visual world becomes
constricted, of course,

03:05:21.850 --> 03:05:24.670
but also that we start
slicing time more finely.

03:05:24.670 --> 03:05:26.740
And the dopamine
system tends to start

03:05:26.740 --> 03:05:30.700
doing anticipation and trying
to guide and direct things

03:05:30.700 --> 03:05:34.065
in that narrow tunnel of view,
whereas when we literally

03:05:34.065 --> 03:05:36.190
take on panoramic vision,
so not necessarily moving

03:05:36.190 --> 03:05:37.732
our head around,
although, one could,

03:05:37.732 --> 03:05:40.150
broadening our field of view
or looking at a horizon,

03:05:40.150 --> 03:05:41.350
especially if we're walking.

03:05:41.350 --> 03:05:42.640
But getting that kind of--

03:05:42.640 --> 03:05:44.830
not kind of getting a
broader field of view,

03:05:44.830 --> 03:05:47.290
we slice time differently.

03:05:47.290 --> 03:05:50.320
Things don't feel
as imposing on us.

03:05:50.320 --> 03:05:52.900
This is the physiological
substrate underlying

03:05:52.900 --> 03:05:54.170
what you're describing.

03:05:54.170 --> 03:05:56.860
And I think it
goes a step further

03:05:56.860 --> 03:06:01.030
because in that
open, larger aperture

03:06:01.030 --> 03:06:04.240
of visual understanding,
there's an open larger window

03:06:04.240 --> 03:06:08.380
of cognitive understanding, and
new options start to surface.

03:06:08.380 --> 03:06:09.545
I mean, I think this is--

03:06:09.545 --> 03:06:11.170
I have long been
fascinated by the fact

03:06:11.170 --> 03:06:12.295
that this actually became--

03:06:12.295 --> 03:06:13.690
I'll tell this real quick story.

03:06:13.690 --> 03:06:18.700
In 2015, I went over to Spain
to do some mountaineering

03:06:18.700 --> 03:06:19.840
with Wim Hof.

03:06:19.840 --> 03:06:21.710
And this wasn't because
I wanted a podcast.

03:06:21.710 --> 03:06:22.570
I didn't have any social media.

03:06:22.570 --> 03:06:23.410
I just went over
there because I'd

03:06:23.410 --> 03:06:25.960
heard this guy, Wim, contacted
him, and somehow arranged

03:06:25.960 --> 03:06:26.710
a trip for myself.

03:06:26.710 --> 03:06:29.470
And I went over there, and
we did some crazy dangerous

03:06:29.470 --> 03:06:31.870
mountaineering that I
had no business doing.

03:06:31.870 --> 03:06:35.500
Almost ripped my left
leg off in a stunt

03:06:35.500 --> 03:06:37.580
that was organized
by others there

03:06:37.580 --> 03:06:38.830
that I never should have done.

03:06:38.830 --> 03:06:42.250
In any case, one
day I look and Wim

03:06:42.250 --> 03:06:43.810
is like crouched
on the ground next

03:06:43.810 --> 03:06:46.810
to like a curb in this
parking lot before our hike.

03:06:46.810 --> 03:06:49.090
And he's down there on the
ground with a little stick.

03:06:49.090 --> 03:06:50.570
And I was like,
what are you doing?

03:06:50.570 --> 03:06:51.610
He's like, look.

03:06:51.610 --> 03:06:53.630
And there were ants
climbing up this thing.

03:06:53.630 --> 03:06:58.430
And he's like, there
mountaineering this curb.

03:06:58.430 --> 03:07:02.510
And I thought, this
guy's different.

03:07:02.510 --> 03:07:05.360
But then I realized we were
about to do the same thing

03:07:05.360 --> 03:07:06.530
up this big face.

03:07:06.530 --> 03:07:10.250
And I thought, wow, he's able to
think at these different scales

03:07:10.250 --> 03:07:11.240
and see similarities.

03:07:11.240 --> 03:07:11.840
That's pretty cool.

03:07:11.840 --> 03:07:13.548
I would have never
stopped to look there,

03:07:13.548 --> 03:07:14.990
and I still remember it.

03:07:14.990 --> 03:07:16.085
And was it profound?

03:07:16.085 --> 03:07:16.585
No.

03:07:16.585 --> 03:07:18.410
But was it interesting?
yeah, in the sense

03:07:18.410 --> 03:07:21.770
that things are happening
at all scales all the time.

03:07:21.770 --> 03:07:25.410
And we think we know the
scale to pay attention to,

03:07:25.410 --> 03:07:27.570
and we think that that's
the one that matters most.

03:07:27.570 --> 03:07:29.570
And I think it's fair to
say that in a gunfight,

03:07:29.570 --> 03:07:31.100
there is a scale
that matters most

03:07:31.100 --> 03:07:34.370
but new options, new
perspectives actually come

03:07:34.370 --> 03:07:36.077
from that broader
field of view, which

03:07:36.077 --> 03:07:37.160
is what you're describing.

03:07:37.160 --> 03:07:39.680
And later that day-- it's
interesting because this group

03:07:39.680 --> 03:07:44.120
going up, some of them were
really challenged in the climb.

03:07:44.120 --> 03:07:47.250
And Wim went back
to this example

03:07:47.250 --> 03:07:49.402
of how the ants would stack
on top of one another.

03:07:49.402 --> 03:07:51.360
And he used an analogy
from that to help people

03:07:51.360 --> 03:07:52.160
through this climb.

03:07:52.160 --> 03:07:53.460
And there was a beautiful
pool at the top,

03:07:53.460 --> 03:07:54.335
et cetera, et cetera.

03:07:54.335 --> 03:07:56.760
Anyway, I think
that these examples

03:07:56.760 --> 03:07:58.920
are in fact meaningful,
especially the ones

03:07:58.920 --> 03:08:01.600
that you gave because they
don't just relate to military.

03:08:01.600 --> 03:08:03.600
I mean, you can imagine
around the dinner table.

03:08:03.600 --> 03:08:04.470
I've had this.

03:08:04.470 --> 03:08:06.270
Kids are there and
partners there.

03:08:06.270 --> 03:08:08.670
And sometimes it's really
nice to sit back and just

03:08:08.670 --> 03:08:11.100
hear it all and bask in it all.

03:08:11.100 --> 03:08:14.520
But oftentimes, new
information will surface.

03:08:14.520 --> 03:08:17.717
Like you said, all I'm hearing
is worries out of this person.

03:08:17.717 --> 03:08:19.050
Or they're not even really here.

03:08:19.050 --> 03:08:20.550
They're all talking
about what we're

03:08:20.550 --> 03:08:22.620
going to do next time,
next time, next time.

03:08:22.620 --> 03:08:25.380
And you can reanchor people
like, hey, look, maybe

03:08:25.380 --> 03:08:27.090
let's focus on what
we're doing here.

03:08:27.090 --> 03:08:28.710
Or sometimes people
are hyperfocused

03:08:28.710 --> 03:08:29.640
on what's happening
there, and they

03:08:29.640 --> 03:08:31.290
need to think about
something in the future.

03:08:31.290 --> 03:08:31.827
I love this.

03:08:31.827 --> 03:08:34.410
I need to practice detachment
in a number of different domains

03:08:34.410 --> 03:08:35.640
in my life.

03:08:35.640 --> 03:08:38.880
One thing that I am realizing
after hearing you describe

03:08:38.880 --> 03:08:40.500
the process that I
really need to do

03:08:40.500 --> 03:08:46.400
is I need to start taking
some time away from my work.

03:08:46.400 --> 03:08:50.060
Maybe even a little bit of
time parallel to relationship

03:08:50.060 --> 03:08:52.040
to get better perspective on it.

03:08:52.040 --> 03:08:54.770
Because I think the problem
solving nature in us

03:08:54.770 --> 03:08:57.650
really makes us myopic,
really makes us nearsighted.

03:08:57.650 --> 03:08:59.320
JOCKO WILLINK: Yeah that's--

03:08:59.320 --> 03:09:00.750
you see this a lot.

03:09:00.750 --> 03:09:02.430
I mean, I get to see this a lot.

03:09:02.430 --> 03:09:05.600
We do events with
companies, businesses.

03:09:05.600 --> 03:09:11.470
And we go offsite somewhere
where they're detached.

03:09:11.470 --> 03:09:14.110
They're just they're
defacto detached

03:09:14.110 --> 03:09:16.095
from their day-to-day business.

03:09:16.095 --> 03:09:18.220
You pull someone out of
their business for two days

03:09:18.220 --> 03:09:20.650
and all of a sudden, they
start seeing the solutions.

03:09:20.650 --> 03:09:24.250
They never see them like it's a
lot harder for them to see them

03:09:24.250 --> 03:09:25.972
when they're in the firefight.

03:09:25.972 --> 03:09:28.180
You get them out of the
firefight, it's hard for them

03:09:28.180 --> 03:09:31.165
to see it when they're in that
acquisition that they're doing.

03:09:31.165 --> 03:09:33.040
How are we going to
merge these two cultures?

03:09:33.040 --> 03:09:34.332
This is going to be impossible.

03:09:34.332 --> 03:09:35.500
Boom, pull them out.

03:09:35.500 --> 03:09:37.390
All right, let's talk
about the two cultures.

03:09:37.390 --> 03:09:39.970
And let's talk about what
possible outcomes are.

03:09:39.970 --> 03:09:43.450
And all of a sudden, when you
get them to take a step back,

03:09:43.450 --> 03:09:45.250
the solutions will appear.

03:09:45.250 --> 03:09:47.030
And it is true in a gunfight.

03:09:47.030 --> 03:09:50.260
Now, listen, if it's a
one-on-one gunfight, even then

03:09:50.260 --> 03:09:55.000
the ability to take a step
back and look around or use

03:09:55.000 --> 03:09:58.002
your peripheral vision, you
have to be able to do this.

03:09:58.002 --> 03:09:58.960
It's going to increase.

03:09:58.960 --> 03:10:01.690
It's exponential
how much it-- that's

03:10:01.690 --> 03:10:02.960
why it's like a superpower.

03:10:02.960 --> 03:10:03.970
It's like cheating.

03:10:03.970 --> 03:10:05.440
It's like cheating.

03:10:05.440 --> 03:10:08.110
I was speaking of Seth Stone.

03:10:08.110 --> 03:10:10.910
Seth Stone took over--

03:10:10.910 --> 03:10:13.400
he became a task unit
commander, troop commander

03:10:13.400 --> 03:10:15.043
when we got back from Ramadi.

03:10:15.043 --> 03:10:16.460
And so now he's
the guy in charge,

03:10:16.460 --> 03:10:18.320
and now I was
running the training.

03:10:18.320 --> 03:10:24.350
And a couple of months into his
training, he broke his neck.

03:10:24.350 --> 03:10:26.430
Broke his vertebrae in his neck.

03:10:26.430 --> 03:10:30.320
His spinal cord was OK, so he
was the guy with the big neck

03:10:30.320 --> 03:10:33.080
brace on, and he couldn't
do any arduous training.

03:10:33.080 --> 03:10:36.920
And his SEAL task unit,
which is two platoons,

03:10:36.920 --> 03:10:40.010
was going through their
land warfare training.

03:10:40.010 --> 03:10:41.072
And he couldn't do it.

03:10:41.072 --> 03:10:43.530
He couldn't carry a rucksack,
couldn't carry a machine gun,

03:10:43.530 --> 03:10:44.508
so he couldn't do it.

03:10:44.508 --> 03:10:45.800
So I said, hey, I'm coming out.

03:10:45.800 --> 03:10:48.080
Let's go out, and you
can observe your guys,

03:10:48.080 --> 03:10:49.890
and see how they're doing.

03:10:49.890 --> 03:10:50.960
And so there we are.

03:10:50.960 --> 03:10:52.130
We're out in the desert.

03:10:52.130 --> 03:10:54.800
His troop is going through
field training exercise,

03:10:54.800 --> 03:10:56.150
our full mission profile.

03:10:56.150 --> 03:10:57.590
So it's like a big
fake operation,

03:10:57.590 --> 03:10:59.465
there's a fake target,
there's fake bad guys.

03:10:59.465 --> 03:11:02.720
We're using these high
speed laser guns to shoot.

03:11:02.720 --> 03:11:07.310
And we're standing
on this little berm.

03:11:07.310 --> 03:11:10.220
And at a certain point
in the operation,

03:11:10.220 --> 03:11:13.310
his whole task
unit, like 40 guys,

03:11:13.310 --> 03:11:15.830
gets pinned down in
this little ravine.

03:11:15.830 --> 03:11:18.290
And so we're standing in
the ravine with these guys,

03:11:18.290 --> 03:11:20.840
and no one's making
any decisions.

03:11:20.840 --> 03:11:24.710
And the enemy with these laser
guns are starting to maneuver

03:11:24.710 --> 03:11:26.510
and starting to--
they're killing guys

03:11:26.510 --> 03:11:29.390
because these laser
guns you can die.

03:11:29.390 --> 03:11:32.052
And Seth he hits me on the arm.

03:11:32.052 --> 03:11:33.510
He said, can I tell
him what to do?

03:11:33.510 --> 03:11:34.460
And I was like, no.

03:11:34.460 --> 03:11:35.630
Let them figure it out.

03:11:35.630 --> 03:11:38.930
So another 30 seconds
pass, another guy

03:11:38.930 --> 03:11:40.910
gets killed with laser.

03:11:40.910 --> 03:11:43.640
He hits me again, bro, let
me say something to them.

03:11:43.640 --> 03:11:46.070
And I was like, no,
let them figure it out.

03:11:46.070 --> 03:11:49.880
Another minute goes by,
two more guys are now dead.

03:11:49.880 --> 03:11:52.460
Just laser dead
but they're dead.

03:11:52.460 --> 03:11:55.220
And he hits me again, and he's
like, bro, let me tell them.

03:11:55.220 --> 03:11:57.380
And I go, all right,
go ahead and tell them.

03:11:57.380 --> 03:11:59.870
And so he just crouches down
next to one of the guys,

03:11:59.870 --> 03:12:03.510
and bangs him on the
shoulder, goes, peel right.

03:12:03.510 --> 03:12:06.660
And which is again, it's just
a fundamental basic call.

03:12:06.660 --> 03:12:08.520
And the guy shouts
it out, peel right.

03:12:08.520 --> 03:12:09.360
And they start
peeling peeling right.

03:12:09.360 --> 03:12:11.568
And another minute later,
they're all out of the kill

03:12:11.568 --> 03:12:13.560
zone and everything's OK.

03:12:13.560 --> 03:12:16.890
And then Seth looks at
me, and he goes, man, this

03:12:16.890 --> 03:12:20.770
is so easy way up here.

03:12:20.770 --> 03:12:23.905
And I said, bro,
look at where we are.

03:12:23.905 --> 03:12:27.430
We are in the ravine
with the guys.

03:12:27.430 --> 03:12:30.070
Now, we're on a knee and
the guys are laying down,

03:12:30.070 --> 03:12:31.600
but it's not this--

03:12:31.600 --> 03:12:35.090
we weren't on some
elevated position.

03:12:35.090 --> 03:12:38.470
And I said, hey, it's not that
we're in an elevated position.

03:12:38.470 --> 03:12:40.900
It's just that we're
detached and looking around.

03:12:40.900 --> 03:12:41.980
And he goes, oh, my God.

03:12:41.980 --> 03:12:43.540
And I said, hey, you
remember when you and I went

03:12:43.540 --> 03:12:44.380
through this training?

03:12:44.380 --> 03:12:45.130
And he goes, yeah.

03:12:45.130 --> 03:12:48.080
And I go, this is what it
was like for me all the time.

03:12:48.080 --> 03:12:50.540
I was constantly
just looking around,

03:12:50.540 --> 03:12:53.410
so that's why it seems
like a magic power, right?

03:12:53.410 --> 03:12:57.100
It's like a superpower because
Seth's down there with his gun,

03:12:57.100 --> 03:12:57.850
and he's shooting.

03:12:57.850 --> 03:13:00.262
And I'm like, hey, bro, move
your guys over to that--

03:13:00.262 --> 03:13:02.470
go to that ridgeline right
there, set security, boom.

03:13:02.470 --> 03:13:05.290
And how did Jocko see that?

03:13:05.290 --> 03:13:06.700
Jocko must be a tactical genius.

03:13:06.700 --> 03:13:08.330
No, I'm not a tactical genius.

03:13:08.330 --> 03:13:11.470
I'm just taking a step
back and looking around.

03:13:11.470 --> 03:13:13.570
And like you just said,
it applies to everything

03:13:13.570 --> 03:13:14.320
that we do.

03:13:14.320 --> 03:13:17.800
If you're having a conversation
with your significant other,

03:13:17.800 --> 03:13:21.190
and you start to see that
they're getting frustrated

03:13:21.190 --> 03:13:23.110
about something,
now, look, if you're

03:13:23.110 --> 03:13:27.135
in the conversation 100%, you're
going to get frustrated too.

03:13:27.135 --> 03:13:29.260
You're going to get
frustrated, they're frustrated.

03:13:29.260 --> 03:13:33.050
Next thing you know you've got
an emotional argument going on,

03:13:33.050 --> 03:13:35.980
whereas if you take a mental
step back and say, wait,

03:13:35.980 --> 03:13:38.320
why are they
frustrated right now?

03:13:38.320 --> 03:13:41.780
Oh, because I'm trying to solve
their problem when really what

03:13:41.780 --> 03:13:43.030
they're looking to do is vent.

03:13:43.030 --> 03:13:44.050
OK, got it.

03:13:44.050 --> 03:13:44.800
Let them vent.

03:13:44.800 --> 03:13:45.460
OK, cool.

03:13:45.460 --> 03:13:47.198
Oh, that sounds horrible.

03:13:47.198 --> 03:13:48.740
What do you think
you're going to do?

03:13:48.740 --> 03:13:49.900
Instead of saying, well,
if that's the problem,

03:13:49.900 --> 03:13:51.970
here's what you should do
because people aren't always

03:13:51.970 --> 03:13:52.730
looking for that.

03:13:52.730 --> 03:13:54.640
So yeah, this
thing, this ability

03:13:54.640 --> 03:13:57.417
is-- and it's something that
can absolutely be trained,

03:13:57.417 --> 03:13:58.750
and that's what's cool about it.

03:13:58.750 --> 03:14:00.730
It can absolutely be trained.

03:14:00.730 --> 03:14:02.260
It's not a natural gift.

03:14:02.260 --> 03:14:05.380
It's some people we're be
better at it than others,

03:14:05.380 --> 03:14:07.330
but it's something
that you can train.

03:14:07.330 --> 03:14:08.930
And I used to see
guys develop it.

03:14:08.930 --> 03:14:11.560
And I see people develop it
now in the business world where

03:14:11.560 --> 03:14:15.490
they'll report back to me, we
had a meeting with the union

03:14:15.490 --> 03:14:17.860
today, and the union
started escalating

03:14:17.860 --> 03:14:19.040
what they wanted to do.

03:14:19.040 --> 03:14:19.990
And I just took--

03:14:19.990 --> 03:14:22.667
I detached, and we
ended up de-escalating,

03:14:22.667 --> 03:14:23.750
and now we got a solution.

03:14:23.750 --> 03:14:27.280
So this is an absolute skill
set that can be learned,

03:14:27.280 --> 03:14:31.600
and that's what makes
it especially nice.

03:14:31.600 --> 03:14:33.220
Because there's
some people-- look,

03:14:33.220 --> 03:14:37.840
if you're very articulate,
born very-- some people

03:14:37.840 --> 03:14:40.040
are born more articulate
than other people.

03:14:40.040 --> 03:14:43.390
Some people are
born with an ability

03:14:43.390 --> 03:14:45.340
to simplify things
more than others.

03:14:45.340 --> 03:14:46.090
And you can train.

03:14:46.090 --> 03:14:47.500
You can become more articulate.

03:14:47.500 --> 03:14:51.300
You can become-- you can
learn to simplify things more.

03:14:51.300 --> 03:14:53.800
And some people are going to
be naturally good at detaching,

03:14:53.800 --> 03:14:56.050
but everyone can
get better at it,

03:14:56.050 --> 03:14:57.258
and that's a beautiful thing.

03:14:57.258 --> 03:14:58.925
ANDREW HUBERMAN: It's
a beautiful thing,

03:14:58.925 --> 03:15:00.573
and I'm highly
incentivized to do this.

03:15:00.573 --> 03:15:01.990
I mean, there are
areas of my life

03:15:01.990 --> 03:15:04.960
that are going really well that
I also want to apply it there.

03:15:04.960 --> 03:15:08.110
I think that I've tended to
rely on people close to me

03:15:08.110 --> 03:15:11.440
as a way to access
this detachment.

03:15:11.440 --> 03:15:14.830
I will be very direct
in saying that I am not

03:15:14.830 --> 03:15:16.360
the leader of my podcast.

03:15:16.360 --> 03:15:18.980
There is a leader, it is not me.

03:15:18.980 --> 03:15:21.680
I'm part of the team,
but it is not me.

03:15:21.680 --> 03:15:25.060
And I often rely on his input.

03:15:25.060 --> 03:15:27.340
And sometimes that
input is solicited

03:15:27.340 --> 03:15:28.930
and sometimes it's not.

03:15:28.930 --> 03:15:31.960
One place for instance where
you see people getting really

03:15:31.960 --> 03:15:35.920
myopic is on social media.

03:15:35.920 --> 03:15:38.410
And I've experienced this.

03:15:38.410 --> 03:15:41.140
I'd love to say that
I'm always nonreactive.

03:15:41.140 --> 03:15:45.110
And I think in general I take
the stance that I have filters.

03:15:45.110 --> 03:15:47.530
So I have filters.

03:15:47.530 --> 03:15:49.015
I know why I'm there.

03:15:49.015 --> 03:15:50.890
I'm interested in being
a teacher and a giver

03:15:50.890 --> 03:15:54.490
and informing people about the
beauty and utility of biology.

03:15:54.490 --> 03:15:57.350
That's why I say, it's not a
mission statement, it's a fact.

03:15:57.350 --> 03:15:58.600
That's what I care about.

03:15:58.600 --> 03:16:01.085
Anything that doesn't
fit through that filter,

03:16:01.085 --> 03:16:02.710
I don't really have
any business doing.

03:16:02.710 --> 03:16:04.918
But occasionally, I like to
make a joke or something.

03:16:04.918 --> 03:16:06.760
But occasionally
something comes through.

03:16:06.760 --> 03:16:08.770
And I find myself
saying, wait a second.

03:16:08.770 --> 03:16:11.350
And you get sucked
into that tunnel.

03:16:11.350 --> 03:16:14.163
And sometimes it's observing
other people in tunnels.

03:16:14.163 --> 03:16:15.580
And when you're
not in the tunnel,

03:16:15.580 --> 03:16:17.920
it's so obvious
what's happening.

03:16:17.920 --> 03:16:20.123
You're watching, in some
cases, people just dragging

03:16:20.123 --> 03:16:22.540
their lives, and some cases,
sinking their entire careers.

03:16:22.540 --> 03:16:24.580
I mean, the former
chair of Psychiatry

03:16:24.580 --> 03:16:26.560
at Columbia
University in New York

03:16:26.560 --> 03:16:32.140
made an absolutely foolish,
truly insensitive, totally

03:16:32.140 --> 03:16:33.850
inappropriate tweet.

03:16:33.850 --> 03:16:34.940
That's my opinion.

03:16:34.940 --> 03:16:36.940
And I think it was the
opinion of all the people

03:16:36.940 --> 03:16:38.107
that fired him from his job.

03:16:38.107 --> 03:16:40.660
This person was at the
apex of his career.

03:16:40.660 --> 03:16:44.380
Lost his job for saying
something terrible.

03:16:44.380 --> 03:16:46.715
And in retrospect,
was said something

03:16:46.715 --> 03:16:48.340
like, I don't know
what I was thinking.

03:16:48.340 --> 03:16:51.520
And so this guy
is a psychiatrist,

03:16:51.520 --> 03:16:54.640
so he lives in the
study and the treatment

03:16:54.640 --> 03:16:57.430
of the mind, which just goes
to show that everyone I think

03:16:57.430 --> 03:17:00.310
is susceptible to being
pulled into these tunnels.

03:17:00.310 --> 03:17:03.790
And fortunately, everyone
is susceptible to learning

03:17:03.790 --> 03:17:06.770
to teach themselves how to
latch themselves out of it.

03:17:06.770 --> 03:17:08.733
So I love this idea
of a teachable skill.

03:17:08.733 --> 03:17:10.150
I'm certainly going
to practice it

03:17:10.150 --> 03:17:13.180
in one on one and
in group situations

03:17:13.180 --> 03:17:14.980
and in a variety of situations.

03:17:14.980 --> 03:17:19.217
I think that the tunnel
has a gravitational pull.

03:17:19.217 --> 03:17:20.800
There's like an
allure to that tunnel.

03:17:20.800 --> 03:17:23.197
And I always just go right
back to the neurochemistry.

03:17:23.197 --> 03:17:25.030
I think that there's
something about solving

03:17:25.030 --> 03:17:28.510
a problem inside of a tunnel
like an animal on a chase.

03:17:28.510 --> 03:17:32.650
But at some point, that
animal could get picked off,

03:17:32.650 --> 03:17:34.150
run over by a truck
because it was--

03:17:34.150 --> 03:17:36.070
didn't have enough
situational awareness.

03:17:36.070 --> 03:17:40.300
I'm definitely going to practice
this through opening the gaze

03:17:40.300 --> 03:17:41.650
and broadening gaze.

03:17:41.650 --> 03:17:45.190
And I think I also I'm due for
a couple of days off from things

03:17:45.190 --> 03:17:47.020
to just walk and
think about work.

03:17:47.020 --> 03:17:49.770
On these retreats, do
people work on work

03:17:49.770 --> 03:17:51.520
or are they just there
to do other things,

03:17:51.520 --> 03:17:53.135
and that's where
the ideas surface?

03:17:53.135 --> 03:17:55.010
JOCKO WILLINK: We'll do
a little bit of both.

03:17:55.010 --> 03:17:57.640
So we'll do some stuff
that is focused on work,

03:17:57.640 --> 03:17:59.320
but then we'll pull
out and do things

03:17:59.320 --> 03:18:03.280
that are completely unrelated
to work for that very reason.

03:18:03.280 --> 03:18:05.690
Whether we do
something physical,

03:18:05.690 --> 03:18:07.960
whether we do some
mental exercise,

03:18:07.960 --> 03:18:09.460
but we do things
that are completely

03:18:09.460 --> 03:18:12.310
unrelated to their
work and take those

03:18:12.310 --> 03:18:17.200
breaks in order for
them to free their mind.

03:18:17.200 --> 03:18:19.450
And you know what bothers
you about social media?

03:18:19.450 --> 03:18:24.850
This is when you say there's
some things that make you mad--

03:18:24.850 --> 03:18:27.100
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I mean,
not me, but it could be.

03:18:27.100 --> 03:18:29.350
Of course, I'm human.

03:18:29.350 --> 03:18:30.850
Little things
prevent-- and it's not

03:18:30.850 --> 03:18:31.660
the things that are obvious.

03:18:31.660 --> 03:18:33.400
It's actually not
direct critique of me.

03:18:33.400 --> 03:18:36.670
It's when people exploit
misunderstandings

03:18:36.670 --> 03:18:39.640
to try and create a greater
misunderstanding that

03:18:39.640 --> 03:18:40.407
doesn't exist.

03:18:40.407 --> 03:18:42.490
That's what gets me because
to a scientist, that's

03:18:42.490 --> 03:18:44.110
like the most irritating thing.

03:18:44.110 --> 03:18:46.510
I don't know what the analogy
would be in the SEAL Teams,

03:18:46.510 --> 03:18:49.030
but it's someone
hijacking something--

03:18:49.030 --> 03:18:52.900
They didn't mean that but then
they distort the argument.

03:18:52.900 --> 03:18:55.210
The word gaslighting gets
thrown around a lot now.

03:18:55.210 --> 03:18:56.920
A lot of people actually
think that anytime someone

03:18:56.920 --> 03:18:57.850
states a boundary.

03:18:57.850 --> 03:18:59.860
No, I don't believe
that's gaslighting.

03:18:59.860 --> 03:19:02.020
Trust me, the psychiatrist, who
are all professionally trained,

03:19:02.020 --> 03:19:03.370
tell me that is not gaslighting.

03:19:03.370 --> 03:19:04.953
Gaslighting is a
very particular thing

03:19:04.953 --> 03:19:08.140
where you're trying to alter
someone's reality in a very

03:19:08.140 --> 03:19:11.410
active almost like
sociopathic way.

03:19:11.410 --> 03:19:13.810
So I that's a little
editorializing right there.

03:19:13.810 --> 03:19:19.060
What bothers me is
when people hijack--

03:19:19.060 --> 03:19:21.380
sometimes someone
in an argument isn't

03:19:21.380 --> 03:19:23.630
as sophisticated with their
language as somebody else.

03:19:23.630 --> 03:19:25.870
So someone will hijack
that lack of sophistication

03:19:25.870 --> 03:19:27.400
and try and flip
them on their back.

03:19:27.400 --> 03:19:29.740
That sort of thing
really gets under my skin

03:19:29.740 --> 03:19:34.322
because I feel that creates
an unnecessary divide.

03:19:34.322 --> 03:19:35.530
There are a few other things.

03:19:35.530 --> 03:19:37.030
But I always joke in my lab.

03:19:37.030 --> 03:19:38.960
And I'll say, I have
3,000 pet peeves,

03:19:38.960 --> 03:19:40.900
but I also have
3,000 flaws to match

03:19:40.900 --> 03:19:42.430
each one of those pet peeves.

03:19:42.430 --> 03:19:45.487
JOCKO WILLINK: So
yeah, whenever--

03:19:45.487 --> 03:19:47.320
This is not just social
media, but it's just

03:19:47.320 --> 03:19:52.150
life when somebody says
something about me or to me

03:19:52.150 --> 03:19:54.130
that I don't like.

03:19:54.130 --> 03:19:56.043
What I realized years
ago is the reason

03:19:56.043 --> 03:19:57.460
I don't like it
is because there's

03:19:57.460 --> 03:19:58.877
some truth in what
they're saying.

03:19:58.877 --> 03:20:02.710
And the best thing to do is
to say either to yourself

03:20:02.710 --> 03:20:04.810
if you're by
yourself or to them,

03:20:04.810 --> 03:20:07.990
is to say, yeah, you're right.

03:20:07.990 --> 03:20:10.420
I am a knucklehead sometimes.

03:20:10.420 --> 03:20:12.730
Or yeah, you're right.

03:20:12.730 --> 03:20:14.590
I sometimes do jump
to conclusions.

03:20:14.590 --> 03:20:15.940
Or yeah, you're right.

03:20:15.940 --> 03:20:18.250
I was completely
wrong about that.

03:20:18.250 --> 03:20:23.170
And that is just so
much more liberating

03:20:23.170 --> 03:20:26.448
and healthy than
saying, you don't

03:20:26.448 --> 03:20:27.740
know what you're talking about.

03:20:27.740 --> 03:20:29.030
Or no, I don't--

03:20:29.030 --> 03:20:31.190
just going into
that defensive mode

03:20:31.190 --> 03:20:34.040
and trying to close your mind
instead of opening your mind up

03:20:34.040 --> 03:20:36.230
to listen to what somebody
else has to say, and say,

03:20:36.230 --> 03:20:38.140
yeah, that's a good point.

03:20:38.140 --> 03:20:40.910
You're spot on with that one.

03:20:40.910 --> 03:20:43.758
Next question, next
comment, let's go.

03:20:43.758 --> 03:20:45.800
ANDREW HUBERMAN: One thing
I appreciate about you

03:20:45.800 --> 03:20:48.830
on social media is
the limited number

03:20:48.830 --> 03:20:50.930
of words in each
of your responses.

03:20:50.930 --> 03:20:54.830
It's a great thing forcing you
to be efficient and concise

03:20:54.830 --> 03:20:56.700
actually is a huge advantage.

03:20:56.700 --> 03:21:01.460
It also forces you to be
precise at least about category.

03:21:01.460 --> 03:21:03.447
I think there's something
to be said for that.

03:21:03.447 --> 03:21:05.280
JOCKO WILLINK: Well,
there's a good example.

03:21:05.280 --> 03:21:07.940
So I was on Twitter
the other day

03:21:07.940 --> 03:21:13.015
since Twitter is getting a
lot of traction right now.

03:21:13.015 --> 03:21:14.390
There's a lot of
mayhem going on.

03:21:14.390 --> 03:21:15.590
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah,
the other gun fight.

03:21:15.590 --> 03:21:16.880
JOCKO WILLINK: And
somebody asked me,

03:21:16.880 --> 03:21:18.920
somebody said, hey, I'm
going to boot camp soon.

03:21:18.920 --> 03:21:20.430
What advice do you have for me?

03:21:20.430 --> 03:21:21.810
And I wrote back, enjoy.

03:21:21.810 --> 03:21:22.310
Boom.

03:21:22.310 --> 03:21:23.977
And like you said, I
mean, it's Twitter.

03:21:23.977 --> 03:21:25.730
I'm responding to
a bunch of people.

03:21:25.730 --> 03:21:29.255
And then somebody else
chimed in and said,

03:21:29.255 --> 03:21:32.330
you might as well not
even answer, Jocko

03:21:32.330 --> 03:21:34.380
that's not helping
this guy at all.

03:21:34.380 --> 03:21:35.972
And look at your face.

03:21:35.972 --> 03:21:37.430
For those of you
that are watching,

03:21:37.430 --> 03:21:39.222
your face just got a
little bit mad, right?

03:21:39.222 --> 03:21:40.878
You got a little bit
defensive for me.

03:21:40.878 --> 03:21:43.170
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah, well,
I think, that's my nature.

03:21:43.170 --> 03:21:45.430
I don't like seeing
other people attacked.

03:21:45.430 --> 03:21:46.680
I think that's just my nature.

03:21:46.680 --> 03:21:48.080
JOCKO WILLINK: I had
a split second off

03:21:48.080 --> 03:21:49.220
who the hell is this guy?

03:21:49.220 --> 03:21:51.170
And then I said, you
know what, he's right.

03:21:51.170 --> 03:21:53.660
And then I tweeted again back.

03:21:53.660 --> 03:21:54.560
I said, sorry, man.

03:21:54.560 --> 03:21:55.400
You're right.

03:21:55.400 --> 03:21:58.860
What I should have
said was, hey,

03:21:58.860 --> 03:22:01.690
read the book Leadership
Strategy and Tactics.

03:22:01.690 --> 03:22:03.570
It's a good book
for someone that

03:22:03.570 --> 03:22:06.197
is going to be in an environment
that's going to be challenging

03:22:06.197 --> 03:22:08.280
and where you're going to
be faced with leadership

03:22:08.280 --> 03:22:09.240
challenges.

03:22:09.240 --> 03:22:15.720
And enjoy boot camp because
if your mindset is this sucks

03:22:15.720 --> 03:22:19.112
and this is terrible,
it's going to be terrible,

03:22:19.112 --> 03:22:20.070
and it's going to suck.

03:22:20.070 --> 03:22:23.740
And if you go with a mindset of
hey, this is a cool experience,

03:22:23.740 --> 03:22:25.860
and I should enjoy
it, you're going

03:22:25.860 --> 03:22:27.210
to have a much better time.

03:22:27.210 --> 03:22:28.290
That's my full answer.

03:22:28.290 --> 03:22:31.170
I'm sorry I didn't give you--
and it's perfectly fine.

03:22:31.170 --> 03:22:33.480
That guy was right
to critique me.

03:22:33.480 --> 03:22:35.522
And he was right in saying that.

03:22:35.522 --> 03:22:36.480
And there was a bunch--

03:22:36.480 --> 03:22:38.430
what's funny is a bunch of
other people came to my defense

03:22:38.430 --> 03:22:39.660
and said, this guy--

03:22:39.660 --> 03:22:41.430
that's really great.

03:22:41.430 --> 03:22:43.890
But my point is instead of me
getting defensive and crazy

03:22:43.890 --> 03:22:47.190
and letting it drive me crazy,
open my mind a little bit,

03:22:47.190 --> 03:22:50.040
listen to what they have to say,
accept that there's got to be

03:22:50.040 --> 03:22:51.130
some level of truth in it.

03:22:51.130 --> 03:22:52.140
And there was.

03:22:52.140 --> 03:22:55.710
I gave a guy a very,
very terse response,

03:22:55.710 --> 03:22:57.690
and I could have
expanded on it more.

03:22:57.690 --> 03:22:58.440
And I did.

03:22:58.440 --> 03:23:00.440
No big deal.

03:23:00.440 --> 03:23:01.092
Good times.

03:23:01.092 --> 03:23:02.050
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Right.

03:23:02.050 --> 03:23:04.010
A few minutes ago, I
was thinking to myself,

03:23:04.010 --> 03:23:06.490
I wonder where your mind
is at in the few moments

03:23:06.490 --> 03:23:07.720
before you fall asleep.

03:23:07.720 --> 03:23:12.860
Are you able to make yourself
go mind blank pretty easily?

03:23:12.860 --> 03:23:15.048
It's something I've been
practicing more because I

03:23:15.048 --> 03:23:16.090
tend to, again ruminate--

03:23:16.090 --> 03:23:17.890
I like to drill into
problems, obviously.

03:23:17.890 --> 03:23:20.380
Yesterday I did a solo episode,
which when we do those,

03:23:20.380 --> 03:23:24.010
they are usually anywhere
from five to 20 hours of prep.

03:23:24.010 --> 03:23:27.640
And then the recordings, I
won't say how long they take.

03:23:27.640 --> 03:23:29.380
But I love going
into the tunnel.

03:23:29.380 --> 03:23:32.290
The tunnel is that's
where the juice is for me.

03:23:32.290 --> 03:23:33.865
And finding the structure.

03:23:33.865 --> 03:23:35.740
And I have the benefit
of an amazing producer

03:23:35.740 --> 03:23:37.070
who helps me sort through it.

03:23:37.070 --> 03:23:40.120
And I came out of this
thing, and then went home,

03:23:40.120 --> 03:23:41.093
couldn't eat.

03:23:41.093 --> 03:23:42.760
Because I was like,
I don't want dinner.

03:23:42.760 --> 03:23:44.620
And then I was
explaining a call I had

03:23:44.620 --> 03:23:46.810
with a colleague the other day.

03:23:46.810 --> 03:23:51.070
My partner-- my girlfriend
she was just like, OK.

03:23:51.070 --> 03:23:51.920
I'm going to sleep.

03:23:51.920 --> 03:23:54.970
And I was up and texting and
thinking and writing notes out.

03:23:54.970 --> 03:23:57.010
And I thought, oh,
man, I'm like Costello

03:23:57.010 --> 03:23:59.650
like chewing on the stick
and chewing into my paw.

03:23:59.650 --> 03:24:00.250
I need sleep.

03:24:00.250 --> 03:24:01.220
I need to go to sleep.

03:24:01.220 --> 03:24:04.870
So I think I have a bit of
a harder time disengaging,

03:24:04.870 --> 03:24:06.380
clearly.

03:24:06.380 --> 03:24:09.370
And this is why I never
touch cocaine or amphetamine

03:24:09.370 --> 03:24:11.680
because I think
that some of us have

03:24:11.680 --> 03:24:14.440
a love of the dopamine
circuitry that--

03:24:14.440 --> 03:24:17.530
I always sensed if I were to
have tried drugs like that,

03:24:17.530 --> 03:24:23.630
they might have been
the thing that would hit

03:24:23.630 --> 03:24:25.220
my neural circuits just right.

03:24:25.220 --> 03:24:26.510
Some people talk about
alcohol that way.

03:24:26.510 --> 03:24:28.302
I've read books by
alcoholics like the book

03:24:28.302 --> 03:24:29.810
Dry and few other--

03:24:29.810 --> 03:24:31.980
Rich Roll talks about
this, that he drank alcohol

03:24:31.980 --> 03:24:33.230
for the first time in college.

03:24:33.230 --> 03:24:34.850
It was like this
elixir that filled

03:24:34.850 --> 03:24:37.860
his body that made him feel
right for the first time.

03:24:37.860 --> 03:24:39.860
That is not how I feel
after a couple of drinks.

03:24:39.860 --> 03:24:43.070
I feel a little relaxed,
but I can do without it.

03:24:43.070 --> 03:24:47.600
But this drilling into
something is really that's my--

03:24:47.600 --> 03:24:49.040
I guess, maybe that's my--

03:24:49.040 --> 03:24:51.830
I don't think it's a superpower,
but I have some strength there.

03:24:51.830 --> 03:24:54.455
But I think it's also the thing
that can cut on the other side.

03:24:54.455 --> 03:24:56.163
JOCKO WILLINK: Yeah,
when you started off

03:24:56.163 --> 03:24:57.740
this conversation
early you talked

03:24:57.740 --> 03:25:00.020
about getting up in the
morning, and you just get up,

03:25:00.020 --> 03:25:00.853
and you have these--

03:25:00.853 --> 03:25:04.220
I was about to go down
the path of it's--

03:25:04.220 --> 03:25:06.290
at nighttime when I'm
trying to go to sleep

03:25:06.290 --> 03:25:09.740
and I have some random
thought about some thing, that

03:25:09.740 --> 03:25:10.880
can be a hard--

03:25:10.880 --> 03:25:14.270
I have a visual thing
that's going on.

03:25:14.270 --> 03:25:16.040
It seems like I'm
on a roller coaster,

03:25:16.040 --> 03:25:18.410
and I'm going in like
a new idea comes,

03:25:18.410 --> 03:25:21.170
and I'm just on
this awesome ride,

03:25:21.170 --> 03:25:22.430
and it's not going to stop.

03:25:22.430 --> 03:25:25.020
And I can't-- I don't
know how to stop that.

03:25:25.020 --> 03:25:26.020
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Oh, no.

03:25:26.020 --> 03:25:29.850
I mean, I think it goes with
people who are very driven

03:25:29.850 --> 03:25:32.440
and like to master
different crafts.

03:25:32.440 --> 03:25:34.440
I have a colleague, his
name is Karl Deisseroth.

03:25:34.440 --> 03:25:35.232
He's a bioengineer.

03:25:35.232 --> 03:25:38.830
He has five children,
and he's a psychiatrist.

03:25:38.830 --> 03:25:41.520
He's like an incredible-- one
of these people that does a ton.

03:25:41.520 --> 03:25:43.270
Very likely will
win the Nobel Prize.

03:25:43.270 --> 03:25:45.270
I mean, he's an amazing
scientists, amazing guy.

03:25:45.270 --> 03:25:47.082
And he does this
practice that he does,

03:25:47.082 --> 03:25:48.540
which is not a
meditation, which he

03:25:48.540 --> 03:25:51.450
sits for an hour late at
night after his kids have gone

03:25:51.450 --> 03:25:54.210
to sleep, and he
forces himself, forces

03:25:54.210 --> 03:25:56.190
himself to think in
complete sentences

03:25:56.190 --> 03:25:58.920
with punctuation
about some problem.

03:25:58.920 --> 03:26:02.970
I tried doing that for about
five minutes, and I fell off.

03:26:02.970 --> 03:26:05.200
But it's something that
he's cultivated in himself,

03:26:05.200 --> 03:26:06.505
which helps him in his career.

03:26:06.505 --> 03:26:08.880
I don't think-- it's certainly
not something I recommend.

03:26:08.880 --> 03:26:11.430
If I did that at 1:00
or 2:00 in the morning,

03:26:11.430 --> 03:26:13.093
I think that would
be bad for my sleep.

03:26:13.093 --> 03:26:14.760
Because falling asleep
actually requires

03:26:14.760 --> 03:26:16.840
drifting into these
kind of liminal states.

03:26:16.840 --> 03:26:19.215
It's one of the reasons why
I'm a big proponent of things

03:26:19.215 --> 03:26:22.050
like nonsleep deep rest, or
these yoga nidra practices,

03:26:22.050 --> 03:26:23.610
which are basically
body scans where

03:26:23.610 --> 03:26:27.750
you're trying to learn to
detach from the sensory world.

03:26:27.750 --> 03:26:29.393
And they're very effective at--

03:26:29.393 --> 03:26:30.810
certainly for me
they've been very

03:26:30.810 --> 03:26:32.880
effective at teaching
me to turn off

03:26:32.880 --> 03:26:34.950
thinking, which is
an interesting notion

03:26:34.950 --> 03:26:35.800
in its own right.

03:26:35.800 --> 03:26:38.010
But I like the
idea of detachment

03:26:38.010 --> 03:26:39.990
by stepping back and
getting perspective.

03:26:39.990 --> 03:26:42.150
My father is much
better at that.

03:26:42.150 --> 03:26:44.995
He's a very calm guy in
the world of confrontation.

03:26:44.995 --> 03:26:47.370
I always knew it when I was
a kid because he would blink,

03:26:47.370 --> 03:26:48.840
which meant like something
was going to happen

03:26:48.840 --> 03:26:50.220
like I was going to get it.

03:26:50.220 --> 03:26:54.195
And now-- but I've always
known that he can control

03:26:54.195 --> 03:26:56.700
his responses, his behavior.

03:26:56.700 --> 03:26:59.550
Others of us in the family you
know beside from New Jersey,

03:26:59.550 --> 03:27:01.080
we're more like
go to loggerheads.

03:27:01.080 --> 03:27:02.202
So I don't know.

03:27:02.202 --> 03:27:03.660
This raises a
question, and I think

03:27:03.660 --> 03:27:07.020
it's one that I and several
other people I talked to

03:27:07.020 --> 03:27:09.210
in anticipation of this
podcast were asking.

03:27:09.210 --> 03:27:10.710
I think one reason
why people are

03:27:10.710 --> 03:27:14.320
drawn to people who have
been in the SEAL Teams,

03:27:14.320 --> 03:27:19.710
and you in particular, are that
I think everybody, not just

03:27:19.710 --> 03:27:21.760
males but females too.

03:27:21.760 --> 03:27:27.060
I think everybody wants to know
like their calibration point

03:27:27.060 --> 03:27:29.640
on their level of toughness.

03:27:29.640 --> 03:27:30.717
I think people wonder.

03:27:30.717 --> 03:27:32.550
I think when people
talk about BUD/S and all

03:27:32.550 --> 03:27:34.092
that, I think a lot
of people wonder,

03:27:34.092 --> 03:27:35.235
would I make it through?

03:27:35.235 --> 03:27:36.360
I've certainly wondered it.

03:27:36.360 --> 03:27:37.800
I haven't spent hours on it.

03:27:37.800 --> 03:27:38.740
I went my path.

03:27:38.740 --> 03:27:41.160
I'm happy for the path I went.

03:27:41.160 --> 03:27:44.190
But I think people wonder,
do I have this thing

03:27:44.190 --> 03:27:46.260
that supposedly
BUD/S selects for?

03:27:46.260 --> 03:27:49.965
And if I don't, how tough
am I or not tough am I?

03:27:49.965 --> 03:27:54.015
I think that we all can look
at other people physically--

03:27:54.015 --> 03:27:55.890
and I'm not somebody
that does a lot of this.

03:27:55.890 --> 03:27:58.057
I know some people are
really obsessed by this like,

03:27:58.057 --> 03:28:01.230
oh, that person has an eight
pack with veins on their leg.

03:28:01.230 --> 03:28:02.640
I don't understand that.

03:28:02.640 --> 03:28:04.860
That's not me, but I
understand some people do that

03:28:04.860 --> 03:28:06.940
even to the point of pathology.

03:28:06.940 --> 03:28:11.760
But I think most people
wonder, how resilient am I?

03:28:11.760 --> 03:28:14.040
And they can look to experiences
that they've survived

03:28:14.040 --> 03:28:17.400
and say, oh, I made it through,
or I'm resilient or not.

03:28:17.400 --> 03:28:20.460
But is there a way that we can--

03:28:20.460 --> 03:28:22.890
certainly that we can train
it by doing hard things?

03:28:22.890 --> 03:28:26.440
Cold showers, this kind of thing
are small examples of those.

03:28:26.440 --> 03:28:31.270
But do you think it's even
an important question to ask?

03:28:31.270 --> 03:28:36.310
And if it is, how does
one go about thinking,

03:28:36.310 --> 03:28:37.900
how resilient am I?

03:28:37.900 --> 03:28:41.410
Should we put ourselves into
situations of discomfort just

03:28:41.410 --> 03:28:42.250
to test that?

03:28:42.250 --> 03:28:44.110
Because I will say, I
think a lot of people

03:28:44.110 --> 03:28:49.840
look to SEAL Teams and team guys
in particular as a calibration

03:28:49.840 --> 03:28:52.880
point of like, OK, they
know how to do hard things.

03:28:52.880 --> 03:28:54.490
They were selected
for the ability

03:28:54.490 --> 03:28:57.130
to carry logs, and get into
cold water over and over,

03:28:57.130 --> 03:29:00.367
and roll in the sand, and go
without sleep for a week or so.

03:29:00.367 --> 03:29:02.200
But that's probably not
what they were doing

03:29:02.200 --> 03:29:03.408
when they were on deployment.

03:29:03.408 --> 03:29:07.450
It's clearly a pressure
test for something else.

03:29:07.450 --> 03:29:10.090
JOCKO WILLINK: Yeah, it's
a strange, strange thing.

03:29:10.090 --> 03:29:12.820
The Basic Underwater
Demolition SEAL training.

03:29:12.820 --> 03:29:19.080
And quite frankly, going
and getting wet and cold

03:29:19.080 --> 03:29:27.790
and being miserable is
actually nothing compared

03:29:27.790 --> 03:29:31.480
to being on deployment.

03:29:31.480 --> 03:29:34.690
And a good example that
I use to compare this to

03:29:34.690 --> 03:29:41.515
is, when I was on deployment
in 2006 in Ramadi, there was--

03:29:41.515 --> 03:29:48.520
as you were driving off base
to go into the city and conduct

03:29:48.520 --> 03:29:53.740
operations, as you drove out
of the compound, on your right

03:29:53.740 --> 03:29:58.870
was this area that was
called the vehicle graveyard.

03:29:58.870 --> 03:30:01.970
And the vehicle graveyard was
exactly what it sounds like.

03:30:01.970 --> 03:30:06.920
It was probably
75 or 100 vehicles

03:30:06.920 --> 03:30:12.140
that were blown up,
destroyed, burned,

03:30:12.140 --> 03:30:17.510
in various twisted conditions
that had been dragged back

03:30:17.510 --> 03:30:19.730
through the city and put
into this vehicle graveyard.

03:30:19.730 --> 03:30:22.250
And as you drove by
that vehicle graveyard,

03:30:22.250 --> 03:30:25.010
you know without
question that every one

03:30:25.010 --> 03:30:32.910
of those twisted vehicles
represented one, two, three,

03:30:32.910 --> 03:30:41.850
four, five, American casualties
horribly wounded, killed.

03:30:41.850 --> 03:30:46.630
And there you are in a
vehicle about to roll out

03:30:46.630 --> 03:30:50.980
into that city where
what you're looking at

03:30:50.980 --> 03:30:54.340
can easily be you in
the next three minutes.

03:30:54.340 --> 03:30:56.325
And you're going
to do that today.

03:30:56.325 --> 03:30:57.700
You're going to
do that tomorrow.

03:30:57.700 --> 03:31:00.325
You can do that the next day and
the day after that and the day

03:31:00.325 --> 03:31:02.632
after that.

03:31:02.632 --> 03:31:04.890
That compared to--
and by the way,

03:31:04.890 --> 03:31:06.640
this isn't just seals
that are doing that.

03:31:06.640 --> 03:31:09.250
This is Marines, this is the
army guys that are over there.

03:31:09.250 --> 03:31:11.870
This is what everyone is doing.

03:31:11.870 --> 03:31:14.370
And they do it.

03:31:14.370 --> 03:31:16.950
They do it.

03:31:16.950 --> 03:31:19.350
I talk about Marc Lee
who was one of my guys.

03:31:19.350 --> 03:31:20.560
First CO killed in Iraq.

03:31:20.560 --> 03:31:27.340
And he was the lead turret
gunner in the lead Humvee.

03:31:27.340 --> 03:31:32.040
And in Vietnam, if you were
the point man in Vietnam,

03:31:32.040 --> 03:31:34.017
if you're in infantry
patrol, you're

03:31:34.017 --> 03:31:35.850
the point man in Vietnam,
you were at risk--

03:31:35.850 --> 03:31:37.650
booby traps, ambush.

03:31:37.650 --> 03:31:39.750
So they rotated you out.

03:31:39.750 --> 03:31:41.820
You didn't have to stay
up there all the time.

03:31:41.820 --> 03:31:46.740
You do an hour up as point man,
they get someone else up there.

03:31:46.740 --> 03:31:50.155
And that guy, the
lead turret gunner

03:31:50.155 --> 03:31:54.790
in a Humvee column of four,
or five, or six vehicles,

03:31:54.790 --> 03:31:58.510
if you hit an IED, that's the
vehicle that's going to hit it.

03:31:58.510 --> 03:32:00.250
If you go into an
ambush, that's the guy

03:32:00.250 --> 03:32:01.292
that's going to get hits.

03:32:01.292 --> 03:32:06.280
The guy that's standing up in
a 50 caliber turret, that's

03:32:06.280 --> 03:32:08.590
the guy that's going to die.

03:32:08.590 --> 03:32:14.410
And Marc-- he was
a new guy, so he's

03:32:14.410 --> 03:32:16.630
in that lead turret, 50 Cal.

03:32:16.630 --> 03:32:22.590
And he never asked
to get rotated out.

03:32:22.590 --> 03:32:25.010
And I remember he was a very--

03:32:25.010 --> 03:32:26.190
I like to say very.

03:32:26.190 --> 03:32:35.130
He was extremely charismatic,
funny, gregarious comedian.

03:32:35.130 --> 03:32:38.130
And we got all kinds
of stories about Marc.

03:32:38.130 --> 03:32:40.920
But one of them, we were in
Vegas, and we're all gambling.

03:32:40.920 --> 03:32:44.160
And I come down
from my hotel room,

03:32:44.160 --> 03:32:47.850
and I see Marc across the--
he's playing blackjack.

03:32:47.850 --> 03:32:49.380
When he sees me,
he goes, hey, sir,

03:32:49.380 --> 03:32:51.330
when are the new
Cadillacs coming out.

03:32:51.330 --> 03:32:54.930
Like he's just lighting
up everybody, just having

03:32:54.930 --> 03:32:56.110
a fantastic time.

03:32:56.110 --> 03:32:58.650
But I remember one night he's
getting ready to roll out.

03:32:58.650 --> 03:33:01.740
And if I wasn't going
out with the platoons,

03:33:01.740 --> 03:33:04.368
I would go out see the guys
off, give them a hands salute

03:33:04.368 --> 03:33:05.160
as they're leaving.

03:33:05.160 --> 03:33:06.827
And I'm like, how are
you feeling, Marc?

03:33:06.827 --> 03:33:07.660
How you doing, Marc?

03:33:07.660 --> 03:33:08.550
Are you good to go?

03:33:08.550 --> 03:33:10.920
And he's like,
feeling lucky, sir.

03:33:10.920 --> 03:33:12.960
That was his attitude.

03:33:12.960 --> 03:33:15.510
And he's a guy that's going
to drive by that vehicle

03:33:15.510 --> 03:33:17.778
graveyard, drive right
out of that city,

03:33:17.778 --> 03:33:20.320
and he's going to do it the next
day, and the day after that,

03:33:20.320 --> 03:33:21.278
and the day after that.

03:33:21.278 --> 03:33:25.977
So-- and like I said, that's
what the army guys are doing.

03:33:25.977 --> 03:33:27.810
That's what the Marine
Corps guys are doing.

03:33:27.810 --> 03:33:29.620
They're doing it.

03:33:29.620 --> 03:33:34.170
And so as much as the mythology
around basic SEAL training

03:33:34.170 --> 03:33:43.360
goes, to me that experience
in combat and what guys do

03:33:43.360 --> 03:33:48.340
is infinitely harder and
infinitely more important.

03:33:51.160 --> 03:33:54.961
Now, all that being
said, basic SEAL training

03:33:54.961 --> 03:33:58.090
is a very strange
laboratory for human beings.

03:33:58.090 --> 03:34:00.954
It is a very strange
laboratory for human beings.

03:34:00.954 --> 03:34:10.160
And it's crazy the way it works.

03:34:10.160 --> 03:34:13.570
It's obviously extremely
difficult, but there's no--

03:34:13.570 --> 03:34:16.050
I wouldn't put money.

03:34:16.050 --> 03:34:17.790
You could put odds
on somebody making it

03:34:17.790 --> 03:34:19.610
through like, hey,
that guy seems

03:34:19.610 --> 03:34:20.860
like he's going be good to go.

03:34:20.860 --> 03:34:23.070
But I wouldn't put a
bunch of money on it.

03:34:23.070 --> 03:34:26.160
And I wouldn't take like 100%,
I would never take 100% bet

03:34:26.160 --> 03:34:28.080
on anybody because
there's no one that's

03:34:28.080 --> 03:34:30.060
100% going to make it
through that training.

03:34:30.060 --> 03:34:33.963
And there's just
random-- some people say,

03:34:33.963 --> 03:34:35.130
well, it's because your why.

03:34:35.130 --> 03:34:36.480
There's people that make
it through SEAL training

03:34:36.480 --> 03:34:39.120
because their ex-girlfriend
said they couldn't make it.

03:34:39.120 --> 03:34:40.620
There's some other
guy that makes it

03:34:40.620 --> 03:34:43.962
through because they promised
God that they would make it.

03:34:43.962 --> 03:34:45.420
There's some other
guy that made it

03:34:45.420 --> 03:34:47.550
through because their dad
said they could never.

03:34:47.550 --> 03:34:50.092
There's lie every one of these
examples you can come up with.

03:34:50.092 --> 03:34:53.340
And it's good enough for some
random dude to make it through.

03:34:53.340 --> 03:34:55.320
And it doesn't matter
what your pedigree is.

03:34:55.320 --> 03:34:56.820
It doesn't matter
where you're from.

03:34:56.820 --> 03:34:58.140
There's guys from Iowa.

03:34:58.140 --> 03:34:59.580
There's guys from Florida.

03:34:59.580 --> 03:35:03.220
There's guys from
wherever that make it.

03:35:03.220 --> 03:35:05.470
And those guys from Iowa,
and those guys from Florida,

03:35:05.470 --> 03:35:06.940
and those guys from
wherever, that don't make it.

03:35:06.940 --> 03:35:07.900
Guys from farms.

03:35:07.900 --> 03:35:10.390
Guys from silver
spoon in their mouth,

03:35:10.390 --> 03:35:12.340
and you just can't predict it.

03:35:12.340 --> 03:35:15.280
And I mean, it has to have
something to do with the fact

03:35:15.280 --> 03:35:18.250
that how bad you
actually want to do it.

03:35:21.020 --> 03:35:23.120
That's-- it's a strange thing.

03:35:23.120 --> 03:35:26.150
And I wouldn't try and--

03:35:26.150 --> 03:35:29.672
if I was in the world, if
I didn't do that training,

03:35:29.672 --> 03:35:31.880
I wouldn't be trying to
figure out if I could make it

03:35:31.880 --> 03:35:34.570
or not because you don't know.

03:35:34.570 --> 03:35:37.520
You don't know.

03:35:37.520 --> 03:35:39.710
It's a very strange thing.

03:35:39.710 --> 03:35:44.970
And it's so mythical
almost right now.

03:35:44.970 --> 03:35:48.660
It's mythical that
how hard it is.

03:35:48.660 --> 03:35:51.550
And this is not too many
people make it through, man.

03:35:51.550 --> 03:35:52.050
It's--

03:35:52.050 --> 03:35:53.033
ANDREW HUBERMAN: 15%.

03:35:53.033 --> 03:35:53.950
JOCKO WILLINK: --yeah.

03:35:53.950 --> 03:35:55.658
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah,
from all the folks

03:35:55.658 --> 03:35:59.280
that I've talked to there, or
gone through, been instructors

03:35:59.280 --> 03:36:02.580
there, some we
both know, it seem

03:36:02.580 --> 03:36:06.480
that that 15% number is unlikely
to change as long as they

03:36:06.480 --> 03:36:08.110
keep the process the same.

03:36:08.110 --> 03:36:10.290
It just seems
about 15% of people

03:36:10.290 --> 03:36:13.200
seem to have something
in them that can perhaps

03:36:13.200 --> 03:36:14.400
grow during that training.

03:36:14.400 --> 03:36:18.480
But that it is being
identified and selected for

03:36:18.480 --> 03:36:20.400
rather than somehow
being built up

03:36:20.400 --> 03:36:23.310
across at least that phase,
the early phases of training.

03:36:23.310 --> 03:36:26.220
And then at some point they
build on that capacity.

03:36:26.220 --> 03:36:29.700
And this gets to this really
somewhat controversial issue

03:36:29.700 --> 03:36:32.370
frankly, are people
wired differently?

03:36:32.370 --> 03:36:36.510
And listen, I started off
in neural development.

03:36:36.510 --> 03:36:39.060
And I'll tell you that there
are some universal properties

03:36:39.060 --> 03:36:41.910
of neural development
in all surviving humans.

03:36:41.910 --> 03:36:44.100
Like that you're going
to breathe without having

03:36:44.100 --> 03:36:44.850
to think about it.

03:36:44.850 --> 03:36:45.870
Your heart is going
to beat without having

03:36:45.870 --> 03:36:46.620
to think about it.

03:36:46.620 --> 03:36:49.470
But beyond that, there's a lot
of variation in natural levels

03:36:49.470 --> 03:36:52.800
of dopamine and serotonin.

03:36:52.800 --> 03:36:55.943
There's nature plays a
powerful role and nurture.

03:36:55.943 --> 03:36:57.360
And what's
interesting, though, is

03:36:57.360 --> 03:37:00.360
we can't always
predict from parents

03:37:00.360 --> 03:37:01.560
what nature is going to do.

03:37:01.560 --> 03:37:03.185
Recently we had
someone on the podcast.

03:37:03.185 --> 03:37:05.550
I'm excited for you to listen
to it if perhaps you will

03:37:05.550 --> 03:37:06.383
if I send it to you.

03:37:06.383 --> 03:37:10.500
There's a guy who talks about
inheritable acquired traits.

03:37:10.500 --> 03:37:12.360
You don't expect that
because you work out

03:37:12.360 --> 03:37:14.340
that your grandkids
will be more muscular

03:37:14.340 --> 03:37:15.990
and have better endurance.

03:37:15.990 --> 03:37:17.670
But there's actually
some evidence

03:37:17.670 --> 03:37:19.230
that that may be the case.

03:37:19.230 --> 03:37:20.850
And you go, well,
how could that be?

03:37:20.850 --> 03:37:22.480
We got two kinds of
cells in your body.

03:37:22.480 --> 03:37:23.280
It turns out you
have what are called

03:37:23.280 --> 03:37:24.570
somatic cells, which
are all of them.

03:37:24.570 --> 03:37:26.028
Then you have the
germ cells, which

03:37:26.028 --> 03:37:28.560
are your sperm and
your wife's eggs.

03:37:28.560 --> 03:37:32.400
Well, why wouldn't the DNA of
the sperm cells and the egg

03:37:32.400 --> 03:37:34.290
cells be modifiable
by experience

03:37:34.290 --> 03:37:35.490
if all the other cells are?

03:37:35.490 --> 03:37:37.740
And it turns out there's
some evidence that maybe it's

03:37:37.740 --> 03:37:39.780
not the DNA but the rNRA.

03:37:39.780 --> 03:37:40.650
Think about that.

03:37:40.650 --> 03:37:42.540
That means that whether-- and
we know this that people that

03:37:42.540 --> 03:37:44.700
have been in a famine,
several generations later,

03:37:44.700 --> 03:37:47.350
their implications for
blood sugar regulation

03:37:47.350 --> 03:37:48.940
in their great grandkids.

03:37:48.940 --> 03:37:52.110
So the idea that experience
and acquired traits

03:37:52.110 --> 03:37:56.420
can change us is actually
has some validity.

03:37:56.420 --> 03:37:58.380
And this gets into
really complicated things

03:37:58.380 --> 03:37:59.880
to people who go, oh, this
is like the giraffe that

03:37:59.880 --> 03:38:01.860
had to crane its neck,
and then gave birth

03:38:01.860 --> 03:38:03.150
to longer necked giraffes.

03:38:03.150 --> 03:38:07.860
And it's like, well, not exactly
but also not entirely untrue

03:38:07.860 --> 03:38:08.400
either.

03:38:08.400 --> 03:38:11.880
So I love the idea that
there are inherited traits

03:38:11.880 --> 03:38:14.640
and that nature and
nurture play a role,

03:38:14.640 --> 03:38:19.050
but that hard work may actually
transmit across generations.

03:38:19.050 --> 03:38:20.970
JOCKO WILLINK: Yeah,
there's in SEAL training

03:38:20.970 --> 03:38:24.090
you have kids that come through
that they call legacies, which

03:38:24.090 --> 03:38:25.680
means that they have a dad--

03:38:25.680 --> 03:38:28.680
I think a dad,
brother, whatever.

03:38:28.680 --> 03:38:32.670
And they do have a better
chance of making it,

03:38:32.670 --> 03:38:35.730
but it's not a
guaranteed chance at all.

03:38:35.730 --> 03:38:38.910
And my personal
opinion is I think

03:38:38.910 --> 03:38:42.000
a legacy kid would have a
better chance of making it just

03:38:42.000 --> 03:38:44.622
due to the Thanksgiving
dinner that you're

03:38:44.622 --> 03:38:46.830
going to have to go through
for the rest of your life

03:38:46.830 --> 03:38:48.000
with your family--

03:38:48.000 --> 03:38:48.660
ANDREW HUBERMAN: If you don't.

03:38:48.660 --> 03:38:50.577
JOCKO WILLINK: --yeah,
if you're even invited,

03:38:50.577 --> 03:38:53.010
which you might be on your own.

03:38:53.010 --> 03:38:56.260
But yeah, maybe there's
something to that as well.

03:38:56.260 --> 03:38:59.660
But I think that's
just more the pressure

03:38:59.660 --> 03:39:02.270
that someone must feel
like, hey, there's

03:39:02.270 --> 03:39:05.480
no way I'm going to be
allowed back in my home

03:39:05.480 --> 03:39:07.530
if I don't make it
through this training.

03:39:07.530 --> 03:39:10.250
So I'm going to have to just
go ahead and suck it up.

03:39:10.250 --> 03:39:13.010
But not everyone makes it.

03:39:13.010 --> 03:39:15.355
And it's a bummer
when that does occur.

03:39:15.355 --> 03:39:16.730
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Well, for people

03:39:16.730 --> 03:39:19.130
who are not thinking about going
through SEAL training, or who

03:39:19.130 --> 03:39:21.422
miss the opportunity, or who
are not interested in that

03:39:21.422 --> 03:39:23.750
for whatever
reason, do you think

03:39:23.750 --> 03:39:26.600
there's value to
doing things each day

03:39:26.600 --> 03:39:28.430
that suck a little bit?

03:39:28.430 --> 03:39:30.410
Or from time to
time doing something

03:39:30.410 --> 03:39:33.497
that puts one into a state
of deliberate discomfort?

03:39:33.497 --> 03:39:34.580
JOCKO WILLINK: Yeah, 100%.

03:39:34.580 --> 03:39:36.140
Yeah, I mean, 100%.

03:39:36.140 --> 03:39:39.140
I mean, even in order
to improve yourself,

03:39:39.140 --> 03:39:42.890
you've got to impose some
discipline on yourself.

03:39:42.890 --> 03:39:44.540
If you want to get
stronger, you've

03:39:44.540 --> 03:39:46.855
got to do things that
require strength.

03:39:46.855 --> 03:39:48.230
If you want to be
tougher, you've

03:39:48.230 --> 03:39:50.270
got to do things that
require you to be tougher.

03:39:50.270 --> 03:39:51.890
I think that's pretty
straightforward.

03:39:51.890 --> 03:39:52.940
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Does
that mean doing things

03:39:52.940 --> 03:39:54.060
that are not pleasurable?

03:39:54.060 --> 03:39:57.950
So for instance, I've
done some long podcasts.

03:39:57.950 --> 03:40:02.493
And a few weeks ago, we were
doing a series with Andy Galpin

03:40:02.493 --> 03:40:04.160
all about exercise
and exercise science.

03:40:04.160 --> 03:40:06.743
And we did six podcasts in that
week, the most I've ever done,

03:40:06.743 --> 03:40:09.740
which made doing four or five
the next week not so bad.

03:40:09.740 --> 03:40:11.840
But I loved every second of it.

03:40:11.840 --> 03:40:14.090
And I love every
second of podcasting.

03:40:14.090 --> 03:40:17.707
And so it didn't suck, but it
built up a greater capacity.

03:40:17.707 --> 03:40:19.790
I guess, I'm asking
specifically about things that

03:40:19.790 --> 03:40:21.740
really feel like a splinter.

03:40:21.740 --> 03:40:23.690
Is there any value to that?

03:40:23.690 --> 03:40:26.090
Because I have to say there
are some people I know,

03:40:26.090 --> 03:40:27.950
some of them are former team--

03:40:27.950 --> 03:40:30.928
are team guys, I guess, you
don't say former team guys.

03:40:30.928 --> 03:40:33.470
They are out of the Teams now,
but they're team guys forever,

03:40:33.470 --> 03:40:36.980
who seem to not be
rattled by little things.

03:40:36.980 --> 03:40:38.878
Those guys in particular,
they don't seem

03:40:38.878 --> 03:40:40.170
to be rattled by little things.

03:40:40.170 --> 03:40:44.500
And then I know people that
they get the wrong size

03:40:44.500 --> 03:40:46.250
coffee at a coffee
shop, and they dissolve

03:40:46.250 --> 03:40:48.260
into a puddle of tears.

03:40:48.260 --> 03:40:50.000
So there does seem
to be something

03:40:50.000 --> 03:40:51.980
to this whole like
mental resilience thing

03:40:51.980 --> 03:40:53.900
and flexibility thing.

03:40:53.900 --> 03:40:58.910
And I try and do something
that's uncomfortable to me

03:40:58.910 --> 03:41:01.325
about once a week, something
I really don't like.

03:41:04.270 --> 03:41:06.270
It doesn't matter what
that is, but I try and do

03:41:06.270 --> 03:41:08.738
something that's unpleasant.

03:41:08.738 --> 03:41:10.530
Or do something in a
way that's unpleasant.

03:41:10.530 --> 03:41:13.108
I guess, the example would be
getting into the cold water

03:41:13.108 --> 03:41:14.400
the first thing in the morning.

03:41:14.400 --> 03:41:17.700
And making that decision
from under the blankets

03:41:17.700 --> 03:41:19.830
is a rough one for me.

03:41:19.830 --> 03:41:20.933
But then it gets easier.

03:41:20.933 --> 03:41:23.100
And then you wonder, is it
still serving the purpose

03:41:23.100 --> 03:41:24.570
that it's building me up?

03:41:24.570 --> 03:41:28.350
So should people seek
truly bad experiences

03:41:28.350 --> 03:41:30.277
provided that they're
done in a safe way?

03:41:30.277 --> 03:41:31.110
JOCKO WILLINK: Yeah.

03:41:31.110 --> 03:41:33.930
Yeah.

03:41:33.930 --> 03:41:36.750
I think that you're
going to, just like you,

03:41:36.750 --> 03:41:39.690
would develop your
legs by doing squats.

03:41:39.690 --> 03:41:42.330
And you would develop your
back by doing pull ups.

03:41:42.330 --> 03:41:44.610
I think you would
develop your resiliency

03:41:44.610 --> 03:41:49.800
by doing repetitions of things
that require you to be tougher.

03:41:49.800 --> 03:41:51.300
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
That actually suck.

03:41:51.300 --> 03:41:52.160
OK, good.

03:41:52.160 --> 03:41:54.660
And the reason I ask this is
because I think a lot of people

03:41:54.660 --> 03:41:56.118
think, well, I
work out every day.

03:41:56.118 --> 03:41:58.410
But then you probe them a
little bit, and they're like,

03:41:58.410 --> 03:41:59.557
but I love exercise.

03:41:59.557 --> 03:42:01.140
And then well, then
that doesn't quite

03:42:01.140 --> 03:42:03.450
qualify as something
that makes you tougher.

03:42:03.450 --> 03:42:06.090
Or they think oh, the last
reps of a set are really tough.

03:42:06.090 --> 03:42:08.940
But if you love hitting
failure on a set because that's

03:42:08.940 --> 03:42:09.900
what I seek in the gym.

03:42:09.900 --> 03:42:11.820
I love that aspect
of the training.

03:42:11.820 --> 03:42:13.230
That's actually where I
know I'm getting better.

03:42:13.230 --> 03:42:15.120
It no longer serves as
resilience training,

03:42:15.120 --> 03:42:17.745
it more just serves as training.

03:42:17.745 --> 03:42:19.620
In any case, I think
that the point is clear,

03:42:19.620 --> 03:42:21.600
and I appreciate your answer.

03:42:21.600 --> 03:42:23.910
I have to ask about something.

03:42:23.910 --> 03:42:25.950
This is going to seem
like a total divergence

03:42:25.950 --> 03:42:29.310
but it's not, which is animals.

03:42:29.310 --> 03:42:30.810
Because first of
all, they're a love

03:42:30.810 --> 03:42:33.270
of mine in terms of
understanding the animal

03:42:33.270 --> 03:42:35.490
kingdom and placing humans
into the animal kingdom.

03:42:35.490 --> 03:42:38.760
Second of all, I
know you're a hunter.

03:42:38.760 --> 03:42:41.160
And also I know you own dogs.

03:42:41.160 --> 03:42:44.580
And the question I have is,
do you ever look at people,

03:42:44.580 --> 03:42:47.610
or did you ever work
in Teams of guys

03:42:47.610 --> 03:42:50.653
when you were on active duty--

03:42:50.653 --> 03:42:52.320
see that the difference
is you mentioned

03:42:52.320 --> 03:42:54.570
before, this person is really
good at problem solving,

03:42:54.570 --> 03:42:57.605
this person's a little
bit more creative.

03:42:57.605 --> 03:42:58.980
Do you ever wonder
whether or not

03:42:58.980 --> 03:43:03.810
people embody different
animal archetypes?

03:43:03.810 --> 03:43:04.800
Because I do.

03:43:04.800 --> 03:43:07.790
JOCKO WILLINK: Well, that thing
where people say that dogs

03:43:07.790 --> 03:43:10.290
or owners look like their dogs,
dogs look like their owners,

03:43:10.290 --> 03:43:11.070
I think that's--

03:43:11.070 --> 03:43:12.642
I've seen all kinds of examples.

03:43:12.642 --> 03:43:14.100
You can go on the
internet and find

03:43:14.100 --> 03:43:16.200
a bunch of examples
of dogs that look

03:43:16.200 --> 03:43:18.570
like their owners and owners
that look like their dogs.

03:43:18.570 --> 03:43:20.050
So I think that's true.

03:43:20.050 --> 03:43:23.803
And I think my dog is awesome.

03:43:23.803 --> 03:43:25.470
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Tell
me about your dog.

03:43:25.470 --> 03:43:26.280
What kind of dog is it?

03:43:26.280 --> 03:43:28.072
JOCKO WILLINK: My dog
is a German Shepherd.

03:43:28.072 --> 03:43:33.690
His name is Odin, and
he's an awesome dog.

03:43:33.690 --> 03:43:35.250
And he's got a personality.

03:43:35.250 --> 03:43:36.930
He's got an interesting
personality.

03:43:36.930 --> 03:43:46.065
So like he doesn't
like to cuddle.

03:43:46.065 --> 03:43:48.190
My kids will be like oh,
he doesn't like to cuddle.

03:43:48.190 --> 03:43:48.790
No.

03:43:48.790 --> 03:43:52.060
Even when we go to
bed at night, he

03:43:52.060 --> 03:43:55.650
goes to 4 feet away
from the foot of my bed.

03:43:55.650 --> 03:43:57.058
Even if I was
like, hey, jump up.

03:43:57.058 --> 03:43:58.350
I've told him, jump up in here.

03:43:58.350 --> 03:43:59.922
We want to pet you.

03:43:59.922 --> 03:44:01.380
He'll jump up in
there, and he just

03:44:01.380 --> 03:44:04.530
like goes in that
low crouch position.

03:44:04.530 --> 03:44:07.505
And then waits until
I say, free dog,

03:44:07.505 --> 03:44:09.630
and then he goes back down,
and he goes 4 feet away

03:44:09.630 --> 03:44:11.460
from the foot of the
bed and sits there.

03:44:11.460 --> 03:44:15.150
Because that's
his personality is

03:44:15.150 --> 03:44:19.170
to protect, and set
security, and do his job.

03:44:19.170 --> 03:44:20.890
And that's what he's like.

03:44:20.890 --> 03:44:23.805
And so you've got
other dogs that are--

03:44:26.580 --> 03:44:28.950
they're in a totally
different mindset.

03:44:28.950 --> 03:44:31.590
So yeah, dogs have
definite personalities.

03:44:31.590 --> 03:44:34.800
And look, I also have--

03:44:34.800 --> 03:44:36.120
it's not all genetic.

03:44:36.120 --> 03:44:42.595
So two of my friends got
dogs that were brothers.

03:44:42.595 --> 03:44:43.470
What are they called?

03:44:43.470 --> 03:44:45.215
Dogo Argentinos.

03:44:45.215 --> 03:44:45.840
You know those?

03:44:45.840 --> 03:44:46.350
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Oh, the Dogoes?

03:44:46.350 --> 03:44:48.150
Yeah, those are hard
to get in the US.

03:44:48.150 --> 03:44:50.310
They're not-- They
might not even

03:44:50.310 --> 03:44:52.380
be legal in the United
States, so don't tell me

03:44:52.380 --> 03:44:53.070
who these people are.

03:44:53.070 --> 03:44:54.300
Not that I care, but I'm
not going to report them.

03:44:54.300 --> 03:44:55.110
JOCKO WILLINK: I won't
tell you who they are.

03:44:55.110 --> 03:44:56.027
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

03:44:56.027 --> 03:44:57.660
JOCKO WILLINK: But
these two guys--

03:44:57.660 --> 03:45:00.270
and those two guys had
different personalities.

03:45:00.270 --> 03:45:02.850
One guy is a very
happy-go-lucky,

03:45:02.850 --> 03:45:07.200
likes to smoke pot, likes
to hang out, very just

03:45:07.200 --> 03:45:09.655
a chill, playful guy.

03:45:09.655 --> 03:45:10.530
The other guy is not.

03:45:10.530 --> 03:45:14.610
He's the opposite
in every category.

03:45:14.610 --> 03:45:16.740
And they both got these dogs.

03:45:16.740 --> 03:45:20.640
And you fast
forward like a year,

03:45:20.640 --> 03:45:24.180
the dog that was owned
by the playful guy,

03:45:24.180 --> 03:45:27.240
his dog was just a
big puppy licking

03:45:27.240 --> 03:45:29.580
at-- just wagging the tail.

03:45:29.580 --> 03:45:32.670
The other dog, you had
to keep it in a cage

03:45:32.670 --> 03:45:34.810
or it would murder
everything in sight.

03:45:34.810 --> 03:45:37.620
And these dogs were brothers
from the same litter,

03:45:37.620 --> 03:45:39.700
and they were
completely opposite.

03:45:39.700 --> 03:45:47.040
And so I think it has a lot
more to do with nurture than it

03:45:47.040 --> 03:45:48.060
does to do with nature.

03:45:48.060 --> 03:45:53.550
But that being said, when
you look at Malinois I mean,

03:45:53.550 --> 03:45:56.790
Malinois have a personality
that is very distinct compared

03:45:56.790 --> 03:45:57.840
to a German Shepherd.

03:45:57.840 --> 03:45:59.700
Now, look there's
outlying Malinois,

03:45:59.700 --> 03:46:01.260
there's outlying
German Shepherds,

03:46:01.260 --> 03:46:04.650
there's outlying you name
whatever kind of Golden

03:46:04.650 --> 03:46:09.630
Retriever or whatever dog is
known for being more playful.

03:46:09.630 --> 03:46:13.800
You get around the Malinois,
Malinois are Malinois.

03:46:13.800 --> 03:46:14.730
And they're that way.

03:46:14.730 --> 03:46:15.930
Have you been around
Malinois before?

03:46:15.930 --> 03:46:16.620
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Mm-hmm.

03:46:16.620 --> 03:46:17.160
Oh, uh--

03:46:17.160 --> 03:46:17.930
JOCKO WILLINK: The
Belgian Malinois?

03:46:17.930 --> 03:46:19.472
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Yeah, a few of them.

03:46:19.472 --> 03:46:23.820
My neighbor has one, and that
thing is not terribly friendly.

03:46:23.820 --> 03:46:26.610
But it's a security dog,
so I don't expect it to be.

03:46:26.610 --> 03:46:27.568
JOCKO WILLINK: Exactly.

03:46:27.568 --> 03:46:29.790
ANDREW HUBERMAN: It's
yeah, I know they use them

03:46:29.790 --> 03:46:30.960
for work in the Teams.

03:46:30.960 --> 03:46:32.982
And I've heard that you
have to keep a close eye

03:46:32.982 --> 03:46:35.190
on your relationship with
them because if you get lax

03:46:35.190 --> 03:46:36.418
about it, they'll bite you.

03:46:36.418 --> 03:46:37.710
JOCKO WILLINK: That's for true.

03:46:37.710 --> 03:46:44.060
The first time I saw one
we were doing a drill using

03:46:44.060 --> 03:46:45.060
dogs for the first time.

03:46:45.060 --> 03:46:47.770
And then one of the Team
guide dog handlers came out.

03:46:47.770 --> 03:46:49.710
And so we hit this
target building,

03:46:49.710 --> 03:46:52.057
and they prebriefed
us like, hey, you

03:46:52.057 --> 03:46:53.640
hit this target
building, and this guy

03:46:53.640 --> 03:46:56.400
is going to be a
runner, a squirter.

03:46:56.400 --> 03:46:59.100
And so we pull up
in the Humvees,

03:46:59.100 --> 03:47:01.620
assault team jumps out,
I'm staying external,

03:47:01.620 --> 03:47:03.550
I want to see what's
going to happen.

03:47:03.550 --> 03:47:06.030
So the squirter
goes running off.

03:47:06.030 --> 03:47:09.630
And the dog handler--

03:47:09.630 --> 03:47:15.352
whatever-- tracks his dog on
this guy, and then releases it,

03:47:15.352 --> 03:47:16.560
gives him whatever commands--

03:47:16.560 --> 03:47:17.060
[GROWLS]

03:47:17.060 --> 03:47:18.900
--or whatever the commands are.

03:47:18.900 --> 03:47:22.113
That thing is totally primed.

03:47:22.113 --> 03:47:24.030
Unlike anything you've
ever seen in your life.

03:47:24.030 --> 03:47:25.020
It is just primed.

03:47:25.020 --> 03:47:26.640
It's tracking that guy.

03:47:26.640 --> 03:47:28.650
He hits that release
on that leash,

03:47:28.650 --> 03:47:32.130
and that thing takes off
at 1,000 miles an hour.

03:47:32.130 --> 03:47:36.210
It jumps like, I'm
not kidding, 15 feet,

03:47:36.210 --> 03:47:41.580
maybe 10 feet in the air away
and just chomps onto this dude.

03:47:41.580 --> 03:47:42.540
The dude goes down.

03:47:42.540 --> 03:47:45.255
It was freaking awesome.

03:47:45.255 --> 03:47:47.880
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah, I've seen
some videos of those Malinois.

03:47:47.880 --> 03:47:48.672
I guess, I didn't--

03:47:48.672 --> 03:47:50.088
forgive me because
before I didn't

03:47:50.088 --> 03:47:52.290
know what you were referring
to Belgian Malinois.

03:47:52.290 --> 03:47:54.090
Those dogs like
running up trees,

03:47:54.090 --> 03:47:56.900
jumping over little rivers.

03:47:56.900 --> 03:47:57.990
Yeah, it's crazy.

03:47:57.990 --> 03:47:59.580
Incredibly powerful animals.

03:47:59.580 --> 03:48:00.150
Yeah.

03:48:00.150 --> 03:48:02.223
Well, the idea that
they mimic their owners

03:48:02.223 --> 03:48:04.140
has me a little concerned
because my last dog,

03:48:04.140 --> 03:48:04.650
I had to put him down.

03:48:04.650 --> 03:48:05.775
It was my bulldog Costello.

03:48:05.775 --> 03:48:07.530
He's a Bulldog Mastiff.

03:48:07.530 --> 03:48:11.945
I got him because I went to
pick out a puppy, basically.

03:48:11.945 --> 03:48:13.570
And there were eight
of these bulldogs.

03:48:13.570 --> 03:48:14.730
And all of them
were running around,

03:48:14.730 --> 03:48:16.350
and then there's one in the
background just eating out

03:48:16.350 --> 03:48:17.400
of all of their bowls.

03:48:17.400 --> 03:48:19.185
And I was like, I want that one.

03:48:19.185 --> 03:48:21.030
Big bulldog, biggest
one in the litter.

03:48:21.030 --> 03:48:23.670
Laziest creature.

03:48:23.670 --> 03:48:26.200
Not just dog but laziest
creature that ever existed.

03:48:26.200 --> 03:48:28.598
But if you need to
activate, he would.

03:48:28.598 --> 03:48:30.390
He was just very
efficient with his energy.

03:48:30.390 --> 03:48:33.000
And I don't think I have
a bulldog personality,

03:48:33.000 --> 03:48:35.550
and that's why I got
him to balance me out.

03:48:35.550 --> 03:48:39.090
Never retrieved,
never did anything.

03:48:39.090 --> 03:48:42.510
Stole and destroyed every toy,
every dog park in San Diego.

03:48:42.510 --> 03:48:43.950
He was famous there.

03:48:43.950 --> 03:48:47.013
I had to bring $5 bills to
pay people for all the balls

03:48:47.013 --> 03:48:48.180
and things he would destroy.

03:48:48.180 --> 03:48:50.520
So anyway, my apologies
to all the dog owners.

03:48:50.520 --> 03:48:51.090
Not really.

03:48:51.090 --> 03:48:52.385
I miss him.

03:48:52.385 --> 03:48:54.760
Did you train your dog, or
did someone else in the house?

03:48:54.760 --> 03:48:55.110
JOCKO WILLINK: I did.

03:48:55.110 --> 03:48:56.568
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
And was he trained

03:48:56.568 --> 03:48:59.580
to be a security dog, or
family dog, or a mixture?

03:48:59.580 --> 03:49:03.495
JOCKO WILLINK: Yeah, I mean,
they're true working dogs.

03:49:03.495 --> 03:49:07.110
Unless you have the time
and effort to put into him,

03:49:07.110 --> 03:49:12.630
or you buy them that way, you
don't want one of those dogs

03:49:12.630 --> 03:49:14.430
that I was just
describing in your house.

03:49:14.430 --> 03:49:18.540
They're not for a house
unless there's that level.

03:49:18.540 --> 03:49:20.400
My dog is not that level.

03:49:20.400 --> 03:49:21.690
He's awesome.

03:49:21.690 --> 03:49:24.690
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Did you apply
some of the same principles

03:49:24.690 --> 03:49:28.200
that you use in leadership
of humans with your dog?

03:49:28.200 --> 03:49:30.810
JOCKO WILLINK: Yeah, there are
a lot of similar principles,

03:49:30.810 --> 03:49:34.890
but there's some differences.

03:49:34.890 --> 03:49:41.310
They're pack animals, and they
respond to the pack leader.

03:49:41.310 --> 03:49:41.940
it's funny.

03:49:41.940 --> 03:49:47.490
My dog obeys me as if
it's the command of God.

03:49:47.490 --> 03:49:51.210
And my wife, he's like
maybe I'll do what you say.

03:49:51.210 --> 03:49:53.432
So they pick up on that stuff.

03:49:53.432 --> 03:49:55.140
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Dogs
are very intuitive.

03:49:55.140 --> 03:49:57.570
I love this idea that I
was told early on that they

03:49:57.570 --> 03:49:58.770
can feel your emotions.

03:49:58.770 --> 03:50:02.250
I think they actually can
sense how we feel not just

03:50:02.250 --> 03:50:04.140
by the intonation of our voice.

03:50:04.140 --> 03:50:07.645
But I hope someday someone will
figure out in a noninvasive way

03:50:07.645 --> 03:50:09.270
because I don't like
the idea of people

03:50:09.270 --> 03:50:11.747
doing experiments on
dogs in an invasive way.

03:50:11.747 --> 03:50:13.080
Like what they're picking up on?

03:50:13.080 --> 03:50:15.538
Like for instance, we know that
sharks are paying attention

03:50:15.538 --> 03:50:18.360
to the amount of activity
in the lateral line of fish.

03:50:18.360 --> 03:50:20.492
Fish have these
stretches of neurons

03:50:20.492 --> 03:50:22.950
that they call the lateral line
that allows them to school,

03:50:22.950 --> 03:50:24.270
and know the distance
to different things,

03:50:24.270 --> 03:50:25.687
and be able to
steer around coral.

03:50:25.687 --> 03:50:26.607
They feel proximity.

03:50:26.607 --> 03:50:28.440
It would be like if
you're turning a corner,

03:50:28.440 --> 03:50:29.160
you go-- vrrt.

03:50:29.160 --> 03:50:32.700
--and they can even recognize
specific lateral line

03:50:32.700 --> 03:50:33.360
signatures.

03:50:33.360 --> 03:50:35.220
So it'd be like you and
Marc Lee walking together

03:50:35.220 --> 03:50:35.928
through the dark.

03:50:35.928 --> 03:50:37.170
But maybe people sift around.

03:50:37.170 --> 03:50:38.910
But you're like, you don't have
to look at them, that's Marc.

03:50:38.910 --> 03:50:40.470
You learn him intuitively.

03:50:40.470 --> 03:50:41.400
A fish can do that.

03:50:41.400 --> 03:50:46.600
Sharks can sense whether or
not the lateral line is--

03:50:46.600 --> 03:50:49.860
let's say vibrating but firing
at a particular frequency

03:50:49.860 --> 03:50:52.650
to know oh, that fish
is a little bit slower

03:50:52.650 --> 03:50:53.340
than the rest.

03:50:53.340 --> 03:50:56.280
I mean, hunting
animals just they

03:50:56.280 --> 03:50:58.020
develop these incredible senses.

03:50:58.020 --> 03:51:02.670
And I think humans have
some of these senses in more

03:51:02.670 --> 03:51:03.353
rudimentary way.

03:51:03.353 --> 03:51:05.520
We're just not forced to
use them unless, of course,

03:51:05.520 --> 03:51:09.270
you become a hunter of
animals, or a hunter of humans,

03:51:09.270 --> 03:51:11.310
and you tap into these
neural circuits that

03:51:11.310 --> 03:51:13.710
are very primitive and
hardwired in everybody.

03:51:13.710 --> 03:51:17.610
But of course, they're
honed in warriors.

03:51:17.610 --> 03:51:21.270
Well, I could spiral off into
animal biology in ways that

03:51:21.270 --> 03:51:22.960
truly would take us 26 hours.

03:51:22.960 --> 03:51:25.720
I don't want to do that.

03:51:25.720 --> 03:51:27.460
I'm almost hesitant
to ask this question,

03:51:27.460 --> 03:51:31.870
but I'm going to do it anyway.

03:51:31.870 --> 03:51:35.080
Many times online, you
are asked whether or not

03:51:35.080 --> 03:51:37.300
you will run for office?

03:51:37.300 --> 03:51:41.860
And I want to say that I
think it's a true compliment.

03:51:41.860 --> 03:51:44.860
I don't think people are asking
just to entertain themselves.

03:51:44.860 --> 03:51:47.560
I think that this
country certainly

03:51:47.560 --> 03:51:51.180
and a lot of the world is
desperate for certain kinds

03:51:51.180 --> 03:51:52.930
of leaders and people
that have experience

03:51:52.930 --> 03:51:55.870
in high risk, high consequence,
chaotic situations,

03:51:55.870 --> 03:51:59.740
and have shown prowess at
leadership in multiple domains.

03:51:59.740 --> 03:52:03.650
And you are certainly
one of those individuals.

03:52:03.650 --> 03:52:06.640
And so they ask for that
reason among others.

03:52:06.640 --> 03:52:09.130
And I've heard you
give your answer,

03:52:09.130 --> 03:52:10.720
and you can repeat
it again here.

03:52:10.720 --> 03:52:16.930
But as a more broad theme that I
think people are interested in,

03:52:16.930 --> 03:52:20.320
do you think it's an
important criteria

03:52:20.320 --> 03:52:23.020
or it would be
great to see people

03:52:23.020 --> 03:52:27.250
in positions of leadership
who've had wartime experience?

03:52:27.250 --> 03:52:29.500
And do you think that some
of the shifts that we've

03:52:29.500 --> 03:52:32.560
seen in terms of
patterns of leadership

03:52:32.560 --> 03:52:34.750
over the last-- let's
just make it real broad

03:52:34.750 --> 03:52:37.720
so that this isn't related
to any particular person

03:52:37.720 --> 03:52:39.890
or stretch of history--
but over the last,

03:52:39.890 --> 03:52:42.460
let's just say 25
years, reflect the fact

03:52:42.460 --> 03:52:44.410
that we haven't
seen a lot of that,

03:52:44.410 --> 03:52:47.410
at least at the top
tiers of leadership?

03:52:47.410 --> 03:52:50.100
JOCKO WILLINK: Yeah, I think
it'd be excellent if people--

03:52:50.100 --> 03:52:54.060
if the president had
military experience for sure.

03:52:54.060 --> 03:52:57.150
I think that then they
understand the way

03:52:57.150 --> 03:52:59.040
the military works better.

03:52:59.040 --> 03:53:01.978
They understand that each--
the civilians that control

03:53:01.978 --> 03:53:02.520
the military.

03:53:02.520 --> 03:53:04.680
Because a lot of times
people that are civilians

03:53:04.680 --> 03:53:07.920
don't understand that the
civilians control the military.

03:53:07.920 --> 03:53:14.500
And I think that you do get to
appreciate what war actually is

03:53:14.500 --> 03:53:16.930
and what the costs are.

03:53:16.930 --> 03:53:22.850
I think that I've seen in
the same vein of people

03:53:22.850 --> 03:53:25.550
asking me to run for
political office,

03:53:25.550 --> 03:53:27.890
I've heard, seen
comments saying, oh,

03:53:27.890 --> 03:53:29.570
that's what we
need, another war--

03:53:29.570 --> 03:53:31.820
a warmonger in office.

03:53:31.820 --> 03:53:34.020
And I've responded
a few of those.

03:53:34.020 --> 03:53:37.940
I think if there's any group
of people that don't want war,

03:53:37.940 --> 03:53:39.620
it's people that have seen it.

03:53:39.620 --> 03:53:44.660
People that understand
what the sacrifices are.

03:53:44.660 --> 03:53:48.860
And I think that
being in the military,

03:53:48.860 --> 03:53:50.280
people understand that better.

03:53:50.280 --> 03:53:52.460
So yeah, I think it'd be
a great qualification.

03:53:52.460 --> 03:53:53.758
I don't think it's mandatory.

03:53:53.758 --> 03:53:54.800
I mean, clearly it's not.

03:53:54.800 --> 03:53:57.050
We've had a bunch of
presidents that haven't ever

03:53:57.050 --> 03:53:58.065
served anything.

03:53:58.065 --> 03:54:00.440
Really, we've had a bunch of
presidents that haven't ever

03:54:00.440 --> 03:54:02.480
served anything but themselves.

03:54:02.480 --> 03:54:06.100
So yeah, hopefully, we'll
get some more people that

03:54:06.100 --> 03:54:08.590
have some experience
in the military,

03:54:08.590 --> 03:54:11.770
some combat experience
would be especially nice.

03:54:11.770 --> 03:54:15.118
And that would be
good in my opinion.

03:54:15.118 --> 03:54:16.910
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Well
I'm certainly not one

03:54:16.910 --> 03:54:18.620
to tell people what to do,
and I'm certainly not going

03:54:18.620 --> 03:54:19.578
to tell you what to do.

03:54:19.578 --> 03:54:21.110
But should you
ever choose to run,

03:54:21.110 --> 03:54:24.770
I would certainly be very
enthusiastic about that.

03:54:24.770 --> 03:54:29.542
And I will just say
that with that stated,

03:54:29.542 --> 03:54:31.250
I hope people do hear
what you just said.

03:54:31.250 --> 03:54:32.930
I share that sentiment.

03:54:32.930 --> 03:54:35.960
People who have led others
besides themselves, I think

03:54:35.960 --> 03:54:37.520
is the key statement there.

03:54:37.520 --> 03:54:38.353
JOCKO WILLINK: Yeah.

03:54:38.353 --> 03:54:39.350
And look, I just--

03:54:39.350 --> 03:54:41.300
I have friends that
are politicians,

03:54:41.300 --> 03:54:44.250
and I really appreciate
what they're doing.

03:54:44.250 --> 03:54:47.300
And it looks miserable to me.

03:54:47.300 --> 03:54:48.830
I don't like what--

03:54:48.830 --> 03:54:49.970
good for them.

03:54:49.970 --> 03:54:53.820
I'm happy that they're in there
trying to make a difference.

03:54:53.820 --> 03:54:57.810
And I guess, this is me being
selfish of me saying, look,

03:54:57.810 --> 03:54:59.520
I don't think I
could stomach that.

03:54:59.520 --> 03:55:06.260
And I also think that right
now, I'm trying to help out.

03:55:06.260 --> 03:55:08.420
For instance, we have
obviously got the leadership

03:55:08.420 --> 03:55:09.560
consulting in
Echelon Front, we're

03:55:09.560 --> 03:55:10.940
trying to help businesses grow.

03:55:10.940 --> 03:55:13.610
I've got Origin
USA, we're bringing

03:55:13.610 --> 03:55:15.050
manufacturing back to America.

03:55:15.050 --> 03:55:18.200
We've got 100, 400,
450 employees right

03:55:18.200 --> 03:55:21.770
now that are here in
America working and growing

03:55:21.770 --> 03:55:23.038
that business.

03:55:23.038 --> 03:55:24.830
Obviously, that
supplements-- so everything

03:55:24.830 --> 03:55:31.030
that I'm doing here right
now is to try and move

03:55:31.030 --> 03:55:33.760
the needle with America.

03:55:33.760 --> 03:55:36.340
Bringing manufacturing back,
helping the economy as much

03:55:36.340 --> 03:55:37.450
as I can right now.

03:55:37.450 --> 03:55:39.820
So that's what I'm
doing right now.

03:55:39.820 --> 03:55:43.240
And my standard answer,
which you alluded to,

03:55:43.240 --> 03:55:47.700
is if things got
bad enough, then

03:55:47.700 --> 03:55:50.750
I would do what I had to do.

03:55:50.750 --> 03:55:54.550
But I don't think people
appreciate my level of bad.

03:55:54.550 --> 03:55:56.770
I'm talking real bad.

03:55:56.770 --> 03:55:58.300
So it's not there yet.

03:55:58.300 --> 03:56:00.140
And hopefully, it
never will get there.

03:56:00.140 --> 03:56:03.280
I'd rather surf and
hang out with my friends

03:56:03.280 --> 03:56:06.790
and hang out with my
family than do that.

03:56:06.790 --> 03:56:12.850
And hopefully, America can
find some level of balance.

03:56:12.850 --> 03:56:15.190
I think that's the problem
that we're having right now.

03:56:15.190 --> 03:56:17.573
And a lot of these things
that you talked about,

03:56:17.573 --> 03:56:19.240
specifically the thing
you talked about,

03:56:19.240 --> 03:56:22.510
social media is not very
good for political balance.

03:56:22.510 --> 03:56:25.750
It's actually horrible
for political balance,

03:56:25.750 --> 03:56:27.885
and a lot of it has to
do with just the way

03:56:27.885 --> 03:56:29.260
that those
conversations are had.

03:56:29.260 --> 03:56:33.340
A lot of it has to do with ego
as well because I don't ever

03:56:33.340 --> 03:56:36.550
want to admit that I'm
wrong about anything.

03:56:36.550 --> 03:56:38.710
And if I can find
something that I

03:56:38.710 --> 03:56:40.630
think you might be
wrong about, it's

03:56:40.630 --> 03:56:44.200
so satisfying to my ego to
just call you out on that thing

03:56:44.200 --> 03:56:44.880
and attack you.

03:56:44.880 --> 03:56:47.380
And I think that's what a lot
of people are doing right now.

03:56:47.380 --> 03:56:51.172
Now, that being said, I also
usually say this as well.

03:56:51.172 --> 03:56:52.880
I travel around the
country all the time.

03:56:52.880 --> 03:56:55.193
I work with companies
of all sizes, work

03:56:55.193 --> 03:56:56.860
with people in every
different industry.

03:56:56.860 --> 03:57:00.640
And they're not sitting
around arguing with each other

03:57:00.640 --> 03:57:03.495
about the political scenery.

03:57:03.495 --> 03:57:05.870
They're talking about, hey,
how can we grow our business?

03:57:05.870 --> 03:57:06.910
How can we take
care of our workers?

03:57:06.910 --> 03:57:07.990
How can we take
care of our clients?

03:57:07.990 --> 03:57:09.532
How can we take care
of our customer?

03:57:09.532 --> 03:57:11.050
That's what people
are focused on.

03:57:11.050 --> 03:57:13.420
And when you jump
on social media,

03:57:13.420 --> 03:57:18.310
you can get sucked into the
political scene very easily.

03:57:18.310 --> 03:57:21.490
And that being said,
also we do have

03:57:21.490 --> 03:57:25.810
to pay attention
because we as citizens

03:57:25.810 --> 03:57:30.070
have to make sure
that America stays

03:57:30.070 --> 03:57:35.410
on the correct path within
the guardrails of what

03:57:35.410 --> 03:57:36.920
this country is based on.

03:57:36.920 --> 03:57:40.430
So we do have to pay attention.

03:57:40.430 --> 03:57:43.570
But I will be doing
my part as a civilian

03:57:43.570 --> 03:57:46.690
until there's total mayhem
and chaos in the streets.

03:57:46.690 --> 03:57:49.030
Then I'll probably just
be a benevolent dictator

03:57:49.030 --> 03:57:49.840
that takes over.

03:57:49.840 --> 03:57:50.417
[LAUGHS]

03:57:50.417 --> 03:57:52.750
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Should be
an interesting one, but, hey,

03:57:52.750 --> 03:57:55.060
you would be the man to
lead under any conditions.

03:57:55.060 --> 03:57:57.183
But thank you for
stating your threshold.

03:57:57.183 --> 03:57:58.600
Certainly, you've
earned the right

03:57:58.600 --> 03:58:01.130
to make whatever decision is
that you feel is right for you.

03:58:01.130 --> 03:58:03.200
And I want to say that I agree.

03:58:03.200 --> 03:58:05.200
I feel like we are a
country that still includes

03:58:05.200 --> 03:58:07.650
a ton of generators
and a ton of projectors

03:58:07.650 --> 03:58:09.400
that are interested
in projecting the good

03:58:09.400 --> 03:58:10.600
and growing the good.

03:58:10.600 --> 03:58:15.345
I do believe both those
phenotypes are important.

03:58:15.345 --> 03:58:16.720
I also want to
just say thank you

03:58:16.720 --> 03:58:20.380
for being a generator of
so much useful knowledge.

03:58:20.380 --> 03:58:25.120
In science, we have a saying,
which is somebody is an n of 1.

03:58:25.120 --> 03:58:28.150
This is a rare thing
to hear about oneself

03:58:28.150 --> 03:58:30.310
or to hear about somebody
because what it means

03:58:30.310 --> 03:58:32.800
is that somebody is in a
category in which pretty

03:58:32.800 --> 03:58:34.600
much everything that
they do and they say

03:58:34.600 --> 03:58:37.330
matters and serves
a purpose, which

03:58:37.330 --> 03:58:40.040
is a useful and important
building purpose.

03:58:40.040 --> 03:58:44.260
And I will look at you and tell
you that you are an n of 1.

03:58:44.260 --> 03:58:47.680
You certainly would meet that
criteria under any conditions.

03:58:47.680 --> 03:58:50.170
And it's evident in
the many companies

03:58:50.170 --> 03:58:52.600
that you're running and the
leadership that you're doing.

03:58:52.600 --> 03:58:54.100
And also in your
online presence.

03:58:54.100 --> 03:58:56.770
I mean, that's how I initially
came to learn about you.

03:58:56.770 --> 03:59:00.970
I'm now fortunate to have two
lengthy conversations with you

03:59:00.970 --> 03:59:02.980
and a few interspersed as well.

03:59:02.980 --> 03:59:06.370
And I think of myself as
a reasonable perhaps good

03:59:06.370 --> 03:59:08.740
observer of how people
behave in different domains.

03:59:08.740 --> 03:59:12.430
And every time you post,
or every time you speak,

03:59:12.430 --> 03:59:15.890
or every time you go on a
podcast or host a podcast,

03:59:15.890 --> 03:59:18.460
it's clear that not
only are you prepared

03:59:18.460 --> 03:59:23.250
and not only are you approaching
it with a spirit of seriousness

03:59:23.250 --> 03:59:24.910
that it deserves
but sometimes also

03:59:24.910 --> 03:59:26.140
lightness that it deserves.

03:59:26.140 --> 03:59:28.468
But there's always
an element of give

03:59:28.468 --> 03:59:30.760
and that you're trying to
encourage people to do better

03:59:30.760 --> 03:59:31.490
for themselves.

03:59:31.490 --> 03:59:34.060
So as somebody who's greatly
benefited from the knowledge

03:59:34.060 --> 03:59:36.435
that you've put out there from
the very first Tim Ferriss

03:59:36.435 --> 03:59:38.680
and Joe Rogan episodes
to your own podcast,

03:59:38.680 --> 03:59:40.870
I want to extend
a personal thanks.

03:59:40.870 --> 03:59:42.850
I also want to
extend great thanks

03:59:42.850 --> 03:59:44.410
for coming on here
today, talking

03:59:44.410 --> 03:59:46.750
to a geek scientist
who also happens

03:59:46.750 --> 03:59:47.950
to be a fellow punk rocker.

03:59:47.950 --> 03:59:51.190
Because that spirit and
the heart that's behind it,

03:59:51.190 --> 03:59:53.950
I think some people think it's
all about noise and chaos,

03:59:53.950 --> 03:59:56.053
it's actually about being
really true to yourself.

03:59:56.053 --> 03:59:57.970
That's how I think about
the punk rock spirit.

03:59:57.970 --> 03:59:59.110
It's really about
being true to yourself

03:59:59.110 --> 04:00:00.910
and realizing that the
thing that you like,

04:00:00.910 --> 04:00:02.560
while it might be
quite different,

04:00:02.560 --> 04:00:06.160
is actually, if that's you,
you have to live in that vein

04:00:06.160 --> 04:00:06.970
and stick with it.

04:00:06.970 --> 04:00:08.200
It certainly served
me well, and it

04:00:08.200 --> 04:00:09.492
sounds like it served you well.

04:00:09.492 --> 04:00:13.498
But mostly I just want to
extend an enormous thank you.

04:00:13.498 --> 04:00:15.040
As a civilian, thank
you for the work

04:00:15.040 --> 04:00:17.260
you did in the military
but also teaching people

04:00:17.260 --> 04:00:18.088
about the military.

04:00:18.088 --> 04:00:19.630
I think a lot of
people don't realize

04:00:19.630 --> 04:00:21.430
what it's about at any level.

04:00:21.430 --> 04:00:23.200
And learning about
your experience

04:00:23.200 --> 04:00:25.090
there, and what you've
observed, bringing

04:00:25.090 --> 04:00:28.030
other people's experiences
from the military more broadly

04:00:28.030 --> 04:00:29.137
is super important.

04:00:29.137 --> 04:00:30.970
And sharing this and
being able to entertain

04:00:30.970 --> 04:00:32.710
some of my scientific riffs.

04:00:32.710 --> 04:00:37.620
So thank you, thank
you, thank you.

04:00:37.620 --> 04:00:40.710
JOCKO WILLINK: Well,
I appreciate it.

04:00:40.710 --> 04:00:42.947
it's weird you say all
these nice things to me.

04:00:42.947 --> 04:00:44.280
I definitely don't deserve them.

04:00:44.280 --> 04:00:47.670
I'm a regular dude
that just showed up,

04:00:47.670 --> 04:00:50.970
I guess, at the right
time and told some stories

04:00:50.970 --> 04:00:53.280
about some guys that
were true heroes,

04:00:53.280 --> 04:00:57.960
and just trying to
share my perspective.

04:00:57.960 --> 04:01:00.060
But it's not just
my perspective,

04:01:00.060 --> 04:01:03.120
I'm talking about
stories that I lived.

04:01:03.120 --> 04:01:04.505
But there's plenty
of people that

04:01:04.505 --> 04:01:05.880
have done way more
than I've ever

04:01:05.880 --> 04:01:10.410
done and sacrificed infinitely
more than I ever sacrificed.

